/**
 * Teach Me explanations for economics, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["economics"] = {
 "|Which of the following best defines 'opportunity cost'?|The total cost of producing a good|The value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made|The cost of raw materials only|The market price of a product": "Because resources are scarce and wants are unlimited, every choice means giving something up. Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. If Chidi has ₦5,000 and his scale of preference ranks a textbook first and a pair of sandals second, buying the textbook costs him the sandals: that is the opportunity cost, not the ₦5,000. It is real cost measured in alternatives, not money: B.\nMemory tip: Opportunity cost = the \"second choice\" you let go.",
 "|The diagram shows supply and demand curves. At price P₁, there is a situation of:|Equilibrium|Excess demand (shortage)|Excess supply (surplus)|Zero demand": "Equilibrium is where the demand and supply curves cross (point E): quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. In the diagram, P₁ lies below the equilibrium price. At this low price buyers want more (Qd moves right along the demand curve) while sellers offer less (Qs moves left along the supply curve). So Qd is greater than Qs, and the gap Qd − Qs is excess demand, a shortage: B. The shortage makes buyers bid the price up until equilibrium is restored.\nThe trap: C (excess supply) is the answer when the price is ABOVE equilibrium, so always check which side of E the price line lies.\nMemory tip: Price too Low → shortage; price too High → surplus (\"Low-Short, High-Heap\").",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of a perfectly competitive market?|Few large sellers|Product differentiation|High barriers to entry|Many buyers and sellers dealing in a homogeneous product": "Perfect competition has: many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous (identical) product, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge of the market, and perfect mobility of factors. Because each firm is tiny and the product is identical, no firm can affect the price; each is a price-taker facing a horizontal demand curve: D. Few large sellers describes oligopoly, product differentiation describes monopolistic competition, and high barriers to entry describe monopoly.\nMemory tip: Perfect competition = \"many, same, free, know\" (many firms, same product, free entry, full knowledge).",
 "|A fall in the price of good A leads to a fall in the demand for good B. This means goods A and B are:|Complements|Substitutes|Inferior goods|Giffen goods": "Substitutes are goods that serve the same purpose, such as Peak and Three Crowns milk, or rice and beans for a meal. If the price of A falls, people buy more A and switch away from B, so the demand for B falls (its whole curve shifts left). For substitutes, the price of one and the demand for the other move in the SAME direction, and cross elasticity is positive: B. For complements (car and petrol) a fall in A's price would RAISE demand for B.\nThe trap: A (complements) catches students who mix up the two directions; for complements the price of one and demand for the other move in opposite directions.\nMemory tip: Substitutes \"Same direction\"; Complements \"Contrary direction\".",
 "|National income is best defined as:|The total taxes collected by government|The total money value of all goods and services produced in a country over a period|The amount of money in circulation|The government's annual budget": "National income is the total money value of all final goods and services produced by a country in a given period, usually one year. GDP counts what is produced inside Nigeria's borders; GNP = GDP + net factor income from abroad; NNP = GNP − depreciation; national income in the strict sense is NNP at factor cost. It is measured by the output, income and expenditure methods, and in Nigeria the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS) publishes it: B. Taxes, money in circulation and the budget are different things.\nMemory tip: National income = value of a year's FINAL output (no double counting).",
 "|The production possibility curve (PPC) shows:|The maximum combination of two goods an economy can produce given its resources|The minimum level of production needed to sustain the economy|The imports and exports of a country|The population growth of a country": "The production possibility curve (frontier) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce when all its resources are fully and efficiently used with the given technology: A. Points on the curve are efficient; a point inside shows unemployed or wasted resources; a point outside cannot be reached now. Moving along the curve shows opportunity cost: more of one good means less of the other. Growth (more resources, better technology) shifts the curve outward.\nMemory tip: PPC = the \"menu limit\" of an economy; its slope is opportunity cost.",
 "|Price elasticity of demand is said to be elastic when:|PED = 1|PED > 1 (percentage change in quantity demanded > percentage change in price)|PED < 1|PED = 0": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price (sign ignored). If PED > 1, quantity demanded changes by a larger percentage than price, so demand is elastic: B. Example: price rises 10% and quantity demanded falls 25%: PED = 25 ÷ 10 = 2.5 (elastic). PED = 1 is unitary, PED < 1 is inelastic, and PED = 0 is perfectly inelastic. When demand is elastic, a price rise lowers total revenue.\nThe trap: A (PED = 1) is unitary elasticity, which is the borderline, not \"elastic\".\nMemory tip: Elastic = Exceeds 1; Inelastic = Inside 1.",
 "|Which of the following is a tool of monetary policy?|Government expenditure|Changes in tax rates|Open market operations (buying and selling of government securities)|Subsidies on essential goods": "Monetary policy is action by the central bank (in Nigeria, the CBN) to control money supply, credit and interest rates. Its tools include open market operations (buying and selling government securities such as Treasury bills), the monetary policy rate, the cash reserve ratio, the liquidity ratio, special deposits and moral suasion: C. When the CBN sells securities, money leaves the banks and money supply falls. Government expenditure, tax rates and subsidies are fiscal policy tools, used by the government through the budget.\nMemory tip: Monetary = Money (CBN); Fiscal = Finance of government (budget: tax and spending).",
 "|Inflation that is caused by an increase in production costs (e.g., rising wages or oil prices) is called:|Demand-pull inflation|Cost-push inflation|Imported inflation|Hyperinflation": "Cost-push inflation happens when the costs of production rise, for example higher wages, higher fuel prices after a subsidy removal, costlier imported raw materials or a weaker naira, and firms pass the costs on as higher prices: B. Demand-pull inflation is the opposite cause: too much money chasing too few goods. Imported inflation comes from rising prices of imports, and hyperinflation is extremely rapid inflation (for example over 50% a month).\nThe trap: A (demand-pull) is the other main type; remember it starts from buyers, not costs.\nMemory tip: Cost-push = sellers' costs PUSH prices up; Demand-pull = buyers PULL prices up.",
 "|The 'invisible hand' concept was introduced by:|John Maynard Keynes|Karl Marx|Adam Smith|Milton Friedman": "Adam Smith, the Scottish economist often called the father of economics, wrote \"An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations\" (1776). He argued that when individuals pursue their own interest in free markets, an \"invisible hand\" guides them to produce what society wants. This idea underlies the capitalist (free market) system: C. Keynes (1936) argued for government intervention, Karl Marx inspired socialism, and Milton Friedman is linked with monetarism.\nMemory tip: Smith = 1776 = invisible hand = capitalism.",
 "|Which of the following best describes a 'public good'?|A good sold by the government|A good that is non-excludable and non-rival — everyone can use it without reducing its availability to others|A good given free to the poor|A good with high production costs": "A public good has two features: it is non-rival (my use does not reduce what is left for you) and non-excludable (nobody can be stopped from enjoying it, even if they do not pay): B. Examples are national defence, street lights and lighthouses. Because people can \"free ride\", private firms cannot charge for them, so the government provides them from taxes. A good sold by the government (like NNPC fuel in the past) is not automatically a public good.\nThe trap: A (a good sold by the government) sounds right because of the word \"public\", but the definition depends on non-rivalry and non-excludability.\nMemory tip: Public good = \"can't stop you, can't use it up\".",
 "|An increase in government spending, holding taxes constant, will most likely cause:|A decrease in national income|An increase in national income through the multiplier effect|A fall in aggregate demand|A rise in interest rates only": "Government spending (G) is an injection into the circular flow, like investment. Holding taxes constant, a rise in G increases aggregate demand. The people who receive the money spend part of it, which becomes someone else's income, and so on. Total income rises by more than the first injection: ΔY = multiplier × ΔG, where multiplier = 1/(1 − MPC). For example, with MPC = 0.8, a ₦10bn rise in G raises income by 5 × ₦10bn = ₦50bn: B.\nMemory tip: Injections (I, G, X) raise income; withdrawals (S, T, M) lower it.",
 "|The law of diminishing marginal returns states that:|As more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, total output eventually falls|As more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, marginal product eventually decreases|Output always increases with more inputs|Fixed costs always decrease": "The law of diminishing returns (variable proportions) says that when more and more units of a variable factor (labour) are added to a fixed factor (land), marginal product eventually falls: B. Example: on 1 hectare, the 1st worker adds 10 bags of maize, the 2nd adds 14, the 3rd adds 8 and the 4th adds 4. Marginal product starts falling after the 2nd worker, while total output still rises. Total output falls only later, when marginal product becomes negative.\nThe trap: A (total output falls) describes a later stage; the law is about MARGINAL product falling first.\nMemory tip: Marginal falls first; total falls last.",
 "|In a closed economy with no government: Y = C + I. If C = 0.7Y and I = ₦500m, find national income (Y).|₦1,000m|₦1,500m|₦1,667m|₦2,000m": "In a two-sector economy, equilibrium income is where Y = C + I.\nStep 1: Substitute: Y = 0.7Y + 500.\nStep 2: Collect Y terms: Y − 0.7Y = 500, so 0.3Y = 500.\nStep 3: Divide: Y = 500 ÷ 0.3 = ₦1,666.67m ≈ ₦1,667m: C.\nCheck with the multiplier: MPC = 0.7, MPS = 0.3, multiplier = 1/0.3 = 3.33, and 3.33 × ₦500m ≈ ₦1,667m.\nThe trap: B (₦1,500m) is what you get by multiplying 500 by 3 instead of dividing by 0.3.\nMemory tip: Y = I ÷ MPS (when C = bY): divide autonomous spending by the leakage.",
 "|Economies of scale refer to:|Cost advantages gained as output increases in the long run|The disadvantage of being too large a firm|The benefits of international trade|Price reductions in a competitive market": "Economies of scale are the cost advantages a firm (or industry) gains when it expands its scale of production in the long run, so that average (unit) cost falls: A. Internal economies come from within the firm: bulk buying, technical (bigger machines), managerial, financial (cheaper loans), marketing and risk-bearing. External economies come from the growth of the industry, such as skilled labour and ancillary firms clustered around Lagos or Aba. Beyond a point, diseconomies of scale make unit cost rise.\nMemory tip: Economies of scale = \"bigger is cheaper per unit\" (until it isn't).",
 "|Which of the following is a regressive tax?|Income tax with higher rates for higher earners|A flat rate tax where all pay the same percentage|A specific commodity tax (e.g., petrol tax) which takes a higher proportion of income from the poor|Inheritance tax": "Taxes are classed by how the tax rate relates to income. Progressive: the rate rises as income rises (PAYE income tax). Proportional: the same percentage for everyone (a flat rate tax). Regressive: the tax takes a larger share of a poor person's income than a rich person's. Indirect taxes on goods everyone buys, such as a tax on petrol or VAT, are regressive in effect because ₦500 of tax is a bigger part of a small income: C.\nThe trap: B (flat rate) is proportional, not regressive, because everyone pays the same percentage.\nMemory tip: Progressive = Poor pay a lower rate; Regressive = Rich pay a lower share.",
 "|The balance of trade is defined as:|Total imports minus total exports of goods and services|The difference between the value of visible exports and visible imports (goods only)|The government budget balance|Foreign exchange reserves": "The balance of trade is the difference between the value of a country's visible exports and visible imports, that is, physical goods only, such as crude oil, cocoa and imported machinery: B. If visible exports exceed visible imports, the balance of trade is favourable (surplus). Services such as shipping, banking, insurance and tourism are invisibles; together with goods they make up the current account of the balance of payments.\nThe trap: A mentions goods AND services and is the wrong way round; that describes neither the balance of trade nor the current account.\nMemory tip: Trade = Things you can see and touch (visibles).",
 "|A devaluation of a country's currency makes its exports:|More expensive abroad and imports cheaper at home|Cheaper abroad and imports more expensive at home|Cheaper both at home and abroad|The same price — devaluation has no effect on trade": "Devaluation is the deliberate lowering of a currency's official exchange rate. If ₦1,000 = $1 becomes ₦1,500 = $1, a foreign buyer's dollar buys more Nigerian goods, so Nigerian exports become cheaper abroad; but a $10 import now costs ₦15,000 instead of ₦10,000, so imports become dearer at home: B. It aims to boost exports and cut imports, helping the balance of payments, but it works only if demand for exports and imports is elastic (Marshall-Lerner condition).\nThe trap: A states the exact opposite and catches students who think a \"weaker\" naira makes exports dearer.\nMemory tip: Devalue = Exports cheaper, imports dearer (\"sell more, buy less\").",
 "|Which of the following is an example of structural unemployment?|A worker laid off because the economy is in recession|A worker between jobs looking for a better position|A coal miner who is unemployed because coal mines have closed permanently due to shift to renewable energy|A seasonal farm worker out of work in winter": "Structural unemployment arises from a lasting change in the structure of the economy, such as new technology, a change in demand or the decline of an industry, leaving workers whose skills are no longer needed: C. In Nigeria, the decline of coal mining at Enugu after oil became the main fuel is a classic example. Cyclical unemployment comes from a recession (A), frictional unemployment from people moving between jobs (B), and seasonal unemployment from the season (D; in Nigeria, farm workers in the dry season).\nMemory tip: Structural = the job itself disappears; frictional = between jobs.",
 "|The demand curve for a Giffen good:|Slopes downward like normal goods|Slopes upward — as price rises, quantity demanded also rises|Is perfectly elastic|Is perfectly inelastic": "A Giffen good is a very inferior staple on which poor households spend a large share of income (the classic example is bread or potatoes for the very poor). When its price rises, they can no longer afford better foods, so they buy MORE of the staple. Quantity demanded rises with price, so the demand curve slopes upward: B. The negative income effect is stronger than the substitution effect. Giffen goods are an exception to the law of demand, along with goods of ostentation and goods bought in expectation of further price rises.\nMemory tip: Giffen = \"Goes up together\" (price and quantity).",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a function of money?|Medium of exchange|Store of value|Unit of account|Source of unlimited wealth": "Money performs four functions: medium of exchange (it removes the need for a double coincidence of wants under barter), unit of account (measure of value), store of value, and standard of deferred payment (for debts and future payments). \"Source of unlimited wealth\" is not a function; money itself is limited and loses value with inflation: D.\nMemory tip: \"Many Useful Services Daily\": Medium, Unit, Store, Deferred payment.",
 "|A monopsony in the labour market refers to:|A single seller of labour (trade union)|A single buyer of labour (employer)|A labour market with many employers|Government control of wages": "In a market, \"mono\" means one and \"-poly\" means seller, while \"-psony\" means buyer. A monopsony is a market with a single buyer; in the labour market, a single employer, for example a large mine or company that is the only employer in a town: B. A monopsonist can pay a wage below what workers would get in a competitive market. A single seller of labour, such as a strong trade union, is a monopoly seller of labour.\nThe trap: A (single seller of labour) is the mirror image; monopsony is the BUYER side.\nMemory tip: monoPSony = one PurchaSer.",
 "|When marginal cost (MC) equals marginal revenue (MR), a firm is:|Making maximum losses|At the profit-maximising level of output|At the shutdown point|At the break-even point only": "Any firm maximises profit (or minimises loss) at the output where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), with MC rising through MR: B. If MR > MC, an extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so the firm should produce more; if MC > MR it should produce less. The shutdown point is where price equals minimum average variable cost, and break-even is where total revenue equals total cost (P = AC), so C and D are different conditions.\nMemory tip: Keep producing while the next unit adds more to revenue than to cost; stop where MC = MR.",
 "|The multiplier in Keynesian economics is calculated as:|1 / (1 − MPC)|MPC / (1 − MPS)|MPS × investment|1 / MPS × income": "The multiplier (k) shows how many times national income rises after an initial rise in spending. k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/MPS: A. Example: MPC = 0.75, so MPS = 0.25 and k = 1/0.25 = 4; a ₦20bn rise in investment raises income by ₦80bn. Option B simplifies to MPC/MPC = 1, and D multiplies the multiplier by income, which is not a formula for k.\nThe trap: D contains 1/MPS, which is correct by itself, but \"× income\" turns it into a wrong formula.\nMemory tip: The bigger the MPC (spending), the bigger the multiplier.",
 "|Comparative advantage in international trade means:|A country should produce what it produces better than all others (absolute advantage)|A country should specialise in producing goods in which it has a lower opportunity cost than other countries|Larger countries always have an advantage in trade|Free trade is always beneficial to all countries": "David Ricardo's principle of comparative advantage says a country should specialise in the good it can produce at a lower opportunity cost than others, even if another country is better at producing everything (absolute advantage): B. Example: if Nigeria gives up 2 units of cloth for 1 tonne of cocoa while Britain gives up 4, Nigeria has the comparative advantage in cocoa and should export it. Trade then lets both countries consume more.\nThe trap: A describes absolute advantage (Adam Smith), the closely related idea.\nMemory tip: Comparative = Compare opportunity costs; lowest cost wins.",
 "|Which of the following is an automatic stabiliser in an economy?|Discretionary government spending on infrastructure|Progressive income taxes and unemployment benefits|Central bank's decision to change interest rates|Government's decision to nationalise industries": "Automatic (built-in) stabilisers are parts of the budget that change by themselves with the economy, without any new decision. In a boom, incomes rise, people move into higher tax bands under progressive taxation and fewer people claim unemployment benefits, so spending is cooled; in a recession the reverse happens: B. Discretionary spending on infrastructure and nationalisation require deliberate government decisions, and interest rate changes are monetary policy.\nThe trap: A (discretionary spending) is fiscal policy too, but it needs a decision, so it is not automatic.\nMemory tip: Automatic = \"on autopilot\": taxes and benefits adjust without a vote.",
 "|Stagflation refers to:|Low inflation and economic stagnation|High unemployment, high inflation, and slow or negative economic growth occurring simultaneously|Rapid economic growth with deflation|A budget surplus during recession": "Stagflation = stagnation + inflation: rising prices at the same time as slow or negative growth and high unemployment: B. It is hard to cure because policies that reduce inflation tend to deepen unemployment. It was seen worldwide after the 1970s oil price shocks, and Nigeria experienced it in 2016–2017 and again in recent years, with double-digit inflation alongside weak growth and high unemployment.\nThe trap: A says LOW inflation; stagflation needs HIGH inflation.\nMemory tip: STAG (no growth) + FLATION (rising prices).",
 "|The World Bank and IMF were established at the:|Yalta Conference (1945)|Bretton Woods Conference (1944)|San Francisco Conference (1945)|Paris Peace Conference (1919)": "In July 1944, delegates from 44 countries met at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA, to plan the post-war economy. They created the International Monetary Fund (IMF), to help countries with balance of payments problems and keep exchange rates stable, and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD, part of the World Bank Group), to finance reconstruction and development: B. These are called the Bretton Woods institutions. Nigeria joined both in 1961.\nThe trap: A (Yalta 1945) and C (San Francisco 1945, which founded the UN) are close in date and both about the post-war order.\nMemory tip: Bretton Woods 1944 = \"B for Banks\" (IMF and World Bank).",
 "|If supply is perfectly inelastic, the supply curve is:|Horizontal|Upward sloping at 45°|Vertical|Downward sloping": "Price elasticity of supply (PES) = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price. If supply is perfectly inelastic (PES = 0), quantity supplied stays the same whatever the price, so the supply curve is vertical: C. Examples are land in a city centre, seats in a stadium on match day, or an antique. A horizontal curve is perfectly elastic (PES = infinity).\nThe trap: A (horizontal) is perfectly ELASTIC; students often swap the two.\nMemory tip: Inelastic = I (a vertical line, like the letter I); Elastic = flat like the horizon.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of an external cost (negative externality)?|A factory that trains workers who then work elsewhere|Air pollution from a factory that harms local residents|A beekeeper whose bees pollinate neighbouring farms|Education benefits that increase social productivity": "An externality is a cost or benefit that falls on a third party who is not part of the transaction. A negative externality (external cost) harms others: smoke and noise from a factory, gas flaring and oil spills in the Niger Delta, or a generator next door: B. The other options are positive externalities (external benefits): workers trained by one firm, bees pollinating a neighbour's farm, and the social gains from education. Social cost = private cost + external cost.\nMemory tip: Externality = a \"side effect\" on outsiders; negative = harm, positive = gain.",
 "|The Phillips Curve illustrates the relationship between:|Inflation and output|Unemployment and inflation — showing an inverse trade-off|Interest rates and investment|Money supply and price levels": "A. W. Phillips (1958) found, using British data, that when unemployment was low, wages (and so prices) rose fast, and when unemployment was high, they rose slowly. The Phillips curve therefore shows an inverse relationship, a trade-off, between unemployment and inflation: B. A government could buy lower unemployment at the cost of higher inflation. Stagflation in the 1970s showed that this trade-off can break down in the long run.\nThe trap: A (inflation and output) is close because output and unemployment are linked, but the curve is drawn with unemployment.\nMemory tip: Phillips: Prices up, unemployment down (and vice versa).",
 "|In economics, the term 'ceteris paribus' means:|With all things changed|Holding all other factors constant (all other things being equal)|In the long run|Under monopoly conditions": "\"Ceteris paribus\" is Latin for \"other things being equal\". Economists use it to study the effect of one variable while holding all other influences constant: B. For example, the law of demand says that, ceteris paribus, a rise in the price of garri reduces the quantity demanded; we hold income, tastes and the prices of rice and yam constant so we can see the price effect alone.\nMemory tip: Ceteris paribus = \"keep everything else still\".",
 "|Which of the following is the most liquid asset?|Real estate property|Shares in a company|Government bonds|Cash (currency in circulation)": "Liquidity is how quickly and easily an asset can be turned into cash without loss of value. Cash is money itself, so it is perfectly liquid: D. Government bonds and Treasury bills can be sold quickly but may lose value; shares depend on the stock market price; land and buildings take a long time to sell and are the least liquid. Keynes's liquidity preference theory is about why people want to hold cash.\nMemory tip: Liquidity ladder: cash → deposits → Treasury bills/bonds → shares → land.",
 "|A merit good is one that the government believes:|The market will overproduce|Individuals undervalue and underconsume relative to the socially optimal level, justifying government provision or subsidy|Should be taxed heavily|Only the rich should consume": "A merit good is one the government believes people undervalue and so consume too little of if left to the market, often because they lack information about its benefits; such goods also bring wider benefits to society. Examples are education, health care and immunisation, so government provides or subsidises them (for example Universal Basic Education in Nigeria): B. The opposite is a demerit good, such as cigarettes or alcohol, which is overconsumed and often taxed heavily.\nMemory tip: Merit = Most people should have More (underconsumed); Demerit = too much.",
 "|The 'terms of trade' of a country refer to:|The legal conditions governing international trade agreements|The ratio of export prices to import prices — indicating purchasing power of exports in terms of imports|The total value of exports|The tariff rates applied to imports": "The terms of trade compare the prices of a country's exports with the prices of its imports. Terms of trade = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100: B. Example: export price index 120, import price index 100: (120 ÷ 100) × 100 = 120, a favourable movement, since each unit of exports now buys more imports. When the price of crude oil falls, Nigeria's terms of trade usually worsen.\nMemory tip: Terms of trade = Export prices OVER import prices (E over I).",
 "|Dumping in international trade occurs when:|A country bans all imports|A country exports goods at a price below the cost of production or below the domestic price|A country imposes high import tariffs|A multinational corporation repatriates profits": "Dumping is selling goods in a foreign market at a price below their cost of production or below the price charged at home: B. Firms may do it to get rid of surplus output or to drive out local competitors. Dumped imports such as cheap textiles can harm Nigerian industries, so governments impose anti-dumping duties, and the WTO allows such duties when dumping causes injury.\nMemory tip: Dumping = \"dumping\" goods abroad cheaply.",
 "|If the government imposes a price ceiling BELOW the equilibrium price, the result is:|A surplus (excess supply)|A shortage (excess demand)|No change in the market|Higher prices": "A price ceiling (maximum price) is the highest price sellers are legally allowed to charge. Set below the equilibrium price, it makes quantity demanded greater than quantity supplied, so there is a shortage (excess demand): B. This leads to queues, rationing, hoarding and black markets, as was seen with controlled petrol prices in Nigeria. A minimum price (price floor), set above equilibrium, causes a surplus.\nThe trap: A (surplus) is the effect of a minimum price set above equilibrium.\nMemory tip: Ceiling below → Shortage; Floor above → Surplus.",
 "|The Lorenz curve is used to measure:|Economic growth over time|The distribution of income or wealth — its deviation from perfect equality|Inflation rates|Government budget deficits": "The Lorenz curve shows how income (or wealth) is shared in a country. It plots the cumulative percentage of households (poorest first) against the cumulative percentage of income they receive. The 45° line is perfect equality; the further the curve bends away from it, the greater the inequality: B. The Gini coefficient measures that gap from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality).\nMemory tip: Lorenz = Line of equality vs the bend of inequality.",
 "|The concept of 'consumer sovereignty' means:|The government determines what consumers buy|Consumers, through their purchasing decisions, ultimately determine what is produced in a free market|Consumers have no choice in the market|Producers dictate prices to consumers": "Consumer sovereignty means that in a free market the consumer is \"king\": by spending money on some goods and not others, consumers signal what firms should produce. Goods people buy earn profit and expand; goods they reject disappear: B. It is a feature of the capitalist (market) system. It is limited by advertising, monopoly, government controls and low incomes.\nMemory tip: Consumers \"vote\" with their naira.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a natural monopoly?|A supermarket chain|A taxi company|A water supply or electricity distribution network|A pharmaceutical company": "A natural monopoly arises when the fixed cost of the network is so large, and economies of scale so great, that one firm can supply the whole market at lower average cost than two or more firms could. Examples are water supply pipes, electricity transmission lines and railways: C. In Nigeria, the Transmission Company of Nigeria is still government owned, while electricity distribution is now done by privatised DisCos after NEPA/PHCN was unbundled in 2013.\nMemory tip: Natural monopoly = one set of pipes or wires is enough.",
 "|The concept of diminishing marginal utility states that:|Total utility always decreases as consumption increases|As a person consumes more units of a good, the additional satisfaction from each extra unit eventually declines|A good becomes cheaper as more is consumed|Marginal utility is always zero": "Utility is the satisfaction from consuming a good. Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. The law of diminishing marginal utility says that, other things equal, as a person consumes more units, the MU of each extra unit eventually falls: B. Example: the first sachet of water on a hot day gives great satisfaction, the fourth much less. Total utility keeps rising while MU is positive; it is highest when MU = 0.\nThe trap: A says TOTAL utility falls; it is MARGINAL utility that diminishes.\nMemory tip: Each extra bite is less exciting than the last.",
 "|In which market structure does a firm act as a price-taker?|Monopoly|Oligopoly|Monopolistic competition|Perfect competition": "A price-taker is a firm that must accept the market price because it is too small to influence it. Under perfect competition there are many firms selling an identical product, so if one firm raised its price it would lose all its customers: D. Its demand curve is horizontal, so price = AR = MR. Monopolists, oligopolists and firms in monopolistic competition are price-makers to some extent.\nMemory tip: Perfect competition: Price is Given.",
 "|The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures:|The total output of an economy|The average change in prices paid by consumers for a basket of goods and services over time|Corporate profit margins|Government expenditure": "The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures the average change over time in the prices of a fixed basket of goods and services bought by households, compared with a base year (= 100). In Nigeria, the NBS publishes it monthly, and the inflation rate is the percentage change in the CPI: B. Example: CPI rises from 200 to 230: inflation = (30 ÷ 200) × 100 = 15%.\nMemory tip: CPI = the price \"thermometer\" of a household's shopping basket.",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of a public good?|It is rival in consumption and excludable|It is non-rival and non-excludable|It generates no externalities|It is only provided by the government": "Public goods are non-rival (one person's use does not reduce what is available to others) and non-excludable (nobody can be prevented from using them): B. Examples are street lights, national defence and lighthouses. Because people can enjoy them without paying (the free-rider problem), the market will not provide them, so government does. Option D is a result, not a feature: not everything provided by government is a public good, and some public goods could in principle be provided privately.\nThe trap: D (only provided by the government) sounds right but is not the defining characteristic.\nMemory tip: Public good: non-rival + non-excludable.",
 "|The formula for calculating GDP using the income approach is:|GDP = C + I + G + (X−M)|GDP = Wages + Rent + Interest + Profit|GDP = Total Output × Price Level|GDP = Government Spending + Private Consumption": "National income can be measured three ways: output (value added by each sector), expenditure (C + I + G + (X − M)) and income. The income method adds the rewards to the factors of production: wages and salaries (labour), rent (land), interest (capital) and profit (entrepreneur): B. This gives GDP at factor cost; add indirect taxes and subtract subsidies to get GDP at market prices. Transfer payments such as pensions and gifts are excluded because they are not payments for production.\nThe trap: A (C + I + G + (X − M)) is a correct formula but for the EXPENDITURE method.\nMemory tip: Income method = factor rewards: Wages, Rent, Interest, Profit (\"WRIP\").",
 "|Price elasticity of supply measures:|How consumer income affects quantity demanded|The responsiveness of quantity supplied to a change in price|How price affects profits|The relationship between supply and demand": "Price elasticity of supply (PES) measures how much the quantity supplied responds to a change in price: PES = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price: B. Example: price rises from ₦100 to ₦120 (20%) and quantity supplied rises from 50 to 60 units (20%): PES = 20 ÷ 20 = 1 (unitary). Supply is more elastic in the long run, when goods can be stored, and when spare capacity exists. Option A describes income elasticity of demand.\nMemory tip: Elasticity always = % change in quantity ÷ % change in the cause.",
 "|Devaluation of a country's currency means:|The official reduction in the exchange rate of the currency against other currencies in a fixed exchange rate system|The currency becomes stronger|Inflation automatically falls|Exports become more expensive": "Devaluation is an official decision by the government or central bank to lower the value of its currency in a fixed exchange rate system: A. In a floating system, a fall in value caused by market forces is called depreciation. Devaluation makes exports cheaper and imports dearer. Nigeria devalued the naira under SAP in 1986 (through SFEM); since 2023 the naira has been allowed to float, so recent falls are depreciation.\nThe trap: D (exports become more expensive) is the reverse of the effect.\nMemory tip: DEvaluation = DEcision (fixed rate); DEpreciation = market drift (floating rate).",
 "|The 'invisible hand' concept introduced by Adam Smith suggests:|Government should control the economy|Individuals pursuing self-interest in a free market inadvertently promote the social good|Markets always fail without regulation|Wages should be set by unions": "Adam Smith in \"The Wealth of Nations\" (1776) argued that people serving their own interest, for example a baker baking bread to earn profit, are led \"as if by an invisible hand\" to serve the needs of society, without government direction: B. This is the basis of laissez-faire capitalism, where the price mechanism allocates resources. Critics point to market failures such as monopoly, externalities and public goods, which justify some government action.\nMemory tip: Invisible hand = the price mechanism working on its own.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a fiscal policy tool?|Setting the repo rate|Adjusting the reserve ratio|Changing the corporation tax rate|Open market operations": "Fiscal policy is the use of government revenue (taxation) and expenditure to influence the economy. Raising or lowering the companies income tax rate changes firms' after-tax profits and so their investment, so it is a fiscal tool: C. The monetary policy rate (repo rate in some countries), the reserve ratio and open market operations are monetary tools used by the CBN. In Nigeria, fiscal policy is set by the Federal Ministry of Finance through the annual budget passed by the National Assembly.\nMemory tip: Fiscal = Federal budget (tax and spending); Monetary = CBN.",
 "|The term 'balance of payments' refers to:|A government's budget balance|A systematic record of all economic transactions between a country's residents and the rest of the world over a period|The trade deficit only|The amount of foreign currency reserves held by the central bank": "The balance of payments (BOP) is a systematic record of all economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world over a period, usually a year: B. It has the current account (visible trade, invisibles such as services, income and transfers), the capital and financial account (investment and loans), and official reserves. In accounting terms it always balances; a \"deficit\" means a deficit before official financing. The CBN compiles Nigeria's BOP.\nThe trap: C (the trade deficit only) confuses the BOP with the balance of trade, which is only one part of it.\nMemory tip: Balance of TRADE = goods only; balance of PAYMENTS = everything.",
 "|Which of the following best describes the concept of 'crowding out' in macroeconomics?|Increased government borrowing raises interest rates, reducing private sector investment|Private firms crowd out government companies from the market|Inflation crowds out savings|High taxes crowd out consumption": "When the government borrows heavily to finance a budget deficit, it competes with private firms for the limited loanable funds. This pushes interest rates up, and firms then borrow and invest less. Government spending has \"crowded out\" private investment: A. In Nigeria, high-yield government bonds and Treasury bills can draw bank funds away from lending to businesses. Crowding out weakens the multiplier effect of government spending.\nMemory tip: Crowding out = government borrows, interest rises, private investment falls.",
 "|A negative income elasticity of demand (YED < 0) indicates:|A luxury good|A normal good|An inferior good|A Giffen good": "Income elasticity of demand (YED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income. If YED is negative, quantity demanded falls as income rises: the good is an inferior good, such as cheap local substitutes people abandon as they get richer: C. Normal goods have positive YED; luxuries have YED greater than 1.\nThe trap: D (Giffen good) is close because every Giffen good is inferior, but a negative YED alone does not make a good Giffen; a Giffen good also needs an upward-sloping demand curve.\nMemory tip: YED sign: + normal, > 1 luxury, − inferior.",
 "|The equilibrium wage in a perfectly competitive labour market is determined by:|The government setting a minimum wage|The intersection of labour demand (MRP of labour) and labour supply|Trade union negotiations only|The cost of living index": "In a perfectly competitive labour market, many firms hire many workers and no one can set the wage. The demand for labour is a derived demand, given by the marginal revenue product of labour (MRP = MP × MR). The supply of labour comes from workers. The wage settles where the demand and supply curves intersect: B. Minimum wages (such as Nigeria's national minimum wage of ₦70,000 set in 2024) and trade union bargaining are interventions that move wages away from this market wage.\nMemory tip: Wage = price of labour, set like any price: demand meets supply.",
 "|Which of the following is a measure of economic development (not just growth)?|Real GDP growth rate|Human Development Index (HDI)|Total exports|Government budget surplus": "Economic growth is a rise in real GDP; economic development is wider: better living standards, less poverty, better health and education. The Human Development Index (HDI), published by the UNDP since 1990, combines life expectancy (health), years of schooling (education) and GNI per capita (income), giving a value from 0 to 1: B. Nigeria's HDI is low (about 0.55), even though its GDP is one of the largest in Africa. Real GDP growth measures growth, not development.\nThe trap: A (real GDP growth rate) is the standard measure of GROWTH, which the question excludes.\nMemory tip: HDI = Health, Degrees (education), Income.",
 "|The law of demand states:|As price rises, quantity demanded rises (other things equal)|As price falls, quantity demanded rises (other things equal)|Supply always equals demand|Demand is unaffected by price changes": "The law of demand states that, other things being equal, the lower the price of a good, the higher the quantity demanded, and the higher the price, the lower the quantity demanded: B. This inverse relationship gives a demand curve that slopes downward from left to right. A change in price moves the buyer along the curve; a change in income, tastes or prices of related goods shifts the whole curve. Exceptions include Giffen goods and goods of ostentation.\nThe trap: A states the opposite (a direct relationship), which describes the law of supply.\nMemory tip: Demand: price and quantity move in opposite directions (a \"see-saw\").",
 "|If the price elasticity of demand for a good is 0.43, an increase in the price of the good will result in|an increase in profit by 43%|a net gain|a decrease in profit by 43%|a net loss": "Price elasticity of demand measures how strongly quantity demanded responds to a price change. A value of 0.43 is less than 1, so demand is inelastic: if price rises by 10%, quantity falls by only about 4.3%. Because the price rise outweighs the fall in sales, total revenue (price × quantity) goes up, so the seller makes a net gain: B. Options A and C misread 0.43 as a 43% change in profit, but elasticity is a ratio of percentage changes, not a profit figure. Memory tip: Inelastic demand: raise the price, raise the revenue; elastic demand: cut the price to raise revenue.",
 "|In a free market economy, the price system allocates resources|under government's directives|to ensure general welfare|to their best alternative uses|to reduce poverty": "In a free market (capitalist) economy there is no central planner. Prices act as signals: when consumers want more of a good its price and profits rise, attracting land, labour and capital into producing it, and resources leave goods people want less. In this way the price mechanism (the \"invisible hand\" described by Adam Smith) moves resources to their best alternative uses, where they earn the highest reward: C. Government directives (A) describe a command economy, while general welfare and poverty reduction (B, D) are social aims that the market does not guarantee. Memory tip: Prices are the market's traffic lights, directing resources where they are most wanted.",
 "|At consumer equilibrium, the slope of the indifference curve is|half the slope of the budget constraint line|equal to the slope of the budget constraint line|greater than the slope of the budget constraint line|less than the slope of the budget constraint line": "An indifference curve shows combinations of two goods giving equal satisfaction; its slope is the marginal rate of substitution (MRS). The budget line shows what the consumer can afford; its slope is the ratio of the two prices. The consumer reaches the highest possible satisfaction where the budget line just touches (is tangent to) an indifference curve. At a point of tangency the two lines have the same slope, so MRS = Px/Py: B. If the slopes differed, the consumer could reshuffle spending and move to a higher indifference curve. Memory tip: Equilibrium = Tangency = Equal slopes (MRS = price ratio).",
 "|For a supply curve, an increase in the price of a commodity will result in|a decrease in supply|a decrease in the quantity supplied|an increase in supply|an increase in the quantity supplied": "Economists separate two ideas. A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the supply curve, called a change in quantity supplied. A change in anything else (costs, technology, weather, taxes) shifts the whole curve, called a change in supply. Since the supply curve slopes upward, a higher price moves the firm up the curve to a larger quantity: an increase in the quantity supplied: D. The trap: C, \"an increase in supply\", sounds almost the same but means a rightward shift of the whole curve, which a price change does not cause. Memory tip: Own price = movement (quantity supplied); other factors = shift (supply).",
 "|A normal good with close substitutes is likely to have price elasticity of demand|between zero and one|equal to unity|less than unity|greater than unity": "Elasticity of demand depends mainly on the availability of substitutes. If a good has close substitutes, a rise in its price makes buyers switch quickly to the alternatives, so quantity demanded falls by a larger percentage than the price rose. That is elastic demand, with a coefficient greater than one: D. Examples are different brands of soap or soft drinks. Options A and C (between zero and one, less than unity) describe inelastic demand, typical of necessities with few substitutes such as salt; B (unity) is the special case where percentage changes are equal. Memory tip: More Substitutes = More Sensitive (elastic, greater than 1).",
 "|Economics is regarded as a social science because it|adopts the scientific method in production|is an agent of socialization|adopts the scientific method in the study of human behaviour|deals with social problems": "A social science studies people in society using a systematic, scientific approach. Economics studies how people behave in using scarce resources to satisfy wants, and it does this by the scientific method: observing facts, forming hypotheses, and testing them to build laws such as the law of demand. Because its subject is human behaviour and its method is scientific, it is a social science: C. Option A wrongly places the scientific method \"in production\"; B describes institutions like the family or school; D is too vague, since many subjects deal with social problems. Memory tip: Social (human behaviour) + Science (scientific method) = social science.",
 "|The diagram shows the total utility curve. At the point M, marginal utility|increases|is unity|diminishes|is zero": "Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit. As long as total utility (TU) is rising, MU is positive; when TU is at its maximum, MU is zero; when TU falls, MU is negative. In the diagram, M is the peak of the total utility curve, so the next unit adds nothing: marginal utility is zero: D. Beyond M the curve falls, showing negative marginal utility. The trap: C, \"diminishes\", describes MU on the rising part of the curve before M, not at the peak itself. Memory tip: Top of TU = Zero MU.",
 "|A change in supply is best described as a|movement along the supply curve|shift of the supply curve to the left or to the right|shift of the supply curve to the left|shift of the supply curve to the right": "A change in supply means the whole supply schedule changes because of a non-price factor such as cost of production, technology, weather or taxation. On a graph this is a shift of the supply curve: to the right when supply increases and to the left when supply decreases. Since the question asks generally about a change, the best description includes both directions: B. A movement along the curve (A) is a change in quantity supplied caused by the good's own price. The trap: C and D are each half true, because each names only one direction of shift. Memory tip: \"Change in supply\" = the curve moves house, left or right.",
 "|When a generalization is made based on observed facts, it is known as|deductive reasoning|inductive reasoning|theoretical reasoning|normative reasoning": "Economists use two kinds of reasoning. Inductive reasoning starts from particular observed facts and draws a general conclusion; for example, after observing many markets where buyers bought less at higher prices, economists generalised this into the law of demand. Deductive reasoning works the other way, from general assumptions to particular conclusions. A generalisation built from observed facts is therefore inductive reasoning: B. Normative reasoning (D) involves value judgements about what ought to be. The trap: A, deductive reasoning, is the closely related opposite method and is often mixed up with induction. Memory tip: INduction brings facts IN to build a rule; DEduction DErives facts from a rule.",
 "|A major problem facing all economies is how to|increase consumption of imported goods|improve trade among nations|transform from a developing to a developed economy|allocate scarce resources": "The basic economic problem arises because human wants are unlimited while the resources to satisfy them (land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) are scarce. Every economy, whether capitalist, socialist or mixed, developed or developing, must therefore decide what to produce, how to produce it and for whom, which together mean deciding how to allocate scarce resources among competing uses: D. Increasing imports, improving trade or becoming developed (A, B, C) are goals of particular countries at particular times, not problems facing all economies. Memory tip: Scarcity forces Choice, and choice is about Allocation.",
 "|A vertical supply curve indicates that|the quantity supplied is responsive to price|the same fixed quantity will be supplied no matter the price|an increase in price will result in an increase in the quantity supplied|there is a fixed price for the commodity below which no supply will be made": "A vertical supply curve means that whatever the price, the same quantity is offered for sale. Price elasticity of supply is zero (perfectly inelastic). Examples are land in a fixed area, seats in a stadium on match day, or perishable goods already brought to market that must be sold. So the curve shows that the same fixed quantity is supplied no matter the price: B. Options A and C describe a normal upward-sloping supply curve, and D describes a supply price below which nothing is offered, which a vertical line does not show. Memory tip: Vertical = Very fixed (elasticity zero); horizontal = infinitely elastic.",
 "|In a demand curve the relationship between price and quantity is|indeterminate|direct|nil|inverse": "The law of demand states that, other things being equal, the higher the price of a good the smaller the quantity demanded, and the lower the price the greater the quantity demanded. Price and quantity move in opposite directions, which is an inverse relationship, so the demand curve slopes downward from left to right: D. A direct (positive) relationship (B) is what we find on a supply curve. Exceptions such as Giffen goods or goods of ostentation are rare and do not change the general rule. Memory tip: Demand Down-slopes: price up, quantity down.",
 "|If the arithmetic mean of 1, 2, 5, 6, x, 16 and 18 is 8.0, find the value of x.|7.0|6.0|8.5|8.0": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of the values divided by the number of values, so the sum = mean × number.\nStep 1: There are 7 values (1, 2, 5, 6, x, 16, 18), so the total must be 8.0 × 7 = 56.\nStep 2: Add the known values: 1 + 2 + 5 + 6 + 16 + 18 = 48.\nStep 3: x = 56 − 48 = 8.0: D.\nCheck: (48 + 8) ÷ 7 = 56 ÷ 7 = 8. Memory tip: when a mean is given, rebuild the total first (mean × n), then subtract what you know.",
 "|The fixing of the price of an item above or below the equilibrium price is most likely to take place in a|centrally planned economy|free market economy|developed economy|mixed economy": "In a free market, prices are set by demand and supply and settle at equilibrium. In a centrally planned (command) economy, a central authority decides what to produce and also fixes prices administratively. These official prices are not set by the market, so they are usually above or below the equilibrium price, leading to surpluses or shortages, queues and rationing, as happened in the former Soviet Union: A. Mixed economies also use occasional price controls, but fixing prices is the normal practice only under central planning. A free market economy (B) by definition lets prices find equilibrium. Memory tip: Command economy = Commanded prices.",
 "|A consumer's scale of preference is an arrangement of his|scarce resources in order of importance|needs in order of importance|sources of income and their importance|requirement and how to satisfy them": "Because income is limited, a consumer cannot satisfy all his wants at once. A scale of preference is a list of his needs (wants) arranged in order of importance or urgency, so that he satisfies the most pressing first and leaves the less urgent for later. For a student it might be food, rent, textbooks, then new clothes. So it is an arrangement of his needs in order of importance: B. The wants left out when he chooses the first item on the list are his opportunity cost. Option A confuses wants with resources. Memory tip: Scale of preference = needs ranked from Most to Least urgent.",
 "|A firm's shut-down point is reached when the average revenue fails to cover the|average variable cost|marginal cost|average total cost|average fixed cost": "In the short run a firm has fixed costs that it must pay whether it produces or not. It should keep producing as long as price (average revenue) covers its average variable cost, because any revenue above AVC helps to pay part of the fixed costs. If AR falls below AVC, producing adds to the loss, so the firm shuts down: A. The trap: C, average total cost, gives the break-even point (normal profit), not the shut-down point; a firm can keep running for a while while covering only AVC. Memory tip: AVC is the \"stay open\" line; ATC is the \"break even\" line.",
 "|Internal economies of scale are expected to bring about|an increase in short-run average cost|an increase in long-run average cost|a decrease in long-run average cost|a decrease in short-run average cost": "Internal economies of scale are cost savings a firm enjoys as it grows larger, such as technical (bigger, better machines), managerial (specialised managers), financial (cheaper loans), marketing (bulk buying) and risk-bearing economies. Growing in size means changing all factors, including plant and buildings, which happens only in the long run. These economies therefore make the long-run average cost (LRAC) curve fall as output expands: C. The trap: D, \"a decrease in short-run average cost\", sounds right because costs do fall, but in the short run the firm's scale is fixed, so scale economies cannot apply. Memory tip: Scale changes in the Long run, so economies of scale lower Long-run average cost.",
 "|In the long-run a monopolist maximizes his profit when the marginal cost equals|total revenue|marginal revenue|average cost|price": "Every firm maximises profit where marginal cost (MC) equals marginal revenue (MR): if MR is above MC, producing more adds to profit; if MC is above MR, producing less does. For a monopolist the demand curve slopes downward, so MR is below price, and the monopolist charges a price above MC. The equilibrium condition is still MC = MR: B. The trap: D, price, is the rule P = MC that holds only for a perfectly competitive firm, where price and MR are the same. Memory tip: MC = MR for all firms; P = MC only under perfect competition.",
 "|The long run is a period during which a firm|sells input to purchase fixed asset|varies all its inputs|sources all its inputs from within|replaces all its inputs": "Economists divide time by how far a firm can change its inputs. In the short run some factors (such as buildings and machinery) are fixed and only variable inputs like labour and raw materials can be changed. The long run is a period long enough for the firm to vary all its inputs, including plant size, so there are no fixed costs: B. The very long run also allows technology to change. Options A, C and D describe particular business actions, not a time period in economic analysis. Memory tip: Short run: Some fixed; Long run: aLL variable.",
 "|An important feature of perfect competition is that|the movement of goods and services is restricted|there is adequate knowledge of existing prices|prices are centrally administered|individual economic units can influence prices": "Perfect competition has these features: many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous product, free entry and exit, free mobility of factors, and perfect knowledge of market conditions, especially prices. Because every buyer and seller knows the ruling price, no seller can charge more without losing all customers. So adequate knowledge of existing prices is a feature: B. Restricted movement (A) and centrally administered prices (C) are the opposite of a free, perfect market, and in perfect competition each firm is a price taker that cannot influence price (D). Memory tip: Perfect competition = Perfect knowledge, Price takers.",
 "|If government fixes price below the equilibrium price, what effect will it have on demand?|Quantity demanded and supplied will be equal.|Quantity supplied will be greater than quantity demanded.|Quantity demanded will increase.|Quantity demanded will decrease.": "A maximum (ceiling) price fixed below equilibrium makes the good cheaper than the market price. By the law of demand, a lower price raises the quantity demanded, while producers offer less because the price is less rewarding. Demand therefore exceeds supply and a shortage appears, often leading to queues, rationing and black markets. The effect on demand is that quantity demanded increases: C. Quantity supplied greater than quantity demanded (B) is the surplus that follows a minimum price above equilibrium, and equality (A) occurs only at equilibrium. Memory tip: Ceiling below equilibrium = Shortage; floor above equilibrium = Surplus.",
 "|An imperfect market exists where|the product is homogenous|there is perfect information among the few buyers and sellers|both buyers and sellers have free entry into and free exit from the market|the location of some sellers gives them an advantage over others": "A perfect market requires a homogeneous product, perfect information, many buyers and sellers, and free entry and exit, so that one price rules everywhere. An imperfect market exists when any of these conditions fails. If some sellers are better located, for example a shop near a bus park or a filling station on a busy road, customers prefer them and they can charge a little more, so different prices exist for the same good: D. Options A and C are features of perfect competition, and B describes perfect information. Memory tip: Location, branding and ignorance make markets imperfect.",
 "|A production function relates|cost to output|wage to profit|cost to input|output to input": "A production function shows the technical relationship between the quantity of output a firm produces and the quantities of inputs (land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) it uses, written Q = f(L, K, ...). It tells how much output a given combination of inputs can produce, and it underlies the laws of returns. So it relates output to input: D. The trap: A, \"cost to output\", describes a cost function, a closely related idea that comes from the production function once input prices are known. Memory tip: Production function = Physical quantities (inputs in, output out); cost function = money.",
 "|The table below shows demand schedule for eggs: \nPrice per dozen (₦) ... Qty demanded (in dozen) ... Qty. supplied (in dozen)\n100 ... 410 ... 570\n90 ... 450 ... 530\n80 ... 490 ... 490\n70 ... 530 ... 450\n60 ... 570 ... 410\n\nWhat is the equilibrium price?|₦100|₦110|₦60|₦80": "Equilibrium price is the price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, so the market clears.\nStep 1: Compare the two columns row by row: at ₦100, 410 vs 570 (surplus); at ₦90, 450 vs 530 (surplus); at ₦80, 490 vs 490 (equal); at ₦70, 530 vs 450 (shortage); at ₦60, 570 vs 410 (shortage).\nStep 2: The quantities match only at ₦80, with 490 dozen bought and sold: D.\nAbove ₦80 there is a surplus and below it a shortage, which pushes price back to ₦80. ₦110 (B) is not in the table at all. Memory tip: equilibrium is the row where the two quantity columns read the same.",
 "|A major advantage of specialization and division of labour is that|time wastage will be minimized|trade unions will be encouraged|there will be a ready market for labour|workers will be adequately rewarded": "Division of labour breaks production into small tasks, with each worker specialising in one task. Its advantages include greater skill through repetition, use of machines, increased output, and saving of time, because workers no longer move from one job or tool to another. So a major advantage is that time wastage is minimised: A. Encouraging trade unions (B) is not a purpose of specialisation, and specialisation does not guarantee a ready market for labour or adequate pay (C, D); in fact over-specialised workers may find it hard to change jobs. Memory tip: Specialisation Saves time, Sharpens skill, Swells output.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a fixed cost?|Fuel cost.|Electricity bill.|Rent on building.|Transportation cost.": "Fixed costs do not change with the level of output in the short run; they must be paid even when output is zero. Examples are rent on buildings, insurance premiums, interest on loans and salaries of permanent staff. Rent on a factory building is paid whether the firm produces nothing or works at full capacity: C. Fuel, electricity and transport costs rise as the firm produces more, so they are variable costs. The trap: B, the electricity bill, is often thought fixed because it arrives regularly, but the amount rises with the machines and hours used. Memory tip: if you would still pay it with the factory shut, it is a fixed cost.",
 "|In the agricultural sector, improved extension services facilitate|acquisition of techniques|arable crop production|mobility of labour|livestock production": "Agricultural extension services link research institutes and farmers. Extension officers visit farmers, run demonstration plots and give advice so that farmers learn and adopt improved techniques such as better seeds, fertiliser use, pest control and storage methods. Their central role is therefore to help farmers acquire modern techniques: A. These techniques may then raise arable or livestock output (B, D), but those are results in particular branches, not what extension services themselves provide. In Nigeria, the Agricultural Development Programmes (ADPs) were the main channel for extension work. Memory tip: Extension = Education of farmers in new techniques.",
 "|A disadvantage of concentrating industries in an area is that it could|lead to collusion among the firms|result in environmental pollution|bring about diseconomies of scale|increase the cost of production": "Localisation of industry is the concentration of many firms in one area, such as industrial estates in Lagos or Kano. It brings benefits (skilled labour, ancillary firms, banking facilities) but also problems: congestion, housing shortages, rising land costs, regional imbalance and, especially, environmental pollution from smoke, noise, chemicals and waste discharged by many factories in one place: B. Concentration does not by itself cause collusion (A), and costs often fall rather than rise because of external economies (D). Memory tip: Many factories in one place = Much pollution in one place.",
 "|The losses suffered by a sole proprietor are|usually equal to the amount invested|usually less than the amount invested|limited to the amount invested|not limited to the amount invested": "A sole proprietorship is owned and controlled by one person and is not a separate legal entity from its owner. The owner has unlimited liability: if the business cannot pay its debts, creditors can claim the owner's personal property, such as his house or car. His losses are therefore not limited to the amount he invested: D. Limited liability (C) belongs to shareholders of a limited company, who can lose only what they paid for their shares. Partners in an ordinary partnership also have unlimited liability. Memory tip: Sole trader = Sole risk, with no limit.",
 "|To facilitate the exportation of crude oil from Nigeria special|airstrips were constructed for jets to evacuate the oil|roads were built to connect the major oil wells to the ports|pipelines were laid to connect the major oil wells to the ports|railway lines were constructed to connect the major oil wells to the ports": "Nigeria's crude oil is produced mostly in the Niger Delta, onshore and offshore. To export it, oil companies laid a network of pipelines carrying the crude from the oil fields to export terminals and ports such as Bonny, Forcados, Brass and Escravos, where it is loaded into tankers: C. Pipelines are the cheapest and safest way of moving large volumes of oil continuously. Oil is not carried by jets (A), and roads and railways (B, D) are used only for small quantities of refined products. Pipelines also link refineries and depots, though vandalism remains a problem. Memory tip: Crude flows by Pipe to Port, then by tanker abroad.",
 "|An industry is a|company producing unrelated commodities|group of firms producing related commodities|group of firms producing distinct commodities|firm producing differentiated commodities": "A firm is a single business unit, while an industry is the group of all firms producing the same or similar (closely related) products. For example, all firms making cement form the cement industry, and all textile mills form the textile industry. So an industry is a group of firms producing related commodities: B. A single company or firm (A, D) is not an industry, and firms producing distinct, unrelated goods (C) belong to different industries. Memory tip: Firm = one tree; industry = the whole forest of similar trees.",
 "|The raising of funds by selling stocks to the public is called|equity financing|stock financing|debt financing|loan financing": "A company can raise long-term finance in two broad ways. Equity financing means selling shares (stocks) to the public, usually through the stock exchange; buyers become part-owners and receive dividends, and the money need not be repaid. Debt financing means borrowing, through loans or debentures, which must be repaid with interest. Raising funds by selling stocks is therefore equity financing: A. The trap: B, \"stock financing\", sounds natural because stocks are sold, but it is not the recognised name; the term is equity financing. Memory tip: Shares = Equity = Ownership; Loans = Debt = Repayment.",
 "|A tax that increases at a higher percentage as income increases is called|a proportional tax|a regressive tax|a progressive tax|an income tax": "Taxes are classified by how the rate changes with income. A progressive tax takes a higher percentage of income as income rises; Nigeria's personal income tax bands work this way, so the rich pay a larger share. A proportional tax takes the same percentage from everyone, and a regressive tax takes a higher percentage from the poor (most indirect taxes act this way). So a tax whose rate increases as income increases is progressive: C. The trap: D, \"an income tax\", is tempting because the tax depends on income, but an income tax can be progressive, proportional or regressive; it names the base, not the rate structure. Memory tip: Progressive rates Progress upward with income.",
 "|The price paid for labour services is the|commission rate|wage rate|income rate|salary rate": "Each factor of production earns a reward, which is its price: land earns rent, labour earns wages, capital earns interest and the entrepreneur earns profit. The price paid for labour services is therefore the wage rate: B. The wage rate is set by the demand for and supply of labour, collective bargaining and government minimum wage laws. The trap: D, \"salary rate\", is a closely related everyday word for monthly pay, but in economic theory every payment for labour is called wages. Commission (A) is a payment for a particular sales service. Memory tip: Land-Rent, Labour-Wages, Capital-Interest, Entrepreneur-Profit.",
 "|The capital market is a market for trading of financial assets such as|bankers acceptances|treasury bills|long-term securities|commercial papers": "Financial markets are divided by the length of the funds traded. The money market deals in short-term funds (up to one year) with instruments such as treasury bills, bankers' acceptances, commercial papers and call money. The capital market deals in long-term funds through long-term securities: shares (stocks), government development stocks and bonds, and debentures, traded mainly on the stock exchange (in Nigeria, the Nigerian Exchange) and regulated by the SEC: C. The trap: B, treasury bills, are government securities too, but they mature within a year, so they belong to the money market. Memory tip: Money market = Months; Capital market = years.",
 "|In national income accounting, NNP is derived by subtracting from the GNP|transfer payments|indirect taxes|allowance for wastage|capital consumption": "Gross National Product (GNP) is the total value of final goods and services produced by a country's citizens in a year. In producing this output, machines, buildings and equipment wear out. The value of this wear and tear is called depreciation or capital consumption allowance. Subtracting it gives Net National Product: NNP = GNP − capital consumption: D. Subtracting indirect taxes (B) is the step from NNP at market prices to NNP at factor cost (national income). The trap: C, \"allowance for wastage\", sounds like the \"capital consumption allowance\", but wastage is not the accounting term. Memory tip: Gross minus depreciation = Net.",
 "|Economic growth can be accelerated through|balancing of savings and consumption|postponement of current consumption|planned current consumption|increased current consumption": "Economic growth means a sustained increase in a country's real output. Growth needs capital formation (more machines, factories, roads), and capital formation needs saving. When people postpone (put off) current consumption, they save more, and these savings can be channelled through banks into investment. The larger stock of capital raises future production. So growth is accelerated through postponement of current consumption: B. Increasing current consumption (D) reduces saving and investment, and simply \"balancing\" or \"planning\" consumption (A, C) does not by itself free more resources for investment. Memory tip: Consume less today, Save, Invest, Grow tomorrow.",
 "|An increase in the discount rate is an indication of a Central Bank's intention to pursue|an expansionary monetary policy|a disciplined monetary policy|a dynamic monetary policy|a contractionary monetary policy": "The discount rate (also called the bank rate, or in Nigeria the Monetary Policy Rate) is the rate at which the central bank lends to commercial banks. When the central bank raises it, banks pay more to borrow and in turn raise their lending rates. Borrowing becomes dearer, credit and money supply fall, and spending slows. This is a tight or contractionary monetary policy, usually used to fight inflation: D. Lowering the rate would be an expansionary policy (A). \"Disciplined\" and \"dynamic\" (B, C) are not technical names of policy types. Memory tip: Rate Up, money Tight; rate down, money loose.",
 "|The efficiency of labour can be increased by|constant training of workers|retirement of workers on a regular basis|engaging expatriates only|placing an embargo on recruitment": "Efficiency of labour is the ability of workers to produce more and better output with the same effort and time. It depends on education, health, working conditions, motivation, wages and, above all, training. Constant (regular) training keeps workers' skills up to date with new methods and machines, so their productivity rises: A. Regular retirement of workers (B) loses experience, engaging only expatriates (C) does not improve the existing labour force, and an embargo on recruitment (D) only limits the number of workers. Memory tip: Train to Gain: skills raise efficiency.",
 "|In Nigeria, revenue sharing from the Federation account is between the|executive the judiciary and the legislature|federal and state governments and the parastatals|federal and state governments and the Senate|federal, state and local governments": "All federally collected revenue in Nigeria (oil revenue, company income tax, customs duties and so on) is paid into the Federation Account. It is then shared vertically among the three tiers of government, federal, state and local governments, according to a formula recommended by the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) and approved by the National Assembly: D. The arms of government (A) and parastatals or the Senate (B, C) are not tiers of government that receive a share. The Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC) meets monthly to share the funds. Memory tip: Three tiers share the pot: Federal, State, Local.",
 "|The reduction of high fertility rate is a measure in population control designed to make the|female population higher than the male|female and male population equal|male population higher than the female|growth in population more sustainable": "Fertility rate is the average number of children born to a woman. A high fertility rate causes rapid population growth that can outrun food supply, jobs, schools and health facilities. Population control measures such as family planning, education of women and later marriage aim to reduce fertility so that population growth is slower and can be supported by the country's resources, that is, more sustainable: D. Reducing fertility does not aim to change the balance between males and females (A, B, C), because births of boys and girls are roughly equal. Memory tip: Fewer births per woman = growth the economy can Sustain.",
 "|National debt is an expected outcome of a|deficit budget|consolidated budget|balanced budget|surplus budget": "A budget is the government's plan of revenue and expenditure for a year. In a deficit budget expenditure exceeds revenue, and the gap is financed mainly by borrowing at home (treasury bills, bonds, central bank loans) or abroad. Borrowing adds to the national (public) debt, so national debt is the expected outcome of a deficit budget: A. A surplus budget (D) has revenue above spending and can be used to repay debt, while a balanced budget (C) has no gap to borrow for. A consolidated budget (B) simply combines several accounts. Memory tip: Deficit leads to Debt.",
 "|The distribution channel that contributes most to the cost of production is|producer-wholesaler-consumer|producer-retailer-consumer|producer-retailer-wholesaler-consumer|producer-wholesaler-retailer-consumer": "A channel of distribution is the route goods take from producer to consumer. Each middleman (wholesaler, retailer) adds a profit margin and costs of transport, storage and handling, so the longer the channel, the higher the final price. The common long channel producer-wholesaler-retailer-consumer uses the most middlemen and therefore adds most to the cost of goods to the consumer: D. The short channels in A and B have only one middleman each. Option C lists the same number of stages but in a reversed order that is not a normal trade channel, since wholesalers buy from producers and sell to retailers. Memory tip: More middlemen, more margins, more cost.",
 "|Money may not be accepted as a store of value if|its value is unstable|it is easily transferable|its value is stable|it is easily divisible": "Money performs four main functions: medium of exchange, measure (unit) of value, store of value and standard of deferred payment. To serve as a store of value, money must keep its purchasing power over time. If its value is unstable, for example during high inflation when prices rise quickly, people lose by holding it and prefer to store wealth in land, buildings, gold or foreign currency: A. A stable value (C) is exactly what makes money a good store of value, while transferability and divisibility (B, D) are useful qualities, not reasons for rejection. Memory tip: Inflation eats savings, so unstable money is a poor store.",
 "|In order to accelerate economic development a country must|stimulate her population growth rate|pursue a high population growth rate|pursue a rational population policy|pursue an incremental population policy": "Economic development means improved living standards, not just more output. Rapid population growth can swallow the gains of development by raising the dependency ratio and the demand for food, schools, health care and jobs. A country should therefore pursue a rational population policy, one that balances population growth with available resources through education, family planning and good data from censuses: C. Stimulating or pursuing high population growth (A, B) works against development in most developing countries. \"Incremental population policy\" (D) is not a recognised policy. Nigeria's National Population Policy (1988, revised 2004) follows this approach. Memory tip: Plan the People to Plan the Progress.",
 "|If the labour force in a given community is 2,000,000, those in the wage employment 200,000 and the unemployed are 80,000, what is the unemployment rate in the community?|0.4%|40.0%|10.0%|4.0%": "The unemployment rate measures the share of the labour force that is willing and able to work but has no job.\nStep 1: Formula: unemployment rate = (unemployed ÷ labour force) × 100.\nStep 2: 80,000 ÷ 2,000,000 = 0.04.\nStep 3: 0.04 × 100 = 4.0%: D.\nThe figure for wage employment (200,000) is not needed. The trap: B (40.0%) comes from dividing 80,000 by the 200,000 in wage employment instead of by the whole labour force; A (0.4%) is a slip in the decimal point. Memory tip: always divide by the whole labour force.",
 "|GNP is not a good measure of social welfare because there are unrecorded|indirect taxes|social costs|government subsidies|transfer payments": "GNP measures the market value of output, so it is a weak guide to social welfare (people's well-being). It ignores leisure, the distribution of income, non-market output such as housework, and especially social costs: pollution, congestion, accidents, health damage and environmental destruction that come with production. These costs are borne by society but not recorded or subtracted in GNP, so a rising GNP can overstate welfare: B. The trap: D, transfer payments such as pensions, are also left out of GNP, but on purpose, because they are not payments for current production; leaving them out is correct accounting, not a weakness. Memory tip: GNP counts the factory's output, not the smoke.",
 "|The loans from the World Bank to developing countries are mainly to support|the establishment of more universities|capital-intensive technology|infrastructural investments|their low level of consumption": "The World Bank (International Bank for Reconstruction and Development), set up at Bretton Woods in 1944, together with its soft-loan arm the International Development Association (IDA), lends to developing countries for long-term development. Its loans mainly finance infrastructure and development projects: roads, power stations, water supply, irrigation, ports, and also agriculture, health and education programmes: C. It does not lend to raise consumption (D), and it does not mainly fund new universities or favour capital-intensive technology (A, B). Short-term balance of payments help comes from the IMF, not the World Bank. Memory tip: World Bank builds (projects); IMF balances (payments).",
 "|The major reason why nations engage in international trade is that|it allows free movement of factors of production|it makes possible the use of capital intensive method of production|nations are substantially different in their endowments of economic resources|it affords the trading nations the opportunity to earn high profit": "Countries differ greatly in their resources: Nigeria has crude oil and a tropical climate, Japan has advanced technology and skilled labour, Saudi Arabia has oil but little farmland. Because of these differences in endowments, each country can produce some goods more cheaply than others. By specialising according to comparative advantage and trading, all countries gain: C. International trade moves goods, not mainly factors of production (A); capital-intensive methods (B) are not its basis; and while traders seek profit (D), the underlying reason nations trade is the difference in resources. Memory tip: Different gifts make trade worthwhile.",
 "|A country with over-valued currency will|expect balance of payments surplus|have increased demand for her exports|increase her foreign reserve|decrease her foreign reserve": "A currency is over-valued when its official exchange rate is higher than its true market value. Foreigners then need more of their own money to buy the country's goods, so exports become expensive and fall, while imports become cheap and rise. The balance of payments moves into deficit, and the central bank must use foreign reserves to pay for the excess imports and defend the rate, so its foreign reserves decrease: D. A surplus, higher export demand and rising reserves (A, B, C) are the results of an under-valued currency. Nigeria's naira was over-valued before SAP (1986), when it was devalued. Memory tip: Over-valued = Over-importing = reserves Out.",
 "|If goods P and Q are purchased by a consumer, a fall in the price of P with the price of Q unchanged will cause the budget line to|rotate outwards away from the origin|shift parallel inwards|rotate inwards towards the origin|shift parallel outwards": "A budget line shows every combination of P and Q a consumer can buy with a fixed income. Its end points are income ÷ price of P on the P axis and income ÷ price of Q on the Q axis. When the price of P falls, the P intercept moves outwards (more P can be bought), but the Q intercept does not move because the price of Q and income are unchanged. So the line pivots outwards from the Q axis, away from the origin: A. The trap: D (shift parallel outwards) is what happens when income rises or both prices fall in the same proportion, not when one price falls. Memory tip: one price changes, the line Rotates; income changes, the line shifts in Parallel.",
 "|One of the reasons for an exceptional demand curve is the|availability of credit facilities|availability of substitutes|change in the price of the commodity|expectation of a future change in price": "The normal demand curve slopes downwards: as price rises, people buy less. Exceptional (abnormal) demand curves slope upwards, and the textbook causes are Giffen goods, goods of ostentation (snob or Veblen goods), ignorance, and the expectation of a future change in price. If buyers believe prices will keep rising, for example during a fuel scarcity or before a budget, they rush to buy more now even though the price is already higher: D. A change in the commodity's own price (C) only causes a movement along a normal curve, and availability of substitutes or credit does not make demand slope upwards. Memory tip: GIVE: Giffen, Ignorance, Veblen, Expectation.",
 "|The elasticity of supply of perishable goods is|elastic|zero|inelastic|unitary": "Elasticity of supply measures how much the quantity supplied responds to a change in price. Sellers of perishable goods such as tomatoes, fresh fish and vegetables cannot store them to wait for a better price, and cannot quickly grow more when price rises. Whatever is harvested must be sold quickly, so the quantity supplied changes little when price changes: supply is inelastic, C. Zero elasticity (B) would mean supply does not change at all, which is the extreme case of fixed supply such as land or a single day's catch already landed, not the general rule for perishables. Durable, storable goods have more elastic supply. Memory tip: if it cannot wait, its supply cannot change much.",
 "|In the pie chart above, the percentage for children is equivalent to|288°|144°|90°|72°": "In a pie chart the whole circle, 360°, stands for 100%, so each 1% is 3.6°.\nStep 1: Children are 40% of the total.\nStep 2: Angle = 40/100 × 360° = 0.4 × 360° = 144°: B.\nCheck: women 35% = 126°, men 25% = 90°; 144° + 126° + 90° = 360°.\nThe trap: C (90°) is the angle for men, the 25% sector, and D (72°) is a fifth of the circle, so read the label carefully. Memory tip: degrees = percentage × 3.6.",
 "|In the diagram above, the marginal rate of substitution of X for Y for a movement from S to T is|5:1|1:5|18:4|13:3": "The marginal rate of substitution (MRS) of X for Y measures how many units of Y a consumer is willing to give up to get one more unit of X while staying on the same indifference curve (the same satisfaction).\nStep 1: Read the two points. S is at 2 units of X and 18 units of Y; T is at 4 units of X and 15 units of Y.\nStep 2: Y given up = 18 − 15 = 3; X gained = 4 − 2 = 2.\nStep 3: MRS of X for Y = ΔY ÷ ΔX = 3 ÷ 2 = 1.5, that is 3:2.\nNone of the options gives 3:2, so this question has no correct answer. Had the consumer given up 5 units of Y (18 to 13) for one more unit of X, the MRS would be 5:1, option A. MRS falls as you move down an indifference curve, which is why the curve is convex to the origin. Memory tip: MRS of X for Y = Y given up ÷ X gained.",
 "|A scientific approach in economics entails|a normative method only|both inductive and deductive methods|a deductive method only|both inductive and normative methods": "Economics is called a social science because it studies human behaviour using scientific methods. The deductive method starts from assumptions (for example, that people seek maximum satisfaction) and reasons logically to conclusions such as the law of demand. The inductive method starts from observed facts and data and builds general laws from them. Economists use both: theories are deduced and then tested against real-world data: B. Normative economics deals with what ought to be (value judgements), so it is not part of the scientific method; positive economics deals with what is. Memory tip: Deduction goes Down from theory to facts; Induction goes Into theory from facts.",
 "|In a centrally planned economy, the economic problem of deciding what to produce is dependent on|state command|relative prices|average cost|average profit": "Every economy must answer what to produce, how, for whom and how to use resources efficiently. In a free-market (capitalist) economy, prices and profits answer these questions. In a centrally planned (command or socialist) economy, a central planning authority owned by the state decides what goods are produced, in what quantities, and how they are shared, and producers follow its directives: A. Relative prices (B) are the guide in a market economy, and average cost or profit (C, D) are signals that private firms use. The former Soviet Union was the classic example of a command economy. Memory tip: Command economy = Central planners Choose.",
 "|A shift in supply curve indicates that a different quantity will be supplied at each possible price because|supply is facing competition|consumers are willing to pay higher prices|price has changed|other factors than price have changed": "The supply curve is drawn for a given set of conditions: costs of production, technology, prices of other goods, weather, taxes and subsidies, and the goals of firms. If only the good's own price changes, sellers move along the existing curve (a change in quantity supplied). When one of those other conditions changes, a different quantity is offered at every price, so the whole curve shifts (a change in supply): D. For example, a fall in the cost of fertiliser shifts the supply of maize to the right. The trap: C (price has changed) describes a movement along the curve, not a shift, and is the classic confusion. Memory tip: own price Moves you along; anything else Shifts the curve.",
 "|The demand for factors of production is an example of|competitive demand|derived demand|composite demand|joint demand": "Firms do not want land, labour, capital or entrepreneurs for their own sake. They want them because consumers want the bread, cars or cloth those factors help to produce. The demand for a factor therefore depends on, or is derived from, the demand for the final product: B. If demand for houses rises, demand for bricklayers and cement rises too. Joint demand (D) is for goods used together, like cars and petrol; composite demand (C) is one good wanted for several uses; competitive demand (A) is for substitutes. Memory tip: Derived = Depends on the demand for the final good.",
 "|A student has ₦30.00 with which to buy a ruler costing ₦18.00 and an exercise book costing ₦25.00. If he buys the exercise book, his opportunity cost is|the exercise book|₦25.00|₦18.00|the ruler": "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. It is measured in the real alternative given up, not in money.\nStep 1: The ruler and exercise book together cost ₦18.00 + ₦25.00 = ₦43.00, more than his ₦30.00, so he can buy only one.\nStep 2: He buys the exercise book, so the alternative he gives up is the ruler: D.\nThe trap: C (₦18.00) is the price of the ruler; opportunity cost is the ruler itself, not its money price. A and B describe the item he actually chose. Memory tip: opportunity cost = what you could have had instead.",
 "|Import substitution aims at|substituting agricultural products with industrial ones|substituting imports with agricultural exports|improving the balance of payments|the diversification of industries": "Import substitution industrialisation means setting up local factories to produce goods that a country used to import, for example assembling vehicles or making textiles at home, usually protected by tariffs and import bans. Its aims include saving foreign exchange, creating jobs, and reducing dependence on foreign goods. By reducing the import bill, it is meant to improve the balance of payments: C. It does not mean replacing farm products with industrial ones (A) or paying for imports with farm exports (B), and diversification (D) is a possible side effect rather than its main aim. Nigeria followed this strategy in the 1960s and 1970s. Memory tip: make it here so you do not have to import it.",
 "|Some of the key indicators of underdevelopment in a country are|poverty, low level of literacy and low income|poverty, low income and low unemployment level|poverty, high level of literacy and low income|low level of illiteracy, high income and poverty": "Economists judge development by indicators such as per capita income, literacy, life expectancy, health and the level of poverty. An underdeveloped country typically has widespread poverty, a low level of literacy (many people cannot read and write) and a low income per head: A. Low unemployment (B), high literacy (C) and high income (D) are signs of a developed country, so each of the other options mixes in a feature that does not fit. These indicators are combined in the Human Development Index (HDI) used by the United Nations. Memory tip: the three P's of underdevelopment: Poverty, Poor education, Poor income.",
 "|ECOWAS has taken a giant step towards economic integration by|introducing a common currency for travelers|presenting a united front against foreign economic exploiters|holding frequent meetings at various state capitals|increasing economic stability among member states": "ECOWAS, the Economic Community of West African States, was formed in Lagos in 1975 to promote economic integration: free movement of people, goods and services, a customs union and eventually a single market and currency. Integration is meant to make member economies stronger and more stable together. So increasing economic stability among member states (D) is right, and so is A: in 1999 ECOWAS introduced the ECOWAS travellers' cheque, a common means of payment for travellers in all member states and a real step towards a common currency. Both are accepted: D or A. Presenting a united front against exploiters (B) and holding meetings (C) are not integration measures in themselves. Memory tip: ECOWAS = Economic Community; integration aims at a stronger, more stable region.",
 "|One of the major factors militating against industrialization in Nigeria is the|frequent breakdown of equipment|failure to get foreign partners and supporters|inadequacy of infrastructural facilities|absence of government participation": "Industries need reliable electricity, good roads and railways, water, and communication networks. In Nigeria, the most often cited obstacle to industrialisation is inadequate infrastructure: erratic power supply forces factories to run costly generators, and poor roads raise transport costs, so many firms close or operate below capacity: C. Equipment breakdown (A) is largely a result of poor maintenance and power surges rather than the root cause, and government has always participated heavily in industry, so D is false. Other factors include shortage of capital, skilled labour and raw materials. Memory tip: no power, no roads, no factories.",
 "|The types, sources and uses of government income are mainly concerned with|public budget|public finance|public expenditure|public revenue": "Public finance is the branch of economics that deals with the income and expenditure of government: the types of revenue (taxes, fees, royalties, loans), where it comes from, and how it is spent on defence, education, health and so on. Because the question covers types, sources and uses of government income together, the whole subject, public finance, is the answer: B. Public revenue (D) covers only sources of income, public expenditure (C) covers only spending, and a budget (A) is a yearly statement of planned revenue and spending. Memory tip: Revenue + Expenditure = Public Finance.",
 "|The present privatization policy in Nigeria is aimed at|reducing the prices of goods and services|increasing efficiency in production|poverty alleviation|reducing income inequality": "Privatization is the sale of all or part of government-owned enterprises to private investors. Nigeria's privatization programme began under the Structural Adjustment Programme in 1988 and was expanded by the Bureau of Public Enterprises from 1999. Its main aim is to make these enterprises more efficient: private owners have a profit motive to cut waste, improve management and serve customers, and government is relieved of subsidies to loss-making corporations: B. Lower prices (A) do not always follow, and poverty alleviation and income equality (C, D) are not its main purpose. Memory tip: Privatize to Perform better.",
 "|One of the roles of the NNPC in the production and refining of crude oil is to|secure maximum government revenue and regulate price|protect the local operators in the petroleum industry|prevent the smuggling of petroleum products|satisfy the domestic consumption of petroleum products": "The Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation (NNPC) was formed in 1977 to manage the country's oil interests. It explores for and produces crude oil through joint ventures, and it owns the refineries at Port Harcourt, Warri and Kaduna, which were built to refine crude oil into petrol, kerosene and diesel for the home market. One of its roles is therefore to satisfy domestic demand for petroleum products: D. Today the company is NNPC Limited, a commercial company under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021, and the Dangote refinery also supplies the local market. Preventing smuggling (C) is a job for customs, and regulation is done by separate regulators. Memory tip: NNPC refines to supply Nigeria first.",
 "|One way of correcting the balance of payments problem of a country is to|devalue the currency|introduce import-promotion measures|deemphasize import|buy investments abroad": "A balance of payments deficit means a country pays out more foreign exchange than it earns. Devaluation lowers the value of the home currency against foreign currencies, so exports become cheaper for foreigners and imports become dearer at home. If demand for exports and imports is elastic enough, exports rise, imports fall and the deficit narrows: A. Promoting imports (B) worsens the deficit, and buying investments abroad (D) is an outflow of funds. Other remedies include tariffs, import quotas, exchange control and export promotion; \"deemphasize import\" (C) is too vague to count as a named policy. Memory tip: devalue to make exports cheap and imports dear.",
 "|One of the problems arising from the localization of industries is|high prices of output|structural unemployment|the exportation of output|the scarcity of foreign exchange": "Localization of industry means the concentration of a particular industry in one area, such as textiles in Kaduna or the oil industry around Port Harcourt. It brings external economies, but it has dangers. If demand for that industry's product falls, or the industry is hit by competition or a change in technology, many workers with the same specialised skills lose their jobs at once and cannot easily find work in other industries. This mismatch is structural unemployment: B. High prices (A) and foreign exchange scarcity (D) are not caused by localization, and exporting output (C) is not a problem. Memory tip: all eggs in one basket: when the industry falls, the whole area is out of work.",
 "|The major purpose of the African Development Bank is to|educate peasant farmers in new techniques|provide loans for development banks in distress|provide loans for infrastructural development|provide loans for trade development": "The African Development Bank (AfDB) was set up in 1964 with headquarters in Abidjan, Côte d'Ivoire. Its main purpose is to contribute to the economic and social development of its regional members by lending for development projects, especially infrastructure such as roads, power, water supply and telecommunications, and by providing technical assistance: C. It does not mainly rescue distressed banks (B) or train peasant farmers (A), and financing trade (D) is not its central aim. Nigeria is one of its largest shareholders. Memory tip: AfDB builds Africa's roads, power and water.",
 "|As a country gets more developed, the percentage of labour engaged in agriculture tends to|switch over to trading|remain constant|increase steadily|decrease steadily": "In a poor, less developed country most people work on farms with simple tools. As development proceeds, farming becomes mechanised, so fewer workers are needed to produce the food, while manufacturing and services grow and attract labour to towns. The share of the labour force in agriculture therefore falls steadily: D. In many developed countries less than 5% of workers are farmers, compared with well over half in many poor countries. Total farm output can still rise, because each farmer produces more. Memory tip: the richer the country, the fewer the farmers.",
 "|A deficit budget can be used to|starve the economy of funds for economic development|protect the economy from inflation|stimulate recovery from trade depression|provide measures to remedy the balance of payments": "A deficit budget is one in which planned government expenditure is greater than expected revenue, the gap being financed by borrowing. Following Keynes, it is used in a depression or recession: the extra government spending raises aggregate demand, creates jobs and income, and through the multiplier helps the economy recover: C. A deficit budget pumps funds into the economy, so it does not starve it of funds (A), and it tends to cause rather than prevent inflation (B); a surplus budget is used to fight inflation. Memory tip: Deficit in Depression, Surplus against inflation.",
 "|In the diagrams above, the opportunity cost of a unit of cotton in terms of cocoa is|20 for Ghana, 60 for Nigeria|1/4 for Ghana, 1/2 for Nigeria|5 for Ghana, 30 for Nigeria|4 for Ghana, 2 for Nigeria": "On a production possibility line, the opportunity cost of one unit of cotton is the cocoa given up, which is the cocoa intercept divided by the cotton intercept.\nStep 1: Ghana: 20 tonnes of cocoa or 5 tonnes of cotton, so 1 tonne of cotton costs 20 ÷ 5 = 4 tonnes of cocoa.\nStep 2: Nigeria: 60 tonnes of cocoa or 30 tonnes of cotton, so 1 tonne of cotton costs 60 ÷ 30 = 2 tonnes of cocoa: D.\nNigeria has the lower opportunity cost, so it has the comparative advantage in cotton.\nThe trap: B (1/4 and 1/2) is the opportunity cost of cocoa in terms of cotton, the ratio turned upside down. Memory tip: cost of X = what you give up ÷ what you get.",
 "|External finance for a limited liability company is mainly sourced through|the issuing of shares|the leasing of equipment|trade credits|loans": "A limited liability company can raise funds internally, from retained (ploughed-back) profits and depreciation provisions, or externally, from outside sources. The main external sources are borrowing: bank loans, overdrafts and debentures. These loans bring in money without changing who owns the company: D. Issuing shares (A) is often listed as a source of capital in its own right, which makes this item debatable, while leasing (B) and trade credit (C) supply equipment and goods rather than finance in the main. Memory tip: inside money = retained profit; outside money = loans.",
 "|One of the major criticisms of the 1962-1968 National Development Plan was that|its execution was based largely on foreign-sourced financial resources|it failed to incorporate lessons gained from earlier plans to enhance efficiency|planned expenditure based too heavily on expected earnings from crude oil|it came too quick after the country's independence": "Nigeria's First National Development Plan (1962–1968) aimed at growth through investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure. Its major weakness was its dependence on foreign finance: about half of the planned public investment was expected to come from foreign aid and loans, but much of it never came, so many projects stalled: A. The civil war (1967–1970) also disrupted it. Oil was not yet the main revenue source in the early 1960s, so C does not fit; that criticism applies to later plans. Memory tip: First plan, Foreign money.",
 "|Which of the following yields more revenue to Nigeria?|Value added tax|Royalties|Direct tax|Indirect tax": "Government revenue in Nigeria comes from direct taxes (on income and profits), indirect taxes (customs and excise duties, VAT), royalties and rents, and other sources. The petroleum profits tax paid by oil companies is a direct tax, and together with company income tax and personal income tax it gives more revenue than any of the other options: C. VAT (A), introduced in 1993, is one indirect tax, and royalties (B), though large, are smaller than the petroleum profits tax. Memory tip: oil profits are taxed directly, and that is where the big money comes from.",
 "|In the short run a firm's marginal cost curve above the point of shut-down is its|supply curve|revenue curve|demand curve|cost curve": "In the short run a firm will keep producing as long as price covers average variable cost (AVC); the lowest point of AVC is the shut-down point. Above it, a profit-maximising firm produces where price = marginal cost. So for each price, the quantity supplied is read off the marginal cost curve, making the MC curve above the shut-down point the firm's short-run supply curve: A. A firm's demand curve (C) and revenue curve (B) come from buyers, not from costs. Memory tip: above AVC, MC = Supply.",
 "|The benefits that accrue to a firm as a result of an improvement in the industry it belongs to are called|internal economies of scale|market economies|external economies of scale|economies of scale": "Economies of scale are cost advantages from large-scale production. Internal economies arise inside the firm as it grows: bulk buying, specialisation, better machinery, easier borrowing. External economies arise outside the firm, from the growth or improvement of the whole industry: a pool of skilled labour, ancillary firms supplying parts, research institutes, better roads and specialised banks in the area. Every firm in the industry enjoys them whatever its own size, so the benefits described are external economies of scale: C. The trap: A (internal economies) sounds close, but internal means from the firm's own expansion. Memory tip: Internal = In the firm; External = Every firm in the industry.",
 "|As a firm increases its output the average fixed cost|tends to decrease continuously|rises and then falls|remains constant|tends to rise continuously": "Average fixed cost is total fixed cost divided by output. Fixed costs, such as rent and the salary of a manager, stay the same whatever is produced. For example, with fixed cost of ₦1,000: at 10 units AFC is ₦100, at 100 units it is ₦10, at 1,000 units it is ₦1. As output rises, the same fixed cost is spread over more units, so AFC keeps falling, approaching zero without ever reaching it: A. The trap: B (rises and then falls) describes no cost curve; AVC, ATC and MC fall and then rise (U-shaped), and that U shape is easy to apply wrongly to AFC. Memory tip: fixed cost spread thinner and thinner.",
 "|The economic policy of deregulation is aimed at encouraging|a competitive market structure|an oligopolistic market structure|a duopolistic market structure|a monopolistic market structure": "Deregulation means removing government controls, licences, price controls and legal barriers that restrict who can enter an industry and how they operate. With barriers removed, new firms can come in and compete, prices are set by the market, and efficiency improves. Its aim is therefore a competitive market structure: A. Oligopoly, duopoly and monopoly (B, C, D) all involve few sellers, which is what deregulation tries to move away from. Examples in Nigeria include the deregulation of telecommunications, which brought GSM operators in 2001, and of the downstream petroleum sector. Memory tip: Deregulate to let more players in.",
 "|The effect of an increase in demand for a commodity accompanied by a decrease in supply will be to|decrease the equilibrium quantity and affect the price in an indeterminate way|raise the price of the commodity and affect the quantity in an indeterminate way|lower its price while affecting the equilibrium quantity in an indeterminate way|raise its price as well as the equilibrium quantity": "Work out each change separately. An increase in demand (demand curve shifts right) raises both price and quantity. A decrease in supply (supply curve shifts left) raises price but lowers quantity. Together, both push price up, so price certainly rises. But for quantity, one change pushes it up and the other pushes it down; the net effect depends on which shift is larger, so it is indeterminate: B. The trap: D (raise price as well as quantity) is true only if the rise in demand is larger than the fall in supply, so it cannot be the general answer. Memory tip: when the two shifts agree on one variable, that one is certain; the other is uncertain.",
 "|Under conditions of perfect competition a firm's supply curve is determined by its|fixed cost curve|variable cost curve|total cost curve|marginal cost curve": "A firm in perfect competition is a price taker: it sells as much as it wants at the market price. To maximise profit it produces where price (which equals marginal revenue) equals marginal cost. So at each possible price the firm's output is read off its MC curve, as long as price covers average variable cost. The firm's supply curve is therefore determined by its marginal cost curve: D. Fixed costs (A) do not affect the output decision at all, and variable and total cost curves (B, C) are used only to find the shut-down and break-even points. Memory tip: competitive firm: P = MR = MC.",
 "|The short-run equilibrium output for a monopolist is determined by the|intersection of the marginal cost and marginal revenue curves|highest point on the total revenue curve|intersection of the average revenue and average cost curves|minimum point on the average cost curve": "All profit-maximising firms, whatever the market structure, produce where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), with MC cutting MR from below. Beyond that point an extra unit adds more to cost than to revenue. The monopolist then charges the price read off its demand (AR) curve at that output: A. The highest point on the total revenue curve (B) maximises revenue, not profit; AR = AC (C) gives only normal profit, the long-run result in monopolistic competition; and minimum AC (D) is the efficient output reached in long-run perfect competition, not by a monopolist. Memory tip: MC = MR is the golden rule for every firm.",
 "|In the diagram above, what is the excess demand at the price of ₦60?|300|100|600|200": "Excess demand is the amount by which quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a price below equilibrium.\nStep 1: Read the diagram at ₦60: the demand curve gives 300 units.\nStep 2: The supply curve at ₦60 gives 100 units.\nStep 3: Excess demand = 300 − 100 = 200: D.\nEquilibrium is at about 200 units, where the two curves cross above ₦60. The trap: A (300) is only the quantity demanded, not the shortage. Memory tip: excess demand = demanded minus supplied, measured across at the same price.",
 "|The equilibrium wage in an economy is determined by the|supply and demand for labour|rate of inflation|workers' union|public service": "Wages are the price of labour, and in a free labour market they are set like any other price. Employers' demand for labour (which falls as wages rise) and workers' supply of labour (which rises as wages rise) meet at the equilibrium wage, where the number of workers firms want equals the number willing to work: A. Trade unions (C) and government (D) can influence wages through bargaining or minimum wage laws, and inflation (B) affects the real wage, but the equilibrium itself is where supply and demand meet. Memory tip: wage = price of labour, set by supply and demand.",
 "|If the growth rate of available resources continuously outpaces that of the population, a country will eventually experience|under population|maximum population|optimal population|over population": "The optimum population is the size at which, given the available resources and technology, output per head is highest. If population is below the optimum, there are not enough people to make full use of the resources: underpopulation. If resources keep growing faster than the population, the population becomes ever smaller relative to what the resources could support, so the country experiences underpopulation: A. Overpopulation (D) is the opposite case, where population outgrows resources, the situation Malthus feared. Memory tip: more resources than people = under; more people than resources = over.",
 "|The middlemen in the chain of distribution are|wholesalers and retailers|manufacturers and consumers|consumers and wholesalers|retailers and consumers": "The chain of distribution links the producer to the consumer: manufacturer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer. The middlemen are those who stand between the producer at one end and the consumer at the other, buying and reselling goods. These are the wholesalers, who buy in bulk from producers, and the retailers, who sell in small quantities to consumers: A. Manufacturers and consumers (B, C, D) are the two ends of the chain, not the middle. Other middlemen include agents, brokers and commission agents. Memory tip: Producer – Wholesaler – Retailer – Consumer: the middle two are the middlemen.",
 "|The Malthusian theory of population growth is often said to be|oversimplified|ambiguous|optimistic|pessimistic": "Thomas Malthus, in his Essay on the Principle of Population (1798), argued that population tends to grow in a geometric progression (1, 2, 4, 8 …) while food supply grows only in an arithmetic progression (1, 2, 3, 4 …). Population would therefore outrun food, and be held down by positive checks such as famine, disease and war unless people practised moral restraint. Because it predicts misery for mankind, the theory is described as pessimistic: D. Critics note that technology and the green revolution raised food output far more than he expected. Memory tip: Malthus = Misery: geometric people, arithmetic food.",
 "|Improved labour efficiency can be measured by|the constancy of input-output ratio|an increase in input-output ratio|an increase in output-input ratio|a decrease in output-input ratio": "Efficiency, or productivity, is measured as output per unit of input: output ÷ input. If the same number of workers produce more goods, or the same goods are produced by fewer workers, the output-input ratio rises, and labour has become more efficient: C. An increase in the input-output ratio (B) means more input for each unit of output, which is a fall in efficiency, and a decrease in the output-input ratio (D) is the same thing. The trap: B uses the right words in the wrong order, the ratio turned upside down. Memory tip: productivity = Output over Input; up is good.",
 "|National income estimates can be used to|compare a country's growth rate with that of another over a period of time|differentiate between the rich and the poor in a country|prepare a country's annual budget|project the level of a country's economic development": "National income statistics measure the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. Their uses include measuring the standard of living (income per head), measuring growth over time, comparing one country's performance with another's, and guiding economic planning. Comparing a country's growth rate with another's over a period is a standard use: A. National income totals do not show how income is shared, so they cannot separate rich from poor within a country (B); budgets (C) rely on revenue and expenditure estimates, and projections (D) are a planning exercise rather than what the estimates themselves show. Memory tip: national income figures compare across time and across countries.",
 "|In Nigeria, the bank that can correctly be described as a unit bank is|the Central Bank|Community Bank|People's Bank|Mortgage Bank": "A unit bank operates from one office only, with no branches, and serves its local area; branch banking is the opposite, with a head office and many branches. In Nigeria, community banks, set up from 1990, were each licensed to operate as a single unit bank owned by and serving one community: B. The Central Bank of Nigeria (A) has branches in many state capitals and is the banker to government and banks, not a unit bank. People's Bank and mortgage banks also operated branches. Since 2007 community banks have been converted into microfinance banks, some of which are still unit banks. Memory tip: one Community, one office = unit bank.",
 "|Creeping inflation implies that there is a|rapid but not permanent increase in the general price level|gentle but not permanent increase in the general price level|rapid and persistent increase in the general price level|gentle and persistent increase in the general price level": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. It is classified by speed. Creeping (mild) inflation is a slow, gentle rise, typically a few per cent a year, that goes on persistently over a long period: D. Galloping inflation is a rapid rise, and hyperinflation is an extremely rapid rise in which money loses its value. A rise that is not persistent (A, B) is not inflation at all but a once-for-all price change. The trap: B uses \"gentle\" but adds \"not permanent\", which contradicts the meaning of inflation. Memory tip: Creeping = Crawling slowly but Continuously.",
 "|In a closed economy, the marginal propensity to consume is 0.6 and the average propensity to consume 0.8. The value of the multiplier is|2.7|2.6|2.4|2.5": "The multiplier depends on the marginal propensity to consume (MPC), the share of each extra naira spent, not on the average propensity.\nStep 1: MPS = 1 − MPC = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4.\nStep 2: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ MPS = 1 ÷ 0.4 = 2.5: D.\nThe APC of 0.8 is extra information put in to distract; using it would give 1 ÷ (1 − 0.8) = 5, which is not an option. Memory tip: k = 1 / (1 − MPC) = 1 / MPS.",
 "|Excess demand inflation can be controlled through|contractionary trade policy|expansionary monetary policy|contractionary fiscal policy|expansionary fiscal policy": "Excess demand (demand-pull) inflation occurs when total spending is greater than the economy can produce at current prices: too much money chasing too few goods. The cure is to reduce total demand. Contractionary fiscal policy does this: government cuts its own spending and raises taxes, leaving households and firms less to spend: C. Expansionary monetary or fiscal policy (B, D) adds to demand and makes inflation worse. Contractionary monetary policy (higher interest rates, open market sales) would also work, but it is not offered. Memory tip: too much demand? Contract it.",
 "|The primary objective of the NDIC is to|provide regulations guiding the finance houses|give protection to banks against fraud|give protection to bank depositors|give protection to the CBN against fraud": "The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) was established in 1988 to insure the deposits of banks. Insured banks pay premiums to it, and if an insured bank fails, NDIC pays depositors up to a set limit. It also supervises banks to reduce the risk of failure and acts as liquidator of failed banks. Its primary objective is therefore the protection of bank depositors: C. Regulation of finance houses (A) is mainly a CBN function, and protecting banks or the CBN against fraud (B, D) is not its purpose. Today it works under the NDIC Act 2023. Memory tip: NDIC = Depositors' Insurance Cover.",
 "|Liquidity preference refers to the|wish to hold more funds for precautionary purposes|need to increase the money supply in order to lower the interest rate|demand to hold money as assets rather than as stocks|need to borrow money for short periods to meet some temporary crises": "John Maynard Keynes used \"liquidity preference\" for the demand for money: the desire of people to hold their wealth in the form of cash (the most liquid asset) rather than in interest-earning assets such as bonds and stocks. He gave three motives: transactions, precautionary and speculative. The question's definition is the demand to hold money as an asset rather than as stocks: C. Option A gives only one of the three motives, so it is too narrow, and B and D describe policy and borrowing, not preference for money. Memory tip: liquidity preference = preferring cash to securities, for Transactions, Precaution and Speculation.",
 "|If the country's GNP in the current year is ₦65 billion and the GNP in the base year is ₦50 billion, the GNP deflator is|30.0|130.0|15.0|1.3": "A GNP deflator, as used here, compares the current-year GNP with the base-year GNP, with the base year set at 100.\nStep 1: Deflator = current-year GNP ÷ base-year GNP × 100.\nStep 2: = ₦65 billion ÷ ₦50 billion × 100 = 1.3 × 100 = 130.0: B.\nThis means prices are 30% higher than in the base year.\nThe trap: D (1.3) forgets to multiply by 100, and A (30.0) is the percentage rise, not the index. Memory tip: an index always starts at 100 in the base year.",
 "|Insurance companies invest mainly in instruments traded on the|commodity market|open market|capital market|money market": "Insurance companies collect premiums now and pay claims later, often many years later in life assurance. They hold large long-term funds, so they invest mainly in long-term securities: shares, government bonds, debentures and stocks, which are traded on the capital market: C. The money market (D) deals in short-term instruments such as treasury bills, which insurers hold only for liquidity. The commodity market (A) deals in goods, and \"open market\" (B) refers to central bank operations. Memory tip: long-term funds go to the long-term market.",
 "|The solution to the problem of double coincidence of wants requires a buyer and a seller whose demands are precisely|complementary|supplementary|composite|competitive": "Under barter, goods are exchanged directly for goods. A trade can take place only if each party has exactly what the other wants and wants exactly what the other has: the farmer with yams who wants cloth must find a weaver who has cloth and wants yams. This \"double coincidence of wants\" means the two traders' wants must match or complement each other precisely: A. The difficulty of finding such a match is the main reason money was invented as a medium of exchange. Supplementary, composite and competitive (B, C, D) are not terms for matching wants. Memory tip: barter needs two matching wants; money needs only one.",
 "|The stock exchange is a market for the buying and selling of|existing shares|monetary instruments|new shares|treasury bills": "A stock exchange, such as the Nigerian Exchange (formerly the Nigerian Stock Exchange, Lagos, 1960), is a market where securities already issued by companies and government are bought and sold. It is chiefly a secondary market: when you buy shares there, you buy them from another investor, not from the company. So it is mainly a market for existing shares: A. New shares (C) are sold to the public in the primary market, though they are then listed on the exchange. Treasury bills (D) and other short-term monetary instruments (B) belong to the money market. Memory tip: Stock Exchange = second-hand market for shares.",
 "|The price of a good rises from ₦5 to ₦8 and the quantity demanded falls from 200 to 190 units. Over this price range, the demand curve is|fairly inelastic|fairly elastic|perfectly inelastic|perfectly elastic": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) compares how strongly quantity responds to a price change.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 190 − 200 = −10, so %ΔQ = 10 ÷ 200 × 100 = 5%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦8 − ₦5 = ₦3, so %ΔP = 3 ÷ 5 × 100 = 60%.\nStep 3: PED = 5% ÷ 60% ≈ 0.08.\nA value between 0 and 1 means demand is inelastic. It is not perfectly inelastic (C), because quantity did fall a little: A.\nThe trap: C catches students who see a tiny change in quantity and forget that perfectly inelastic means no change at all (PED = 0).\nMemory tip: PED below 1 = Inelastic (Insensitive); exactly 0 = perfectly inelastic.",
 "|In economic analysis, a statement is said to be normative if it|can be tested scientifically|relates to value judgment|is contradictory|is incorrect": "Economists separate two kinds of statements. A positive statement describes what is, was or will be, and can be tested against facts (\"raising the price of fuel reduces its consumption\"). A normative statement says what ought to be (\"government should subsidise fuel\"); it depends on opinions and values, so evidence alone cannot settle it. That is why a normative statement relates to value judgment: B. Being testable (A) is the mark of a positive statement, and a normative statement need not be contradictory (C) or incorrect (D); it simply cannot be proved either way.\nThe trap: A is the definition of the opposite kind of statement, positive economics, which students often mix up.\nMemory tip: Normative has \"ought\" in it; Positive can be Proved.",
 "|Utility is the satisfaction derived from|production|distribution|consumption|demand": "Utility is the want-satisfying power of a good, or the satisfaction a person gets when he or she consumes it. Eating a plate of rice, using a phone or riding in a bus all yield utility to the consumer. Utility is the basis of consumer behaviour theory: total utility, marginal utility and the law of diminishing marginal utility all describe how satisfaction changes as more units are consumed. So utility is derived from consumption: C. Production (A) and distribution (B) create and deliver goods, while demand (D) is the desire backed by ability to pay, which follows from expected utility.\nMemory tip: Utility is Used up in consumption; you get it when you Use the good.",
 "|If there is an increase in demand without a corresponding increase in supply, there will be|fall in price|rise in price|shift in supply curve to the right|shift in demand curve to the left": "Price is set where demand equals supply. If demand increases (the demand curve shifts to the right) and supply does not increase as well, buyers want more than sellers offer at the old price. This shortage, or excess demand, makes buyers compete for the limited goods and sellers raise their prices, until a new, higher equilibrium price is reached with a somewhat larger quantity: B. A fall in price (A) would follow a rise in supply or a fall in demand. A shift of supply to the right (C) is exactly what the question rules out, and a leftward demand shift (D) is a fall, not a rise, in demand.\nMemory tip: More Demand, same supply = More Dearness.",
 "|Use the production possibility curve of a country represented in the diagram to answer the question.\n\nAn improvement in technology will enable the country to produce at|V|W|X|Z": "A production possibility curve (PPC) shows the largest combinations of two goods a country can produce with its present resources and technology. Points on the curve (V, Y, W) are efficient, a point inside it (X) means idle or wasted resources, and a point outside it (Z) cannot be reached for now. Better technology lets the same resources produce more of both goods, so the whole curve shifts outwards. A point such as Z, which was once unattainable, then becomes possible: D. V and W are already attainable on the present curve, and X is inside it.\nMemory tip: Inside = Idle, On = Optimal, Outside = Out of reach (until growth or technology shifts the curve).",
 "|Use the production possibility curve of a country represented in the diagram to answer the question.\n\nIf the country is currently producing at point Y, it can increase production of producer goods by moving to the point|V|W|X|Z": "In the diagram, producer goods are measured along the horizontal axis and consumer goods up the vertical axis. Y is on the curve, so the economy is fully employed; it cannot get more of one good without giving up some of the other (opportunity cost). Moving down the curve to the right, from Y to W, increases producer goods and reduces consumer goods: B. Moving to V (A) would give more consumer goods and fewer producer goods. X (C) is inside the curve, with less of both, and Z (D) is outside the curve and cannot be reached with present resources.\nMemory tip: along a PPC, \"more of one means less of the other\": that sacrifice is the opportunity cost.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nQ ... 1 ... 2 ... 3 ... 4 ... 5 ... 6 ... 7 ... 8\nMUy ... 16 ... 14 ... 12 ... 10 ... 8 ... 6 ... 4 ... 2\nMUx ... 11 ... 10 ... 9 ... 8 ... 7 ... 6 ... 5 ... 4\n\nIn the table above, the price of commodity y is ₦2 and that of x is ₦1 while the individual has an income of ₦12. Determine the combination of the two commodities the individual should consume to maximize his utility|3y and 3x|6y and 4x|3y and 6x|5y and 5x": "A consumer maximises utility when the marginal utility per naira is the same for both goods and the whole income is spent: MUy/Py = MUx/Px and 2y + 1x = ₦12.\nStep 1: MUy per naira (MUy ÷ ₦2) is 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1; MUx per naira is MUx itself (price ₦1).\nStep 2: Find matching values: 6 occurs at y = 3 and x = 6.\nStep 3: Spending = 3 × ₦2 + 6 × ₦1 = ₦12, exactly the income: C.\nThe trap: A (3y and 3x) uses the right y but spends only ₦9; D (5y and 5x) costs ₦15, more than the income.\nMemory tip: equal \"MU per naira\" AND spend all the money: both conditions must hold.",
 "|When two variables are positively related, the graph of the relationship|is a straight line|is a downward-sloping curve|is an upward sloping curve|has a negative intercept": "Two variables are positively (directly) related when they move in the same direction: as one rises, the other rises too, as with the price of a good and the quantity supplied. Plotted on a graph, such a relationship climbs from the bottom left to the top right, so the curve slopes upwards: C. A downward-sloping curve (B) shows a negative (inverse) relationship, such as price and quantity demanded. A straight line (A) may slope either way, so it does not tell us the relationship is positive, and the intercept (D) says nothing about the direction of the relationship.\nMemory tip: Positive = uPward; Negative = dowNward.",
 "|For an inferior good, a decrease in real income will lead to|an outward shift of the demand curve|an inward shift of the demand curve|a lowered equilibrium price|a change in quantity demanded": "For a normal good, demand rises when income rises. An inferior good behaves the opposite way: as real income falls, people can no longer afford better substitutes, so they buy more of the inferior good. A change in income is a non-price determinant, so it shifts the whole demand curve. More demand at every price means the curve shifts outwards to the right: A. An inward shift (B) would happen if income rose. A movement along the curve, a change in quantity demanded (D), is caused only by a change in the good's own price.\nThe trap: B is what happens to a normal good, which is the case students usually learn first.\nMemory tip: Inferior goods go In the Opposite direction to income.",
 "|In a planned economy, the emphasis is on|individual choice and decisions|public ownership and control|private ownership and control|prices and competition": "A planned (command or socialist) economy is one in which the state owns the means of production (land, factories, mines) and a central planning authority decides what to produce, how to produce and for whom. The emphasis is therefore on public ownership and control: B. Individual choice (A), private ownership (C) and prices and competition (D) are the features of a capitalist (free market) economy, in which the price mechanism allocates resources. A mixed economy, like Nigeria's, combines both public and private sectors.\nMemory tip: Planned = Public; Capitalist = private Choice.",
 "|Price elasticity of supply is a ratio of the change in|quantity supplied to the change in demand|original quantity to a change in new quantity|quantity supplied to the change in price|price to the change in quantity supplied": "Price elasticity of supply measures how responsive the quantity supplied is to a change in the price of the good.\nFormula: Es = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price.\nSo it is the ratio of the change in quantity supplied to the change in price: C. Option D turns the ratio upside down, and A uses demand instead of price. If Es is greater than 1, supply is elastic (producers can easily expand output, as with manufactured goods); if it is less than 1, supply is inelastic (as with cocoa in the short run).\nThe trap: D has the same two items as C but the wrong way round; the quantity always goes on top.\nMemory tip: elasticity is always \"Quantity over Price\": Q on top.",
 "|The long-run average cost curve is called a planning curve because it shows what happens to costs when|variable inputs are increased|fixed factors are increased|different sizes of plants are built|a bigger size of plants is built": "In the long run all factors are variable, so a firm can choose any size of plant. For each plant size there is a short-run average cost (SAC) curve. The long-run average cost (LRAC) curve touches the lowest-cost part of all these short-run curves (it is their \"envelope\"). It therefore shows the lowest cost of producing each output when different sizes of plant can be built, and a firm uses it before investing to decide which plant to build. That is why it is called a planning curve: C. Changing only variable inputs (A) is the short run, and D refers to just one bigger plant, not the full range of plant sizes.\nMemory tip: LRAC = Long-run Range of All plant sizes = the Planning envelope.",
 "|Imperfect market is characterized by|many buyers and few sellers|a large number of buyers and sellers|non-preferential treatment|perfect mobility of factors of production": "A perfect market has many buyers and many sellers, a homogeneous product, perfect knowledge, free entry and exit, perfect mobility of factors and no preferential treatment of buyers. An imperfect market lacks one or more of these features. Its typical sign is a small number of sellers (as in monopoly or oligopoly) facing many buyers, so each seller has some control over price: A. A large number of buyers and sellers (B), non-preferential treatment (C) and perfect mobility of factors (D) are all features of a perfect market.\nMemory tip: Few sellers = Price-makers (imperfect); Many sellers = Price-takers (perfect).",
 "|Short-run period in production is a period too short for a firm to be able to change its|variable inputs|total outputs|total revenue|scale of operation": "The short run is a period during which at least one factor of production, such as the factory building, machinery or land, is fixed. It is too short for the firm to build a bigger or smaller plant, so it cannot change its scale of operation: D. In the short run the firm changes output only by varying inputs such as labour and raw materials, so variable inputs (A) are exactly what it CAN change. Total output (B) and total revenue (C) are results of production, not what defines the period. In the long run all factors are variable and the scale can change.\nThe trap: A is what the firm can change in the short run; the question asks what it is too short to change.\nMemory tip: Short run = Some factors fixed; Long run = aLL factors variable.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput produced per day (unit) ... Fixed cost per day (₦) ... Total cost per day (₦)\n20 ... 60 ... 100\n30 ... 60 ... 120\n40 ... 60 ... 130\n50 ... 60 ... 135\n60 ... 60 ... 150\n70 ... 60 ... 170\n80 ... 60 ... 190\n\nThe ATC at 30 units of output is|₦120.00|₦60.00|₦4.00|₦3.00": "Average total cost (ATC) is total cost per unit of output.\nStep 1: From the table, at 30 units the total cost is ₦120.\nStep 2: ATC = TC ÷ Q = ₦120 ÷ 30 = ₦4.00: C.\nCheck: ₦4 × 30 = ₦120.\nThe trap: A (₦120.00) is the total cost itself, the figure students copy from the table when they forget to divide by output. B (₦60.00) is the fixed cost.\nMemory tip: \"Average\" always means divide by output: ATC = TC/Q, AFC = FC/Q, AVC = VC/Q.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput produced per day (unit) ... Fixed cost per day (₦) ... Total cost per day (₦)\n20 ... 60 ... 100\n30 ... 60 ... 120\n40 ... 60 ... 130\n50 ... 60 ... 135\n60 ... 60 ... 150\n70 ... 60 ... 170\n80 ... 60 ... 190\n\nAt 60 units of output, the AVC is|₦150.00|₦90.00|₦2.50|₦1.50": "Average variable cost (AVC) is variable cost per unit of output, and variable cost is total cost minus fixed cost.\nStep 1: At 60 units, TC = ₦150 and FC = ₦60.\nStep 2: VC = ₦150 − ₦60 = ₦90.\nStep 3: AVC = ₦90 ÷ 60 = ₦1.50: D.\nThe trap: C (₦2.50) is the average total cost (₦150 ÷ 60), the slip of forgetting to remove fixed cost; B (₦90.00) is total variable cost, not per unit.\nMemory tip: TC = FC + VC, so VC = TC − FC; then divide by Q.",
 "|If the demand curve facing a firm is sharply downward sloping, the firm is likely to be|a perfect competitor|a monopolistic competitor as it can have a limited influence on price|an oligopolist as it can collude with other firms to have some influence on price|a monopolist as it can have a great influence on price": "The slope of a firm's demand curve shows how much power it has over price. A perfect competitor (A) faces a horizontal (perfectly elastic) demand curve, because it is a price taker. A monopolistic competitor (B) faces a gently sloping, fairly elastic curve, because there are many close substitutes. A monopolist is the only seller of a product with no close substitutes, so buyers cannot easily switch; its demand curve is the whole market demand curve and slopes sharply downwards (inelastic). The monopolist can therefore have a great influence on price: D.\nThe trap: B also says the firm influences price, but only a little, which matches a gentle slope, not a sharp one.\nMemory tip: the steeper the demand curve, the stronger the seller's grip on price.",
 "|A normal supply curve is usually positively sloped because the relationship between|demand and price is positive|supply and price is negative|price and demand is negative|price and supply is positive": "The law of supply states that, other things being equal, the higher the price, the greater the quantity supplied, and the lower the price, the less is supplied. Higher prices make production more profitable and draw in more resources and producers. Because price and quantity supplied move in the same direction, the relationship is positive (direct), and a normal supply curve slopes upwards from left to right: D. Options A and C talk about demand, which is not what shapes a supply curve, and B wrongly says the supply relationship is negative.\nThe trap: B names the right pair of variables (supply and price) but gives the wrong sign.\nMemory tip: Supply goes up the Slope: higher price, more supplied.",
 "|The best method of production in an under-populated country is|labour-extensive|land-intensive|capital-intensive|labour-intensive": "A country should use most of the factor it has in plenty and economise on the factor that is scarce. An under-populated country has too few people to make the best use of its land and other resources, so labour is scarce and dear. The sensible choice is to substitute machines for workers, that is, capital-intensive methods, as in heavily mechanised farming: C. Labour-intensive methods (D) suit densely populated countries with abundant cheap labour. \"Labour-extensive\" (A) and \"land-intensive\" (B) do not solve the problem of too few workers.\nThe trap: D is the method a crowded country with cheap labour would choose, the opposite case.\nMemory tip: Few people, more Capital; Many people, more labour.",
 "|Insurance companies, pension and provident funds and unit trusts are all examples of|government financial agencies|non-governmental organizations|non-bank financial institutions|rural-based revenue mobilisers": "Financial institutions are grouped into banks and non-bank financial institutions. Commercial banks accept deposits that can be withdrawn by cheque and can create credit. Insurance companies, pension and provident funds, unit trusts, building societies and finance houses also collect savings and invest them, but they do not accept chequeable deposits or create money. They are therefore non-bank financial institutions: C. They are not all government agencies (A), most are private businesses, and they are not NGOs (B) or rural revenue collectors (D).\nMemory tip: if it pools savings but issues no cheques, it is a Non-Bank Financial Institution.",
 "|For a firm, value added can be defined as the difference between the|value of its output and the cost of production|total revenue and total cost|input prices and product prices|value of its output and inputs purchased from other firms": "Value added is the extra value a firm creates at its own stage of production. It equals the value of the firm's output minus the value of the inputs (raw materials, fuel, components and services) it bought from other firms: D. Example: a baker sells bread worth ₦100,000 made from flour and other inputs costing ₦60,000; its value added is ₦40,000, which pays its workers, rent, interest and profit. Adding the value added at every stage gives national output without double counting. A and B describe profit, not value added, because cost of production includes the firm's own wages.\nThe trap: A looks similar but subtracts all costs, including wages, which leaves profit rather than value added.\nMemory tip: Value Added = Output − Bought-in inputs.",
 "|Securities are described as listed when they are|bought and sold purely for development purposes|mobilized as short-term debt instruments|mobilized as long-term debt instrument|traded on a recognized stock market": "A security (share, bond or debenture) is \"listed\" or \"quoted\" when it has been admitted to the official list of a recognised stock exchange, so that it can be bought and sold there, and its price is published daily: D. In Nigeria this is the Nigerian Exchange Limited (formerly the Nigerian Stock Exchange, demutualised in 2021). Listing requires the company to meet the exchange's rules and publish its accounts. Being long-term or short-term (B, C) describes the kind of instrument, not whether it is listed, and A is not a definition.\nMemory tip: Listed = on the List of a stock exchange.",
 "|The commercial banks differ from non-bank financial institutions because they|accept deposits withdrawable by cheque|mobilize savings|invest surplus funds|contribute to economic development": "Commercial banks and non-bank financial institutions both mobilise savings (B), invest funds (C) and contribute to development (D), so these do not separate them. What is special about commercial banks is that they accept current-account (demand) deposits that can be withdrawn by cheque. Cheques drawn on these deposits are used as money, which is why commercial banks can create credit and why the Central Bank watches them closely: A. Insurance companies, pension funds and unit trusts cannot issue chequebooks against your savings.\nMemory tip: Cheques = Commercial banks.",
 "|Inflation that is usually associated with periods of trade boom is|cost-push inflation|demand-pull inflation|creeping inflation|stagnation": "In a trade boom, incomes, employment, investment and spending all rise. Aggregate demand grows faster than the economy can increase output, so \"too much money chases too few goods\" and prices are pulled up: demand-pull inflation, B. Cost-push inflation (A) starts from rising costs of production, such as wages or imported inputs, and can happen even in a slump. Creeping inflation (C) describes only a slow rate of price increase, not its cause, and stagnation (D) is the opposite of a boom.\nThe trap: A is the other main cause of inflation; link \"boom\" to demand and \"rising costs\" to cost-push.\nMemory tip: Boom = Buyers pull prices up.",
 "|The term \"Near Money\" is best described as|a financial asset that is convertible to cash|a financial instrument that is readily convertible to cash|government financial instrument that is convertible to cash|time deposits with low interest rates": "Near money (quasi-money) consists of assets that are not generally accepted in payment but can be converted into cash quickly and with little or no loss of value: savings and time deposits, treasury bills, bills of exchange and certificates of deposit. The key idea is that conversion is ready (easy and quick), so the best description is a financial instrument readily convertible to cash: B. Option A leaves out \"readily\", and almost any asset is convertible to cash in the end. C wrongly limits near money to government instruments, and D picks one example only.\nThe trap: A is almost the same as B but drops the word \"readily\", which is the heart of near money.\nMemory tip: Near money is Nearly cash: one quick step away.",
 "|The distinguishing characteristic between the money market and the capital market lies in whether the|funds mobilized are private or public|securities are primary or secondary|debt instruments provided are long-term or short-term|securities are in debentures or ordinary shares": "Both markets bring together lenders and borrowers; what separates them is the period of the funds. The money market deals in short-term funds and instruments, usually up to one year, such as treasury bills, call money, commercial papers and certificates of deposit. The capital market deals in long-term funds, such as shares, government development stocks and debentures, traded on the stock exchange. So the distinction is whether the instruments are short-term or long-term: C. Primary and secondary (B) is a division within the capital market, and both markets handle private and public funds (A).\nMemory tip: Money market = Months; Capital market = years.",
 "|If Nigeria's composite price index in 1999 was 140.03% and 144.05% in 2000, the rate of inflation in 2000 was|4.02%|2.10%|2.06%|1.03%": "The rate of inflation is the percentage change in the price index.\nStep 1: Change in index = 144.05 − 140.03 = 4.02 points.\nStep 2: Rate = 4.02 ÷ 140.03 × 100 ≈ 2.87%.\nSo strictly the inflation rate in 2000 was about 2.87%, which is not among the options. The answer the examiners intended is A (4.02%), the rise in the index in points, as if it were measured from a base of 100. This question is marked as not fully correct, because A is a point change, not a percentage change: A.\nMemory tip: inflation rate = (new index − old index) ÷ old index × 100; always divide by the old index.",
 "|The monetary control instrument most effectively used by the Central Bank of Nigeria is the|discount rate|reserve ratio|margin requirements|open market operations": "The CBN controls money supply with instruments such as the bank rate (discount rate, now the Monetary Policy Rate), the cash reserve ratio, liquidity ratio and open market operations (OMO). OMO is the buying and selling of government securities, mainly treasury bills, to take money out of or put money into the banking system. After Nigeria moved from direct controls (credit ceilings) to indirect, market-based control in 1993, OMO became the CBN's principal and most effectively used tool: D. Margin requirements (C) are a minor tool for stock-market lending. Today the CBN still relies heavily on OMO, alongside the MPR and the cash reserve ratio.\nMemory tip: OMO = Open Market, Often used.",
 "|The major objectives of a revenue allocation formula in a country is to|share revenue between the public and private sectors|ensure the financial viability of the country|share revenue between the different tiers of government|divert revenue from areas of surplus to areas of need": "In a federation such as Nigeria, most revenue (especially oil revenue) is collected centrally into the Federation Account. A revenue allocation formula sets out how this money is shared vertically among the federal, state and local governments, and horizontally among the states using principles like population, equality of states and derivation. Its major objective is therefore to share revenue between the different tiers of government: C. Sharing with the private sector (A) is not its job. D describes one horizontal principle (need), not the main purpose.\nMemory tip: Revenue allocation = sharing the national \"cake\" among Tiers (federal, state, local).",
 "|The effectiveness of devaluation as a solution to a balance of payments problems depends on the|relative elasticities of demand and supply of imports and exports|foreign investments of business|value of visible and invisible goods|value of invisible items of both domestic and foreign trades": "Devaluation lowers the value of a country's currency, making its exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home. Whether this improves the balance of payments depends on how buyers and sellers respond: foreign demand for exports must rise enough, and local demand for imports must fall enough. If demand for exports and imports is elastic (the Marshall–Lerner condition: the elasticities add up to more than 1) and export supply can expand, devaluation works; if demand is inelastic, foreign exchange earnings may even fall. So its effectiveness depends on the relative elasticities of demand and supply of imports and exports: A.\nMemory tip: Devaluation needs Elastic trade to Deliver.",
 "|IBRD as an international monetary institution is concerned with the|balance of payments problems of member nations|financing of private business in member nations|development of infrastructure in member nations|improvement of trade among member nations": "The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD), the main arm of the World Bank, was set up at Bretton Woods in 1944 with the IMF. Its original aim was to rebuild war-damaged Europe; today it gives long-term loans and technical help to member countries for development projects such as roads, power, water, agriculture and education, that is, development of infrastructure: C. Balance of payments problems (A) are the IMF's area. Lending to private business (B) is the role of its sister body, the International Finance Corporation (IFC).\nMemory tip: IMF = Money problems (balance of payments); IBRD = Building Roads and Development.",
 "|An advantage of the sole proprietorship over the partnership form of business organization is that|it enjoys limited liability for debt in the event of failure|its existence is limited to an individual's lifespan|it relies on the decision of friends to succeed|the possibility of conflict in management is virtually non-existent": "A sole proprietorship has one owner, who provides the capital, takes all decisions and keeps all the profit. In a partnership, two or more partners share decisions, and they can disagree, sometimes causing the business to break up. Because the sole trader alone decides, there is virtually no possibility of conflict in management: D. A is false, because the sole trader has unlimited liability. B (limited lifespan) and C (relying on friends) are disadvantages, not advantages.\nThe trap: A sounds like an advantage, but unlimited liability is exactly what sole traders and ordinary partners share.\nMemory tip: Sole = Single boss = no quarrels, but no limit on liability.",
 "|The distinction between capital and recurrent expenditure lies in the|amount of expenditure involved|source of the revenue generated|nature of the goods and services to be provided|time frame of the expenditure": "Government spending is divided into recurrent and capital expenditure. Recurrent expenditure covers items used up within the financial year that recur year after year, such as salaries, pensions, maintenance and stationery. Capital expenditure is on projects and assets that last for many years, such as roads, schools, hospitals and dams. So the line between them is drawn by how long the spending lasts, the time frame of the expenditure: D. The amount (A) or the source of revenue (B) does not decide the class. C is close, because capital items are durable assets, but the classification turns on their lasting nature over time.\nMemory tip: Recurrent = Repeats every year; Capital = lasts for years.",
 "|Under floating exchange rate regime, the determinant of the exchange rate is|the system of government|demand for and supply of foreign goods|the highest denomination of the currency|an act of the national assembly": "Under a floating (flexible) exchange rate system, the value of a currency is not fixed by government but is determined by market forces, the demand for and supply of foreign exchange. Demand for foreign currency comes mainly from importers of foreign goods and services, and supply comes from exporters selling to foreigners. So the exchange rate rises or falls with the demand for and supply of foreign goods: B. The system of government (A), the highest currency note (C) and an act of the National Assembly (D) do not set a floating rate; a law setting the rate would mean a fixed system.\nMemory tip: Floating = Free market forces of demand and supply.",
 "|To compare the standard of living among nations, the most widely used economic indicator is the|per capita nominal income|real domestic output|real gross national income|real per capita income": "To compare living standards, economists divide national income by population to get income per head (per capita income), and use real figures to remove the effect of inflation. Real per capita income shows the goods and services the average person can actually buy, so it is the most widely used indicator: D. Nominal per capita income (A) is distorted by price levels. Real domestic output (B) and real gross national income (C) ignore population size, so a large, poor country could look richer than a small, rich one. \nThe trap: C is \"real\" too, but it is a total for the whole country, not per person.\nMemory tip: Real + Per head = true living standard.",
 "|Statutory organizations usually established by Acts of parliament are called|public enterprises|public corporations|cooperative societies|joint-stock companies": "A public corporation is a government-owned enterprise set up by a special Act of parliament (or decree), which gives it its name, powers, duties and management board. Examples in Nigeria included NEPA, NITEL, the Nigerian Railway Corporation and NNPC. They are statutory organisations because a statute creates them: B. \"Public enterprises\" (A) is a wider term that also covers government-owned companies registered under company law. Cooperatives (C) and joint-stock companies (D) are registered under ordinary law. Note that NEPA was replaced by PHCN in 2005 and the power companies were privatised in 2013.\nThe trap: A is the broad family name, while B is the specific statutory form.\nMemory tip: Statute = Special Act = State corporation.",
 "|A greater burden of the taxes on essential goods is borne by the|higher income group|newly recruited workers|lower income group|contract workers": "Taxes on essential goods (food, kerosene, soap) are indirect taxes. Rich and poor pay the same tax on each unit, but poor households spend a much larger share of their income on essentials. So the tax takes a bigger proportion of a low income than of a high one: it is regressive, and the lower income group bears the greater burden: C. The higher income group (A) pays the same amount per unit but it is a smaller part of their income. Newly recruited workers (B) and contract workers (D) are not a basis for the tax burden.\nMemory tip: taxes on necessities hit the Needy hardest.",
 "|Privatization and commercialization of public enterprises in Nigeria is necessitated by|the IMF conditions|the IBRD conditions|the government's loss of interest in them|their operational inefficiency": "In the 1980s Nigeria's public enterprises (NEPA, NITEL, Nigeria Airways and others) were widely seen as inefficient: they made losses, gave poor service, had too many staff and depended on government subsidies. The Privatisation and Commercialisation Decree of 1988, under the Structural Adjustment Programme, aimed to make them efficient and reduce the burden on the treasury. So their operational inefficiency was the main reason: D. IMF and World Bank conditions (A, B) encouraged reform, but the root cause was inefficiency. Since 1999 the Bureau of Public Enterprises has continued privatisation, including the 2013 sale of the PHCN power companies.\nMemory tip: Privatise because public firms Performed Poorly.",
 "|The theory of comparative advantage states that a commodity should be produced in that nation where the|absolute cost is least|absolute money cost is least|opportunity cost is least|production possibility curve increases": "David Ricardo's theory of comparative advantage says that even if one country is better at producing everything (absolute advantage), trade still benefits both countries if each specialises in the good in which it is relatively more efficient, that is, the good it can produce at the lowest opportunity cost (the least amount of the other good given up): C. Absolute cost (A) or absolute money cost (B) is Adam Smith's absolute advantage. An increase in the production possibility curve (D) is economic growth.\nThe trap: A and B describe absolute advantage, the older idea that comparative advantage improves on.\nMemory tip: Comparative = Cost in terms of what you Give up (opportunity cost).",
 "|The effect of an increase in the personal income tax is to|raise the absolute price level|distort the economy|reduce unemployment|reduce the disposable income": "Disposable income is the income a person has left to spend or save after paying direct taxes such as personal income tax: disposable income = income − direct taxes. If the rate of personal income tax goes up, more is deducted, so the taxpayer's disposable income falls: D. Lower disposable income tends to reduce consumer spending, which is why higher taxes are used to fight inflation, so raising the price level (A) is the opposite effect, and higher taxes do not reduce unemployment (C). B is too vague to be the direct effect.\nMemory tip: Tax up, take-home down.",
 "|An important factor hindering the rapid development of the industrial sector in Nigeria is|excessive demand for finished products|the dominance of the oil sector|rural-urban migration|inadequate infrastructure facilities": "Industries need reliable electricity, good roads and railways, water and communications. In Nigeria, poor power supply forces firms to run their own generators, bad roads raise transport costs, and these extra costs make local products dear and uncompetitive. Inadequate infrastructure facilities is therefore a major factor hindering rapid industrial development: D. Excessive demand for finished products (A) would encourage, not hinder, industry. Dominance of the oil sector (B) and rural-urban migration (C) are real issues but are not the direct, important hindrance named in the syllabus.\nMemory tip: no Power, no Roads, no Progress in industry.",
 "|The demand for labour is an example of|competitive demand|derived demand|composite demand|joint demand": "Demand for a factor of production is derived demand: it depends on the demand for the goods and services that the factor helps to produce. Employers hire bricklayers because people want houses, and bakers because people want bread; if demand for bread falls, demand for bakers falls too. So the demand for labour is derived demand: B. Joint demand (D) is when goods are demanded together (car and fuel), composite demand (C) is when one good has several uses (sugar for tea and for soft drinks), and competitive demand (A) is for substitutes.\nMemory tip: Derived = Depends on the product's demand.",
 "|The major role of multi-national companies in the petroleum industry in Nigeria is|oil prospecting only|oil marketing only|the establishment of refineries|oil marketing and prospecting": "Nigeria's oil industry has been run largely through joint ventures between NNPC and multinational oil companies such as Shell, ExxonMobil, Chevron, Total and Agip. These firms explore for (prospect) and produce crude oil, and several also market petroleum products through their filling stations. Their major role is therefore both prospecting and marketing: D. Options A and B say \"only\", which leaves out half of what they do. Refineries (C) in Nigeria were built and owned mainly by the government through NNPC. Since the Petroleum Industry Act 2021, NNPC has become NNPC Limited, a commercial company.\nMemory tip: Multinationals: Mine (prospect) and Market.",
 "|Fixing the prices of agricultural products can be a problem because of the|unpredictable output of farmers|instability of government policies|activities of marketing boards|size of agricultural exports": "Agricultural output depends on weather, rainfall, pests, diseases and the number of farmers who plant a crop, so harvests vary unpredictably from year to year. When output is large, prices tend to fall; when it is small, they rise. A price fixed in advance may therefore cause gluts or shortages, and maintaining it needs costly buying and storage. That is why fixing agricultural prices is a problem: the unpredictable output of farmers, A. Unstable government policy (B) and marketing boards (C) are about how prices were fixed, not why fixing them is difficult. The commodity marketing boards were abolished in 1986 under SAP.\nMemory tip: Uncertain harvest, Uncertain price.",
 "|Occupational mobility as applied to factors of production means the ease by which|factors can be transferred from one place to another|factors can be transferred from one form of use to another|resources can be transferred from one place to another|resources can be transformed from one form to another": "Factors of production can be mobile in two ways. Occupational mobility is the ease with which a factor can move from one use or job to another, for example a teacher becoming a banker, or land used for cassava being used for maize: B. Geographical mobility is the ease of moving from one place to another, as in options A and C. Option D talks about transforming resources, which is production, not mobility. Training and general skills raise occupational mobility.\nThe trap: A and C describe geographical mobility, the closely related twin concept.\nMemory tip: Occupational = Occupation (job) changes; Geographical = Geography (place) changes.",
 "|The labour force of a country can be determined by examining the|sex distribution of the population|occupational distribution of the population|age structure of the population|geographical distribution of the population": "The labour force (working population) is made up of people of working age, usually 15 to 64 years in Nigeria, who are working or are able and willing to work. To know how big it is, we look at the age structure (age distribution) of the population, which shows how many people fall in the young dependent, working-age and old dependent groups: C. Sex distribution (A) tells us male and female numbers, occupational distribution (B) shows how those already working are spread over jobs, and geographical distribution (D) shows where people live.\nThe trap: B sounds close because it is about jobs, but it shows how workers are spread, not how many there are.\nMemory tip: Labour force = working Age, so look at the Age structure.",
 "|Two basic features common to an industry are|similarity of products and production processes|similarity of products and source of raw materials|production processes and source of capital|source of capital and labour supply": "A firm is a single business unit; an industry is a group of firms producing the same or similar products, such as the cement industry or the textile industry. Firms in one industry usually use similar production processes as well, with similar raw materials and machines. So the two basic common features are similarity of products and of production processes: A. Option B includes the source of raw materials, which can differ (one textile mill may use local cotton, another imported yarn). The source of capital (C, D) and labour supply (D) differ from firm to firm.\nThe trap: B shares \"similarity of products\" with A, so it looks right at first glance.\nMemory tip: Industry = Identical products, Identical processes.",
 "|A country is said to be over populated when|there are too many able-bodied men and women in the country|the resources are more than enough to cater for the population|the size of the population is greater than the annual budget|the resources are inadequate to cater for the population": "Over-population, under-population and optimum population all compare the size of the population with the resources (land, capital, technology) available. A country is over-populated when its population is too large for its resources, so resources are inadequate and income per head is lower than it would be with fewer people: D. B describes under-population (resources more than enough). Many able-bodied people (A) can be an asset if resources match them, and comparing population with the annual budget (C) makes no sense.\nThe trap: B uses the same words but describes the opposite situation, under-population.\nMemory tip: Over-populated = Over-stretched resources.",
 "|When a union is composed of workers with same skill, it is called|an industrial union|a workers' union|a craft union|a technical union": "Trade unions are organised in different ways. A craft union is made up of workers who share the same skill or trade, wherever they work, for example electricians or carpenters: C. An industrial union (A) brings together all workers in one industry, whatever their skills, such as all workers in the petroleum industry. A general (workers') union (B) recruits workers of many skills from many industries. \"Technical union\" (D) is not a standard type.\nThe trap: A is the other main type of union, organised by industry instead of by skill.\nMemory tip: Craft = same Craft (skill); Industrial = same Industry.",
 "|An adverse environmental impact of the petroleum industry on the economy is|the vandalisation of oil equipment|oil spillage|communal riots|community development": "The petroleum industry earns most of Nigeria's foreign exchange, but it also has harmful effects on the environment. Oil spillage from pipelines, wells and tankers pollutes rivers, creeks and farmland in the Niger Delta, killing fish and crops and destroying the livelihoods of farmers and fishermen: B. Gas flaring is another serious environmental effect. Vandalisation of equipment (A) and communal riots (C) are social problems linked to the industry, not environmental impacts of it, and community development (D) is a benefit, not a harm.\nMemory tip: Environmental harm = what the oil Does to land, water and air (spills, flaring).",
 "|By advertising, a monopolistic competitive firm tries to shift its|supply curve to the right|demand curve to the right|supply curve to the left|demand curve to the left": "A firm in monopolistic competition sells a product that is slightly different from its rivals' products, so it can use advertising and branding to win customers. Successful advertising makes more people want the product at every price, so the whole demand curve for the firm's product moves to the right (and often becomes less elastic, because buyers become loyal to the brand). Advertising does not change the firm's costs of producing each unit in a way that shifts supply, and a firm would never pay to push its own demand to the left: B. Memory tip: Adverts Add buyers, so demand moves Right.",
 "|Given that Y = C + I and C = bY where b = 0.8, what is the multiplier?|5|10|4|2": "In this simple economy Y = C + I and consumption C = bY, so b is the marginal propensity to consume (MPC). The multiplier tells us how much income rises for each ₦1 of new investment.\nStep 1: MPC = b = 0.8, so MPS = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2.\nStep 2: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5.\nSo every ₦1 of extra investment raises national income by ₦5: A. The trap: D (2) is what students get if they divide by the MPC wrongly or mix up the formula; always divide 1 by the MPS. Memory tip: k = 1/MPS, and a high MPC means a big multiplier.",
 "|The role of the Central Bank in the economic development of Nigeria is to|give short-and medium-term loans to the public|give financial and technical advice to customers|use the cheques and bank drafts to facilitate business transactions|maintain a stable price system": "The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN), set up in 1958 and opened in 1959, is the bankers' bank and the government's bank. It does not lend to members of the public, advise individual customers or handle their cheques; those are jobs of commercial banks. Its contribution to development is through monetary policy: controlling money supply, interest rates and credit so that prices stay stable and the naira keeps its value, which encourages saving and investment: D. Options A, B and C describe what commercial banks do. Memory tip: CBN = Controls money, Banks for banks, No public customers.",
 "|The primary function of non-bank financial institutions is to|manage investment portfolios for government|mobilize saving for investment|grant loans to banks|receive deposits from banks": "Non-bank financial institutions include insurance companies, pension funds, building societies (primary mortgage institutions), finance houses and unit trusts. They do not take ordinary current-account deposits or create money as banks do. Their main job is to collect savings from people (premiums, pension contributions, savings plans) and channel them into long-term investment such as mortgages, shares and government stocks: B. They do not exist mainly to lend to banks or to manage government's portfolio. Memory tip: Non-banks are savings funnels: Savings in, Investment out.",
 "|At the maximum point of the total product curve of a firm, marginal product is|decreasing|increasing|constant|zero": "Total product (TP) is total output; marginal product (MP) is the extra output from one more unit of a variable factor such as labour. While MP is positive, TP keeps rising. When MP falls to zero, adding another worker adds nothing, so TP stops rising and is at its maximum. If more workers are added after that, MP becomes negative and TP falls. So at the peak of the TP curve, MP is zero: D. MP is decreasing on the way to the peak, but at the peak itself its value is zero. Memory tip: TP at the top, MP hits the floor (zero).",
 "|In an open economy, the GNP is measured as|C + I + G + X|C + I + G + (X − M)|C + I + G + M|C + I + G": "Expenditure on a country's output comes from households (C), firms (I) and government (G). In a closed economy that is all. In an open economy foreigners also buy our exports (X), while part of our spending goes on imports (M), so we add net exports: C + I + G + (X − M): B. Strictly, this expression gives GDP at market prices; adding net factor income from abroad turns it into GNP, but of the options only B correctly includes the foreign sector. A forgets imports, C adds them instead of subtracting, and D is a closed economy. Memory tip: Open economy = add X, take away M.",
 "|The diagram above represents the short-run position of a monopolist. The profit-maximizing output is|Q3|Q4|Q1|Q2": "Every firm, including a monopolist, maximises profit where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), with MC cutting MR from below. A monopolist's MR line lies below its AR (demand) curve and falls twice as steeply. In the diagram, the steep straight line falling from the price axis is MR, and it crosses the MC curve directly above output Q2, so Q2 is the profit-maximising output: D. The other marked outputs are not where MC equals MR, so producing there would either leave extra profit unearned or add units that cost more than they bring in. Memory tip: Profit peaks where MC = MR.",
 "|The capital market provides business firms with the avenue to|lease capital equipment|obtain long-term funds|obtain short-term funds|purchase capital equipment": "Financial markets are split by the length of the loan. The money market (treasury bills, call money, commercial papers) provides short-term funds. The capital market, including the stock exchange, is where firms raise long-term funds by selling shares, debentures and bonds, and where governments sell development stocks: B. Option C describes the money market, so do not mix the two. Leasing or buying equipment (A and D) is what firms may do with the funds, not what the market provides. The trap: C (short-term funds) catches students who confuse the capital market with the money market. Memory tip: Capital market = Long life money; money market = short.",
 "|Which of the financial institutions is charged with the formulation of monetary policies in Nigeria?|The Central Bank of Nigeria|The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation|The Abuja Commodity Exchange|The Lagos Stock Exchange": "The Central Bank of Nigeria is responsible for formulating and implementing monetary policy, using tools such as the monetary policy rate, open market operations, cash reserve ratio and liquidity ratio. NDIC insures bank deposits and helps supervise banks, the Abuja Commodity Exchange was set up for trading farm commodities, and the Lagos Stock Exchange (today the Nigerian Exchange, NGX) is a market for shares and bonds: A. Memory tip: Monetary policy belongs to the Monetary authority, the CBN.",
 "|GDP differs from GNP because GDP|is inclusive of depreciation|emphasizes the final goods and services produced within the country at a particular period|emphasizes the goods and services produced within the country|emphasizes the market value of all final goods and services produced by nationals": "Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the value of all final goods and services produced within a country's borders in a period, whoever produces them. Gross National Product (GNP) is the value produced by the country's citizens and their firms, wherever they are: GNP = GDP + net factor income from abroad. So the key difference is \"within the country\" versus \"by nationals\": C. Option B says the same thing in fuller words, so it is also accepted; D describes GNP and A (depreciation) is true of both gross measures. Memory tip: Domestic = inside the Door; National = our Nationals anywhere.",
 "|The distinction between perfect competition and monopolistic competition is that the latter is characterized by|a single market price|many buyers and sellers|product differentiation|homogeneity of product": "Perfect competition and monopolistic competition both have many buyers and sellers and free entry. The key difference is the product: in perfect competition every firm sells an identical (homogeneous) product at a single market price, while in monopolistic competition each firm's product is differentiated by brand, packaging, quality or location: C. Options A, B and D are features of perfect competition or of both structures. Memory tip: Monopolistic competition = Many sellers, Many brands.",
 "|The downstream activities of oil companies in Nigeria involve|refining and distribution|prospecting and exploration|off-shore drilling|management of spillage and pollution": "The oil industry is divided into stages. Upstream covers exploration, prospecting and drilling to produce crude oil, including offshore drilling. Downstream covers what happens to the oil after it is produced: refining it into petrol, kerosene and diesel, then transporting, storing and selling these products through depots and filling stations. So downstream activities involve refining and distribution: A. Options B and C are upstream activities. Memory tip: Upstream finds the oil; Downstream delivers the products.",
 "|The movement of goods and services from the producers to the consumers is referred to as|external trade|the distribution chain|the circular flow|consumption": "Production is not complete until goods reach the people who use them. The route goods follow from the producer (manufacturer) through wholesalers and retailers to the final consumer is called the chain (or channel) of distribution: B. The circular flow describes payments and goods moving between households and firms in the whole economy, external trade is trade with other countries, and consumption is the final use of goods. Memory tip: Producer, Wholesaler, Retailer, Consumer: the links of the distribution chain.",
 "|The purpose of conducting a census is to enable a country to|plan her capital inflow and outflow|determine the growth rate of population|determine the optimum level of population|plan her growth and development": "A census counts the whole population and records age, sex, occupation, location and other details. Government uses these facts for planning: how many schools, hospitals, houses and jobs are needed, where to build roads, how to share revenue and seats. So the purpose is to help the country plan her growth and development: D. A census can show the growth rate (B), but that figure is itself only useful as an input to planning. Nigeria's last accepted census was in 2006. Memory tip: Count people to Plan for people.",
 "|A major cause of the slow pace of industrialization in Nigeria is inadequate|raw materials|landmass|skilled labour|communal labour": "Nigeria has many raw materials (oil, gas, tin, cotton, cocoa) and a large land area, so A and B are not the problem. A major obstacle to industrialisation has been the shortage of skilled labour: engineers, technicians and managers who can build, run and maintain modern factories. Other problems include poor power supply, weak infrastructure and inadequate capital. Communal labour (D) has nothing to do with factories: C. Memory tip: Machines need Minds: no skills, slow industry.",
 "|The maximum wage legislation seeks to|fix wages above the equilibrium wage rate|set wages below the equilibrium wage rate|deter employers from exploiting their employees|ensure an upper limit to the wages payable": "A maximum wage is a legal ceiling: employers may not pay more than the stated amount. It is an upper limit on wages payable (D). Like any maximum price, it only bites if it is set below the equilibrium wage that the market would otherwise reach (B); a ceiling above equilibrium would change nothing. Because the two options describe the same policy from two angles, both are accepted, and the answer shown is: B. A describes a minimum wage, and C is the aim of minimum wage laws. Memory tip: Maximum = ceiling, set below; Minimum = floor, set above.",
 "|The marketing of agricultural commodities in Nigeria is hampered by|lack of adequate storage facilities|the existence of farmers' associations|the existence of commodity exchange|the abolition of marketing boards": "Many Nigerian farm products, especially food crops like tomatoes, yams and vegetables, are perishable. Without good storage (silos, cold rooms, warehouses), farmers must sell immediately at harvest when prices are lowest, and much of the produce rots before reaching markets. This lack of adequate storage facilities is a major marketing problem: A. Farmers' associations and commodity exchanges help marketing rather than hamper it. Other problems include poor roads and too many middlemen. Memory tip: No Store, No Sale at a good price.",
 "|Import duty concessions on industrial inputs directly benefit the|consumers|external sector|commercial sector|manufacturers": "An import duty concession lowers or removes the tax on goods brought into the country. When the concession is on industrial inputs (machinery, spare parts, raw materials), the people who import and use those inputs, the manufacturers, pay less, so their production costs fall directly: D. Consumers may gain later if lower costs bring lower prices, but that benefit is indirect. The policy is meant to encourage local manufacturing. Memory tip: Inputs are for Industry, so the Manufacturer gains first.",
 "|The labour force in Nigeria falls between the ages of|18 and 45|18 and 55|18 and 60|18 and 30": "The labour force (working population) is the people of working age who are able and willing to work. In Nigerian economics textbooks of the time, working age was taken as from about 18, after secondary school, up to 60, the normal retirement age in the public service: C. Today the National Bureau of Statistics follows the international standard of 15 to 64 years. The trap: B (18 and 55) catches students who remember an older retirement age. Memory tip: Work from 18 to 60, then retire.",
 "|Government's involvement in agricultural business in Nigeria is most manifested in the|establishment of farm settlements in each state of the federation|establishment of credit guarantee schemes for small-scale farmers|formation of farmers' cooperative societies|procurement and distribution of fertilizers": "Government takes part in agriculture in many ways: research institutes, credit schemes, extension services, farm settlements and input supply. Of these, the most widespread and visible has been the procurement (bulk buying) and distribution of fertilizers to farmers, often at subsidised prices, through federal and state agencies: D. Farm settlements were limited mainly to the old regions in the 1960s, and cooperatives are formed by farmers themselves. Memory tip: Government Feeds the Farms with Fertilizer.",
 "|The difference between birth and death rates of a given population is referred to as the|mortality rate|fertility rate|natural growth rate|linear growth rate": "Birth rate is the number of live births per 1,000 people in a year; death (mortality) rate is the number of deaths per 1,000. The birth rate minus the death rate is the natural growth rate (natural increase) of the population, which ignores migration. For example, a birth rate of 40 per 1,000 and a death rate of 15 per 1,000 gives a natural growth rate of 25 per 1,000, or 2.5%: C. Mortality rate (A) is only the deaths side. Memory tip: Births minus Deaths = Natural growth.",
 "|Fiscal policy is the government's plan to control aggregate demand by manipulating|the demand and supply of money|revenue and expenditure|tastes and preferences of consumers|the structure of production and employment": "Government has two main tools for managing aggregate demand. Fiscal policy works through the budget: changing taxes and other revenue, and changing government spending. Raising spending or cutting taxes boosts demand; cutting spending or raising taxes reduces it: B. Monetary policy, run by the Central Bank, works through the supply of money and interest rates (A). The trap: A (the demand and supply of money) catches students who mix up fiscal and monetary policy. Memory tip: Fiscal = the Fisc, the government's purse: Taxes and Spending.",
 "|A country is said to have absolute advantage when she|has a higher opportunity cost in the production of a good|can sell to other countries without buying from them|can produce more of any good with fewer resources|has a lower opportunity cost in the production of a good": "A country has an absolute advantage in a good when it can produce more of it than another country with the same resources, or the same amount with fewer resources: C. This was Adam Smith's basis for trade. Having a lower opportunity cost (D) is comparative advantage, David Ricardo's idea, which explains why trade benefits both countries even when one is better at everything. The trap: D catches students because it is the definition of comparative, not absolute, advantage. Memory tip: Absolute = Amount (more output); Comparative = Cost (lower opportunity cost).",
 "|The European Union was formed to|promote free trade within member nations|promote economic integration in non-member countries|assist countries to achieve their economic goals|finance development projects in member nations": "The European Union grew from the European Economic Community (EEC), set up by the Treaty of Rome in 1957, and was formally created by the Maastricht Treaty in 1993. Its central aim was to remove tariffs and other barriers so goods, services, capital and people move freely among member states, forming a single market; it later added a common currency, the euro, for many members: A. It does not work for integration among non-member countries (B). Memory tip: EU = Easy movement within the Union.",
 "|If budget deficits are financed by borrowing, the crowding-out effects can be offset by an increase in|government expenditure|savings|interest rates|exchange rates": "When government borrows to finance a deficit, it competes with private firms for the available pool of loanable funds. This raises interest rates, and some private investment that would have happened is \"crowded out\". The effect is reduced if the pool of funds grows, that is, if savings increase, so government and private borrowers can both be served without interest rates rising as much: B. More government spending (A) or higher interest rates (C) would make crowding out worse. Memory tip: More Savings, more room for borrowers: Savings soften crowding out.",
 "|When the importation of a commodity is limited to a definite quantity, the trade control means used is known as|exchange control|tax relief|devaluation|quotas": "Governments restrict imports in several ways. A quota sets a fixed maximum quantity (or value) of a good that may be imported in a given period, for example 50,000 tonnes of rice a year: D. Exchange control rations foreign currency, devaluation lowers the value of the home currency to make imports dearer, and tax relief reduces taxes. Only a quota limits imports to a definite quantity. Memory tip: Quota = Quantity limit.",
 "|The excess of aggregate expenditure over full employment level of output is referred to as|GNP gap|deflationary gap|inflationary gap|recessionary gap": "An inflationary gap exists when aggregate expenditure (C + I + G + X − M) is greater than the value of output the economy can produce at full employment. Since output cannot rise further, the excess spending pushes up prices: C. The opposite, where spending falls short of the full-employment level, is a deflationary (recessionary) gap, so B and D describe the reverse situation. Memory tip: Too much spending, prices Inflate; too little, the economy Deflates.",
 "|The tax levied on goods and services at each stage of production is|surtax|VAT|ad valorem tax|PAYE": "Value Added Tax (VAT) is an indirect tax charged at each stage of production and distribution, on the value added at that stage; each seller claims back the VAT paid on inputs, and the final consumer bears the full tax. Nigeria introduced VAT in 1993 at 5%; the rate is now 7.5%: B. Ad valorem tax (C) is any tax charged as a percentage of value, not specifically at each stage. Surtax is an extra tax on high incomes, and PAYE is income tax deducted from wages. Memory tip: VAT = tax on the Value Added at every step.",
 "|The number of shareholders in a private limited liability company ranges from|two to seven|two to three|two to fifty|two to twenty": "Under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) 1990, which applied when this paper was set, a private limited company needed at least two members and could have no more than fifty (excluding employees and former employees). A public company had a minimum of seven and no maximum: C. Today CAMA 2020 allows a private company to be formed by just one person, with the maximum still fifty. The trap: A (two to seven) mixes up the private company with the public company's minimum of seven. Memory tip: Private: 2 to 50.",
 "|A major reason for having a national development plan is to|ensure efficient use of available resources|involve people in national programmes|make government spend more on production|ensure that more citizens are involved in planning": "Resources (land, labour, capital) are scarce, while needs are many. A national development plan sets out the government's priorities and targets for a period, such as five years, so that scarce resources are directed to the most important uses and duplication and waste are avoided: A. Involving people (B, D) can be a feature of good planning, but the main reason for planning is efficient use of resources. Nigeria had development plans from 1946 onwards, including four national plans from 1962 to 1985. Memory tip: Plan = Put resources where they matter most.",
 "|The difference between payments and receipts for visible trade is called|bilateral trade|current balance|balance of payment|balance of trade": "Visible trade is trade in physical goods, such as crude oil and cocoa exported, or machinery imported. The difference between the value of visible exports (receipts) and visible imports (payments) is the balance of trade: D. The current account balance (B) is broader, adding invisible items like services and transfers, and the balance of payments (C) covers all transactions, including capital flows. The trap: B (current balance) is closely related but includes invisibles, so it is not the right name. Memory tip: Balance of Trade = goods only.",
 "|One of the causes of the present high rate of inflation in Nigeria is|increasing budget surplus|increasing factors costs|exchange rate appreciation|high capacity utilization": "Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level. In Nigeria in the early 2000s a major cause was cost-push: the rising cost of factors of production, such as fuel, electricity (and generators), imported raw materials after the naira's fall, wages and transport. Firms passed these costs on as higher prices: B. A budget surplus (A) takes money out of circulation, exchange-rate appreciation (C) makes imports cheaper, and high capacity utilization (D) means more output; all of these would reduce inflation. Memory tip: Costs go up, prices go up: cost-push.",
 "|The final stage in the production process of any commodity involves its movement from the|retailer to the wholesaler|producer to the wholesaler|retailer to the consumer|wholesaler to the retailer": "In economics, production is not complete until goods reach the final consumer who needs them, because satisfying wants is the purpose of production. The usual chain is producer to wholesaler to retailer to consumer, so the last stage is the movement from the retailer to the consumer: C. Option A reverses the direction, and B and D are earlier links in the chain. Memory tip: The Retailer hands over the baton to the consumer at the finish line.",
 "|A change in demand for a normal good means|a shift in the demand curve|a change in the price elasticity|the demand changes as price changes|a movement along a given demand curve": "Economists distinguish a change in demand from a change in quantity demanded. A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the same demand curve (a change in quantity demanded). A change in demand is caused by other factors, such as income, tastes, prices of related goods or population, and it shifts the whole demand curve to the right (increase) or left (decrease): A. For a normal good, for example, a rise in income shifts demand to the right. Options C and D describe a change in quantity demanded. Memory tip: Price moves you Along; anything else Shifts the curve.",
 "|In the diagram above, ST is the budget line while I, II and III represent indifference curves. A rational consumer will choose to consume at point|M|T|K|L": "A rational consumer wants to reach the highest indifference curve possible, but must stay on or inside the budget line ST. The best point is where the budget line just touches (is tangent to) the highest attainable indifference curve; there the slope of the curve equals the price ratio. In the diagram this is point L on curve II: D. Point M is on the higher curve III but lies outside the budget line, so it cannot be afforded, and K is affordable but lies on the lower curve I, giving less satisfaction. Memory tip: Equilibrium is where the budget Line Lightly touches the curve.",
 "|In order to increase revenue, the seller of a commodity whose demand is fairly elastic is advised to|increase price|reduce output|reduce price|retain price": "When demand is elastic (elasticity greater than 1), the percentage change in quantity demanded is larger than the percentage change in price. So if the seller reduces the price, sales rise by a bigger proportion and total revenue (price × quantity) increases. For example, cutting price by 10% might raise sales by 20%. Raising the price would lose more in sales than it gains in price: C. If demand were inelastic, the opposite advice (raise price) would apply. Memory tip: Elastic: cut price, revenue rises; Inelastic: raise price, revenue rises.",
 "|In any set of data, the median represents the|value that is most representative of the data|mid-position when the values are arranged in ascending or descending order|mid-position when the values are arranged randomly|difference between the highest and the lowest values when arranged in ascending order": "The median is the middle value of a set of data after the values have been arranged in order, either ascending or descending. For 3, 5, 8, 10, 12 the median is 8. With an even number of values, it is the average of the two middle ones: B. The data must be ordered first, so C is wrong. The difference between the highest and lowest values (D) is the range, and A loosely describes an average in general. Memory tip: Median = Middle, but only after you Line them up.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nCombination ... Beans (Unit) ... Rice (Unit)\nK ... 15 ... 19\nL ... 20 ... 17\nM ... 24 ... 16\n\nThe table above shows the combinations of beans and rice that can be purchased by a consumer. The opportunity cost of moving from K to L is|2 units of rice|5 units of beans|17 units of rice|20 units of beans": "Opportunity cost is what you give up to get something else. Read the table carefully.\nStep 1: At K the consumer has 15 beans and 19 rice; at L, 20 beans and 17 rice.\nStep 2: Moving from K to L, beans rise by 20 − 15 = 5 units.\nStep 3: Rice falls by 19 − 17 = 2 units.\nThe extra 5 beans cost the consumer 2 units of rice, so the opportunity cost is 2 units of rice: A. The trap: B (5 units of beans) is what is gained, not what is given up. Memory tip: Opportunity cost is always the thing you Lose.",
 "|One of the assumptions of ordinalist utility in consumer behaviour is that|consumers are irrational|marginal utility rises continuously|utility is measurable|utility can only be ranked": "There are two approaches to utility. The cardinalist (marginal utility) approach assumes utility can be measured in units called utils. The ordinalist approach, used with indifference curves, assumes only that consumers can rank combinations of goods in order of preference (first, second, third) without measuring how much satisfaction each gives: D. It also assumes consumers are rational, so A is wrong, and C is the cardinalist assumption. The trap: C (utility is measurable) catches students who mix up the cardinalist and ordinalist assumptions. Memory tip: Ordinal = Order (rank); Cardinal = Count (measure).",
 "|A large standard deviation is an indication of|the size of the sample|a wide spread around the mean|a wide difference between the mean and the mode|the difference between the highest and the lowest values": "The standard deviation measures dispersion: how far, on average, the values in a set lie from their mean. A small standard deviation means the values are bunched closely around the mean; a large one means they are widely spread out around it: B. The difference between highest and lowest values (D) is the range, a cruder measure of spread, and standard deviation says nothing direct about sample size or the mode. Memory tip: Standard deviation = Spread around the mean.",
 "|A major characteristic of a mixed economy is that|economic decisions are made by both the rich and the poor|both primary and secondary production are undertaken|the federal and the state governments undertake joint projects|economic activities are undertaken by both the public and private sectors": "Economic systems are classified by who owns resources and makes economic decisions. In capitalism, private individuals and firms dominate; in socialism, the state does. A mixed economy combines both: the private sector runs most businesses for profit while government owns and runs some key enterprises and regulates the economy. So economic activities are undertaken by both the public and private sectors: D. Nigeria is an example of a mixed economy. Memory tip: Mixed = Public + Private.",
 "|Given the cost function C = 160 + 36Q, what is the average cost at 20 units of output?|₦720|₦216.00|₦44.00|₦880.00": "In the cost function C = 160 + 36Q, ₦160 is fixed cost and 36Q is variable cost.\nStep 1: At Q = 20, variable cost = 36 × 20 = ₦720.\nStep 2: Total cost = 160 + 720 = ₦880.\nStep 3: Average cost = total cost ÷ output = 880 ÷ 20 = ₦44.00.\nSo the answer is ₦44.00: C. The trap: D (₦880.00) is total cost, which students give when they forget to divide by output, and A (₦720) is only the variable cost. Memory tip: Average = Total ÷ Quantity.",
 "|If an increase in the price of good X leads to an increase in the supply of good Y, X and Y are said to be|jointly supplied|competitive|composite|jointly demanded": "Joint supply happens when producing one good automatically produces another, such as beef and hides from cattle, or petrol and kerosene from crude oil. If the price of X (beef) rises, more cattle are slaughtered to supply more beef, and so more hides (Y) are supplied too: A. With competitive supply (B), the goods compete for the same resources, so a higher price of X would reduce the supply of Y. Jointly demanded goods (D) are complements, like cars and petrol. The trap: B (competitive) is the closely related term where the effect goes the other way. Memory tip: Joint supply = by-product: more of one, more of the other.",
 "|The diagram above shows the shifts in both demand and supply curves. What is the new equilibrium point after the shifts?|E₃|E₁|E₄|E₂": "Equilibrium is where a demand curve crosses a supply curve. In the diagram the original curves are D₁ and S₁, meeting at E₄. Demand has increased to D₂ and supply has increased to S₂. The new equilibrium must be on both new curves, so it is where D₂ crosses S₂, labelled E₂: D. E₃ is D₂ with the old S₁, and E₁ is D₁ with the new S₂; each shows only one shift. The trap: E₃ (A) and E₁ (B) are points after just one of the two shifts. Memory tip: Both curves new? Use both new curves.",
 "|Use the diagram below to answer the question. S₁ and S₂ are supply curves; M and T lie on S₁ and N lies on S₂.\n\nWhen there is a change from T to N, it implies that|supply has increased|quantity supplied has increased|quantity demanded has increased|price has fallen": "In the diagram, point T lies on supply curve S₁ and point N lies on S₂, which is to the right of S₁. Moving from T to N means moving from one supply curve to a new one further right: at each price, more is now offered for sale. This is an increase in supply, caused by something other than the good's own price, such as better technology or lower costs: A. A change in quantity supplied (B) is a movement along one curve. The trap: B (quantity supplied has increased) catches students who do not check whether the two points are on the same curve. Memory tip: New curve = change in supply; same curve = change in quantity supplied.",
 "|Use the diagram below to answer the question. S₁ and S₂ are supply curves; M and T lie on S₁ and N lies on S₂.\n\nA movement from M to T implies that there has been|a decrease in supply|an increase in demand|a decrease in price|an increase in price": "Points M and T both lie on the same supply curve, S₁, with T higher up and to the right of M. A movement along a supply curve is caused only by a change in the good's own price: as price rises, producers offer more, so quantity supplied increases. Moving up from M to T therefore shows an increase in price: D. A decrease in supply (A) would be a shift of the whole curve to the left. Memory tip: Up the supply curve = price Up, quantity supplied Up.",
 "|A necessary condition for specialization in an economy is the existence of|a regulated market|a competitive market|a medium of exchange|adequate capital": "Specialisation means each person or region concentrates on producing one thing or doing one job. A specialist produces far more of one good than he needs and must exchange it for everything else he wants. Barter makes this very hard (double coincidence of wants), so the existence of money as a medium of exchange is a necessary condition for specialisation and division of labour to work: C. Markets and capital help, but without a medium of exchange specialists could not easily trade. Memory tip: No Money, No specialisation: you would eat only what you make.",
 "|A firm operating at full capacity will experience rising short-run total costs when|prices of its variable inputs rise|prices of its variable inputs fall|there is a change in management|labour productivity increases": "A firm at full capacity cannot raise output further in the short run, so its total cost can only rise if what it pays for inputs rises. If the prices of variable inputs, such as raw materials, fuel or wages, go up, the same output costs more to produce, and short-run total cost rises: A. Falling input prices (B) or higher labour productivity (D) would lower costs, and a change in management does not by itself raise costs. Memory tip: Same output, dearer inputs, higher total cost.",
 "|In a textile factory, the cost of cotton used is a typical example of|an average cost|a variable cost|a total cost|a fixed cost": "Costs are fixed or variable depending on whether they change with output. Fixed costs (rent, insurance, depreciation of machines) stay the same whatever the output. Variable costs change with output. In a textile factory, cotton is the main raw material: producing more cloth needs more cotton, and producing nothing needs none. So the cost of cotton is a typical variable cost: B. Average and total cost (A and C) are measures that include both fixed and variable costs. Memory tip: Raw materials Vary with output: Variable cost.",
 "|A black market can occur when|supply is in excess of demand|consumption of the commodity is restricted|prices are set by government above the equilibrium|prices are set by government below the equilibrium": "A black market is illegal trading at prices above the official price. It arises when the government fixes a maximum (controlled) price below the equilibrium price. At that low price, quantity demanded is greater than quantity supplied, creating a shortage; some buyers are willing to pay more than the official price, and sellers secretly sell to them: D. A price set above equilibrium (C) creates a surplus, not a black market, and supply in excess of demand (A) means a glut. The trap: C (above the equilibrium) is the minimum-price case, which causes a surplus instead. Memory tip: Price ceiling below equilibrium, Black market above it.",
 "|A profit-maximizing monopolist should produce within the range where his demand is|inelastic|elastic|infinitely elastic|unitarily elastic": "A monopolist maximises profit where marginal revenue (MR) equals marginal cost (MC). Marginal cost is always positive, so MR must be positive at the best output. On a straight-line demand curve, MR is positive only on the upper, elastic part; at unit elasticity MR is zero, and on the inelastic part MR is negative. If the monopolist were producing where demand is inelastic, cutting output would raise total revenue and also save cost, so profit would rise. Therefore a profit-maximising monopolist always produces where demand is elastic: B. The trap: D (unitarily elastic) is where total revenue is at its maximum, but profit is not maximised there because costs are positive. Memory tip: a monopolist never stays in the inelastic zone, because there MR is below zero.",
 "|In the circular flow diagram above, the arrow marked K indicates|the flow of capital goods|payments to factors of production|payments for goods and services|the flow of economic rent": "The simple circular flow has two sectors, households and firms, and two pairs of flows that run in opposite directions. In the factor market, households supply labour (labour inputs) to firms, and firms pay wages back to households. In the product market, firms send goods and services to households, and households pay money to firms in return. In the diagram, the inner bottom loop carries goods and services from the firms to the households, and the outer bottom loop, K, runs the other way, from households to firms. So K is the money households spend on goods and services: C. Payments to factors (B) are the wages on the top loop. Memory tip: every real flow has a money flow going the opposite way.",
 "|The savings deposit in a commercial bank is called|capital|fund|near money|call money": "Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment. Cash and current-account (demand) deposits, which can be spent by cheque, are money in the narrow sense. Savings and time deposits cannot be used directly to pay for goods, but they can be turned into cash quickly and with little or no loss, so they are called near money, or quasi-money: C. Other examples are treasury bills and bills of exchange. Call money (D) is money that banks lend to discount houses and other institutions, repayable at very short notice; it is not a description of a customer's savings deposit. Memory tip: near money is nearly cash, just one easy step away from spending.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput (kg) ... 240 ... 450 ... 580 ... 630\nMR (₦) ... 75 ... 48 ... −40 ... −22\nMC (₦) ... 41 ... 48 ... 77 ... 20\n\nGiven the table above, what is the firm's short-run profit-maximizing output?|450kg|630kg|240kg|580kg": "A firm maximises profit where MR = MC, with MC cutting MR from below.\nStep 1: At 240kg, MR (₦75) is above MC (₦41), so producing more adds to profit.\nStep 2: At 450kg, MR = ₦48 and MC = ₦48: the last unit adds exactly as much to revenue as to cost, and MC has risen from ₦41 to ₦48.\nStep 3: At 580kg, MR is −₦40 and MC is ₦77, so those extra units lose money; at 630kg MR is still negative.\nSo profit is greatest at 450kg: A. Any output beyond the point where MR = MC adds more to cost than to revenue. Memory tip: produce until the extra revenue just equals the extra cost, MR = MC.",
 "|The regulatory authority of the capital market in Nigeria is the|Central Bank|Securities and Exchange Commission|Nigerian Deposit Insurance Corporation|Stock Exchange Market": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), set up in 1979, is the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market. It registers securities and market operators, supervises the exchanges, and protects investors from fraud: B. The Central Bank of Nigeria (A) regulates banks and the money market, and the Nigerian Deposit Insurance Corporation (C) insures bank deposits. The stock exchange (D) is a market place that the SEC regulates, not the regulator itself. Today the SEC operates under the Investments and Securities Act, and the old Nigerian Stock Exchange is now Nigerian Exchange (NGX). Memory tip: Shares and Exchanges are policed by the SEC.",
 "|In the long run, the equilibrium point of a monopolistic firm is a point where the|marginal cost curve is tangential to the average fixed cost curve|demand curve is tangential to the average variable cost curve|supply curve is tangential to the marginal cost curve|demand curve is tangential to the average cost curve": "Under monopolistic competition, many firms sell similar but differentiated products, and entry is free. In the short run a firm can earn supernormal profit, but this attracts new firms with close substitutes. Each firm's demand curve then shifts to the left until price equals average cost, so only normal profit remains. Graphically, the downward-sloping demand (AR) curve just touches, or is tangential to, the average cost curve: D. At that point the firm is producing to the left of the lowest point of AC, which is why monopolistic competition is said to have excess capacity. Average fixed cost and average variable cost curves (A, B) do not decide long-run equilibrium. Memory tip: long run in monopolistic competition, AR kisses AC.",
 "|Given that Y = C + I, where C = 50 + 0.75Y and I = ₦45m, what is the equilibrium level of income?|₦380m|₦20m|₦180m|₦200m": "In a simple two-sector economy, income is in equilibrium where Y = C + I.\nStep 1: Substitute: Y = 50 + 0.75Y + 45.\nStep 2: Collect terms: Y − 0.75Y = 95, so 0.25Y = 95.\nStep 3: Y = 95 ÷ 0.25 = ₦380m: A.\nCheck: C = 50 + 0.75 × 380 = 50 + 285 = 335; C + I = 335 + 45 = 380. The multiplier is 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 4, and (50 + 45) × 4 = 380 gives the same answer. Memory tip: equilibrium Y = autonomous spending × 1 ÷ (1 − MPC).",
 "|Above full employment level, an expansionary monetary policy will lead to a|fall in aggregate demand|decrease in aggregate supply|fall in the inflation rate|rise in the inflation rate": "Expansionary monetary policy (lower interest rates, more money supply) raises aggregate demand. Below full employment, idle labour and machines can be brought into use, so output rises. Above full employment, all resources are already in use, so output cannot rise much further. The extra spending then simply bids up prices: demand-pull inflation, a rise in the inflation rate: D. Aggregate demand rises, not falls (A), and the policy does not reduce aggregate supply (B). This is why central banks tighten money, not loosen it, when the economy is overheating. Memory tip: at full employment, more money chases the same goods, so prices rise.",
 "|A major assumption in a perfectly competitive market is that|the number of buyers and sellers is small|individuals cannot influence prices|the quality of products remains the same|prices will always remain constant": "Perfect competition rests on several assumptions: very many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous product, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge and perfect mobility of factors. Because each buyer or seller is tiny compared with the whole market, no individual can change the market price by buying or selling more or less; everyone is a price taker: B. The number of buyers and sellers is large, not small (A). Price is set by market demand and supply and can change when they change, so it does not always remain constant (D). Memory tip: in perfect competition the market sets the price and every firm simply takes it.",
 "|A discount house is a market where|various bills are exchanged|short-term and medium-term loans are obtained|shares are bought and sold|short-term loans are obtained": "A discount house is a money-market institution. It borrows short-term funds (call money) from commercial banks and uses them to buy, sell and discount short-term instruments such as treasury bills, treasury certificates and bills of exchange. In effect, it is a market where these bills change hands: A. Shares are bought and sold on the stock exchange, which is part of the capital market (C), and medium-term loans (B) come from banks and development finance institutions. Nigeria's first discount house began operating in 1993; discount houses were later converted into other types of financial institution. Memory tip: discount houses deal in Bills; the stock exchange deals in Shares.",
 "|Idle cash balances are held for|planned purchases|the purchase of bonds|the purchase of shares|unplanned purchases": "Keynes named three motives for holding money: transactions (everyday spending), precautionary (unforeseen needs) and speculative (waiting to buy bonds when their prices are favourable). Money held for transactions is active. Keynes called speculative money idle balances, held ready for the purchase of bonds: B. Many Nigerian textbooks, however, group precautionary money for unplanned purchases with speculative money as idle, because neither is spent in the normal course of business, so D is also accepted. Planned purchases (A) are the transactions motive, which uses active balances. Memory tip: Transactions = active; Speculation (and, in many texts, precaution) = idle.",
 "|In a particular year, the sum of the value added in each sector of the economy was ₦4,550 million. The amount represents the GNP measured by the|factor-price approach|output approach|income approach|expenditure approach": "National income can be measured in three ways. The output (product or value-added) approach adds up the value added by every sector, that is, the value of each firm's output minus the goods and services bought from other firms, to avoid double counting. The income approach adds up wages, rent, interest and profit, and the expenditure approach adds up C + I + G + (X − M). Since the question sums the value added in each sector, it is the output approach: B. The trap: C (income approach) also avoids double counting, but it sums factor incomes, not values added. Memory tip: Value Added = Output method.",
 "|Population density is described as the|average number of people per square kilometre|average number of people in a country|total population excluding migration|total population including net migration": "Population density measures how crowded a country or area is: the number of people living on each unit of land. It is calculated as total population ÷ land area, usually in persons per square kilometre: A. For example, a state of 6 million people on 30,000 km² has a density of 200 people per km². Areas such as Lagos and the South-East have high densities, while much of the North has lower densities. The average number of people in a country (B) is meaningless, and total population with or without migration (C, D) says nothing about land area. Memory tip: density = people ÷ land.",
 "|The short-run inelasticity of supply of agricultural produce causes|stability in prices|a steady rise in prices|a steady decline in prices|instability in prices": "Farm output depends on planting decisions made months earlier and on the weather, so in the short run farmers cannot increase or reduce supply quickly when prices change; supply is inelastic. With inelastic supply, any change in demand, or a good or poor harvest, causes large changes in price. A bumper harvest makes prices crash, and a poor one makes them soar. The result is instability in the prices and incomes of farmers: D. Governments use buffer stocks, guaranteed prices and storage schemes to reduce this instability. Memory tip: inelastic supply + shifting demand or harvest = big price swings.",
 "|The major contribution of the petroleum industry to the Nigerian economy is the|generation of revenue for the oil companies|provision of employment opportunities for communities|generation of revenue for government|promotion of industrial harmony": "Since the 1970s oil boom, petroleum has dominated Nigeria's economy. Through royalties, petroleum profits tax and the sale of the government's share of crude oil, it has provided most of the federal government's revenue and the bulk of Nigeria's foreign exchange earnings: C. The industry is capital-intensive and employs relatively few people (B), and the oil companies' own profits (A) are not its contribution to the national economy. Oil revenue is shared among the three tiers of government through the Federation Account. Today oil is still the largest single source of foreign exchange, though its share of government revenue has fallen. Memory tip: oil money is government money in Nigeria.",
 "|During a conflict between management and workers, the union's bargaining power is based on|work-to-rule|negotiation|picketing|management decision": "Collective bargaining is the process by which a trade union and the employers negotiate wages and conditions of service on behalf of all workers. When a conflict arises, the union's first and main tool is negotiation, backed by the strength of its membership and its ability to speak for all the workers: B. Work-to-rule (A), picketing (C), go-slows and strikes are forms of industrial action used to pressure management when negotiation breaks down. A management decision (D) is the employers' side, not the union's power. Memory tip: unions Negotiate first; they act (strike, picket, work-to-rule) only when talks fail.",
 "|Localisation of industries in Nigeria is mainly influenced by|market size|population groupings|raw materials|external economies": "Localisation of industry means the concentration of firms in one industry in a particular area. In Nigeria the most important influence on where industries settle is nearness to raw materials, especially bulky or perishable ones that are costly to transport: C. Examples are cement factories at Ewekoro, Obajana and Nkalagu (limestone), textile mills in Kaduna and Kano (cotton), and tin smelting on the Jos Plateau. Market size (A) and population (B) matter for some industries, such as bottling. External economies (D), such as a pool of skilled labour and ancillary firms, are benefits that come once an industry is already localised. Memory tip: in Nigeria, factories follow their raw materials.",
 "|If the death rate of a country is 52% and the growth rate is 12%, what is the birth rate of the country?|48%|64%|40%|52%": "The natural growth rate of a population is the birth rate minus the death rate (ignoring migration).\nStep 1: Growth rate = birth rate − death rate.\nStep 2: Rearrange: birth rate = growth rate + death rate.\nStep 3: Birth rate = 12% + 52% = 64%: B.\nThe trap: A (48%) comes from subtracting the growth rate from the death rate (52% − 12%), which would make the population shrink, not grow. Memory tip: Births − Deaths = Growth, so Births = Growth + Deaths.",
 "|Agricultural backward-linkage effect means the establishment of|subsidiary industries to increase the number of industries|main industries to increase output|industries to reduce imports|subsidiary industries to feed the main industry": "Linkages are the ways one industry stimulates others. A backward linkage arises when the growth of a main industry creates demand for its inputs, leading to new firms that supply it. For example, a large brewery or textile mill encourages the growth of farms and processing plants producing sorghum, maize or cotton to feed it. So an agricultural backward linkage means setting up subsidiary industries to supply the main industry: D. A forward linkage works the other way, when an industry's output becomes the input of new firms, such as cassava processed into starch for other factories. Reducing imports (C) is import substitution. Memory tip: Backward = Behind the factory, supplying its inputs.",
 "|The distinction between onshore and offshore operations in oil exploration lies in the|location of sites|output generated|size of production|techniques of production": "Oil exploration and production in Nigeria are divided by location. Onshore operations take place on dry land and in the swamps of the Niger Delta. Offshore operations take place at sea, in shallow or deep water, using platforms and floating production vessels: A. Output, size and techniques (B, C, D) may differ between them, but they are results of the location, not the basis of the distinction. Since the 1990s, Nigeria's deep-offshore fields such as Bonga and Agbami have become very important because they are less exposed to onshore problems like pipeline vandalism. Memory tip: on-shore = on the land; off-shore = off the land, at sea.",
 "|Long-run production is called|diminishing returns to scale|returns to scale|external economies of scale|economies of scale": "In the short run at least one factor, such as the size of the factory, is fixed, and adding more of a variable factor is governed by the law of diminishing returns. In the long run all factors can be changed. Studying how output responds when all inputs are increased in the same proportion is called returns to scale: B. Returns can be increasing (output rises by a larger proportion), constant, or decreasing. Economies of scale (D) refer to the fall in average cost as the scale grows, which is closely related but is a cost idea, not the name of long-run production. Memory tip: short run = returns to a factor; long run = returns to scale.",
 "|For a firm to break even in the long run, the marginal cost curve must cut the|average variable cost curve at its highest point|average cost curve at any two points|total cost curve at its lowest point|average cost curve at its lowest point": "The marginal cost curve always cuts the average cost curve at the lowest point of AC: when MC is below AC, AC falls, and when MC is above AC, AC rises. A firm breaks even, earning only normal profit, when price equals average cost. In the long run, competition pushes price down to the minimum of AC, which is exactly where MC cuts AC: D. MC cuts the average variable cost curve at its lowest point, not its highest (A), and it does not cut AC at two points (B). The total cost curve (C) has no lowest point that MC cuts. Memory tip: MC passes through the bottom of every average curve.",
 "|An increase in the quantity supplied of a commodity suggests|a leftward shift of the supply curve|a rightward shift of the supply curve|a movement along the supply curve|an increase in elasticity of supply": "A change in quantity supplied happens when the good's own price changes and all other things stay the same. The producer simply moves to another point on the same supply curve: a higher price leads to a movement up the curve and a larger quantity supplied: C. A change in supply, caused by costs, technology, weather or taxes, shifts the whole curve (A, B). The trap: B (a rightward shift) also means more is offered for sale, but that is an increase in supply, not in quantity supplied. Memory tip: quantity supplied changes = move along; supply changes = shift.",
 "|An example of a long-run cost of a firm is|fuel and maintenance cost|the planned size of plant and equipment|the existing size of plant and equipment|rent on buildings": "In the short run, a firm's plant and equipment are fixed, so costs such as rent on buildings (D) and the cost of the existing plant (C) are fixed costs, while fuel and maintenance (A) are short-run variable costs. In the long run, all factors are variable: the firm can choose a new size of factory and new machines. The cost of the planned size of plant and equipment is therefore a long-run cost: B. The long-run average cost curve is sometimes called the planning curve for this reason. The trap: C (the existing size of plant) is the fixed factor of the short run, not a long-run cost. Memory tip: long run = planning time, when even the plant can change.",
 "|In a car assembly plant, division of labour is demonstrated by|assigning each worker to a specific activity|assigning each worker a given number to produce|increasing the daily output|producing a specific model": "Division of labour means breaking a production process into many small tasks and giving each worker one task to repeat. In a car assembly plant, one worker fits engines, another fixes doors, another sprays paint and another tests the brakes: A. This raises skill and speed and allows the use of machines, but it can cause boredom and over-dependence. Giving each worker a quota (B) is a production target, raising daily output (C) is a result, and producing a specific model (D) is product specialisation by the firm, not division of labour among workers. Memory tip: division of labour = one worker, one task.",
 "|Under perfect competition, the short-run supply curve of a firm is determined by its|total cost curve|marginal cost curve|average fixed cost curve|average cost curve": "A firm in perfect competition is a price taker and maximises profit where price = marginal cost. At each possible price it produces the output where MC equals that price, so the quantity it supplies at each price is read off its marginal cost curve: B. The firm will not produce at all if price is below average variable cost, so its short-run supply curve is the part of the MC curve above the lowest point of AVC. The average cost curve (D) tells whether the firm makes profit or loss, not how much it supplies. Memory tip: under perfect competition, the MC curve above AVC is the supply curve.",
 "|The hoarding of goods is usually experienced when|supply is greater than demand|the market price is above the equilibrium price|demand is greater than supply|excess demand is greater than excess supply": "Hoarding means keeping goods off the market, usually to sell later at higher prices. It happens when goods are scarce, that is, when demand is greater than supply at the current price: C. Traders expect prices to keep rising, so they hold stocks back, which makes the shortage worse. Examples in Nigeria include fuel scarcity and hoarding of grains before the lean season. When supply exceeds demand (A) or price is above equilibrium (B), there is a surplus, and sellers want to sell, not hoard. Memory tip: shortage breeds hoarding; surplus breeds sales.",
 "|The technical relationship between input combination and maximum attainable output is called|a production function|an indifference curve|an isocost|an isoquant": "A production function is the technical (engineering) relationship between the quantities of inputs used and the maximum output that can be obtained from them, written Q = f(L, K, …): A. It tells the producer how much output any combination of labour and capital can yield with the existing technology. An isoquant (D) is one curve drawn from the production function, joining combinations that give the same output. An isocost (C) shows combinations that cost the same, and an indifference curve (B) is about consumer satisfaction. The trap: D (isoquant) is closely related, but it shows only one level of output, not the whole relationship. Memory tip: function = the whole input-to-output rule.",
 "|If the marginal utility of the last unit of commodity X at ₦2 is 16 and that of commodity Y at ₦4 is 24, the consumer will be at equilibrium when|the price-quantity ratios are equal|equal amounts of X and Y are consumed|less of Y and more of X are consumed|less of X and more of Y are consumed": "A consumer with two goods is in equilibrium when the last naira spent on each gives the same marginal utility: MUx/Px = MUy/Py.\nStep 1: MU per naira of X = 16 ÷ ₦2 = 8.\nStep 2: MU per naira of Y = 24 ÷ ₦4 = 6.\nStep 3: X gives more satisfaction per naira (8 > 6), so the consumer should buy more X, whose MU then falls, and less Y, whose MU then rises, until the ratios are equal: C.\nThe trap: D (less X, more Y) comes from comparing the raw utilities, 24 against 16, without dividing by price. Memory tip: always compare MU per naira, not MU alone.",
 "|A buyer who haggles in the market is applying the principle of|choice|price mechanism|opportunity cost|utility maximisation": "Haggling is bargaining over price, common in Nigerian open markets. A rational buyer has limited money and wants to get the greatest possible satisfaction from it. By pushing the price down, he can buy the item for less and have money left for other things, so he gets more total satisfaction from his income. This is the principle of utility maximisation: D. Haggling does take place in the market where prices are formed, but the question asks what principle the buyer is applying, which is to get the most satisfaction for his money. Memory tip: every naira saved by haggling buys extra satisfaction elsewhere.",
 "|An inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded implies that|the two variables change in opposite directions|the two variables change in the same direction|only one variable changes|the two variables remain unchanged": "Two variables have an inverse (negative) relationship when one rises as the other falls. The law of demand is the best-known example: when price rises, quantity demanded falls, and when price falls, quantity demanded rises, so the demand curve slopes downwards from left to right: A. A direct (positive) relationship, where both move in the same direction (B), is found between price and quantity supplied. Memory tip: inverse = opposite; direct = same direction.",
 "|When the marginal utility of a commodity is zero, the total utility is|at its minimum|upward-sloping|downward-sloping|at its maximum": "Total utility (TU) is the total satisfaction from all units consumed; marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. As consumption rises, MU falls (diminishing marginal utility). While MU is positive, each extra unit adds to TU, so TU rises. When MU reaches zero, the extra unit adds nothing, so TU stops rising and is at its peak. If consumption continues, MU becomes negative and TU falls. So when MU is zero, total utility is at its maximum: D. The trap: A (minimum) catches students who think zero marginal utility means zero satisfaction overall. Memory tip: MU = 0 at the top of the TU hill.",
 "|Economic freedom is a basic feature of|a market economy|an industrialised economy|a planned economy|a developing economy": "In a market (capitalist, free-enterprise) economy, the means of production are privately owned and decisions are made by individuals through the price mechanism. People are free to choose their occupation, to start and run businesses, to own property and to spend their income as they wish. This economic freedom, with consumer sovereignty and the profit motive, is its basic feature: A. In a planned economy (C), the state decides what to produce, so individual freedom is limited. Being industrialised or developing (B, D) describes the level of development, not the economic system. Memory tip: Market = freedom; Planned = state command.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (kg)\n40 ... 6\n48 ... 5\n55 ... 4\n60 ... 3\n70 ... 2\n\nWhat is the median quantity?|5kg|6kg|3kg|4kg": "The median is the middle value of a set of data arranged in order.\nStep 1: The quantities are 6, 5, 4, 3, 2. In ascending order: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\nStep 2: There are 5 values, so the median is the (5 + 1) ÷ 2 = 3rd value.\nStep 3: The 3rd value is 4kg: D.\nThe median is not affected by the prices beside them; only the quantity column is used. Memory tip: arrange first, then pick the middle.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (kg)\n40 ... 6\n48 ... 5\n55 ... 4\n60 ... 3\n70 ... 2\n\nFind the mean price of the distribution.|₦54|₦51|₦56|₦59": "The mean price of the distribution weights each price by the quantity bought at that price.\nStep 1: Multiply each price by its quantity: 40 × 6 = 240; 48 × 5 = 240; 55 × 4 = 220; 60 × 3 = 180; 70 × 2 = 140.\nStep 2: Σ(price × quantity) = 240 + 240 + 220 + 180 + 140 = 1,020.\nStep 3: Σquantity = 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 20.\nStep 4: Mean price = 1,020 ÷ 20 = ₦51: B.\nThe trap: A (₦54) is close to the simple average of the five prices, 273 ÷ 5 = ₦54.60, which ignores the quantities. Memory tip: for a distribution, mean = Σfx ÷ Σf.",
 "|If elasticity of demand is greater than 1 and less than infinity, demand is said to be|elastic|perfectly inelastic|inelastic|perfectly elastic": "Price elasticity of demand compares the percentage change in quantity demanded with the percentage change in price. When it is greater than 1, quantity changes by a larger percentage than price, and demand is elastic: A. When it equals infinity, buyers will buy any amount at one price but nothing above it: perfectly elastic (D), a horizontal demand curve. Between 0 and 1 demand is inelastic (C), and at exactly 0 it is perfectly inelastic (B). The trap: D (perfectly elastic) is the limit the question excludes by saying less than infinity. Memory tip: 0 perfectly inelastic, below 1 inelastic, 1 unitary, above 1 elastic, infinity perfectly elastic.",
 "|In economics, production takes place only when|producers sell to retailers|value has been added to a commodity|modern equipment is used|consumption has taken place": "In economics, production is the creation of utility, or the adding of value, to satisfy human wants; it is not only making things in a factory. Utility is added by changing a good's form, moving it to where it is needed, storing it until it is needed, and the services of traders, transporters and bankers. Production is therefore said to be complete only when the goods reach the final consumer, who consumes them: D. Because production is defined as creating utility, value has been added to a commodity (B) is also accepted. Selling to retailers (A) is only a stage, and modern equipment (C) is not needed. Memory tip: production ends at the consumer.",
 "|The monetization policy of the Nigerian government is aimed at|encouraging public servants to retire early and become self-employed|transferring government properties to retired public servants|reducing government's burden on the provision of fringe benefits to public servants|helping government recover properties held by public servants": "In 2003 the Federal Government introduced the monetisation of fringe benefits for public servants. Instead of providing official houses, cars, drivers, furniture, utilities and domestic staff, government pays officers cash allowances and lets them make their own arrangements. The aim was to cut the huge cost, waste and abuse involved in providing these benefits in kind, so it reduced government's burden: C. As part of the policy, many government houses in Abuja were later sold, often to the officers living in them, but that was a means, not the aim (B). Memory tip: monetise = turn benefits in kind into money.",
 "|The price index computed between two time periods is given as 128%. This implies that the|disposable incomes have increased by 28%|living standards have fallen by 128%|living standards have risen by 128%|living standards have fallen by 28%": "A price index compares prices in a current period with a base period, which is set at 100. An index of 128 means the general price level is 28% higher than in the base period. If money incomes do not change, the same income buys less, so living standards fall, which is the intended answer: D. Strictly, purchasing power falls to 100 ÷ 128 = 78.1% of its former level, a fall of about 22%, not 28%, and if incomes have also risen, living standards may not have fallen at all, so the option is not fully correct. Options B and C wrongly read 128 itself as the change. Memory tip: index − 100 = percentage change in prices.",
 "|The problem of planning in Nigeria is mainly in the|implementation of the plans|appraisal of the plans|allocation of scarce resources|articulation of the plans": "Nigeria has had several development plans: the First National Development Plan (1962–68), Second (1970–74), Third (1975–80) and Fourth (1981–85), then rolling plans and visions. The plans were generally well articulated on paper, but they failed mainly at the implementation stage: A. Causes included corruption, political instability, lack of reliable data, shortage of skilled manpower, poor monitoring, and over-dependence on oil revenue and foreign aid. Appraisal (B) and articulation (D) come before or after, and allocating scarce resources (C) is what planning itself tries to do. Memory tip: Nigerian plans were good on paper but weak in practice.",
 "|The raising of funds by selling stocks to the public is called|equity financing|deficit financing|loan financing|debt financing": "A company can raise long-term funds in two main ways. Equity financing is the sale of shares (stocks) to the public; the buyers become part-owners of the company and receive dividends, and the money need not be repaid: A. Debt financing (D) and loan financing (C) mean borrowing, such as bank loans or debentures, which must be repaid with interest. Deficit financing (B) is a government spending more than its revenue and borrowing to cover the gap. The trap: D (debt financing) is the other main method, often confused with equity, but buying stock gives ownership, not a loan. Memory tip: Equity = ownership; Debt = must be repaid.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nCountry ... Groundnuts (units) ... Cocoa (units)\nNigeria ... 8000 ... 1,000\nGhana ... 1000 ... 8000\nTotal production ... 9000 ... 9000\n\nApplying the law of comparative costs, how many units of groundnuts will Nigeria produce?|7000 units|16000 units|9000 units|8000 units": "Comparative cost: Nigeria is relatively better at groundnuts (8000 against Ghana's 1000), and Ghana at cocoa (8000 against Nigeria's 1000). In this kind of table, each country's figures are taken as the output of half its resources.\nStep 1: Nigeria devotes half its resources to groundnuts and gets 8000 units.\nStep 2: When it specialises, it moves the other half into groundnuts as well, giving 8000 + 8000 = 16000 units: B.\nGhana likewise produces 16000 units of cocoa. The trap: C (9000 units) is the world's total groundnut output before specialisation. Memory tip: specialise in what you do relatively best.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nCountry ... Groundnuts (units) ... Cocoa (units)\nNigeria ... 8000 ... 1,000\nGhana ... 1000 ... 8000\nTotal production ... 9000 ... 9000\n\nThe difference in each country's total output on specialization is|16000 units|9000 units|8000 units|7000 units": "Step 1: Before specialisation, each country produces 8000 units of one good and 1000 units of the other, so each country's total output is 9000 units.\nStep 2: After specialisation (with the same resources, as in the previous question), Nigeria produces 16000 units of groundnuts and Ghana 16000 units of cocoa.\nStep 3: The difference in each country's total output = 16000 − 9000 = 7000 units: D.\nThe world as a whole gains 7000 groundnuts and 7000 cocoa. The trap: A (16000 units) is the new output itself, not the difference. Memory tip: gain from trade = output after specialising − output before.",
 "|The loans from the World Bank to developing countries are mainly to support|capital-intensive technology|the establishment of universities|their infrastructural investment|their level of consumption": "The World Bank (the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development, set up in 1944, with the International Development Association) lends to developing countries for long-term development projects. Most of its lending has gone into infrastructure such as roads, power, water supply, irrigation and telecommunications, which are needed for growth but too costly for poor countries to finance alone: C. It does not lend to finance day-to-day consumption (D), and support for universities (B) is only a small part of its education lending. Today it also funds health, education and social programmes. Memory tip: World Bank = long-term development projects; IMF = short-term balance of payments support.",
 "|Economic growth is different from economic development because economic growth|describes expansion and change|is measurable but not objective|describes expansion and not change|is not measurable but objective": "Economic growth is a sustained rise in a country's real output, usually measured by real GDP or GDP per head. It is a quantitative idea: an expansion of output, without any necessary change in the structure of the economy or in people's welfare. Economic development is wider: it combines growth with change, such as less poverty and inequality, better health and education, and a shift from agriculture to industry and services. So growth describes expansion and not change: C. Growth is easier to measure than development, but both are studied objectively, which rules out B and D. Memory tip: growth is MORE output; development is BETTER lives.",
 "|A long-term capital outflow is recorded in the balance of payments as a|credit on the capital account|credit on the current account|debit on the current account|debit on the capital account": "In the balance of payments, transactions that bring money into the country are credits (+) and those that send money out are debits (−). The current account records trade in goods and services, income and transfers; the capital (and financial) account records investment and lending. A long-term capital outflow, such as a Nigerian firm investing in a factory abroad or lending to a foreign government, is a capital transaction and sends money out, so it is a debit on the capital account: D. The trap: A (credit on the capital account) is right for a capital inflow, such as foreign direct investment into Nigeria. Memory tip: money out = debit; money in = credit.",
 "|Business organizations in which the owner's personal property may be forfeited in the event of bankruptcy are|partnerships and limited liability companies|partnerships and sole proprietorships|cooperatives and Limited Liability Companies|Corporations and sole proprietorships": "Liability is the extent to which owners must pay a business's debts. Sole proprietors and partners in an ordinary partnership have unlimited liability: if the business fails, creditors can take their personal property, such as houses and cars, to recover debts: B. Shareholders of limited liability companies and corporations have limited liability, so they can lose only the money they invested; options A, C and D each include such a body. Cooperatives in Nigeria are registered with limited liability. Memory tip: one owner or partners = unlimited liability; company = limited liability.",
 "|The factor responsible for the current inflationary pressures in Nigeria is the|exchange rate depreciation|increase in exports|low per capita income|budget surplus": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. In the early 2000s, Nigeria imported much of its food, fuel, machinery and raw materials, so the steady depreciation of the naira raised the naira cost of imports and pushed up prices at home: cost-push or imported inflation: A. Rising exports (B) bring in foreign exchange and support the naira; low income per head (C) is a feature of poverty, not a cause of rising prices; and a budget surplus (D) reduces spending, which lowers inflation. The paper refers to 2005, but naira depreciation, especially after the 2023 move to a unified exchange rate, remains a major driver of inflation today. Memory tip: weak naira, dearer imports, higher prices.",
 "|If government increases its expenditure on public works, the desired effect is|a reduction in the use of capital equipment|an increase in the level of employment|an increase in the level of inflation|an increase in the use of capital equipment": "Government spending on public works, such as roads, bridges, schools and housing, is a tool of expansionary fiscal policy. It directly employs workers and contractors, and their spending of the extra income raises demand for other goods, creating still more jobs through the multiplier effect. The main desired effect is therefore an increase in employment: B. Inflation (C) is a possible side effect if the economy is near full employment, not the aim. The use of capital equipment (A, D) may change, but that is not the purpose of the policy. Keynes recommended public works to fight unemployment in a depression. Memory tip: public works put people to work.",
 "|An institution that regulates trade relations among members is the|ADB|OPEC|EU|IMF": "The European Union, which grew out of the European Economic Community set up in 1957, is a customs union and single market. It removes tariffs and quotas on trade among members, applies a common external tariff to outsiders, and negotiates trade agreements for all its members, so it regulates trade relations among them: C. OPEC (B) coordinates its members' oil production and pricing policies through output quotas, but it does not govern trade between the member countries. The IMF (D) deals with exchange rates and balance of payments problems, and the ADB (A) lends for development in Africa. Some readers argue for OPEC because its quotas shape members' oil exports, so the item is debatable, but the EU fits the question better. Memory tip: EU = trade union of nations; OPEC = oil cartel.",
 "|The oil-producing areas in Nigeria are agitating for special compensation owing mainly to|the high level of poverty|constant flooding|environmental pollution|the high level of unemployment": "Crude oil has been produced in the Niger Delta since 1958. Oil spills from pipelines, gas flaring and waste dumping have poisoned rivers, creeks and farmland, killing fish and crops, the main livelihoods of the people. Communities such as Ogoni have therefore demanded special compensation, more revenue (the derivation principle) and clean-up of their land. Poverty and unemployment (A, D) are real in the region, but they are largely consequences of the environmental damage; the main ground for compensation is environmental pollution: C. Bodies such as the Niger Delta Development Commission (2000) and HYPREP for the Ogoni clean-up were set up in response. Memory tip: the oil came out, the pollution stayed in, so compensation is for the damage.",
 "|Minimum wage legislation will result in|reduced income|reduced labour productivity|greater demand for labour|greater supply of labour": "A minimum wage is a legal floor below which employers may not pay workers. To have any effect it is fixed above the free-market wage. At that higher wage more people are willing to offer their labour (the quantity of labour supplied rises), while employers, facing higher costs, hire fewer workers (the quantity demanded falls). The gap is unemployment. So the law brings forth a greater supply of labour: D. It does not raise the demand for labour (C), which falls, and it raises rather than reduces the income of those who keep their jobs (A). The trap: C sounds right because workers gain, but a higher wage makes employers demand less labour, not more. Memory tip: price floor on labour = more sellers of labour, fewer buyers.",
 "|If children and the aged dominate the population of a country, this results in|an increase in the national income|a reduction in the importation of capital goods|an improvement in the standard of living|a fall in the standard of living": "A population dominated by children (under 15) and the aged (over 64) has a high dependency ratio: many dependants who consume but produce little, supported by a small working population. Income per head falls, and government must spend heavily on schools, health care and pensions instead of productive investment. The result is a fall in the standard of living: D. National income does not rise (A), because fewer people are working, and living standards do not improve (C). Option B has no direct link with the age structure of the population. Nigeria, with about 43% of its people under 15, faces this burden. Memory tip: more mouths, fewer hands = lower living standards.",
 "|The term 'oil shock' is used to describe a situation in which|oil prices have fallen drastically|the demand for oil is equal to its supply|the demand for oil exceeds its supply|the supply of oil exceeds its demand": "An oil shock is a sudden, drastic change in the price of crude oil that disturbs economies. For oil-importing countries the famous shocks were the price jumps of 1973 and 1979. For an oil-exporting country like Nigeria, whose government revenue and foreign exchange depend on oil, the shock that hit hardest was the collapse of oil prices in the early 1980s (the oil glut), which led to austerity and SAP in 1986; prices crashed again in 2014–2016 and 2020. Here the answer is that oil prices have fallen drastically: A. The term can also describe a sudden price rise, so the item is debatable, but none of the other options describes a shock at all. Memory tip: shock = sudden swing in oil price; for Nigeria, think price crash.",
 "|A major factor militating against rapid industrialization in Nigeria is|poor market facilities|poor transport facilities|epileptic power supply|inadequate skilled manpower": "Industries in Nigeria face many obstacles: inadequate capital, shortage of skilled manpower, poor roads and small markets. The one most often cited is epileptic (irregular, unreliable) power supply. Without steady electricity, factories must buy diesel or gas for their own generators, which sharply raises costs; many firms have closed or moved to neighbouring countries for this reason. So the major factor is C. Poor transport (B) and inadequate skilled manpower (D) are real problems but come after power in most surveys. Even after the privatisation of PHCN's companies in 2013 into GenCos and DisCos, power supply remains a key constraint today. Memory tip: no light, no factory.",
 "|The marginal productivity theory applies in a|unionised labour market|monopolistic market only|monopolistic super-market only|perfectly competitive market": "The marginal productivity theory of distribution says that a factor of production is paid the value of its marginal product: a firm hires workers until the wage equals the value of the extra output the last worker adds. The theory rests on the assumptions of perfect competition: many firms and workers, homogeneous labour, perfect knowledge, perfect mobility, and a given market price for the product. So it applies in a perfectly competitive market: D. In unionised markets (A) wages are set by bargaining, and under monopoly (B, C) the firm does not take price as given, so the simple theory does not hold. Memory tip: MP theory needs Perfect competition.",
 "|The major problem of conducting a census in Nigeria is|lack of political will|inadequate skilled personnel|urban congestion|financial constraint": "A census is a complete count of the people of a country at a given time. Nigeria's censuses (1962, 1963, 1973, 1991, 2006) have been controversial because population figures decide the sharing of federal revenue, the number of seats in the National Assembly and the creation of states and local governments. Groups therefore inflate their numbers, and governments have often been unwilling to conduct and accept an honest count; the 1962 and 1973 results were cancelled. The major problem is lack of political will: A. Shortage of skilled personnel (B) and funds (D) are also listed as problems. Memory tip: in Nigeria, headcount = headship, so politics spoils the count.",
 "|A major advantage of industrialisation is that it|curbs inflation|leads to growth and development|leads to self-reliance|improves the terms of trade": "Industrialisation is the setting up and expansion of manufacturing industries. It raises national output, creates jobs, adds value to raw materials before export, encourages new skills and technology, and widens the tax base. Together these bring economic growth (more output) and development (better living standards): B. Self-reliance (C) is one possible benefit, but it is part of the wider growth and development that industry brings. Industrialisation does not by itself curb inflation (A) or guarantee better terms of trade (D). Memory tip: factories turn raw materials into growth.",
 "|Mobility of labour is higher when there|are alternative jobs in the same location|is a monopoly of jobs in one location|is intense competition for jobs in the same location|are no alternative jobs in the same location": "Mobility of labour is the ease with which workers move from one job (occupational mobility) or place (geographical mobility) to another. Moving to another town is costly: transport, accommodation, leaving family and adjusting to a new culture. Workers therefore change jobs most easily when other jobs are available where they already live. So mobility is higher when there are alternative jobs in the same location: A. A monopoly of jobs (B) or no alternatives (D) tie workers down, and intense competition for jobs (C) makes it hard to find a new one. Memory tip: jobs next door make moving easy.",
 "|The organization whose aim is to solve the trade problems of less developed nations is|UNECA|UNCTAD|WTO|OECD": "UNCTAD, the United Nations Conference on Trade and Development, was set up in 1964 in Geneva to help developing countries trade on fairer terms. It pushed for better prices for primary products, preferential access for their manufactures to rich markets, and more aid: B. UNECA (A) is the UN Economic Commission for Africa in Addis Ababa, which deals with Africa's general economic development. The WTO (C), which replaced GATT in 1995, regulates world trade for all members. The OECD (D) is a club of rich industrial countries. The trap: C, the WTO, also deals with trade, but its role is rules for all countries, not the problems of poor ones. Memory tip: UNCTAD = UN Cares about Trade And Development.",
 "|Nigeria's exports usually comprise|consumer goods|primary goods|capital goods|intermediate goods": "Primary goods are products of the extractive industries: farming, fishing, forestry and mining. Nigeria's exports have always been mainly primary: cocoa, groundnuts, palm produce and cotton before the 1970s, and crude oil and gas since then, which make up about 80–90% of export earnings. So Nigeria's exports usually comprise primary goods: B. Nigeria imports most of its capital goods (C) such as machinery and many consumer goods (A). This heavy dependence on a single primary product exposes the economy to swings in world oil prices. Memory tip: Nigeria sells what comes from the ground and buys what comes from the factory.",
 "|An association formed by a group of individuals solely for the marketing of their product is a|voluntary organization|consumer cooperative society|credit and thrift society|producers cooperative society": "A cooperative society is a voluntary association of people who pool resources for their common benefit, run on the principle of one member, one vote. A producers' cooperative is formed by producers, often farmers, to process and market their output together, so they get better prices and avoid exploitation by middlemen: D. A consumer cooperative (B) is formed by buyers to purchase goods in bulk and sell to members cheaply. A credit and thrift society (C) collects members' savings and gives them loans. \"Voluntary organization\" (A) is too general. Examples: cocoa farmers' marketing cooperatives in Ondo State. Memory tip: producers sell together; consumers buy together; thrift saves together.",
 "|Economic growth can be accelerated through|increased current investment|excess consumption over investment|excess current consumption|increased current consumption": "Economic growth is a sustained rise in a country's real output (real GDP). Output grows when the economy's capacity grows, and capacity grows through investment: spending on machines, factories, roads, power stations and education. Money spent on current consumption is used up and does not add to future productive capacity. So growth is accelerated through increased current investment: A. Options B, C and D all favour consumption over investment, which slows the build-up of capital. To invest more, a country must save more, which is why savings are encouraged. Memory tip: consume today, enjoy today; invest today, grow tomorrow.",
 "|A characteristic common to partnership and sole proprietorship is|unlimited liability|limited liability|accessibility to loans|transferability of shares": "A sole proprietorship is owned by one person; an ordinary partnership by 2 to 20 people. In both, the business has no separate legal existence from its owners, so if the business cannot pay its debts, creditors can take the owners' personal property. This is unlimited liability: A. Limited liability (B) and transferable shares (D) belong to joint stock companies. Both small forms of business actually find it hard to get loans (C). A limited partner in a limited partnership is the only exception, and at least one partner must still have unlimited liability. Memory tip: small business, unlimited risk.",
 "|Import substitution policy is used to|check government expenditure|correct the balance of payments problem|protect local industries|encourage increased volume of trade": "Import substitution industrialisation means producing locally the goods a country used to import, such as textiles, beverages, cement and assembled vehicles. Its main purpose is to reduce the import bill and save scarce foreign exchange, so as to correct a balance of payments problem: B. To make the new local industries survive, governments protect them with tariffs and import bans, so protection of local industries (C) is the means used to make import substitution work rather than the purpose of the policy. Nigeria followed this strategy in its first and second national development plans (1962–1974). The trap: C is closely tied to import substitution, but it is the tool, while saving foreign exchange is the aim. Memory tip: substitute imports to save forex.",
 "|The exchange rate determined by market forces is known as|fixed exchange rate|floating exchange rate|pegged exchange rate|dual exchange rate": "The exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. Under a floating (flexible) exchange rate, the rate is determined by the demand for and supply of the currency in the foreign exchange market, without government fixing it: B. A fixed or pegged rate (A, C) is set by the government or central bank and defended at that level. A dual exchange rate (D) means two official rates for different transactions, as Nigeria had in the 1980s and 1990s. In June 2023 Nigeria unified its exchange rate windows and moved towards a market-determined rate for the naira. Memory tip: floating rate floats with the market tide.",
 "|A constraint on the expansion of a firm is the|rate of advertisement|level of the producers' income|tastes of the consumers|size of the market": "A firm expands to enjoy economies of scale, but it can only grow as large as the market for its product allows. If the market is small, because of low incomes, small population or poor transport, extra output cannot be sold, so the size of the market limits expansion: D. Adam Smith made the same point when he said that division of labour is limited by the extent of the market. Advertisement (A) and consumers' tastes (C) affect demand but are not the basic constraint, and the producer's own income (B) is not what limits the firm. Other constraints include finance and managerial ability. Memory tip: you cannot grow bigger than your market.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNumber of men ... Total product (units)\n1 ... 200\n2 ... 440\n3 ... 540\n4 ... 600\n5 ... 640\n6 ... 660\n7 ... 660\n8 ... 640\n\nIf the price per unit is ₦2, what is the average revenue when 6 men are employed?|₦220|₦120|₦110|₦330": "Read the table: 6 men produce a total product of 660 units. Here \"average revenue\" means the revenue per man employed (the average revenue product of labour).\nStep 1: Total revenue = total product × price = 660 × ₦2 = ₦1,320.\nStep 2: Revenue per man = ₦1,320 ÷ 6 = ₦220: A.\nStrictly, average revenue per unit of output is simply the price, ₦2, which is not an option, so the question is asking for revenue per worker. The trap: C (₦110) is the average product per man (660 ÷ 6) without multiplying by the price of ₦2. Memory tip: product × price = revenue; divide by men for revenue per man.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNumber of men ... Total product (units)\n1 ... 200\n2 ... 440\n3 ... 540\n4 ... 600\n5 ... 640\n6 ... 660\n7 ... 660\n8 ... 640\n\nWhat is the average product when 3 men are employed?|120 units|540 units|100 units|180 units": "Average product (AP) is the output per unit of the variable factor: AP = total product ÷ number of workers.\nStep 1: From the table, 3 men produce a total product of 540 units.\nStep 2: AP = 540 ÷ 3 = 180 units: D.\nThe trap: B (540 units) is the total product, not the average, and C (100 units) is the marginal product of the third man (540 − 440). Marginal product is the extra output from one more worker; average product is the output per worker. Memory tip: Average = total shared out; Marginal = the extra one adds.",
 "|A rising short-run average cost is a result of|falling marginal costs|diminishing returns|economies of scale|rising fixed costs": "In the short run some factors, such as the factory building and machines, are fixed. As a firm adds more variable factors such as labour to the fixed factors, output first rises fast, but beyond a point each extra worker adds less and less: this is the law of diminishing returns (variable proportions). When returns diminish, the cost of each extra unit rises, and average cost eventually rises too: B. Falling marginal costs (A) pull average cost down, economies of scale (C) are a long-run idea that lower costs, and fixed costs (D) do not change in the short run by definition. Memory tip: diminishing returns make the short-run AC curve turn up into a U.",
 "|The market structure in which there is interdependence of price-output policies is|an oligopoly|a pure monopoly|a pure competition|a monopolistic competition": "An oligopoly is a market dominated by a few large sellers, such as the major telecom operators (MTN, Airtel, Glo) or cement makers in Nigeria. Because each firm has a large share of the market, any price cut or new product by one firm affects the others, who will react. Each firm must therefore guess its rivals' responses before deciding price and output: this is interdependence, the hallmark of oligopoly: A. A pure monopolist (B) has no rivals, and firms in pure (C) or monopolistic (D) competition are too small to affect each other much. The kinked demand curve and price wars come from this interdependence. Memory tip: Oligopoly = a few firms watching One another.",
 "|The diagram above shows the effect of|excess demand over supply at q₃|excess supply over demand at q₂|minimum price legislation at P₂|maximum price legislation at P₂": "Look at the diagram. Demand DD and supply SS cross at the equilibrium price P₁ and quantity q₁. The price P₂ is fixed below equilibrium. A legal price set below the equilibrium price is a maximum (ceiling) price: sellers may not charge more than P₂. At P₂ suppliers offer only q₂ while buyers want q₃, so there is excess demand of q₂q₃, leading to shortages, queues, rationing and black markets. So the diagram shows maximum price legislation at P₂: D. A minimum price (C) would be set above P₁. The trap: A also mentions excess demand, but the shortage is the gap between q₂ and q₃, not something \"at q₃\". Memory tip: a maximum price is a ceiling held below equilibrium.",
 "|If the short-run cost curve of a firm is U-shaped, the marginal and average costs are equal where the|average fixed cost is falling|marginal cost is falling|average cost is minimum|average cost is maximum": "The short-run average cost (AC) curve is U-shaped: it falls at first, reaches a minimum, then rises because of diminishing returns. Marginal cost (MC) is the cost of one extra unit. When MC is below AC, it pulls the average down; when MC is above AC, it pushes the average up. So MC must cut AC exactly at its lowest point: C. Think of class scores: if a new score is below the class average, the average falls; if above, it rises. AC has no maximum point on a U-shaped curve (D), and MC equals AC when MC is rising, not falling (B). Memory tip: MC cuts AC at the bottom of the U.",
 "|Product differentiation in monopolistic competition implies that|sellers determine whom to sell their products to|different buyers pay different prices for the same product|the same product is available in different forms|different products are sold to the same buyer": "Monopolistic competition has many sellers selling similar but not identical products. Each firm makes its product look different through brand names, packaging, colour, quality or after-sales service: this is product differentiation. Examples are the many brands of detergent, bottled water, noodles or toothpaste in a Nigerian supermarket: basically the same product in different forms: C. Charging different buyers different prices for the same product (B) is price discrimination, a different idea. A and D do not describe differentiation. Differentiation gives each firm a small monopoly over its own brand, so its demand curve slopes downwards. Memory tip: same soap, different wrappers.",
 "|The only factor of production that plays an active role in the production process is|land|entrepreneur|labour|capital": "Production needs four factors: land, labour, capital and the entrepreneur. Land (natural resources) and capital (man-made aids such as machines) are passive factors: they produce nothing on their own until human effort is applied to them. Labour, the human physical and mental effort used in production, is the active factor that works on land and capital to produce goods: C. The entrepreneur organises the other factors and bears risk, but in many textbooks the entrepreneur is treated as a special kind of labour. The trap: B (entrepreneur) is tempting because the entrepreneur is also human and directs production, but the factor described as the active one is labour. Memory tip: land and capital wait; labour works.",
 "|A perfectly competitive firm is advised to close down when the|price is equal to the marginal revenue|price is below the average variable cost|marginal revenue is equal to the marginal cost|price is below the marginal cost": "In the short run a firm faces fixed costs, which it must pay even if it produces nothing, and variable costs, which it pays only if it produces. As long as the price covers average variable cost (AVC), the firm should keep producing, because its revenue pays all variable costs and something towards fixed costs, so its loss is smaller than if it shut down. If the price falls below AVC, every unit adds to the loss, so it should close down: B. Price = MR (A) is always true in perfect competition, and MR = MC (C) is the profit-maximising rule. The trap: D (price below marginal cost) only tells the firm to produce less. Memory tip: can't cover variable costs? Shut the door.",
 "|When the federal government guarantees a loan for a state government, such a loan constitutes|a transferred debt|a private debt|a public debt|an inter-governmental debt": "Public debt is the debt owed by government and its agencies, at any level (federal, state or local), to lenders at home or abroad. When a state government borrows, for example from a commercial bank or the World Bank, and the federal government guarantees the loan (agrees to pay if the state defaults), the loan is still a debt of the public sector: public debt, C. Private debt (B) is owed by individuals and firms. An inter-governmental debt (D) would be a loan from one government to another, while here the federal government only guarantees it. Nigeria's Debt Management Office (DMO), set up in 2000, records such debts. Memory tip: if government owes it, it is public debt.",
 "|If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.6 and consumption expenditure changes by ₦10 million, the equilibrium level of income will change by|₦4.0 million|₦2.5 million|₦15.0 million|₦25.0 million": "The investment or expenditure multiplier shows how much national income changes when spending changes.\nStep 1: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.6) = 1 ÷ 0.4 = 2.5.\nStep 2: Change in income = k × change in spending = 2.5 × ₦10 million = ₦25 million: D.\nThe trap: B (₦2.5 million) is the multiplier itself mistaken for the answer, and A (₦4.0 million) comes from multiplying ₦10 million by the MPS, 0.4. Each round of spending becomes someone's income, who spends 60% of it, and so on. Memory tip: k = 1 ÷ MPS; bigger MPC, bigger multiplier.",
 "|The price index is calculated as|(Current price ÷ Base year price) × 100|(Base year price ÷ Current price) × 100|(Weighted price ÷ Current price) × 100|(Current price ÷ Weighted price) × 100": "A price index measures how prices have changed relative to a base year, whose index is set at 100.\nSimple price index = (current price ÷ base-year price) × 100: A.\nExample: a tin of milk cost ₦200 in the base year and ₦300 now. Index = (300 ÷ 200) × 100 = 150, meaning prices have risen by 50%.\nThe trap: B turns the fraction upside down, which would give 66.7 and wrongly show a fall in price. C and D bring in \"weighted price\", which is used in weighted indices such as the Consumer Price Index, but not in this way. Memory tip: Current over Base, times 100: C over B.",
 "|Personal income tax as a source of government revenue is increased when the|tax base is contracted|tax system is proportional|tax rate is raised|retirement age is reduced": "Revenue from any tax equals the tax rate multiplied by the tax base (the total income or value that is taxed). Personal income tax revenue therefore rises if the tax rate is raised, provided people do not stop working or evade the tax: C. A contracted tax base (A) means fewer incomes to tax, so revenue falls. A proportional system (B) says nothing about whether the amount collected rises. Reducing the retirement age (D) takes people out of employment sooner and shrinks the base. Revenue can also be raised by widening the base and improving collection. Memory tip: revenue = rate × base; raise either one.",
 "|If the Central Bank of Nigeria reduces the bank rate, this will cause|commercial banks to merge|commercial banks to reduce lending|money supply to reduce|money supply to increase": "The bank rate (minimum rediscount rate, now called the Monetary Policy Rate, MPR) is the rate at which the Central Bank lends to commercial banks. When the CBN reduces it, borrowing from the CBN becomes cheaper, so banks lower their own lending rates and lend more to the public. More bank lending creates more deposits, so the money supply increases: D. Lowering the rate encourages, not reduces, lending (B), and it has nothing to do with mergers (A). The trap: C is the effect of raising the bank rate, which is used to fight inflation. Memory tip: rate down, money up; rate up, money down.",
 "|The main source of government revenue in Nigeria is|import duties|capital gains tax|company income tax|rents and royalties": "Before the 1970s import and export duties were the main source of government revenue in Nigeria. Since the oil boom of the 1970s, the oil sector has provided most of the federal government's revenue through royalties and rents paid by oil companies for the right to extract crude oil, petroleum profits tax and sales of crude oil. In 2006 oil revenue ran into trillions of naira, while customs duties brought in only a few hundred billion. So the main source is rents and royalties: D. Company income tax (C) and capital gains tax (B) are much smaller. Today non-oil taxes such as VAT are growing, but oil remains central. Memory tip: in Nigeria, the money comes from the oil wells.",
 "|One way of controlling deflation is by|decreasing wages|rigid fiscal policy|contractionary monetary policy|deficit financing": "Deflation is a persistent fall in the general price level, usually caused by too little total spending, so firms cut output and jobs. To control it, government must increase aggregate demand: expansionary fiscal and monetary policy. Deficit financing means government deliberately spends more than its revenue, financing the gap by borrowing, which injects money and demand into the economy: D. Decreasing wages (A), rigid (tight) fiscal policy (B) and contractionary monetary policy (C) all reduce spending and would make deflation worse; they are cures for inflation. Memory tip: deflation needs a Deficit; inflation needs a squeeze.",
 "|The Revenue Mobilization, Allocation and Fiscal Commission in Nigeria has the primary responsibility for|ensuring equity in the sharing of fiscal responsibilities|evolving acceptable revenue-sharing formula|evolving an acceptable wage for public servants|maintaining the fiscal supremacy of the central government": "The Revenue Mobilization, Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) was established in 1989 and is backed by the 1999 Constitution. Its chief duty is to monitor the inflow of revenue into the Federation Account and to review and propose the formula for sharing that revenue among the federal, state and local governments, which the National Assembly then approves: B. It also fixes the pay of political office holders, not the wages of public servants (C), which are negotiated with labour unions. It does not exist to keep the central government supreme (D). Memory tip: RMAFC = the Federation Account's sharing-formula commission.",
 "|The major role of NDIC is to|regulate the activities of insurance companies|accept customers' deposits|ensure adequate capitalization of insurance companies|insure bank deposits": "The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) was established in 1988 and began work in 1989. It insures the deposits of customers in licensed banks, so that if a bank fails, depositors are paid up to a set limit. It also supervises banks, alongside the CBN, to prevent failures, and acts as liquidator of failed banks: D. Accepting deposits (B) is the work of commercial banks themselves, and insurance companies are regulated by NAICOM, not NDIC (A, C). The NDIC Act 2023 now governs it, and the cover for deposit money banks is ₦5 million per depositor. Memory tip: NDIC = Deposits Insured.",
 "|In computing national income, transfers are excluded because|they are not payments for productive activities|the figures reduce the size of national income|it is difficult to get the exact figures|they are received only by the poor": "National income measures the value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. Transfer payments are payments for which no good or service is given in return: pensions, unemployment benefits, scholarships, gifts and remittances from relatives. They merely move income from one person to another without any new production. Counting them would add income that matches no output and would double-count income already earned by someone else. So they are excluded because they are not payments for productive activities: A. They are not excluded because they are small, hard to measure (C) or received only by the poor (D). Memory tip: no production, no national income.",
 "|Choice involves opportunity cost because|available resources are inadequate|there are many goods to select from|goods give different utilities|not all goods have the same price": "Human wants are unlimited, but the resources for satisfying them (land, labour, capital, money, time) are scarce or inadequate. Because we cannot have everything, we must choose, and every choice means giving up the next best alternative. That sacrifice is the opportunity cost. So choice involves opportunity cost because available resources are inadequate: A. Having many goods to choose from (B), different utilities (C) or different prices (D) would not force a sacrifice if resources were unlimited. Scarcity is the root of both choice and opportunity cost. Memory tip: scarcity forces choice; choice creates opportunity cost.",
 "|A straight line indifference curve indicates that the two products are|inferior goods|perfect substitutes|normal goods|close substitutes": "An indifference curve shows combinations of two goods that give a consumer the same satisfaction. Normally it is convex to the origin, because the marginal rate of substitution (MRS) falls as one good is substituted for the other. A straight-line indifference curve has a constant slope, so the consumer is always willing to swap the goods at the same rate however much of each he has. That happens only when the goods are perfect substitutes, such as two brands of identical bottled water: B. The trap: D (close substitutes) gives a curve that is only slightly convex, not straight. An L-shaped curve shows perfect complements. Memory tip: straight line = perfect swap.",
 "|The law of demand can be expressed as|P = f(Qd)|Qd = f(1/P)|P = f(1/Qd)|Qd = f(P)": "The law of demand states that, other things being equal, the quantity demanded of a good varies inversely with its price. In functional form this is written Qd = f(P): quantity demanded is a function of price. The inverse direction is shown by the downward-sloping demand curve, or a negative coefficient as in Qd = a − bP: D. Options A and C put price as the dependent variable, which reverses the usual form. Option B, Qd = f(1/P), also hints at the inverse link and can be argued for, but the standard textbook expression is Qd = f(P). Memory tip: Quantity depends on Price: Qd = f(P).",
 "|A commodity will be demanded only if|consumers' income increases|it has no close substitutes|it has utility|the price is low": "Demand for a commodity exists only if consumers want it, and they want it only if it can satisfy a need or want, which economists call utility. A good with no utility, such as a broken pen, will not be demanded at any price. So a commodity will be demanded only if it has utility: C. Income rising (A), absence of substitutes (B) and a low price (D) affect how much is demanded, but none is a necessary condition: goods are demanded even when income does not rise, substitutes exist or prices are high. Effective demand also needs ability to pay. Memory tip: no utility, no demand.",
 "|The range of the data 14, 13, 15, 18, 20, 35 and 13 is|22|20|18|13": "The range is the simplest measure of dispersion: the difference between the highest and lowest values.\nStep 1: Arrange the data: 13, 13, 14, 15, 18, 20, 35.\nStep 2: Highest = 35; lowest = 13.\nStep 3: Range = 35 − 13 = 22: A.\nThe trap: B (20) is the second highest value, and D (13) is the lowest value (and the mode), not the range. The range is easily affected by one extreme value such as 35. Memory tip: range = top minus bottom.",
 "|The burden of a government tax on a commodity whose demand is inelastic will|be borne only by the government|be shared equally between consumers and producers|fall more heavily on consumers|fall more heavily on producers": "The incidence of a tax is who finally bears its burden. When a tax is placed on a commodity, sellers try to pass it on by raising the price. If demand is inelastic, consumers keep buying almost the same quantity even at the higher price, as with salt, fuel or essential drugs, so sellers can pass most of the tax on. The burden therefore falls more heavily on consumers: C. If demand were elastic, buyers would switch away and producers would bear more (D). Equal sharing (B) happens only in a special case, and government does not bear the tax (A); it collects it. Memory tip: inelastic demand, consumer pays.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nA movement from K to M will|decrease the consumer's utility|increase the price of the commodity|increase the consumer's utility|leave the consumer's utility unchanged": "An indifference curve joins all combinations of two goods X and Y that give a consumer the same level of satisfaction, so the consumer is indifferent between them. In the diagram, K (on the steep part) and M (on the flat part) both lie on the same indifference curve, labelled I. Moving from K to M changes the mix of X and Y but not the total satisfaction, so utility is unchanged: D. Utility would increase (C) only by moving to a higher curve such as II or III. Prices (B) are shown by the budget line ST, not by the indifference curve. Memory tip: same curve, same satisfaction.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nAt point P, the marginal rate of substitution is|less than one|greater than one|zero|unity": "The marginal rate of substitution (MRS) of X for Y is the amount of Y a consumer gives up for one more unit of X while staying equally satisfied: the slope of the indifference curve. At P the budget line ST just touches the highest attainable curve, III, so P is the consumer's equilibrium and the MRS equals the slope of ST (the price ratio). ST runs at about 45°, OS being about equal to OT, so the MRS at P is one: unity, D. This reading is debatable only because the slope comes from the drawing. The trap: C (zero) is the MRS on the flat stretches of the curves, as at M, not at P. Memory tip: at equilibrium, MRS = price ratio = slope of the budget line.",
 "|Derived demand is normally used with reference to|superior goods|the cost of production|the factors of production|inferior goods": "Derived demand means that something is demanded not for its own sake but for what it helps to produce. The term is normally used for the factors of production: firms demand land, labour, capital and entrepreneurs because consumers demand the goods those factors make. If the demand for houses rises, the demand for bricklayers, cement and land rises too: C. Superior and inferior goods (A, D) are about how demand changes with income, and cost of production (B) is a supply idea. Memory tip: Derived demand Depends on the demand for the final product.",
 "|If the marginal utility of good X exceeds that of good Y, this implies that|X will be cheaper than Y|Y is an inferior good|consuming more of X will increase total utility|a rational consumer will buy less of X": "Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit of a good. If the MU of X exceeds that of Y, then the MU of X is positive, and whenever MU is positive, consuming one more unit adds to total utility. So consuming more of X will increase total utility: C. Marginal utility says nothing about which good is cheaper (A) or whether Y is inferior (B), which depends on income. A rational consumer compares MU per naira (MUx/Px with MUy/Py), and if MUx/Px is higher he buys more of X, not less (D). Total utility stops rising only when MU reaches zero. Memory tip: positive MU, rising TU.",
 "|A distribution is said to be positively skewed if it|is bell-shaped|has a long tail to the left|is bimodal|has a long tail to the right": "Skewness describes how lopsided a frequency distribution is. A normal distribution is symmetrical and bell-shaped (A), with mean, median and mode equal. In a positively (right) skewed distribution, most values are bunched on the left and a few very large values stretch a long tail to the right: D. The mean is pulled up by the large values, so mean > median > mode. Income distribution in Nigeria is positively skewed: most people earn modest incomes, a few earn a great deal. A long tail to the left (B) is negative skewness, and bimodal (C) means two peaks. The trap: B is the mirror image, negative skewness. Memory tip: the tail tells the sign: right tail, positive.",
 "|An important determinant of price elasticity of demand is|the ease of substitution|the state of technology|the prices of the commodities|government policy": "Price elasticity of demand measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a change in price. Its main determinants are the availability (ease) of substitutes, whether the good is a necessity or a luxury, the proportion of income spent on it, the number of uses, habit and the time period. The most important is ease of substitution: if close substitutes exist, buyers switch when price rises, so demand is elastic; if not, demand is inelastic: A. The state of technology (B) affects supply, not demand elasticity, and government policy (D) is not a determinant of elasticity. Memory tip: more substitutes, more elastic.",
 "|A decision on input combination solves the economic problem of|for whom to produce|how to produce|when to produce|what to produce": "Every society must answer the basic questions of what to produce, how to produce, for whom to produce, and how efficiently. \"How to produce\" is about the method or technique of production: which combination of inputs (land, labour, capital) to use, for example whether to farm with hoes and many workers (labour-intensive) or with tractors and few workers (capital-intensive). So a decision on input combination solves the problem of how to produce: B. What to produce (D) concerns which goods and in what quantities, and for whom (A) concerns distribution. \"When to produce\" (C) is not one of the basic economic problems. Memory tip: How = the recipe of inputs.",
 "|Given that demand and price remain unchanged, an outward shift of the supply curve will lead to|hoarding|excess demand|a black market|excess supply": "At equilibrium, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. An outward (rightward) shift of the supply curve means producers offer more at every price, for example after a good harvest. If demand and price stay where they were, the new quantity supplied at that price is greater than the quantity demanded, so there is excess supply, a glut: D. Normally this surplus pushes the price down to a new equilibrium. Excess demand (B), hoarding (A) and black markets (C) arise from shortages, as under a maximum price, which is the opposite case. The trap: B is the result of an inward shift of supply or a rise in demand, the reverse situation. Memory tip: more supply at the same price = surplus.",
 "|A major objective of the Federal Government's initiative on cassava production in Nigeria is to|ensure the availability of cassava|make cassava a staple food in the country|provide raw materials for industries|diversify the export base of the economy": "In 2002 the Obasanjo government launched the Presidential Initiative on Cassava Production and Export. Nigeria was already the world's largest producer of cassava, but almost all of it was eaten at home as garri, fufu and lafun. The initiative set out to raise output and processing so that cassava chips, starch, flour and ethanol could be sold abroad, earning foreign exchange and reducing the country's heavy dependence on crude oil exports. That is diversifying the export base: D. Supplying raw materials to local industries (C) was part of the programme, but the headline objective was export earnings. Today the same aim continues under policies promoting cassava flour in bread and local starch production. Memory tip: oil-dependent country plus new export crop equals diversification.",
 "|Upstream oil activities involve the|marketing of finished products|exploration of crude oil|management of crude oil|refining of crude oil": "The oil industry is divided into stages. The upstream sector is everything involved in finding and getting crude oil out of the ground: exploration (seismic surveys), drilling and production. The midstream sector moves and stores it (pipelines, tankers, storage), and the downstream sector refines crude into petrol, diesel and kerosene and markets these products to consumers. Exploration of crude oil is therefore an upstream activity: B. Refining (D) and marketing of finished products (A) are downstream. The trap: D, refining, sounds like an early stage because it handles crude oil, but it is downstream since the crude has already been produced. Memory tip: upstream is up at the source, where the oil is found; downstream flows down to the filling station.",
 "|An upward review of the retirement age will affect the|wage rate|tax rate|supply of labour|demand for labour": "The supply of labour is the number of people willing and able to work at a given wage. If the government raises the retirement age, for example from 60 to 65 years, workers who would have left the workforce stay in it for five more years. The working population, and so the supply of labour, increases: C. The change does not directly set the wage rate (A) or tax rate (B), and the demand for labour (D) depends on employers' need for workers, which this policy does not change. Nigeria raised the retirement age of teachers to 65 in 2022, a real example. Memory tip: who may work affects supply; who wants workers affects demand.",
 "|Environmental degradation is more associated with|decreased urbanization|localization of industries|urban-rural migration|location of industries": "Localization of industries is the concentration of many firms of the same industry in one area, such as the oil companies around Port Harcourt or textile firms in Kano. When many factories crowd together, the area suffers air and water pollution, waste dumping, traffic congestion, slums and pressure on land: these are classic disadvantages of localization and add up to environmental degradation: B. Location of industries (D) only means where a single firm decides to site its plant, which need not degrade anything. The trap: D sounds almost the same as B, but location is a firm's choice of site, while localization is the clustering of an industry. Memory tip: localization is crowding, and crowding brings pollution.",
 "|The Malthusian theory of population has been rendered ineffective by|natural disasters|birth control|technical progress|government policies": "Thomas Malthus (1798) argued that population grows geometrically (2, 4, 8, 16) while food supply grows only arithmetically (1, 2, 3, 4), so population would outrun food and be checked by famine, war and disease. His prediction failed mainly because of technical progress: new farming methods, improved seeds, fertilizers, irrigation and machines (for example the Green Revolution) raised food output far faster than he imagined: C. Birth control (B) has slowed population growth in many countries, but Malthus himself discussed moral restraint, and the decisive reason his theory failed is that food production kept pace through technology. Memory tip: Malthus forgot the machine.",
 "|Industrialization is a prerequisite for balanced economic growth because it|enhances productivity in the manufacturing sector|expands employment opportunities in urban areas|encourages trade liberalization|stimulates development of all sectors": "Balanced growth means developing all sectors of the economy together so that each provides a market for the others. Industrialization is seen as a prerequisite because factories create demand for agricultural raw materials (cotton, cocoa, palm oil), minerals and energy, while supplying tools, fertilizers and processed goods to farmers and services to the rest of the economy. These linkages stimulate development in every sector: D. Higher manufacturing productivity (A) and urban jobs (B) are real benefits, but they concern only part of the economy and do not explain \"balanced\" growth. Trade liberalization (C) is a trade policy, not a result of industrialization. Memory tip: balanced growth means all sectors, so look for the word \"all\".",
 "|A country would develop its agricultural sector first so as to|stabilize the prices of industrial products|create a market for the industrial sector|stabilize the prices of agricultural products|create a market for the agricultural sector": "Many economists argue that a developing country should develop agriculture first because most people live and work on farms. When farm output and incomes rise, rural households can afford manufactured goods such as clothing, bicycles and radios, so agriculture creates a market for the industrial sector: B. A stronger agriculture also supplies food for factory workers, raw materials for industry and foreign exchange to import machinery. Stable agricultural prices (C) may be a by-product of more food, but they are not the reason for developing agriculture first; this item is debatable, and B is the stronger reason. The trap: D reverses the link, since industry is what creates a market for farm produce, not the other way round. Memory tip: rich farmers are customers for factories.",
 "|The labour force of a country best describes|people within the working age|people within the retirement age|the number of people in paid employment|the number of people receiving pension": "The labour force (working population) is the people within the legal working age, roughly 15 to 64 years in Nigeria, who are able and willing to work. It includes both the employed and the unemployed who are actively looking for work: A. People in paid employment (C) are only part of the labour force, because the unemployed job-seekers are left out, and the self-employed farmer or trader is not \"paid\" by an employer but still counts. Retirees and pensioners (B, D) are outside the labour force. The trap: C is tempting because workers come to mind, but it leaves out the unemployed who are still part of the labour force. Memory tip: labour force = employed + unemployed job-seekers of working age.",
 "|The movement of people from rural to urban areas affects the|sex distribution of population|geographical distribution of population|age distribution of population|occupational distribution of population": "Population can be studied by its distribution: by age (young, working, old), by sex (male, female), by occupation (farming, industry, services) and geographically (where people live). Rural-urban migration moves people from villages to towns such as Lagos, Kano and Port Harcourt, so it changes where the population lives, the geographical distribution: B. It may also change the occupational structure over time, as migrants leave farming for trade or factory work, but the direct effect of a movement of people is on location. Memory tip: migration is a movement on the map, so it changes the geographical distribution.",
 "|Foreign investment can be attracted to Nigeria through|a review of interest rates|a review of the naturalization policy|review of the tax policy|the revaluation of the naira": "Foreign investors put money where the after-tax return is highest and safest. Government can attract them through its tax policy: tax holidays (pioneer status) for new industries, lower company income tax, relief on dividends and profits sent home, and duty waivers on imported machinery. A review of the tax policy to offer such incentives is therefore the route given: C. Revaluation of the naira (D) makes Nigerian assets and labour dearer in foreign currency, discouraging investors. A review of interest rates (A) mainly affects local borrowing and portfolio flows, not direct investment. Nigeria today also offers export processing zones and pioneer status to foreign investors. Memory tip: investors follow returns, and tax cuts raise returns.",
 "|In a developing economy, productivity is measured by the|output-labour ratio|output growth rate|output per capita|capital-output ratio": "Productivity is the amount of output produced per unit of input over a period. The most common measure, especially in developing countries where labour is the most plentiful factor, is labour productivity: output divided by the number of workers or hours worked, the output-labour ratio: A. If a factory of 20 workers produces 1,000 bags of flour a day, productivity is 50 bags per worker. Output per capita (C) divides output by the whole population, including children and the old, so it measures average living standards, not productivity. The capital-output ratio (D) shows how much capital is needed per unit of output. Memory tip: productivity is output per worker.",
 "|The survival of a partnership depends on|political stability|the managerial ability of the partners|access to finance|the goodwill of the partners": "A partnership is a business owned by two to twenty partners who share capital, management and profits, usually with unlimited liability. Since there is no separate board of directors, the partners themselves make every decision; their skill in running the business determines whether it grows or collapses. The survival of a partnership therefore depends on the managerial ability of the partners: B. Mutual trust and goodwill among partners (D) also matter, since disagreement can break up a partnership, so this item is debatable. Access to finance (C) and political stability (A) affect all businesses alike. Memory tip: in a partnership the partners are the managers, so their ability decides its fate.",
 "|The main objective of WTO is to|provide a mechanism for tariff reductions|ensure adequate tariff protection for infant industries|help debtor nations solve their problems|help countries with chronic balance of payments problems": "The World Trade Organization was set up on 1 January 1995 to replace GATT (General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade, 1947). Its main purpose is to liberalise world trade by providing a forum and rules for negotiating the reduction of tariffs and other trade barriers, and a mechanism for settling trade disputes between members: A. Helping countries with balance of payments problems (D) is the job of the IMF, and help for debtor nations (C) is mainly from the IMF and World Bank. Protecting infant industries (B) is the opposite of WTO's free-trade aim. Nigeria has been a member since 1995. Memory tip: WTO = Way To Open trade.",
 "|A continuous increase in a nation's income accompanied by the structural transformation of all indices is called|economic reforms|economic planning|economic development|economic growth": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real national income or output. Economic development is broader: it is growth accompanied by structural transformation, meaning a shift from farming to industry and services, better education and health, less poverty, more employment and improved living standards. Because the question adds \"structural transformation of all indices\", the answer is economic development: C. The trap: D, economic growth, is the increase in income alone, without the change in the structure of the economy and welfare. Economic planning (B) and reforms (A) are tools a government uses to achieve development. Memory tip: growth is more; development is more and better.",
 "|One of the ways of correcting a deficit balance of payments is to|increase imports|revalue a country's currency|reduce exports|devalue a country's currency": "A deficit in the balance of payments means a country pays out more foreign exchange (for imports and other items) than it earns. Devaluation lowers the value of the home currency against foreign currencies. Nigerian exports become cheaper for foreigners to buy, so exports rise, while imports become dearer in naira, so imports fall. The gap closes: D. Revaluation (B) does the opposite, making exports dearer and imports cheaper, which would worsen the deficit. Increasing imports (A) and reducing exports (C) also worsen it. The trap: B, revalue, looks similar to devalue but works in the opposite direction. Memory tip: devalue to cut the deficit.",
 "|The minimum number of shareholders for a joint-stock company is|4|2|7|3": "A joint-stock company is a business owned by shareholders, with its capital divided into shares and limited liability. It may be a private limited company (Ltd) or a public limited company (Plc). The minimum number of members for a private company has long been two, so the minimum for a joint-stock company is two: B. In Nigeria, the Companies and Allied Matters Act 1990, in force in 2007, allowed any two persons to form a company, public or private. Seven (C) was the old minimum for a public company under the Companies Act 1968, and this item is debatable because some textbooks still teach that figure. CAMA 2020 now allows one person to form a private company. Memory tip: two to start any company.",
 "|The gains from international trade are shared on the basis of the|terms of trade|bilateral trade agreements|cost of production of each of the traded goods|quantity produced of each of the traded goods": "When countries specialise according to comparative advantage and trade, total world output rises. How that gain is divided depends on the rate at which one country's exports exchange for the other's, the terms of trade. Terms of trade = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100. If a country's export prices rise relative to its import prices, it gets more imports for each unit of exports and takes a bigger share of the gains: A. Costs of production (C) determine comparative advantage, that is whether trade is worthwhile, but not how the gain is shared. Memory tip: comparative advantage creates the gain; terms of trade share it.",
 "|A characteristic of the linear production function is that|the percentage change in output is less than one|the percentage change in inputs exceeds output|output and all inputs change by the same percentage|the percentage change in output is greater than one": "A production function shows how output depends on inputs. A linear (linearly homogeneous) production function is one in which multiplying all inputs by a factor multiplies output by the same factor. If labour and capital both rise by 10%, output rises by exactly 10%. This is constant returns to scale: output and all inputs change by the same percentage: C. Increasing returns to scale (output rises by a greater percentage than inputs, D) and decreasing returns (output rises by a smaller percentage, A and B) are not features of a linear function. The Cobb-Douglas function with exponents adding up to one is the standard example. Memory tip: linear means a straight line, so output keeps pace exactly with inputs.",
 "|The price per unit of a commodity to a buyer is the same as the|normal profit of the seller|average revenue of the seller of the commodity|marginal cost of the commodity|marginal revenue of the seller": "Average revenue is total revenue divided by the number of units sold. Total revenue = price × quantity, so AR = (P × Q) ÷ Q = P. The price per unit the buyer pays is therefore the seller's average revenue: B. Marginal revenue (D) is the extra revenue from selling one more unit; it equals price only under perfect competition, and is below price when the seller must lower price to sell more. The trap: D, marginal revenue, equals price only under perfect competition, so it is not always the same as the price paid. Normal profit (A) and marginal cost (C) are costs to the seller, not the price. Memory tip: AR is always the price; the demand curve is the AR curve.",
 "|If the total fixed cost is the same regardless of output, the average fixed cost will be|decreasing|minimum|maximum|increasing": "Total fixed cost (rent, insurance, salaries of permanent staff) does not change with output. Average fixed cost is TFC divided by output. Suppose TFC = ₦1,000: at 10 units AFC = ₦100; at 100 units AFC = ₦10; at 1,000 units AFC = ₦1. The same fixed amount is spread over more units, so AFC keeps falling as output rises, approaching but never reaching zero: A. AFC has no minimum (B) or maximum (C) point and is never increasing (D). Its curve is a rectangular hyperbola. Memory tip: fixed cost spread thinner over more units.",
 "|The division of labour requires that the task to be performed|requires no special knowledge|is simple but strenuous|must be undertaken by experts|must be done in stages": "Division of labour (specialisation by process) means breaking the production of a good into separate stages or operations, with each worker doing only one. Adam Smith's pin factory is the famous example: one man draws the wire, another straightens it, a third cuts it, and so on. This is only possible if the task can be done in stages: D. The task does not have to be simple, strenuous or free of special knowledge (A, B), and it does not need experts (C); in fact division of labour lets ordinary workers become skilled through repetition. Memory tip: divide the work into stages, divide the stages among workers.",
 "|The diagram above shows a firm operating under conditions of monopolistic competition. The curve W represents its|marginal cost|average cost|average revenue|marginal revenue": "Read the shapes. A monopolistically competitive firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve, which is its average revenue (AR) curve. Its marginal revenue (MR) curve also slopes downward but is steeper, lying below AR. In the diagram, X is the steep U-shaped curve rising sharply, the marginal cost; Y is the flatter U-shaped curve, the average cost; M is the steeper downward line, MR; and W is the flatter downward line above it, AR: C. The trap: D, marginal revenue, is the other downward-sloping line, but MR is always the steeper one lying below AR. Memory tip: of the two falling lines, the upper flatter one is AR, the lower steeper one is MR.",
 "|When a firm's average revenue curve is downward sloping, its price elasticity of demand will be|zero|greater than one|one|between zero and infinity": "Elasticity of demand is measured at each point on a curve. A horizontal AR curve (perfect competition) has infinite elasticity; a vertical one has zero elasticity. Along a downward-sloping straight-line AR curve, elasticity falls from very high near the price axis, through one at the midpoint, to very low near the quantity axis. So for a downward-sloping AR curve the only statement always true is that elasticity lies between zero and infinity: D. A profit-maximising monopolist does produce where demand is elastic (greater than one, B), because marginal revenue must be positive, but the question does not limit itself to that point, so this item is debatable. Memory tip: flat is infinite, vertical is zero, sloping is in between.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNo. of workers ... Total output (kg) ... MP\n1 ... 39 ... -\n2 ... 52 ... 13\n3 ... 60 ... 8\n4 ... 66 ... 6\n5 ... 66 ... 0\n6 ... 63 ... -3\n\nThe maximum number of workers that should be hired is|2|3|4|5": "Step 1: Read the total output column: 39, 52, 60, 66, 66, 63 kg for 1 to 6 workers.\nStep 2: Total output is highest, 66 kg, with 4 workers. The 5th worker adds nothing (MP = 0) and the 6th reduces output (MP = −3 kg).\nStep 3: A firm should never pay a worker who adds no output, so the most it should employ is 4 workers: C.\nSome textbooks say production can go up to the point where MP = 0 (5 workers), so this item is debatable, but hiring the 5th worker only adds to cost. Memory tip: stop hiring when the next worker adds nothing.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNo. of workers ... Total output (kg) ... MP\n1 ... 39 ... -\n2 ... 52 ... 13\n3 ... 60 ... 8\n4 ... 66 ... 6\n5 ... 66 ... 0\n6 ... 63 ... -3\n\nWhat is the marginal productivity when the number of workers is increased from 2 to 3?|8kg|20kg|60kg|180kg": "Marginal product (marginal productivity) is the extra output from employing one more worker.\nStep 1: Total output with 3 workers = 60 kg.\nStep 2: Total output with 2 workers = 52 kg.\nStep 3: MP of the 3rd worker = 60 − 52 = 8 kg: A.\nThe MP column of the table confirms 8. Option C (60 kg) is the total output with 3 workers, not the change.\nThe trap: B (20 kg) is the average product, the slip students make when they divide total output by the number of workers instead of finding the change. Memory tip: marginal means the change, average means the share.",
 "|A firm incurs short-run costs when|it cannot increase prices|operation is at its later stages|operation is at its early stages|some inputs cannot be varied": "The short run is a period too short for a firm to change all its inputs: at least one factor, usually the size of the plant, machinery or buildings, is fixed, while others such as labour and raw materials can be varied. Because some inputs cannot be varied, the firm's costs divide into fixed costs and variable costs; these are short-run costs: D. In the long run every input can be changed, so all costs are variable. The short run is not defined by the stage of the firm's life (B, C) or by its ability to raise prices (A). Memory tip: short run, something stays fixed; long run, everything can change.",
 "|The world market for crude oil refers to the|total demand for and supply of crude oil|total supply by OPEC members|difference between the supply of and demand for oil|excess crude oil produced in the world": "A market in economics is not just a place: it is the whole arrangement through which buyers and sellers of a commodity meet and fix prices. The world market for crude oil is the total world demand for crude oil from refiners and countries everywhere, and the total supply from all producers, OPEC and non-OPEC (the USA, Russia, Norway and others): A. OPEC supplies only part of the world's oil (B). The difference between supply and demand (C) is a surplus or shortage, and excess crude (D) is only a glut; neither is the market. Brent crude is the main world price benchmark, and Nigeria's Bonny Light is priced against it. Memory tip: market = all buyers + all sellers.",
 "|An increase in the marginal cost of production causes|a downward movement along the supply curve|a leftward shift of the supply curve|a rightward shift of the supply curve|an upward movement along the supply curve": "A firm's short-run supply curve is the part of its marginal cost curve above average variable cost. If marginal cost rises, perhaps because wages, fuel or raw materials become dearer, the firm is willing to supply less at every price, or needs a higher price for each quantity. The whole supply curve shifts to the left (upwards): B. A movement along the supply curve (A, D) happens only when the good's own price changes. A rightward shift (C) comes from lower costs or better technology. Memory tip: higher costs, less supply, shift left.",
 "|The choice of what to produce in a command economy is determined by|the central government|the government and labour unions|industrial establishments|labour unions": "In a command (centrally planned or socialist) economy, the state owns most of the means of production, and a central planning authority set up by the central government decides the basic economic questions: what to produce, how to produce it and for whom. Prices and consumer choices play little part. The former Soviet Union and North Korea are examples. So the choice of what to produce is determined by the central government: A. In a capitalist economy the same choice is made by consumers and firms through the price mechanism. Labour unions (B, D) and industrial establishments (C) carry out the plan but do not decide it. Memory tip: command means the government gives the commands.",
 "|The term ceteris paribus implies that|resources are scarce|human problems can be solved|human beings are unpredictable|all factors are fully utilized": "Ceteris paribus is Latin for \"other things being equal\" or \"other things remaining constant\". Economists use it to isolate one relationship: the law of demand says that if price rises, quantity demanded falls, ceteris paribus, meaning income, tastes and the prices of other goods stay unchanged. Economists need this assumption because in real life many things change at once and human behaviour is not fully predictable. None of the options says this exactly. D is the answer intended, the only option about \"all factors\", but \"fully utilized\" is an assumption of the production possibility curve, not the meaning of ceteris paribus, so the intended answer is not fully correct: D. Memory tip: ceteris paribus = hold everything else still.",
 "|A commodity is described as inferior when the|substitution effect is negative|substitution effect is positive|income effect is positive|income effect is negative": "A rise in income has an income effect on demand. For a normal good the income effect is positive: people buy more of it when their income rises. For an inferior good the income effect is negative: as income rises, people buy less of it and switch to better goods, as a family moving from garri to rice or from public buses to cars. So a commodity is inferior when its income effect is negative: D. The substitution effect of a price fall (A, B) always encourages buying more of the cheaper good, for normal and inferior goods alike, so it does not define an inferior good. The trap: C, positive income effect, describes a normal good, the exact opposite. Memory tip: inferior goods go down when income goes up.",
 "|The coefficient of the price elasticity of supply is always|negative|positive|zero|constant": "Price elasticity of supply = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price. By the law of supply, a higher price brings a larger quantity supplied, so the two changes have the same sign and the coefficient is positive: B. For example, if the price of yam rises by 10% and the quantity supplied rises by 5%, elasticity = 5 ÷ 10 = +0.5. By contrast, price elasticity of demand has a negative coefficient because price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions. The value of elasticity of supply can be zero only in the special case of perfectly inelastic supply, and it is not constant along most curves. Memory tip: supply and price move together, so the sign is plus.",
 "|The range is used to|measure the difference between the mean and the mode|calculate the standard deviation|estimate the mean from grouped data|determine the spread of a set of data": "The range is the simplest measure of dispersion: range = highest value − lowest value. For the scores 12, 15, 9, 20 and 11, the range is 20 − 9 = 11. It tells us how widely the data are spread out: D. It does not measure the gap between the mean and the mode (A), is not used to calculate the standard deviation (B), which uses deviations from the mean, and is not used to estimate the mean (C). Its weakness is that it uses only two values, so one extreme figure can distort it. Memory tip: range = top minus bottom = spread.",
 "|If P = ½(Qs + 15), what is the quantity supplied at ₦9.00?|33.0|12.0|3.0|1.5": "The supply function links price (P) and quantity supplied (Qs).\nStep 1: Substitute P = 9: 9 = ½(Qs + 15).\nStep 2: Multiply both sides by 2: 18 = Qs + 15.\nStep 3: Subtract 15: Qs = 18 − 15 = 3.0: C.\nCheck: ½(3 + 15) = ½ × 18 = 9.\nThe trap: A (33.0) is what you get by adding 15 to 18 instead of subtracting it. Memory tip: clear the fraction first, then move the constant.",
 "|The major determinant of cross elasticity of demand is the|level of income of consumers|closeness of the substitutes|price of the good|degree of necessity of the good": "Cross elasticity of demand measures how the demand for one good responds to a change in the price of another: percentage change in quantity demanded of X ÷ percentage change in price of Y. For substitutes it is positive, for complements negative, and for unrelated goods zero. Its size depends mainly on how closely the two goods are related: close substitutes such as Peak and Three Crowns milk have a high positive cross elasticity, while distant substitutes have a low one. So the major determinant is the closeness of the substitutes: B. Income (A), the good's own price (C) and its necessity (D) determine income elasticity or own-price elasticity instead. Memory tip: cross elasticity = how closely two goods are tied.",
 "|The logical sequence of basic concepts in economics is|wants, scarcity, choice and opportunity cost|wants, choice, scarcity and opportunity cost|scarcity, wants, choice and scale of preference|scarcity, choice, scale of preference and opportunity cost": "Economics starts from human wants, which are unlimited. The resources to satisfy them are scarce, which is the basic economic problem. Because of scarcity, we must make a choice among wants, arranging them on a scale of preference. Whatever we choose, we give up the next best alternative, which is the opportunity cost. So the logical sequence is wants, scarcity, choice and opportunity cost: A. B puts choice before scarcity, but there would be no need to choose without scarcity. C and D begin with scarcity, but scarcity only exists relative to wants. The trap: B lists the same four concepts in a slightly different order, so read the order carefully. Memory tip: Wise Students Choose Opportunity (W-S-C-O).",
 "|When the demand for a good increases owing to an increase in income, it means that|inflation has taken place|demand has exceeded supply|there is an increase in demand|there is an increase in quantity demanded": "A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the demand curve, called a change in quantity demanded. A change in any other factor, such as income, tastes or the prices of related goods, shifts the whole demand curve; this is a change in demand. A rise in income shifts the demand curve for a normal good to the right, so there is an increase in demand: C. The trap: D, an increase in quantity demanded, is the term for a movement along the curve caused by a fall in the good's own price, not by income. Demand exceeding supply (B) is a shortage, and inflation (A) is a general rise in prices. Memory tip: own price moves you along; anything else shifts the curve.",
 "|A firm operating at full capacity will have a|fairly inelastic supply curve|fairly elastic supply curve|perfectly elastic supply curve|perfectly inelastic supply curve": "Elasticity of supply depends on how easily a firm can change its output when price changes. A firm with spare capacity can expand output quickly, so its supply is elastic. A firm already at full capacity is using all its machines, space and workers; it cannot produce more, at least in the short run, however high the price goes. Quantity supplied does not respond to price at all, so the supply curve is vertical and supply is perfectly inelastic: D. A perfectly elastic curve (C) is horizontal, the opposite case. The trap: A, fairly inelastic, is close, but at full capacity output cannot rise at all, so supply is perfectly, not fairly, inelastic. Memory tip: full capacity, fixed quantity, vertical line.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nClass mark ... 2 ... 5 ... 8 ... 11 ... 14\nFrequency ... 2 ... 3 ... 1 ... 3 ... 1\nThe table shows the wage distribution of workers.\n\nCalculate the mean wage|₦7.40|₦4.00|₦10.00|₦14.80": "For a frequency table, mean = Σfx ÷ Σf.\nStep 1: Multiply each class mark by its frequency: 2 × 2 = 4; 5 × 3 = 15; 8 × 1 = 8; 11 × 3 = 33; 14 × 1 = 14.\nStep 2: Σfx = 4 + 15 + 8 + 33 + 14 = 74.\nStep 3: Σf = 2 + 3 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 10.\nStep 4: Mean = 74 ÷ 10 = ₦7.40: A.\nThe trap: D (₦14.80) results from dividing 74 by 5, the number of class marks, instead of by 10, the number of workers. Memory tip: divide by the total frequency, never by the number of classes.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nClass mark ... 2 ... 5 ... 8 ... 11 ... 14\nFrequency ... 2 ... 3 ... 1 ... 3 ... 1\nThe table shows the wage distribution of workers.\n\nWhat is the median wage?|₦8.00|₦5.00|₦11.00|₦6.50": "The median is the middle value when all the values are arranged in order.\nStep 1: List all 10 wages: 2, 2, 5, 5, 5, 8, 11, 11, 11, 14.\nStep 2: With 10 values the middle lies between the 5th and 6th values ((10 + 1) ÷ 2 = 5.5th position).\nStep 3: The 5th value is 5 and the 6th is 8.\nStep 4: Median = (5 + 8) ÷ 2 = ₦6.50: D.\nThe trap: A (₦8.00) is the middle class mark of the five, the slip of ignoring the frequencies. Memory tip: spread out every value first, then find the middle.",
 "|An economic system in which the producer and the consumer are the same is referred to as a|capitalist economy|mixed economy|subsistence economy|socialist economy": "In a subsistence economy, households produce mainly to meet their own needs rather than to sell: a farming family grows its own yam and cassava, builds its own house and makes its own tools. Since what is produced is consumed by the people who produced it, the producer and the consumer are the same: C. There is little trade, money or specialisation. In capitalist (A), socialist (D) and mixed (B) economies, goods are produced for others, through markets or state planning. Traditional rural communities in Nigeria were largely subsistence before cash crops spread. Memory tip: subsistence = produce to survive, consume what you make.",
 "|A persistent rise in the prices of inputs will lead to|demand-pull inflation|cost-push inflation|stagflation|hyperinflation": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. Cost-push inflation occurs when the costs of production rise, such as wages, raw materials, imported inputs, fuel and electricity tariffs, and firms raise their prices to protect their profits. A persistent rise in input prices is exactly this: B. A rise in Nigerian diesel and petrol prices that makes transport and manufacturing dearer is a familiar example. Demand-pull inflation (A) is caused by too much spending (\"too much money chasing too few goods\"). Stagflation (C) is inflation combined with stagnant output and unemployment, and hyperinflation (D) is extremely rapid inflation. Memory tip: cost-push starts with costs; demand-pull starts with demand.",
 "|An advantage of electronic banking is that it|enables many customers to operate joint accounts|reduces the risk of bank fraud|enables a customer to operate many accounts from different locations|enables a customer to operate his account from different locations": "Electronic banking lets customers carry out transactions through ATMs, internet banking, mobile apps, POS terminals and USSD codes instead of visiting a branch. Its key advantage is convenience: a customer can check his balance, withdraw cash, pay bills and transfer money from his account wherever he is, at any time, from different locations: D. It does not create joint accounts (A), and it has not removed fraud (B); in fact cyber fraud is a risk of e-banking. The trap: C adds \"many accounts\", which is not the defining advantage; the point is operating one's account from anywhere. Nigeria's NIP instant transfer system and mobile money are today's examples. Memory tip: e-banking = your bank in your pocket.",
 "|The non-bank financial institutions that are entrusted with workers' contributions towards retirement are|mortgage institutions|investment companies|pension funds custodians|pension funds administrators": "Nigeria's Pension Reform Act 2004 set up the contributory pension scheme: the worker and the employer each pay a share of the salary into the worker's retirement savings account. Two kinds of non-bank financial institution handle this money. Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs) manage and invest the savings and pay the benefits, and Pension Fund Custodians (PFCs) receive the contributions and keep the pension assets in safe custody. Both are entrusted with workers' contributions, so the answer marked is pension funds administrators: D. Pension funds custodians (C) are also accepted. The National Pension Commission (PenCom) regulates them, and the scheme is now under the Pension Reform Act 2014. Mortgage institutions (A) and investment companies (B) do not hold retirement contributions. Memory tip: the administrator invests, the custodian keeps safe.",
 "|A reduction in the bank rate encourages commercial banks to create money by|decreasing the interest rate|maintaining the interest rate|doubling the interest rate|raising the interest rate": "The bank rate (now called the Monetary Policy Rate, MPR, in Nigeria) is the rate at which the Central Bank lends to commercial banks. When the CBN reduces it, banks can borrow from the CBN more cheaply, so they reduce the interest rates they charge their own customers. Cheaper loans encourage firms and households to borrow; every new loan creates a new deposit, so the money supply expands: A. Raising (D) or doubling (C) the interest rate would discourage borrowing and reduce money creation. This is expansionary monetary policy. Memory tip: bank rate down, lending rate down, borrowing and money supply up.",
 "|Corrections in the GNP figures for price changes require the use of a|retail price index|producer price index|price index|price deflator": "GNP measured at current prices rises both when real output grows and when prices rise. To find real GNP, we remove the effect of price changes by dividing by the GNP deflator (implicit price deflator): real GNP = nominal GNP ÷ deflator × 100. The deflator covers the prices of all goods and services in GNP, including capital goods and exports, so it is the correct tool: D. The retail price index (A) and consumer price index cover only goods bought by households, and the producer price index (B) only goods sold by producers, so they cannot correct the whole of GNP. Memory tip: deflate GNP with the GNP deflator.",
 "|The monetization policy is an offshoot of|PAYE|NEEDS|MDGs|SAP": "In 2003 the Obasanjo government introduced the monetisation policy: instead of giving public servants free houses, cars, drivers, utilities and other fringe benefits, it paid them their cash value. The aim was to cut government waste and reduce the cost of governance. It was part of the public sector reforms gathered into NEEDS, the National Economic Empowerment and Development Strategy launched in 2004: B. SAP (D) was the Structural Adjustment Programme of 1986 under Babangida. PAYE (A) is a method of collecting income tax, and the MDGs (C) were United Nations goals for 2000 to 2015. NEEDS was later followed by Vision 20:2020 and other plans. Memory tip: monetisation and NEEDS are both Obasanjo-era reforms.",
 "|In the circular flow of income model, the outflow variables represent|income|expenditure|injections|leakages": "The circular flow of income shows money flowing from households to firms (spending) and from firms to households (incomes). Some income leaves the flow instead of being spent on domestic output: savings, taxes and imports. These outflows are called leakages or withdrawals: D. Money entering the flow from outside the household-firm circle, namely investment, government spending and exports, is called injections (C). National income is in equilibrium when total leakages equal total injections (S + T + M = I + G + X). The trap: C, injections, are the inflows, the exact opposite of outflows. Memory tip: leakages leak out; injections are injected in.",
 "|Life insurance companies contribute to economic development by holding a part of their assets in|long-term financial instruments|equipment|cash and near money|money-market instruments": "Life insurance companies collect premiums over many years but pay out mainly on death or when policies mature, often decades later. Because their liabilities are long-term, they invest a large part of their assets in long-term financial instruments such as government bonds, company shares and debentures, and mortgages. This provides long-term capital for industry and government projects, promoting economic development: A. Cash and near money (C) and money-market instruments (D) are short-term assets, held only for day-to-day liquidity. Equipment (B) is a physical asset used in their own offices, not an investment channel. Memory tip: long-life policies, long-term investments.",
 "|One way of solving the problem of double counting is by computing national income using the|value-added method|income method|output method|expenditure method": "Double counting happens when the value of intermediate goods is counted more than once: if a farmer sells wheat to a miller for ₦100, the miller sells flour to a baker for ₦150, and the baker sells bread for ₦200, adding all sales gives ₦450, but the true output is only ₦200. The value-added method counts only the value each firm adds: ₦100 + ₦50 + ₦50 = ₦200, which equals the final value: A. The output method (C), if it counts total sales, is where the problem arises; value added (or counting final goods only) is its correction. Memory tip: count only what each stage adds.",
 "|The production possibility curve can be used to explain the underlying concepts of|scale of preference and choice|opportunity cost and choice|wants and means|opportunity cost and scale of preference": "The production possibility curve (PPC) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce when all its resources are fully used. Because resources are scarce, the economy cannot have everything, so it must choose one point on the curve. Moving along the curve to get more of one good means giving up some of the other, and the amount given up is the opportunity cost. So the PPC illustrates scarcity, choice and opportunity cost: B. Scale of preference is a list of an individual's wants in order of importance; it is not shown by the curve. The trap: D pairs opportunity cost with scale of preference, which sounds close but drops choice. Memory tip: the PPC is a \"menu\" of choices, and every move along it has a price called opportunity cost.",
 "|The deregulation of telecommunication services in Nigeria is a characteristic of|command economy|traditional economy|market economy|feudal economy": "In a market (capitalist) economy, private individuals and firms own resources and decide what to produce, guided by prices and profit, with little government control. Deregulation means removing government restrictions so that private firms can enter and compete. When Nigeria deregulated telecommunications (licensing private GSM operators from 2001, with NITEL's monopoly ended), it moved that sector towards the market system: C. A command economy is run by state planning, a traditional economy follows custom, and a feudal economy rests on lords and serfs. Today the sector is run by private operators such as MTN, Airtel and Glo under the Nigerian Communications Commission as regulator. Memory tip: deregulate, privatise, liberalise all point to the Market.",
 "|If the increase in the price of yams is used to estimate the inflation rate, this is an example of|deductive reasoning|inductive reasoning|normative reasoning|positive reasoning": "Economists reason in two ways. Deductive reasoning starts from a general principle and works down to particular conclusions. Inductive reasoning starts from particular observed facts and builds up to a general conclusion. Using the observed rise in the price of one item, yams, to estimate the general rate of inflation is moving from a particular fact to a general statement, so it is inductive reasoning: B. Normative reasoning involves value judgements about what ought to be, and positive reasoning deals with what is, but neither describes the direction of reasoning here. The trap: A (deductive) is its close partner; remember the direction of the reasoning. Memory tip: INductive builds IN from facts to a general rule; DEductive comes DOWN from a rule to facts.",
 "|If the standard deviation of a given data is 8.2, find its variance.|4.1|67.2|82.0|91.8": "Standard deviation and variance both measure how spread out data are. The variance is the average of the squared deviations from the mean, and the standard deviation is its square root. So to get the variance from the standard deviation, square it.\nStep 1: Variance = (standard deviation)².\nStep 2: 8.2 × 8.2 = 67.24.\nStep 3: To one decimal place, 67.2: B.\nHalving 8.2 (4.1) or multiplying it by 10 (82.0) does not give the variance. Memory tip: SD is the root, variance is the square; go from SD to variance by squaring, and back by taking the square root.",
 "|Use the data below to answer the question: \n54, 36, 62, 79, 83, 36, 62, 42, 62 and 42.\n\nCalculate the mean|45.5|55.6|55.8|65.8": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of all the values divided by the number of values.\nStep 1: Add the scores: 54 + 36 + 62 + 79 + 83 + 36 + 62 + 42 + 62 + 42 = 558.\nStep 2: Count the scores: there are 10.\nStep 3: Mean = 558 ÷ 10 = 55.8: C.\nCheck by grouping: 36 × 2 = 72, 42 × 2 = 84, 62 × 3 = 186, plus 54, 79 and 83 = 216; 72 + 84 + 186 + 216 = 558.\nThe trap: B (55.6) differs only in the last digit, so a small slip in adding gives it; always check the total twice. Memory tip: mean = total ÷ number; check the total by grouping repeated values.",
 "|Use the data below to answer the question: \n54, 36, 62, 79, 83, 36, 62, 42, 62 and 42.\n\nWhat is the modal score?|36|42|54|62": "The mode is the value that occurs most often in a set of data. Count each score in 54, 36, 62, 79, 83, 36, 62, 42, 62 and 42:\nStep 1: 36 appears twice; 42 appears twice; 62 appears three times; 54, 79 and 83 appear once each.\nStep 2: The highest frequency is 3, for 62.\nSo the modal score is 62: D. Note that the mode need not be near the mean (55.8) or the median; it simply records the most frequent value. Memory tip: MOde = MOst Often.",
 "|A major determinant of the demand for a luxury good is|the price of the good|the price of other goods|the income of consumers|tastes and fashion": "A luxury good is one people buy more of as they become richer, such as cars, jewellery and expensive phones. Its demand responds strongly to two things. First, its own price: luxuries have elastic demand, so a rise in price sharply cuts the quantity bought (A). Second, the income of consumers: luxuries have high income elasticity, so demand grows fast as incomes rise (C). Both A and C are correct, so either is accepted. The price of other goods and tastes also affect demand, but less centrally. Memory tip: necessities are bought whatever happens; luxuries depend on what they cost and how much you earn.",
 "|If 100 units and 120 units of commodity X are supplied at ₦80 at different times, it means that there is|a change in supply|a change in the quantity supplied|an increase in the quantity supplied|a decrease in the quantity supplied": "A change in the quantity supplied is a movement along one supply curve caused only by a change in the good's own price. A change in supply is a shift of the whole curve caused by other factors, such as costs of production, technology, weather or the number of firms. Here the price stays the same at ₦80, yet 100 units are offered at one time and 120 units at another. Since price has not changed, the difference must come from other factors shifting the curve: a change in supply, A. The trap: B, C and D all speak of \"quantity supplied\", which can change only when price changes. Memory tip: same price, different quantity = the curve has Shifted.",
 "|If income rises from ₦2000 to ₦4000 and quantity demanded increases from 80 units to 120 units, find the income elasticity of demand.|0.5|1.2|2.5|4.0": "Income elasticity of demand = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in income.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 120 − 80 = 40 units; % change = 40/80 × 100 = 50%.\nStep 2: Change in income = ₦4000 − ₦2000 = ₦2000; % change = 2000/2000 × 100 = 100%.\nStep 3: Income elasticity = 50% ÷ 100% = 0.5: A.\nA positive figure below 1 shows a normal good that is a necessity. Always put the quantity change on top; dividing the other way round gives 2, which is wrong. Memory tip: elasticity of demand always has the change in Quantity on top.",
 "|If the demand elasticity coefficient of petrol is 0.5, it implies that the demand for petrol is|elastic|perfectly inelastic|inelastic|perfectly elastic": "Price elasticity of demand measures how much quantity demanded responds to a change in price. A coefficient greater than 1 means elastic demand; exactly 1 is unitary; between 0 and 1 is inelastic; 0 is perfectly inelastic; infinity is perfectly elastic. A coefficient of 0.5 means a 10% rise in price cuts the quantity demanded by only 5%, so demand for petrol is inelastic: C. This fits petrol, a necessity with few substitutes. The trap: B (perfectly inelastic) applies only when the coefficient is exactly zero. Memory tip: less than one, the buyers hardly run; zero means none.",
 "|As long as marginal utility is positive, total utility must be|negative|increasing|zero|decreasing": "Total utility is the whole satisfaction from all units consumed; marginal utility is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. Each additional unit adds its marginal utility to the total. As long as marginal utility is positive, every extra unit adds something, so total utility keeps rising: B. When marginal utility reaches zero, total utility is at its maximum (the satiation point), and if marginal utility becomes negative, total utility starts to fall. Total utility cannot be negative or zero while units are still adding satisfaction. Memory tip: MU positive, TU climbs; MU zero, TU peaks; MU negative, TU falls.",
 "|Diminishing marginal utility implies that|total utility decreases as consumption increases|marginal utility increases as consumption increases|marginal utility decreases as consumption increases|marginal utility decreases as consumption decreases": "The law of diminishing marginal utility states that as a consumer takes more and more units of a commodity in a given period, the extra satisfaction from each additional unit falls. The first bottle of cold water on a hot day gives great satisfaction; the fourth gives much less. So marginal utility decreases as consumption increases: C. Total utility still rises (as long as MU is positive), so A is wrong. The trap: D also says marginal utility decreases, but ties it to consumption decreasing, which reverses the law. Memory tip: more and more, enjoy it less and less per unit.",
 "|The excess benefit derived from the purchase of goods over the amount paid for them is referred to as consumer|rationality|surplus|sovereignty|credit": "Consumer surplus is the difference between the maximum price a consumer is willing to pay for a good and the price he actually pays. If you would pay ₦500 for a meal but it costs ₦300, your consumer surplus is ₦200. It arises because the market price is set by the marginal unit, while earlier units are worth more to the buyer. This \"excess benefit\" is consumer surplus: B. Consumer sovereignty is the power of consumers to decide what is produced through their spending; it is a different idea. Memory tip: Surplus = what you would pay minus what you do pay.",
 "|An upward movement along the same supply curve results in|an increase in supply|a decrease in price|a decrease in quantity supplied|an increase in quantity supplied": "A supply curve slopes upwards: at higher prices, sellers offer more. Moving up along the same curve means the price has risen and sellers respond by offering a larger quantity. This is an increase (extension) in quantity supplied: D. An increase in supply (A) is different: it is a rightward shift of the whole curve caused by factors other than price, such as better technology. A decrease in price would mean a movement down the curve, and that gives a decrease (contraction) in quantity supplied. Memory tip: along the curve = quantity supplied; shift of the curve = supply.",
 "|Use the information below to answer the question: \nGiven that Qd = 20 − 4P and Qs = 6P + 12.\n\nDetermine the equilibrium quantity.|14.2|16.8|20.8|30.2": "Equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nStep 1: Set Qd = Qs: 20 − 4P = 6P + 12.\nStep 2: Collect terms: 20 − 12 = 6P + 4P, so 8 = 10P and P = 0.8.\nStep 3: Substitute: Qd = 20 − 4(0.8) = 20 − 3.2 = 16.8.\nCheck: Qs = 6(0.8) + 12 = 4.8 + 12 = 16.8. So the equilibrium quantity is 16.8: B. Note that 0.8 is the equilibrium price, not the quantity. Memory tip: solve for P first, then substitute P into both equations to check that they give the same Q.",
 "|Use the information below to answer the question: \nGiven that Qd = 20 − 4P and Qs = 6P + 12.\n\nIf price is increased to ₦3, how much is the excess supply?|30|22|12|8": "When price is set above equilibrium, sellers offer more than buyers want, giving excess supply (a surplus).\nStep 1: At P = ₦3, quantity supplied Qs = 6(3) + 12 = 18 + 12 = 30.\nStep 2: Quantity demanded Qd = 20 − 4(3) = 20 − 12 = 8.\nStep 3: Excess supply = Qs − Qd = 30 − 8 = 22: B.\nThe trap: A (30) and D (8) are the quantities supplied and demanded themselves, not the gap between them. Memory tip: excess supply = Qs − Qd; always find both quantities and subtract.",
 "|One of the factors affecting the supply of manufactured goods is|weather|technology|consumer tastes|availability of inputs": "The supply of a good depends on its price and on other factors such as the cost and availability of factors of production, technology, government policy, the prices of related goods and the number of firms. For manufactured goods, better technology (B) lowers unit costs and increases output, shifting supply to the right. The availability of inputs such as raw materials, power and labour (D) is just as direct: a factory that cannot get raw materials cannot produce. Both B and D are correct, so either is accepted. Weather mainly affects farm supply, and consumer tastes affect demand. Memory tip: supply factors are things that change the producer's costs or ability to produce.",
 "|Individual markets differ from each other according to|the degree of competition|the volume of trade|the number of buyers|its composition": "Markets are classified by their structure, and the main feature that separates them is the degree of competition among sellers. Perfect competition has many sellers of identical goods; monopolistic competition has many sellers of slightly different goods; oligopoly has a few large sellers; monopoly has one seller with no close substitutes. So individual markets differ according to the degree of competition: A. The volume of trade or the number of buyers can vary within any structure, so they do not define the type of market. Memory tip: from Perfect competition to Monopoly, count the sellers and you know the market.",
 "|Use the information below to answer the question.\n\nIn the diagram above, there is an excess|demand for 30 units|supply of 30 units|demand for 20 units|supply of 20 units": "When the price is above the equilibrium price, sellers are willing to supply more than buyers want, so there is excess supply.\nStep 1: The horizontal price line cuts the demand curve DD at 20 units: quantity demanded = 20.\nStep 2: It cuts the supply curve SS at 40 units: quantity supplied = 40.\nStep 3: Excess supply = 40 − 20 = 20 units: D.\nThe equilibrium quantity (where DD and SS cross) is 30, at a lower price. The trap: C names the right figure, 20, but calls it excess demand; excess demand happens only when price is below equilibrium. Memory tip: price above equilibrium gives a surplus; price below gives a shortage.",
 "|To protect farmers during a bumper harvest, the government usually|sets a maximum price|releases products from the buffer stock|sells the excess to consumers|sets a minimum price": "A bumper harvest means a very large supply of farm produce, which pushes prices sharply down and cuts farmers' incomes. To protect them, the government sets a minimum (floor or guaranteed) price above the market equilibrium, below which the produce may not be sold, and often buys up the surplus to keep prices up: D. A maximum price (A) protects consumers when prices are too high, not farmers. Releasing products from a buffer stock (B) increases supply and lowers prices further, which is done in times of scarcity. Memory tip: MINimum price protects the producer's MINimum income; MAXimum price protects the buyer.",
 "|The use of modern weaving machines in the production of local fabric will result in|an increase in the demand for labour|a decrease in the demand for labour|an increase in wages|a decrease in wages": "Labour and capital can often be substituted for each other. When producers of local fabric adopt modern weaving machines, each machine does the work of many hand weavers, so the same output needs fewer workers. The demand for labour in fabric weaving therefore falls: B. Wages may later fall as a result, but the direct effect of the machines is on the number of workers wanted, not on the wage. In the long run new jobs may appear in making and repairing machines, but in weaving itself demand for labour declines. Memory tip: machines that replace hands reduce the demand for hands.",
 "|In a perfectly competitive condition, a firm uses 10 units of labour at ₦25 each and 11 units at ₦36 each. What is the marginal cost of labour?|₦396|₦323|₦250|₦146": "Marginal cost of labour is the addition to total labour cost from employing one more unit of labour.\nStep 1: Total cost of 10 units = 10 × ₦25 = ₦250.\nStep 2: Total cost of 11 units = 11 × ₦36 = ₦396.\nStep 3: Marginal cost of the 11th unit = ₦396 − ₦250 = ₦146: D.\nNote that the wage rose for all workers, not just the last one, which is why the marginal cost (₦146) is far above the new wage (₦36). The trap: A (₦396) and C (₦250) are the two total costs, not the change between them. Memory tip: marginal = new total − old total.",
 "|From the graph above, point M shows that MC|cuts AC at its minimum point|and AC rises simultaneously|cuts AC at its maximum point|is falling": "In the short run, average cost (AC) first falls and then rises, giving a U shape. Marginal cost (MC) falls faster and starts rising earlier. Whenever MC is below AC it pulls the average down, and whenever MC is above AC it pushes the average up. So MC must cut AC exactly at the lowest point of the AC curve, which is point M in the diagram: A. The same rule holds for MC and AVC: MC cuts AVC at its minimum point too. The trap: C says \"maximum point\", which reverses the rule. Memory tip: a new test score below your average pulls it down; above it pushes it up; MC meets AC at the bottom of the U.",
 "|The characteristic of entry and exit ensures that firms|earn excess profit|earn normal profit|break-even|expand their operations": "Free entry and exit is a key feature of perfect competition. If firms are earning supernormal (excess) profits, new firms enter, supply increases, price falls and the extra profit disappears. If firms are making losses, some leave, supply falls, price rises and losses disappear. In the long run, therefore, each firm earns only normal profit, the minimum needed to keep it in the industry: B. Breaking even in the accounting sense is not the same as earning normal profit, which is counted as part of cost. Memory tip: free entry kills extra profit; free exit kills losses; what remains is Normal profit.",
 "|Which of the following producers is closest to being a monopolist?|A baker|A wheat farmer|A large chain store|An automobile plant": "A monopolist is a sole seller of a product with no close substitutes, protected by high barriers to entry. Bakers and wheat farmers are many and sell similar products, so they are close to perfect competition. A large chain store competes with other shops and traders. An automobile plant needs enormous capital, advanced technology and a large market, so very few firms can enter, and each car brand has some control over its price. Of these, it is closest to being a monopolist: D. Memory tip: the higher the barriers to entry, the closer a firm is to monopoly.",
 "|The output approach to measuring national income is based on the computation of|final output|factor cost|value added|profits earned": "National income can be measured by the output, income or expenditure method. The output method, also called the value-added method, adds up the value each firm or industry adds to its inputs at each stage of production. Value added = value of output − value of inputs bought from other firms. Adding the value added at every stage gives the value of final goods without counting intermediate goods more than once: C. Counting the full output of every firm would cause double counting. Factor cost and profits relate to the income method. The trap: A (final output) gives the same total, but the method's working is built on value added at each stage. Memory tip: Output method = sum of Value Added.",
 "|An example of injection into the circular flow of income is|taxes|pensions|imports|exports": "In the circular flow, injections are additions to spending that do not come from households' consumption: investment (I), government expenditure (G) and exports (X). Withdrawals (leakages) take money out of the flow: savings (S), taxes (T) and imports (M). Exports bring foreigners' spending into the domestic economy, so they are an injection: D. Imports and taxes are leakages, and pensions are transfer payments rather than spending on output. Memory tip: injections are I, G, X; leakages are S, T, M.",
 "|The theoretical relationship between money supply and prices is weakened by changes in the|money supply|general price level|velocity of money|interest rate": "The quantity theory of money (Irving Fisher) is MV = PT, where M is money supply, V is the velocity of circulation, P is the price level and T is the volume of transactions. The theory predicts that prices rise in proportion to money supply only if V and T stay constant. If people spend money faster or slower, V changes, so a given change in M no longer gives a proportionate change in P; the relationship is weakened: C. Money supply and price level are the two variables being related, not things that weaken the link. Memory tip: MV = PT works neatly only when V and T sit still.",
 "|The high rate of inflation in Nigeria can be attributed to|increasing cost of production|the appreciation of the naira|decreasing cost of production|high capacity utilization": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. In Nigeria a major cause has been cost-push inflation: rising costs of production from fuel price increases, poor power supply forcing firms to run generators, expensive imported raw materials after the naira's fall, high interest rates and poor transport. Firms pass these higher costs on as higher prices: A. An appreciation of the naira would make imports cheaper and reduce inflation, and decreasing costs and high capacity utilisation would also tend to keep prices down. Memory tip: cost-push: costs rise, prices follow.",
 "|If the required reserves of a bank is 20% and ₦10000 is paid into its demand deposit account, what is the excess reserves?|₦2000|₦8000|₦12000|₦18000": "Banks must keep part of each deposit as a required (legal) reserve; whatever they hold above that is excess reserves, which they can lend.\nStep 1: Required reserve = 20% of ₦10000 = 0.2 × ₦10000 = ₦2000.\nStep 2: Excess reserves = deposit − required reserve = ₦10000 − ₦2000 = ₦8000: B.\nThe trap: A (₦2000) is the required reserve itself, not the excess. The excess reserve is the amount the bank can lend, and with a 20% ratio the banking system can create up to 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5 times the deposit. Memory tip: excess reserve = deposit × (1 − reserve ratio).",
 "|Contractionary monetary policy is used to|control inflation|bridge the deflationary gap|expand the output level|deregulate the economy": "Monetary policy is the use of money supply, interest rates and credit by the Central Bank to achieve economic goals. A contractionary (tight) policy reduces money supply and credit: raising the policy rate, selling securities in open market operations, or raising the cash reserve ratio. Less money and dearer credit reduce spending, which brings down inflation: A. Bridging a deflationary gap and expanding output need the opposite, an expansionary policy. Deregulation is not a monetary policy tool. Memory tip: too much money chasing goods? Contract the money to cool the prices.",
 "|The use of government revenue and expenditure to achieve set objectives is known as|budget|fiscal policy|revenue allocation|monetary policy": "Fiscal policy is the government's use of its revenue (mainly taxation) and expenditure to achieve objectives such as full employment, price stability, growth and fair income distribution. For example, cutting taxes and raising spending during a recession boosts demand. The budget (A) is only the annual statement of planned revenue and spending through which fiscal policy is carried out. Monetary policy uses money supply and interest rates, and revenue allocation is the sharing of federal revenue among the tiers of government. So the answer is B. The trap: A (budget) is the tool, not the policy itself. Memory tip: Fiscal = the government's purse (taxing and spending).",
 "|An example of an indirect tax is|profit tax|sales tax|capital gain tax|poll tax": "A direct tax is paid by the person on whom it is levied and cannot be shifted, such as personal income tax, company profit tax, capital gains tax and poll tax. An indirect tax is levied on goods and services, and the seller can pass its burden on to the buyer through a higher price. Examples are sales tax (value added tax in Nigeria today), excise duty and import duty. So sales tax is the indirect tax: B. Memory tip: taxes on what you earn or own are Direct; taxes on what you buy are Indirect.",
 "|A good indicator of economic development is an increase in|gross national product|per capita income|real national income|real per capita income": "Economic development means a rise in the welfare and living standards of the people, not just in total output. Gross national product or real national income can rise simply because the population grows or prices rise. Per capita income divides income by the population, but money figures may only reflect inflation. Real per capita income removes both effects, showing how much the average person can actually buy: D. Fuller measures today such as the Human Development Index add health and education. The trap: B (per capita income) leaves out the effect of inflation. Memory tip: Real removes prices; Per capita removes population.",
 "|Economic growth takes place when there is an increase in|savings over time|investment over time|population over time|output over time": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real output of goods and services over time, usually measured by the growth rate of real GDP. It is a quantitative change, while economic development is a qualitative improvement in living standards. Savings and investment can lead to growth, but they are causes, not growth itself, and a rising population may even lower output per head. So growth takes place when output increases over time: D. Memory tip: Growth = more Goods over time.",
 "|One of the effects of instability in farmers' income in Nigeria is|lack of infrastructural facilities|rural-urban migration|inadequate supply of fertilizer|low level of technology": "Farmers' incomes in Nigeria are unstable because farm output depends on weather, pests and seasons, and prices of farm produce swing widely between bumper harvests and scarcity. When their income is so uncertain, many young people leave the villages for towns and cities where wages are more regular, causing rural-urban migration: B. Lack of infrastructure, inadequate fertilizer and low technology are causes of low and unstable farm income, not its effects. Memory tip: unstable farm income pushes youths from farm to town.",
 "|A major impediment to the development of agriculture in Nigeria is|land use system|inadequate supply of farm inputs|low income of farmers|limited research on seedlings": "The problems of Nigerian agriculture include the land tenure system, inadequate farm inputs, poor finance, low technology, poor storage and transport, and pests and diseases. The land use (land tenure) system is the one usually singled out: land is held by families and communities and divided among heirs into small scattered plots, making large-scale mechanised farming difficult, and farmers without title cannot use land as collateral for loans. The Land Use Act of 1978 vested land in state governments but did not end these problems: A. The other options are also real problems, so the question is somewhat debatable. Memory tip: no secure land, no large farm, no loan.",
 "|A major consideration in locating a cement factory is the availability of|skilled labour|power|infrastructural facilities|limestone": "Industries are located near the factor that would be most costly to transport. Cement making uses large amounts of limestone, which is bulky and heavy, and loses much of its weight in processing. It is cheaper to build the factory next to the limestone deposit than to carry the raw material far. That is why Nigerian cement plants are at Ewekoro and Shagamu (Ogun), Nkalagu (Ebonyi), Sokoto, Ashaka (Gombe) and Obajana (Kogi), all near limestone: D. Power and infrastructure matter, but they can be brought to the site. Memory tip: weight-losing raw material, build beside the raw material.",
 "|The export-promotion strategy is aimed at producing|consumer goods originally imported|machinery for industries|more goods for exports|more goods for domestic consumption": "Developing countries use two main industrialisation strategies. Import substitution produces at home goods that were formerly imported, protected by tariffs. Export promotion encourages industries to produce more goods for export, often through incentives such as export grants, duty drawbacks and export processing zones, to earn foreign exchange and use large world markets: C. Option A (consumer goods originally imported) describes import substitution. The trap: A is the other strategy and is often confused with export promotion. Memory tip: Import Substitution replaces Imports; Export Promotion pushes Exports.",
 "|The activities in the oil and gas industry are classified into|exploration and transportation|upstream and downstream|discovery and extraction|onshore and offshore": "Activities in the oil and gas industry are divided into upstream and downstream: B. The upstream sector covers exploration (searching for oil and gas) and production (drilling and extracting crude). The downstream sector covers refining crude oil into products such as petrol, kerosene and diesel, and their transport, distribution and marketing to consumers. Onshore and offshore describe where oil is found, not how activities are classified. Today Nigeria's Petroleum Industry Act 2021 also names a midstream sector (gas processing, pipelines, storage), with the NUPRC regulating upstream and the NMDPRA regulating midstream and downstream. Memory tip: Up is getting oil out of the ground; Down is getting products to the pump.",
 "|The economic policy of privatization came up as a result of the poor performance of|commercial banks|small-scale businesses|public enterprises|private enterprises": "Privatisation is the sale of all or part of government-owned enterprises to private investors. Nigeria adopted it (under SAP from 1988, then through the Bureau of Public Enterprises from 1999) because public enterprises such as NEPA, NITEL and the refineries performed poorly: they were inefficient, made losses, depended on government subsidies, were overstaffed and suffered from political interference: C. Commercial banks and private enterprises were already privately owned. Today NEPA's successor PHCN has been unbundled and sold to private generation and distribution companies. Memory tip: privatise the Public firms that are failing.",
 "|Joint-stock companies can raise funds from|the money market|the capital market|various sources|government": "A joint-stock company (private or public limited liability company) can raise funds from many sources: issuing ordinary and preference shares, issuing debentures, bank loans and overdrafts, trade credit, hire purchase and leasing, and retained (ploughed-back) profits. So the best answer is various sources: C. The capital market (B) is one important source, especially for public companies that sell shares on the stock exchange, and the money market provides short-term funds, but each is only one of many. Memory tip: shares, debentures, loans, profits: a company has many purses.",
 "|The active population of a country determines the size of its|economic resources|national income|labour force|gross domestic product": "The active (economically active or working) population is the part of the population of working age, usually 15 to 64 years, who are willing and able to work, whether they are employed or looking for work. This group is the labour force of the country: C. The labour force helps to produce national income and GDP, but it does not by itself determine their size, since output also depends on capital, technology and land. Memory tip: active population = labour force = employed + unemployed.",
 "|From the shaded area of the diagram above, an appropriate population policy will be to|encourage early marriages|discourage immigration|discourage polygamy|encourage the use of contraceptives": "The curve shows output per head rising as population grows, reaching a peak at the optimum population, and then falling. To the left of the peak a country is underpopulated: it has too few people to use its resources fully, so more people would raise output per head. To the right it is overpopulated. The shaded area is on the left, rising part of the curve, so the country is underpopulated and should encourage population growth, for example by encouraging early marriages: A. Contraceptives and discouraging polygamy would reduce population, which suits overpopulation. Memory tip: left of the peak, add people; right of the peak, reduce them.",
 "|The population of a country will decrease if|the death rate exceeds the birth rate|immigration is equal to emigration|the birth rate exceeds the death rate|there is rapid economic growth": "Population changes through births, deaths and migration. Natural increase = birth rate − death rate. If the death rate is higher than the birth rate, more people die than are born each year, so the population falls (assuming migration does not make up the difference): A. If immigration equals emigration, migration has no net effect, so the result depends on births and deaths; if births exceed deaths (C), the population grows. Rapid economic growth tends to lower the death rate. Memory tip: population change = births − deaths + immigrants − emigrants.",
 "|The OPEC marketing policy of fixing minimum price allows members to|sell above minimum price|sell below minimum price|determine the price and quantity to sell|negotiate price with customers": "A minimum price is a price floor: the lowest price at which a product may be sold. When the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) fixed a minimum price for its members' crude oil, members could not undercut each other by selling below it, but they were free to sell at any price above it when market conditions allowed: A. Selling below the floor (B) is exactly what the policy forbids. Today OPEC, working with other producers as OPEC+ since 2016, influences prices mainly through production quotas rather than a fixed minimum price. Memory tip: a floor stops you going down, not up.",
 "|A major trading problem facing ECOWAS is|the absence of common currency|political instability|high poverty level|non-implementation of decisions": "ECOWAS (Economic Community of West African States, 1975) aims to promote trade among its members. A major trading problem is the absence of a common currency: each member uses its own currency, and the French-speaking members use the CFA franc, so traders face currency conversion costs, exchange controls and payment problems, and trade within the region is discouraged: A. Political instability and non-implementation of decisions are general problems of the community. Today a single ECOWAS currency, the Eco, has been planned for many years but has not yet been launched. Memory tip: many currencies, many barriers.",
 "|A high rent on land will affect the cost of producing maize|positively|negatively|at the early state of production|at the later stage of production": "Rent is the payment for the use of land, a factor of production. When rent on land is high, the farmer's cost of producing maize rises, which reduces his profit and can discourage him from producing as much. So high rent affects the production of maize negatively (adversely): B. It affects costs throughout production, not just at the early or later stage. Memory tip: higher factor prices hurt the producer.",
 "|If the active population is 50 million and unemployed is 5 million, what is the unemployment rate?|10%|45%|55%|90%": "The unemployment rate is the percentage of the labour force (active population) that is without work but willing and able to work.\nStep 1: Unemployment rate = number unemployed ÷ active population × 100.\nStep 2: = 5 million ÷ 50 million × 100.\nStep 3: = 0.1 × 100 = 10%: A.\nThe trap: D (90%) is the employment rate (45 million employed ÷ 50 million × 100), not the unemployment rate. B (45%) simply repeats the 45 million who have jobs. Memory tip: unemployment rate = jobless ÷ labour force × 100.",
 "|A classroom teacher is promoted to the rank of a principal. This is an example of|vertical mobility|lateral mobility|social mobility|horizontal mobility": "Mobility of labour is the ease with which workers move between jobs or places. Geographical mobility is a change of place. Occupational mobility is a change of job, and it can be vertical or horizontal. Vertical mobility is moving up or down the ranks within the same occupation, as when a classroom teacher is promoted to principal: A. Horizontal (lateral) mobility is moving to a different job at the same level, for example from teaching to working as a bank clerk with similar pay. Social mobility refers to a change in social class generally. Memory tip: vertical goes up the ladder; horizontal moves across to another ladder.",
 "|Musa has a choice of buying a shirt, a book and a bag. What is the opportunity cost of buying a book?|A bag only|A book and a bag|A shirt and a bag|A shirt only": "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. It is always ONE item, the one you would have chosen next, and it is a real thing, not money. Here the three items are read as Musa's scale of preference: shirt first, book second, bag third. Having settled on the book, the item he must now give up is the next one on his list, the bag. So the opportunity cost of buying the book is a bag only: A. Options B and C list two items, which breaks the rule that opportunity cost is the single next best alternative, and B even includes the book he actually bought. Memory tip: opportunity cost = the NEXT best, never \"the rest\".",
 "|A typical feature of a market economy is that|all producers make profit|full employment exists|consumer sovereignty exists|there is equality of economic agents": "A market (capitalist or free-enterprise) economy is one where resources are privately owned and the price mechanism decides what, how and for whom to produce. Firms make what consumers are willing to pay for; goods that buyers reject make losses and disappear. This power of consumers to direct production through their spending is called consumer sovereignty: C. Not all producers make profit (A), since many firms fail; full employment (B) is not guaranteed, and market economies suffer booms and slumps; and wide inequality (D), not equality, is a common criticism of the system. Memory tip: in a market economy the customer is king, so the consumer is sovereign.",
 "|The privatization exercise in Nigeria is a move towards a|mixed economy|subsistence economy|market economy|command economy": "Privatisation is the sale of all or part of government-owned enterprises to private investors. In Nigeria it was guided by the Privatisation and Commercialisation Act of 1988 and the Public Enterprises Act of 1999, with the Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE) in charge; examples include NITEL and the PHCN power companies (now DisCos and GenCos). Moving ownership from the state to private hands increases the role of private enterprise and the price mechanism, so it is a move towards a market economy: C. Nigeria was already a mixed economy (A), so privatisation is a move away from the public side of that mix. Command economy (D) is the opposite direction. Memory tip: Privatise = Push towards the market.",
 "|Use the pie chart below to answer the question: \nThe percentage contribution of the transport sector is|600.0|166.7|60.0|16.7": "In a pie chart the whole circle, 360°, stands for 100% of the total.\nStep 1: Read the sectors: manufacturing 80°, transport 60°, agric 100°, oil 120° (80 + 60 + 100 + 120 = 360°).\nStep 2: Transport's share = 60/360 × 100 = 16.67%, about 16.7%: D.\nThe trap: C (60.0) is the angle itself, not the percentage. A and B are impossible, since no share of a whole can be more than 100%. Memory tip: percentage = angle ÷ 3.6.",
 "|Use the pie chart below to answer the question: \nIf the contribution of the oil sector is 3.6 million, what is the contribution of the other sectors?|10.8 million|7.2 million|3.6 million|2.4 million": "Each sector's value is in proportion to its angle.\nStep 1: Oil has 120°, worth 3.6 million.\nStep 2: Value of 1° = 3.6 million ÷ 120 = 0.03 million.\nStep 3: The other sectors (manufacturing 80° + transport 60° + agric 100°) cover 240°.\nStep 4: Their contribution = 240 × 0.03 million = 7.2 million: B.\nCheck: oil is one third of the circle (120/360), so the total is 10.8 million and the others are 10.8 − 3.6 = 7.2 million.\nThe trap: A (10.8 million) is the total for all four sectors, including oil. Memory tip: find the value of one degree first, then multiply.",
 "|A cumulative frequency graph is|a histogram|an ogive|a bar chart|a pie chart": "A cumulative frequency is a running total of frequencies: each class's frequency is added to all the ones before it. When cumulative frequencies are plotted against the upper class boundaries and the points are joined with a smooth curve, the result is an S-shaped curve called an ogive: B. The ogive is used to read off the median (at half the total frequency) and the quartiles. A histogram (A) shows ordinary frequencies as touching bars, a bar chart (C) uses separate bars, and a pie chart (D) shows parts of a whole as sectors. Memory tip: Ogive = the curve that keeps On Growing (running totals).",
 "|A movement along the same demand curve is caused by the|price of the product|price of other products|income of the consumer|taste of the consumer": "A demand curve is drawn for one good, holding all other influences constant (ceteris paribus). When the price of the good itself changes, buyers simply move to a new point on the same curve: a fall in price gives an extension of demand, a rise gives a contraction. This is a movement along the curve: A. Changes in the prices of other products (B), the consumer's income (C) or tastes (D) change how much is wanted at every price, so the whole curve shifts to the right or left; that is called an increase or decrease in demand. Memory tip: own price Moves you along; anything else Shifts the curve.",
 "|The demand curve for a luxury good is|negatively sloped|parallel to the price axis|parallel to the quantity axis|positively sloped": "The usual demand curve slopes downwards. Nigerian economics texts list exceptional (upward-sloping) demand curves for Giffen goods, expectation of further price rises, ignorance, and goods of ostentation: luxury goods such as expensive jewellery, designer wear or luxury cars, bought for the prestige of owning something costly. For such goods some buyers want more when the price is higher, so the curve slopes upwards (D). But an ordinary luxury that is not bought for show still obeys the law of demand and has a negatively sloped curve (A), so both D and A are accepted. Memory tip: GIVE: Giffen, Ignorance, Veblen (ostentation), Expectation.",
 "|Price elasticity of demand is expressed as|% Δ in price ÷ % Δ in quantity demanded|% Δ in quantity demanded ÷ % Δ in income|% Δ in income ÷ % Δ in quantity demanded|% Δ in quantity demanded ÷ % Δ in price": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) measures how strongly the quantity demanded responds to a change in the good's own price.\nPED = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price: D.\nFor example, if price rises by 10% and quantity demanded falls by 20%, PED = 20 ÷ 10 = 2, so demand is elastic.\nThe trap: A is the same formula turned upside down, a slip students often make. B is income elasticity of demand (quantity over income), and C turns that one upside down. Memory tip: elasticity is always \"Quantity over the cause\": Q on top.",
 "|A major assumption in cardinal utility theory is that|utility is measurable|utility is not measurable|total utility equals marginal utility|total utility is constant": "There are two approaches to consumer behaviour. The cardinal utility approach (Alfred Marshall and others) assumes that satisfaction can be measured in numbers, called utils: a glass of water might give 10 utils, the second 7, and so on. That is how we can talk about total utility, marginal utility and the law of diminishing marginal utility: A. The ordinal approach, using indifference curves, assumes only that a consumer can rank combinations, so utility is not measurable in numbers (B). Total utility equals marginal utility (C) only for the first unit, and total utility is not constant (D); it changes as more is consumed. Memory tip: Cardinal = Counting utils; Ordinal = Ordering choices.",
 "|A supply curve is positively sloped because|supply always exceeds demand|demand always exceeds supply|price is an incentive to consumers|price is an incentive to producers": "The law of supply says that, other things equal, more is supplied at higher prices and less at lower prices, so the supply curve slopes upwards from left to right. The reason is that a higher price makes production more profitable: existing firms expand output and new producers join, and higher prices also cover the rising costs of producing extra units. In short, price is an incentive to producers: D. Price is a deterrent to consumers, not an incentive (C), and supply and demand are not always unequal (A, B); they meet at equilibrium. Memory tip: higher price, higher profit, higher supply.",
 "|The short-run supply curve for medical doctors is more likely to be|perfectly elastic|perfectly inelastic|fairly inelastic|fairly elastic": "Elasticity of supply depends heavily on time. Producing a doctor requires about six years of medical school plus housemanship, so however high doctors' pay rises today, the number of qualified doctors cannot increase in the short run. The quantity supplied is fixed, giving a vertical supply curve: perfectly inelastic, B. Over the long run, higher pay attracts more students into medicine and the supply becomes more elastic. The trap: C (fairly inelastic) is close, but for the short run the textbook answer treats the stock of doctors as fixed. Memory tip: skills that take years to build have fixed supply today.",
 "|If the price of an item changes by 8% and quantity supplied changes from 600 units to 660 units, the price elasticity of supply is|0.80|1.25|2.00|10.00": "Price elasticity of supply = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 660 − 600 = 60 units.\nStep 2: Percentage change = 60/600 × 100 = 10%.\nStep 3: Elasticity = 10% ÷ 8% = 1.25: B.\nSince 1.25 is greater than 1, supply is elastic.\nThe trap: A (0.80) comes from dividing the wrong way, 8 ÷ 10. Memory tip: the quantity change always goes on top.",
 "|In a regulated market, price is determined by|consumers|producers|auction|government": "In a free market, price is set by the interaction of demand (consumers) and supply (producers). In a regulated or controlled market, the government steps in and fixes prices by law, either a maximum price (price ceiling), as with petrol pump prices in Nigeria for many years, or a minimum price (price floor), such as a guaranteed price for farm produce or a minimum wage. So price is determined by government: D. Auctions (C) are a way of selling where buyers bid against each other, a feature of free markets. Memory tip: Regulated = Rules from government.",
 "|A decrease in supply without a corresponding change in demand will lead to|an increase in equilibrium price and a decrease in equilibrium quantity|a decrease in equilibrium price and an increase in equilibrium quantity|a decrease in equilibrium price and equilibrium quantity|an increase in equilibrium price and quantity": "Draw the usual demand and supply diagram. A decrease in supply (caused, for example, by a poor harvest or higher costs) shifts the supply curve to the left. With demand unchanged, the new supply curve cuts the same demand curve higher up and further to the left. At the old price there is now a shortage, so price is bid up, and as price rises buyers cut back. The result: equilibrium price rises and equilibrium quantity falls: A. A rise in both price and quantity (D) is what an increase in demand causes. Memory tip: supply down = price up, quantity down (they move in opposite directions).",
 "|Production takes place when|output is transformed into input|machines replace human effort|input is transformed into output|there is specialization and division of labour": "Production is the creation of goods and services to satisfy human wants. It happens when inputs, the factors of production (land, labour, capital and entrepreneur), are combined and transformed into output: C. A farmer turning seeds, land and labour into maize, a baker turning flour into bread, and a teacher turning time and knowledge into lessons are all producing. Option A turns the process backwards. Machines replacing labour (B) is mechanisation, and specialisation (D) is one way of organising production, not its definition. Production is complete only when the good reaches the final consumer. Memory tip: production = Inputs In, Outputs Out.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nUse of capital ... Total output (kg) ... AP ... MP\n1 ... 16 ... 16 ... -\n2 ... 64 ... 32 ... 48\n3 ... 126 ... 42 ... 62\n4 ... 212 ... 53 ... 86\n5 ... 425 ... 85 ... 213\n6 ... 684 ... 114 ... 259\n\nThe marginal product of the 5th unit of capital is|42|85|114|213": "Marginal product (MP) is the extra output from one more unit of a variable factor.\nStep 1: Total output with 4 units of capital = 212 kg.\nStep 2: Total output with 5 units = 425 kg.\nStep 3: MP of the 5th unit = 425 − 212 = 213 kg: D.\nThe MP column in the table confirms this.\nThe trap: B (85) is the average product at 5 units (425 ÷ 5), sitting in the next column. Memory tip: Marginal = the extra; Average = the total shared out.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nUse of capital ... Total output (kg) ... AP ... MP\n1 ... 16 ... 16 ... -\n2 ... 64 ... 32 ... 48\n3 ... 126 ... 42 ... 62\n4 ... 212 ... 53 ... 86\n5 ... 425 ... 85 ... 213\n6 ... 684 ... 114 ... 259\n\nDetermine the average product of the 4th unit of capital.|53|86|212|213": "Average product (AP) is total output divided by the number of units of the variable factor.\nStep 1: With 4 units of capital, total output = 212 kg.\nStep 2: AP = 212 ÷ 4 = 53 kg: A.\nThe AP column shows 53 for the 4th unit.\nThe trap: B (86) is the marginal product of the 4th unit (212 − 126), and C (212) is the total output, both in the same row. Memory tip: AP = TP ÷ units; MP = change in TP.",
 "|Which of the following cannot be deduced from the law of variable proportions when total output is rising?|MP is greater than AP|MP is less than AP|MP is equal to AP|MP is equal to zero": "The law of variable proportions (diminishing returns) traces three stages as more of a variable factor is added to a fixed one. While total product rises, marginal product is positive. At first MP is above AP and pulls AP up; where MP = AP, average product is at its maximum; after that MP is below AP and pulls AP down, though TP still rises as long as MP is positive. So A, B and C can all happen while TP is rising. MP = 0 happens only when total product reaches its maximum and stops rising, so it cannot be true while output is still rising: D. Memory tip: TP climbs while MP is positive; TP peaks when MP hits zero.",
 "|As the level of output increases, the average fixed cost of a firm will|continue to increase|remain unchanged|continue to decrease|be equal to the total cost": "Fixed costs, such as rent and the salary of a manager, do not change with output. Average fixed cost is total fixed cost divided by output. If fixed cost is ₦1,000, AFC is ₦1,000 at 1 unit, ₦100 at 10 units and ₦10 at 100 units. As output rises, the same fixed cost is spread over more units, so AFC keeps falling: C. It approaches zero but never reaches it, which is why the AFC curve is a rectangular hyperbola. It cannot rise (A) or stay the same (B), and it is only one part of total cost, so it cannot equal total cost (D) while there are variable costs. Memory tip: spread the fixed cost thinner and thinner.",
 "|The shape of the long-run average cost curve is best explained by the|law of diminishing returns|law of returns to scale|cost of fixed inputs|cost of variable inputs": "In the short run at least one factor is fixed, and the U-shape of the short-run average cost curve comes from the law of diminishing returns (A). In the long run all factors can be varied, so the firm can change its whole scale of operation. As it grows it first enjoys increasing returns to scale (economies of scale, so LRAC falls), then constant returns, and finally decreasing returns (diseconomies of scale, so LRAC rises). This explains the U-shape of the long-run average cost curve: B. The trap: A (law of diminishing returns) explains the short-run curve, not the long-run one. Memory tip: Short run, diminishing returns; Long run, returns to scale.",
 "|The supply curve of a perfectly competitive firm is identical to its|total cost|marginal cost|fixed cost|variable cost": "A perfectly competitive firm is a price taker: it sells at the market price, so price = marginal revenue. It maximises profit where MC = MR = price. So at each price the firm produces the output at which marginal cost equals that price, which means its marginal cost curve tells us how much it will supply at each price. The firm's supply curve is therefore its marginal cost curve above the minimum of average variable cost (below that point it shuts down): B. Total, fixed and variable costs (A, C, D) do not show this price–quantity link. Memory tip: in perfect competition, Supply = MC (above AVC).",
 "|If a monopolist is incurring short-run losses, this means that his|selling price is above the short-run average cost|selling price is below the short-run average cost|average revenue is greater than marginal revenue|average revenue is less than marginal revenue": "A firm makes a loss when its total revenue is less than its total cost, that is, when price (average revenue) is below average cost per unit. A monopolist can make short-run losses if demand is weak, for example a new railway service with few passengers. It produces where MR = MC, but if the price it can charge at that output is below short-run average cost, every unit sold loses money: B. Option A describes a profit. Option C is true of every monopolist because its demand curve slopes downwards, so AR is always above MR, profitable or not; and D is never true. Memory tip: loss means Price below Average cost (P < AC).",
 "|In the long run, one of the characteristics of monopolistic competitive firms is that they|make abnormal profits|suffer losses|make normal profits|collude with each other": "Monopolistic competition has many firms selling differentiated products (brands of soap, hairdressing salons, restaurants) with free entry and exit. In the short run a firm can earn abnormal profit. But this attracts new firms with similar products, which take away customers and shift each firm's demand curve to the left until price just equals average cost. In the long run, therefore, firms earn only normal profit: C. Abnormal profit (A) is possible in the long run only under monopoly, where entry is blocked. Losses (B) drive firms out, and collusion (D) is a feature of oligopoly. Memory tip: free entry kills abnormal profit in the long run.",
 "|In developing countries, it is easier to obtain accurate national income estimates through the|GNP|GDP|NDP|NNP": "GDP (gross domestic product) is the value of all goods and services produced within a country's borders in a year. To get GNP you must add net factor income from abroad, and to get NDP or NNP you must subtract depreciation (capital consumption). In developing countries, records of income earned abroad and of capital depreciation are poor, so these adjustments are hard to estimate accurately. GDP needs neither adjustment, so it is the easiest measure to obtain reliably: B. Nigeria's GDP was rebased in 2014 (from a 1990 to a 2010 base year), which raised its measured size sharply. Memory tip: Domestic = done at home, no extra adjustments.",
 "|Double counting is a problem in computing national income when using the|expenditure method|income method|output method|value added method": "There are three main methods of measuring national income: output (product), income and expenditure. In the output method, the values of output of all firms are added up. Double counting happens when the value of the same item is counted more than once: for example, counting the value of cotton, then the cloth made from it, then the shirt made from the cloth. To avoid this, only the value added at each stage, or the value of final goods, should be counted. Because it adds outputs of firms, the output method is the one most at risk: C. The value added method (D) is the remedy for double counting, not its source. Memory tip: Output method, Over-counting danger.",
 "|In the circular flow of income, an increase in savings causes|an increase in imports|a decrease in the income stream|an increase in household consumption|a decrease in export": "In the circular flow, households earn incomes from firms and spend them on firms' output. Some money leaks out of this flow through savings, taxes and imports (withdrawals), and some is injected through investment, government spending and exports. When households save more, they spend less on goods and services, so firms receive less revenue, produce less and pay out less income. The income stream shrinks: B. Saving reduces household consumption, so C is the opposite of the truth. Unless the extra saving is borrowed and invested by firms, national income falls (the paradox of thrift). Memory tip: savings, taxes and imports Leak out; investment, government spending and exports Inject.",
 "|The ability of commercial banks to create money depends on the|reserve ratio|liquidity ratio|interest rate|capital base": "Commercial banks create money by lending out deposits that are not needed for daily withdrawals, and the loans come back as new deposits. The central bank requires them to keep a fraction of deposits as cash reserves: the reserve ratio. The credit multiplier is 1 ÷ reserve ratio. With a 10% ratio, a new deposit of ₦100,000 can support up to ₦100,000 × 10 = ₦1,000,000 of total deposits. A higher reserve ratio cuts the multiplier and the money banks can create: A. The liquidity ratio (B) also limits lending, but the money-creation formula uses the cash reserve ratio. The trap: B (liquidity ratio) is a close relative of the reserve ratio and also restrains lending. Memory tip: money created = new deposit ÷ reserve ratio.",
 "|The reform in the banking sector in Nigeria is principally motivated by the need to|increase the capital base of banks|provide more money to run the economy|minimise the rate of bank failures|enhance efficiency in bank operations": "In July 2004, CBN Governor Charles Soludo announced the banking consolidation reform: every bank had to raise its minimum capital from ₦2 billion to ₦25 billion by December 2005, and the number of banks fell from 89 to 25 through mergers and acquisitions. Raising capital was the instrument; the aim was a strong, sound and efficient banking system able to fund large projects, compete internationally and protect depositors (D). Raising the capital base (A) was the centrepiece of the reform, so both D and A are accepted. In 2009 the CBN under Sanusi Lamido Sanusi carried out further reforms after a banking crisis. Memory tip: capital was the tool, efficiency the goal.",
 "|A manufacturer who wants to build a new plant will source funds from the|commercial banks|money market|capital market|government": "Firms need two kinds of finance. Short-term funds for working capital (stock, wages, bills) come from the money market: commercial bank overdrafts and short loans, treasury bills and commercial papers. Long-term funds for fixed capital, such as building a new factory, come from the capital market: issuing shares and debentures on the stock exchange, and long-term loans from development banks and insurance companies. A new plant is a long-term investment, so the manufacturer should go to the capital market: C. Commercial banks (A) and the money market (B) mainly provide short-term credit. Nigeria's capital market is regulated by the SEC, and the Nigerian Stock Exchange is now the Nigerian Exchange (NGX). Memory tip: long-term project, capital market; short-term need, money market.",
 "|Cost-push inflation occurs when|production cost is high|factor prices decrease|there is too much money in circulation|government embarks on deficit financing": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. Demand-pull inflation is caused by too much money chasing too few goods: excess demand from easy credit, a large money supply or heavy government deficit spending. Cost-push inflation is caused by rising costs of production, such as higher wages, fuel and transport costs, imported raw materials (especially after a fall in the naira) or higher taxes. Firms pass these costs on as higher prices: A. Options C and D are causes of demand-pull inflation, and a fall in factor prices (B) would lower costs and prices. Memory tip: Cost-push = costs Push prices up; demand-Pull = buyers Pull prices up.",
 "|If government expenditure exceeds revenue, this results in|balanced budget|national debt|budget deficit|budget surplus": "A government budget compares planned revenue with planned expenditure for a year. If expenditure is greater than revenue, the budget is in deficit: C. If revenue exceeds expenditure, it is a surplus (D); if they are equal, it is balanced (A). A deficit can be financed by borrowing at home or abroad or by drawing on reserves. Borrowing to cover deficits adds to the national debt (B), but the debt is the accumulated total of past borrowing, not the name for this year's gap. Governments run deficits deliberately in a recession to boost demand. Memory tip: spend more than you earn = Deficit; earn more than you spend = Surplus.",
 "|The Basic NEEDS Approach to development is directed at|poverty alleviation|provision of educational infrastructure|provision of health services|improvement in workers' income": "The Basic Needs Approach to development, promoted by the International Labour Organisation (ILO) in 1976, says development should be judged by whether everyone has the essentials of life: food, shelter, clothing, clean water, health care and basic education, not just by growth in GDP. Its aim is to lift people out of absolute poverty, so it is directed at poverty alleviation: A. Educational infrastructure (B), health services (C) and better incomes (D) are parts or means of the approach, but each is narrower than the overall goal. Memory tip: Basic Needs = Beat poverty first.",
 "|The major reason why countries strive to achieve optimum growth is to|be self-sufficient|raise general living standards|raise the level of production|reduce aggregate expenditure": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real output (real GDP) over time. Countries pursue growth not because output is valuable in itself, but because more output means more goods and services per person: better food, housing, health care, education and leisure. So the main reason for seeking optimum growth is to raise the general standard of living: B. Raising production (C) is what growth means, not why it is wanted, and self-sufficiency (A) is a narrower aim. Reducing aggregate expenditure (D) would slow growth. Memory tip: growth is the means; better living is the end.",
 "|According to the theory of comparative advantage, specialization will result in|labour-intensive method of production|capital-intensive method of production|efficient allocation of resources|efficient distribution of output": "The theory of comparative advantage, developed by David Ricardo (1817), says that a country should specialise in producing the goods it can make at the lowest opportunity cost, and trade for the rest, even if another country is better at producing everything. When each country does this, resources flow to the uses where they are relatively most productive, so world output rises and both countries can consume more. The result is an efficient allocation of resources: C. Specialisation can lead to labour- or capital-intensive methods (A, B) depending on what each country has, and it is about efficiency in production rather than distribution (D). Memory tip: comparative advantage = lowest opportunity cost.",
 "|Dumping in international trade means selling a good at a|higher price at home than abroad|lower price at home than abroad|price that equates marginal cost with marginal revenue|price above marginal cost abroad": "Dumping is when a firm or country sells a product in a foreign market at a price lower than it charges in its home market, often below cost of production, to win market share or get rid of surplus output. Saying the good sells at a higher price at home than abroad means exactly the same thing: A. Dumping can hurt local industries in the importing country; for example, cheap imported textiles and poultry have been blamed for job losses in Nigeria. Countries may respond with anti-dumping duties, which are allowed under WTO rules. The trap: B turns the price relationship the wrong way round. Memory tip: dumping = cheap abroad, dear at home.",
 "|Guided deregulation as currently practised in Nigeria implies that|market forces determine interest and exchange rates|government alone determines interest and exchange rates|market forces and government determine interest and exchange rates|exchange rate is regulated while interest rate is fixed": "Under the Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) of 1986, Nigeria deregulated interest and exchange rates, letting market forces set them. Full deregulation led to very high and unstable rates, so in the 1990s and 2000s the CBN adopted guided deregulation: rates are largely market-determined, but the CBN intervenes through its monetary policy rate and foreign exchange sales to keep them within acceptable bands. So both market forces and government determine interest and exchange rates: C. Option A describes full deregulation and B describes full regulation. Today the CBN still guides rates through the monetary policy rate and the official foreign exchange market. Memory tip: guided = market leads, government steers.",
 "|An important function of the World Bank is to|provide short-term loans to members|encourage trade between members|promote capital-intensive production|provide long-term loans to members": "The World Bank (International Bank for Reconstruction and Development, IBRD) was set up at the Bretton Woods conference in 1944, along with the IMF. Its main function is to give long-term loans, with guarantees and technical help, to member countries for development projects such as roads, dams, power, education and agriculture: D. Its sister body, the International Development Association (IDA), gives very cheap loans to the poorest countries. Short-term loans for balance-of-payments problems (A) are the work of the IMF, and encouraging trade (B) is the work of the WTO. The trap: A describes the IMF, the World Bank's twin from Bretton Woods. Memory tip: World Bank = long-term development; IMF = short-term balance of payments.",
 "|The major problem confronting a sole proprietor is|high level of risk|limited expertise|limited source of capital|high taxation": "A sole proprietorship is owned and controlled by one person, such as a provision store, tailoring shop or barber's salon. Its capital comes mainly from the owner's own savings, help from family and friends, and small loans, because banks see one-person businesses as risky. This shortage of funds is the main reason most sole proprietorships remain small: C. The owner also bears unlimited liability and may lack some skills (B), but limited capital is the problem that holds back growth most. Taxation (D) is usually low for such businesses. Memory tip: one owner, one pocket, so limited capital.",
 "|A public liability company is run on a daily basis by|the chief executive|the shareholders|the board of directors|financial consultants": "A public limited (liability) company is owned by its shareholders, who elect a board of directors at the annual general meeting. The board sets broad policy and meets from time to time; it appoints a chief executive (managing director) and other managers. The chief executive is in charge of the day-to-day running of the company, carrying out the board's decisions, so the company is run on a daily basis by the chief executive: A. The board of directors (C) is responsible for the overall management and is often given as the answer, but \"on a daily basis\" points to the chief executive. Shareholders (B) own the company but do not run it. Memory tip: Owners own, the Board sets policy, the CEO runs it daily.",
 "|The marketing of agricultural products in Nigeria can be improved by|a massive production of farm produce|re-establishing the marketing boards|establishing more agricultural banks|processing of farm produce": "Agricultural marketing covers everything from the farm gate to the consumer: buying, grading, storing, processing, transporting and selling. Two of the options improve it. Marketing boards (set up from 1947, abolished in 1986 under SAP) bought produce at announced prices, graded and stored it and found buyers, so re-establishing them is a standard suggestion: B. Processing farm produce, for example turning cassava into garri or tomatoes into paste, stops spoilage, eases storage and transport and fetches higher prices, so D is also correct. Massive production (A) without better marketing can cause gluts and low prices, and agricultural banks (C) help production finance. Memory tip: better marketing = organise it (boards) and preserve it (processing).",
 "|To enhance the competitiveness of domestic agricultural products, there is need to|ban the importation of agricultural products|increase the exportation of agricultural products|improve the quality of domestic agricultural products|increase the output of domestic agricultural products": "Competitiveness means the ability of local products to win buyers against imports at home and against other countries' goods in export markets. Buyers compare quality and price, so improving the quality of domestic agricultural products, through better seeds, grading, storage and processing, makes them more attractive and able to compete: C. Banning imports (A) only removes competition and can make local producers less efficient. Increasing exports (B) is a result of competitiveness, not a way of creating it, and higher output (D) alone does not make products better. Memory tip: to compete, improve quality.",
 "|In Nigeria, the location of a steel industry at Ajaokuta is due to|the availability of transport network|the proximity to source of power|the proximity to large deposits of iron ore|political consideration": "Heavy industries that use bulky, weight-losing raw materials, like steel, are usually located near the raw material to cut transport costs. The Ajaokuta Steel Company in Kogi State was sited close to the large iron ore deposits at Itakpe (and limestone nearby), with river transport on the Niger as an added advantage: C. Ajaokuta began construction in 1979 but has never been fully completed, and its revival remains a government priority. Political considerations (D) have also been mentioned, but the textbook reason is the raw material. Memory tip: bulky raw material, so build near the mine.",
 "|Industries for consumer goods are concentrated in urban centres as a result of|large market|power supply|government policy|weather conditions": "Industries that produce consumer goods, such as bread, soft drinks, furniture, soap and plastics, sell directly to households. They tend to locate where there are many buyers close together, in big towns and cities like Lagos, Kano, Onitsha and Aba, to reduce distribution costs and keep close to their customers. So the large market in urban centres attracts them: A. Power supply (B) and government policy (C) can also influence location, but they are not the main reason consumer-goods industries cluster in towns. Weather (D) matters more to agriculture. Memory tip: consumer goods follow the consumers.",
 "|The current industrial development strategy in Nigeria is aimed at encouraging|small-scale industries|medium-scale industries|small- and medium-scale industries|medium and large scale industries": "Since the 1990s, Nigeria's industrial policy has focused on small- and medium-scale enterprises (SMEs), because they need less capital, use local raw materials, create many jobs, spread industry to rural areas and train entrepreneurs. Bodies created to support them include the Small and Medium Enterprises Development Agency of Nigeria (SMEDAN, 2003) and the banks' Small and Medium Enterprises Equity Investment Scheme (SMEEIS, 2001), and today there are CBN and Bank of Industry SME funds: C. The trap: A (small-scale industries) is only half of the policy; medium-scale firms are included. Memory tip: SME = Small AND Medium Enterprises.",
 "|Activities in the oil and gas industry are classified into|oil exploration and distribution|refining and marketing of finished products|upstream and downstream|exploration and drilling": "The oil and gas industry is divided into two broad sectors. The upstream sector covers exploration (searching for oil and gas), drilling and production (getting crude oil out of the ground). The downstream sector covers refining crude oil into petrol, diesel and kerosene, transporting and distributing these products, and marketing them at filling stations: C. Options A, B and D each name only some of these activities. The Petroleum Industry Act of 2021 kept this division, creating the NUPRC to regulate upstream and the NMDPRA for midstream and downstream. Memory tip: Upstream = Under the ground; Downstream = Delivered to the customer.",
 "|High dependency ratio is influenced by|high infant mortality rate|the level of income|high birth rate|inadequate medical care for children": "The dependency ratio compares the dependent population (children under 15 and the elderly over 64) with the working-age population (15 to 64). A high birth rate adds many young children who must be fed, clothed and educated by the working population, so the ratio rises: C. Nigeria, with its high birth rate and young population, has a high dependency ratio. High infant mortality (A) and poor medical care for children (D) reduce the number of surviving children, so on their own they do not raise the ratio, and income (B) is only an indirect influence. Memory tip: more births, more mouths to feed.",
 "|Occupational distribution of population determines the|size of population available and willing to work|population density of a place|dependency ratio of a country|size and categories of the labour force": "The occupational distribution of population shows how the working population is shared among different occupations or sectors: primary (farming, fishing, mining), secondary (manufacturing, construction) and tertiary (trade, transport, banking, teaching). It therefore tells us the size of the labour force in each category: D. In Nigeria a large share of workers is in agriculture, which is typical of developing countries. Population density (B) comes from geographical distribution, the dependency ratio (C) from age distribution, and A is simply the definition of the labour force. Memory tip: occupational = who does what work.",
 "|The mobility of labour is mainly determined by|the age of workers|government policy|trade unions|wage rate differentials": "Mobility of labour is the ease with which workers move from one job to another (occupational mobility) or from one place to another (geographical mobility). Workers move mainly in search of higher pay and better conditions, so differences in wage rates between jobs and places are the main force: D. That is why many workers leave rural areas for cities like Lagos and Abuja. Age (A), government policy (B) and trade unions (C) can help or hinder movement, but they are not the main driving force. Memory tip: labour follows the money.",
 "|When workers have a union, the supply of labour is said to be|monopolistic|oligopolistic|monopsonistic|competitive": "A trade union is an association of workers formed to protect their interests, mainly by bargaining collectively over wages and conditions. When workers join a union, they no longer sell their labour one by one; the union acts as a single seller of their labour and can even withdraw it all through a strike. One seller of a product means monopoly, so the supply of labour is monopolistic: A. The trap: C (monopsonistic) means a single BUYER, such as one big employer in a town, the exact opposite of a union. Competitive supply (D) is when many individual workers sell separately. Memory tip: Monopoly sells, Monopsony buys.",
 "|Economics is the study of human behaviour as it relates to the|efficient allocation of resources|production of goods|operation of companies|generation of income": "Economics exists because of scarcity: human wants are unlimited, but the resources to satisfy them (land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship) are limited and have alternative uses. Lionel Robbins defined economics as the science that studies human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means which have alternative uses. In other words, it is about choosing how to allocate scarce resources efficiently: A. Production of goods, the operation of companies and the generation of income are parts of economic activity, but each is too narrow to define the whole subject. Memory tip: Scarcity forces Choice, and choice is about Allocation.",
 "|The downturn in the prices of shares on stock markets is a highlight of|efficient allocation of resources|the invisible hand|the regulatory nature of the market|consumer rationality": "Adam Smith, the father of economics, wrote in \"The Wealth of Nations\" (1776) that free markets are guided by an \"invisible hand\": the self-interested buying and selling of millions of people sets prices without anyone controlling them. Share prices on a stock exchange work the same way. When many investors want to sell and few want to buy, prices fall; a downturn in share prices is simply the price mechanism at work: B. The regulatory nature of the market (C) refers to rules set by bodies such as the Securities and Exchange Commission, which do not set share prices. Memory tip: free market prices move by the invisible hand of demand and supply.",
 "|If x̄ = 6 and N = 6, determine the value of Σfx.|36|12|1|72": "The mean of a frequency distribution is x̄ = Σfx ÷ N, where N = Σf is the total frequency.\nStep 1: Rearrange the formula: Σfx = x̄ × N.\nStep 2: Substitute: Σfx = 6 × 6 = 36: A.\nThe trap: B (12) is what you get by adding 6 + 6 instead of multiplying, and C (1) comes from dividing 6 by 6; the formula needs a product. Memory tip: mean × number = total.",
 "|The standard deviation of a set of data is|always measured from the mode|the most representative of averages|always measured from the median|a measure of dispersion": "Statistics has two families of measures. Measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode) give a typical or average value. Measures of dispersion (range, mean deviation, variance, standard deviation) show how spread out the values are. Standard deviation is the square root of the average of the squared deviations from the mean, so it tells you how far values typically lie from the mean: D. It is measured from the mean, not from the mode or median (A, C), and it is not an average itself (B). A small standard deviation means the values are close together. Memory tip: Standard Deviation = Spread of Data.",
 "|The mean is the best measure of central tendency because it|is not affected by extreme values in a data|is a midpoint value in an array of data|is a balancing point in an observation|can be calculated from incomplete data": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of the values divided by their number. Its special property is that the deviations of the values above the mean exactly cancel the deviations below it: Σ(x − x̄) = 0. That makes it the balancing point of the data, like the pivot of a see-saw, and it uses every value: C. A is false: the mean is the average most affected by extreme values. B describes the median, the middle value in an ordered array. D is false: you need all the values to compute a mean. Memory tip: the mean is the see-saw's pivot; the median is the middle seat.",
 "|The most popular sizes of dresses and shoes are determined by the|range|mean|mode|median": "The mode is the value that occurs most often in a set of data. A shoe or dress maker wants to know which size customers buy most, so that it can produce more of that size. The most popular size is the most frequent one, the mode: C. The mean size (B) may not even be a size that exists, and the median (D) is just the middle size when sizes are arranged in order. The range (A) only measures spread. The mode is the only average that also works for non-numerical data such as colours. Memory tip: MOde = MOst Often, MOst pOpular.",
 "|If the demand for a good is more elastic than its supply, the tax burden is borne|equally by consumers and producers|more by producers|more by consumers|more by retailers and producers": "When a tax is placed on a good, who really pays it (the incidence) depends on elasticities. The side of the market that can adjust more easily escapes more of the tax. If demand is more elastic than supply, buyers respond to a higher price by buying much less, so sellers cannot pass much of the tax on and must absorb most of it: B. If demand were less elastic than supply, consumers would bear more (C). The burden is shared equally (A) only when the two elasticities are equal. Memory tip: the less elastic side carries the heavier load.",
 "|If the price of a commodity with elastic demand increases, the revenue accruing to the producer will|double|increase|be constant|decrease": "Total revenue = price × quantity sold. Elastic demand means the percentage change in quantity demanded is larger than the percentage change in price. So when price rises, the fall in quantity more than outweighs the higher price, and total revenue falls: D. For example, if price rises 10% and quantity falls 20%, revenue drops. With inelastic demand the opposite happens, which is why sellers of necessities can raise revenue by raising prices. Memory tip: Elastic demand: price and revenue move in opposite directions.",
 "|An excess demand for beans will result from|an increase in the price of beans|a decrease in the price of beans|an increase in the supply of beans|a decrease in the supply of beans": "Excess demand (a shortage) occurs when quantity demanded is greater than quantity supplied at the ruling price. This happens when the price is below the equilibrium price: at a lower price more people want beans, while farmers are willing to sell fewer. So a decrease in the price of beans below equilibrium creates excess demand: B. An increase in price (A) creates excess supply (a surplus), as does an increase in supply (C). Price ceilings set below equilibrium are the classic cause of shortages and queues. Memory tip: price too low, shortage; price too high, surplus.",
 "|Consumer surplus tends to be higher when demand is|inelastic|perfectly elastic|elastic|unitary elastic": "Consumer surplus is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay for a good and what they actually pay, the area under the demand curve and above the price. When demand is inelastic, the demand curve is steep: consumers value the good highly and would pay much more than the market price rather than go without it, as with salt or medicine. The gap between willingness to pay and price is therefore large: A. When demand is perfectly elastic (B), the curve is horizontal at the price, so there is no consumer surplus at all. Memory tip: the more you need it, the bigger your surplus at a given price.",
 "|One of the assumptions of ordinal utility theory is that|choice is not consistent|utility can be ranked|total utility is a function of price|satisfaction is measurable": "There are two approaches to utility. Cardinal utility (Marshall) assumes satisfaction can be measured in units called utils. Ordinal utility (Hicks and Allen, the indifference curve approach) assumes only that the consumer can rank combinations of goods in order of preference: first, second, third, without measuring how much more. So \"utility can be ranked\" is the ordinal assumption: B. D (satisfaction is measurable) is the cardinal assumption. Ordinal theory also assumes choice is consistent and transitive, so A is the opposite. The trap: D sounds like any utility theory but belongs to cardinal theory. Memory tip: Ordinal = Order; Cardinal = Counting.",
 "|The law of diminishing marginal utility explains why|the slope of a normal demand curve is negative|an abnormal demand curve slopes upwards|the slope of a normal demand curve is positive|the consumption of inferior goods increases with income": "The law of diminishing marginal utility states that as a consumer takes more units of a good, the extra satisfaction from each additional unit falls. Since a consumer pays for a unit only up to what it is worth to him, he will buy more units only if the price falls. So more is demanded at lower prices and less at higher prices: the normal demand curve slopes downwards from left to right, a negative slope: A. Abnormal upward-sloping curves (B) and inferior goods (D) are explained by other forces, such as Giffen and income effects. Memory tip: each extra unit pleases less, so it must cost less.",
 "|If a consumer plans to spend 120k on four oranges but spent 80k, his consumer surplus is|₦1.50|₦0.40|₦1.00|₦2.00": "Consumer surplus is the difference between what a consumer is willing to pay and what he actually pays. Remember 100 kobo = ₦1.\nStep 1: Willing to pay = 120k = ₦1.20.\nStep 2: Actually paid = 80k = ₦0.80.\nStep 3: Consumer surplus = ₦1.20 − ₦0.80 = ₦0.40 (40k): B.\nThe number of oranges does not change the answer, since both figures are totals. Memory tip: surplus = willing to pay − actually paid.",
 "|A set of factors that can shift the supply curve are changes in|weather, price and technology|technology, weather and population|technology, price and taste|population, price and taste": "A supply curve is drawn for given conditions of supply. A change in the good's own price causes only a movement along the curve, so any option containing \"price\" describes a movement, not a shift (A, C, D). The curve shifts when other conditions change: technology (better machines cut costs), weather (especially for farm produce) and population (which affects the supply of labour and other inputs). Only B contains no price: B. Taste is a determinant of demand, not supply. Memory tip: own price moves you along; everything else shifts the curve.",
 "|If the coefficient of price elasticity of supply is greater than one, the supply is said to be|perfectly elastic|fairly inelastic|infinitely inelastic|fairly elastic": "Elasticity of supply = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price. The values are named like this: 0 = perfectly inelastic; between 0 and 1 = fairly (relatively) inelastic; 1 = unitary; between 1 and infinity = fairly (relatively) elastic; infinity = perfectly elastic. A coefficient greater than one means quantity supplied changes proportionately more than price, so supply is fairly elastic: D. Perfectly elastic (A) means an infinite coefficient, a horizontal supply curve. The trap: A sounds right for \"greater than one\", but perfectly elastic needs an infinite coefficient. Memory tip: above 1, Elastic; below 1, Inelastic; the extremes are 0 and infinity.",
 "|If commodity X is a by-product of commodity Y, this implies that both commodities are|in competitive supply|in composite supply|jointly supplied|in excess supply": "Joint supply occurs when producing one good automatically produces another, because they come from the same process: beef and hides from cattle, petrol and kerosene from crude oil, cotton lint and cottonseed. A by-product is exactly this kind of good, so X and Y are jointly supplied: C. Composite supply (B) is different: one want satisfied from several sources, such as energy from petrol, gas or electricity. Competitive supply (A) is when producing more of one good means producing less of another from the same resources, such as cocoa or rubber on the same land. The trap: B (composite supply) sounds close to joint supply but describes several sources serving one want. Memory tip: Joint supply = one process, two products.",
 "|In perfect competition, price is determined by the|government|sellers|buyers|market": "Perfect competition has many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous product, free entry and exit, and perfect knowledge. Each firm is so small that it cannot influence price; it is a price taker. The price is set by the interaction of total market demand and total market supply, and each firm simply sells at that price: D. Government (A) does not fix prices in a free market, and neither sellers (B) nor buyers (C) alone can set it. In the diagram, the firm's demand curve is horizontal at the market price. Memory tip: in perfect competition, the market makes the price; the firm takes it.",
 "|In order to reduce hardship faced by consumers due to high prices, the government can introduce|maximum prices|commodity boards|minimum prices|price control boards": "To protect consumers from high prices, the government can fix a maximum price (price ceiling): a legal price above which a good must not be sold, set below the market equilibrium price. Nigeria's controlled pump price of petrol is an example. It makes the good cheaper, though it can cause shortages, queues and black markets: A. A minimum price (C) is set above equilibrium to protect producers, such as farmers or workers (minimum wage), so it would raise prices. The trap: C (minimum prices) is the mirror-image policy and helps producers, not consumers. Memory tip: MAXimum price caps what consumers pay; MINimum price protects producers' income.",
 "|Average product is less than marginal product when|there is constant returns to scale|there is increasing returns to scale|there is decreasing returns to scale|diminishing returns set in": "Short-run production with one variable factor goes through stages. At first marginal product (MP) rises (increasing returns). When diminishing returns set in, MP reaches its peak and starts to fall. But MP is still higher than average product (AP) at that point, so each extra worker still adds more than the average and pulls AP up. AP keeps rising until MP falls to meet it at AP's maximum. So AP is less than MP when diminishing returns set in: D. Options A to C speak of returns to scale, a long-run idea where all inputs change, not the short-run product curves. Memory tip: MP pulls AP up while MP is above it; they meet at AP's peak.",
 "|A firm enjoying economies of scale is said to be|reducing average cost as production increases|benefiting from the activities of other firms|maximizing profits as production increases|having an upward-sloping average cost curve": "Economies of scale are the advantages a firm gains as it grows larger, such as buying raw materials in bulk, using specialised machines, cheaper borrowing and division of labour. Their effect is that cost per unit, average cost, falls as output increases: A. Benefiting from the activities of other firms (B) describes external economies arising from the industry, and an upward-sloping average cost curve (D) shows diseconomies of scale. Large Nigerian firms such as cement makers enjoy economies of scale by producing millions of bags. Memory tip: Economies of scale = Every extra unit is cheaper.",
 "|The rising portion of the long-run average cost curve of a firm is an indication that it is experiencing|increasing efficiency|economies of scale|dis-economies of scale|increasing marginal returns": "The long-run average cost (LRAC) curve is U-shaped. On the falling part, the firm enjoys economies of scale: average cost drops as it expands. At the bottom is the optimum (minimum cost) size. Beyond this, problems of size such as poor coordination, management difficulties, slow decisions and labour unrest push average cost up. These are diseconomies of scale, shown by the rising portion: C. Economies of scale (B) and increasing efficiency (A) would make the curve fall, not rise. Memory tip: the LRAC goes down with Economies and up with Diseconomies.",
 "|An industry's supply curve is more likely to be elastic when firms are|enjoying free entry and exit|operating at full capacity|operating below capacity|maximizing profits": "Elasticity of supply is high when output can be increased quickly as price rises. For an industry, two standard reasons are given. First, when entry and exit are free, a rise in price attracts new firms, so total industry output rises a lot: A. Second, when existing firms are operating below capacity, they have idle machines and space and can expand output quickly without much rise in costs: C. Both are accepted. Firms at full capacity (B) cannot expand quickly, so supply is inelastic. Profit maximisation (D) is the goal of all firms and does not determine elasticity. Memory tip: easy entry and spare capacity both mean easy expansion.",
 "|One of the characteristics of monopolistic competition is that|there is mobility of factors of production|no single seller dominates the market|the firms are price-takers|consumers have perfect knowledge of price": "Monopolistic competition, described by Edward Chamberlin, combines features of monopoly and perfect competition. There are many sellers, each selling a slightly differentiated product (brands of soap, hair salons, restaurants), with fairly free entry and exit. Because there are many firms, each has only a small share of the market and no single seller dominates: B. Firms are not price takers (C): brand loyalty gives each some control over its own price. Perfect knowledge (D) and perfect mobility of factors (A) are assumptions of perfect competition. Memory tip: monopolistic competition = Many sellers, Mildly different products.",
 "|The demand curve for factors of production|is perfect elastic|slopes upwards|slopes downwards|is perfectly inelastic": "The demand for a factor of production is derived from the demand for the goods it helps to make, and it follows the marginal productivity of the factor. Because of diminishing returns, each extra worker adds less output than the one before, so a firm will hire more workers only if the wage falls. More of a factor is demanded at a lower price and less at a higher price, so the factor demand curve slopes downwards from left to right: C. In fact, the demand curve for labour is the downward-sloping part of its marginal revenue product curve. Memory tip: factor demand falls as marginal product falls.",
 "|An agreement among firms on price and segmentation is termed|cartel|collusion|haggling|specialization": "A cartel is a formal agreement among independent firms in an industry to restrict competition, usually by fixing a common price, limiting output or sharing out the market into separate segments (areas or quotas). OPEC, which allocates oil production quotas to its members, is the best-known example. So an agreement on price and market segmentation is a cartel: A. Collusion (B) is the general act of firms secretly cooperating, while a cartel is the actual agreement or organisation. The trap: B (collusion) is closely related; collusion is the behaviour, a cartel is the formal arrangement. Memory tip: Cartel = Contract among firms.",
 "|In national income accounting, tax is determined by the|level of income|level of consumption|level of investment|rate of savings": "In national income analysis, government tax revenue is usually treated as depending on national income: T = tY, where t is the tax rate. When incomes rise, people and firms pay more income tax and company tax, and spend more, paying more indirect taxes too. So the amount of tax collected is determined by the level of income: A. Consumption, investment and saving are themselves shaped by income after tax. This is why taxes act as automatic stabilisers: they rise in booms and fall in slumps. Memory tip: higher income, higher tax take.",
 "|A decrease in aggregate spending in an economy will ultimately lead to|boom|inflation|deflation|recession": "Aggregate spending (aggregate demand) is total spending by households, firms, government and foreigners: C + I + G + (X − M). When it falls below what the economy can produce, there is a deflationary gap. Firms cannot sell their goods, so prices tend to fall (deflation: C) and they cut output and lay off workers, so incomes fall and the economy slides into recession (D). Both are accepted. A boom (A) and inflation (B) come from rising, not falling, aggregate spending. Memory tip: spending falls, so prices and jobs fall with it.",
 "|If MPC is 0.7 while government expenditure increased by ₦150m, the equilibrium national income is|₦214 million|₦45 million|₦105 million|₦500 million": "The multiplier shows how much national income changes for each naira change in spending.\nStep 1: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.7) = 1 ÷ 0.3 = 3⅓.\nStep 2: Change in income = k × change in government expenditure = 3⅓ × ₦150m = ₦500 million: D.\nThe trap: C (₦105m) is 0.7 × ₦150m and B (₦45m) is 0.3 × ₦150m; both multiply by MPC or MPS instead of dividing by MPS. Memory tip: multiplier = 1 ÷ MPS.",
 "|The function of money which makes division of labour possible is its|unit of account|store of value|medium of exchange|standard of deferred payment": "Money has four main functions: medium of exchange, unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Under barter, a specialist such as a tailor would have to find someone who wants his clothes and has exactly what he wants: the double coincidence of wants. As a medium of exchange, money removes this problem: the tailor sells his services for money and uses the money to buy food, shelter and everything else. This makes specialisation and division of labour possible: C. Memory tip: money as a Medium lets people specialise in one job and buy the rest.",
 "|By buying treasury bills, the Central Bank of Nigeria intends to|increase money supply in the economy|reduce the cash reserve ratio for banks|reduce money supply in the economy|increase the capital base of commercial banks": "Open market operations (OMO) are the buying and selling of government securities, such as treasury bills, by the central bank. When the Central Bank of Nigeria buys treasury bills from banks and the public, it pays for them with new money, which adds to bank reserves and lets banks lend more: money supply increases: A. When it sells treasury bills, it takes money out of circulation, reducing money supply (C). The trap: C is the effect of selling, the opposite operation. Today the CBN also uses its own OMO bills for the same purpose. Memory tip: central bank Buys, money Balloons; central bank Sells, money Shrinks.",
 "|The velocity of money is represented as|Money supply ÷ Real GDP|Real GDP ÷ Money supply|Nominal GDP ÷ Money supply|Real GDP ÷ Nominal GDP": "The quantity theory of money in Fisher's form is MV = PT, often written MV = PY, where M is money supply, V velocity of circulation, P the price level and Y real output. P × Y is nominal GDP. Rearranging, V = PY ÷ M = nominal GDP ÷ money supply: C. Velocity is the average number of times each naira changes hands in a year. For example, if nominal GDP is ₦100 billion and money supply is ₦20 billion, V = 5. The trap: B uses real GDP, but velocity is measured against spending in current prices, nominal GDP. Memory tip: Velocity = spending (nominal GDP) ÷ money.",
 "|One of the functions of commercial banks is|maintaining stable price in the economy|regulating monetary policies|granting loans to customers|issuing bank notes and coins": "Commercial banks are profit-making banks that accept deposits (current, savings and fixed), grant loans and overdrafts, provide payment services, keep valuables and advise customers. Granting loans to customers is one of their main functions: C. Maintaining price stability (A), regulating monetary policy (B) and issuing notes and coins (D) are functions of the central bank, which in Nigeria is the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN). Memory tip: commercial banks Collect deposits and Create credit; the central bank Controls.",
 "|A strategy for curbing unemployment is to|implement government stabilization policy|increase taxes and decrease government expenditure|increase government expenditure and decrease taxes|ensure even distribution of job opportunities": "Unemployment rises when aggregate demand is too low for firms to employ everyone who wants to work. Keynes recommended expansionary fiscal policy: increase government expenditure (on roads, schools and so on), which creates jobs directly, and decrease taxes, which leaves households and firms more to spend. Higher spending leads firms to raise output and hire more workers: C. Increasing taxes and cutting spending (B) is contractionary and would worsen unemployment. Even distribution of job opportunities (D) shares out existing jobs without creating new ones. Memory tip: to cure unemployment, spend more and tax less.",
 "|To create more job opportunities in Nigeria, the government is likely to adopt|balanced budgeting|deficit budgeting|surplus budgeting|zero budgeting": "A budget balances revenue against expenditure. A balanced budget (A) means expenditure equals revenue; a surplus budget (C) means revenue is greater, which takes money out of the economy and is used to fight inflation. A deficit budget means the government spends more than it collects, financed by borrowing. The extra spending raises aggregate demand and creates jobs, so it is the budget used to fight unemployment and recession: B. Nigeria has run deficit budgets for most years since independence. Memory tip: Deficit budget = Demand boost = more jobs.",
 "|National development plans in Nigeria fail mainly because of|over dependence on foreign aids|inadequate funding of projects|poor implementation strategies|shortage of skilled manpower": "Nigeria has had several development plans: the First (1962–68), Second (1970–74), Third (1975–80) and Fourth (1981–85) National Development Plans, then rolling plans and visions such as Vision 20:2020. Most plans were well drafted, but they failed mainly because of poor implementation: weak monitoring, corruption, abandoned projects, frequent changes of government and policy, and poor coordination between agencies: C. Inadequate funding (B), shortage of skilled manpower (D) and reliance on foreign aid (A) were problems, but they flow largely from poor execution. Memory tip: good plans fail on poor implementation.",
 "|The ultimate aim of agricultural policies in Nigeria is to achieve|food sufficiency|industrialization|full employment|industrial capacity utilization": "Nigeria's agricultural policies and programmes include the National Accelerated Food Production Programme (1972), Operation Feed the Nation (1976), the River Basin Development Authorities, the Green Revolution (1980), and later the Agricultural Transformation Agenda and Anchor Borrowers' Programme. The ultimate aim running through all of them is self-sufficiency in food, so the country can feed its people and reduce food imports: A. Raw materials for industry, jobs and exports are further aims, but food comes first. Memory tip: Operation Feed the Nation says it all: Food first.",
 "|Government can boost agricultural output in Nigeria primarily by|embarking on buffer stock programmes|placing embargo on food importation|granting subsidies on farm inputs|placing farmers on monthly income": "Farmers in Nigeria are mostly small-scale and poor, so the cost of fertiliser, improved seeds, agrochemicals and tractors limits how much they can produce. When the government subsidises these farm inputs, costs fall and farmers can buy more inputs and raise their output directly: C. A ban on food imports (B) protects farmers' market but does not by itself raise their productivity. Buffer stock programmes (A) aim to stabilise prices, not raise output. Monthly salaries for farmers (D) are not a practical policy. Memory tip: cheaper inputs, more output.",
 "|Localization of industries refers to the|spread of firms producing different products|siting of industries near the market|concentration of firms of an industry|siting of firms producing different products": "Localisation of industry means the concentration of firms in one industry in a particular area. Examples include textile firms in Kaduna, tanneries and leather works in Kano, and oil-related firms around Port Harcourt and Warri. Causes include nearness to raw materials and markets, power supply, skilled labour and historical factors: C. Siting industries near the market (B) is one cause of where an industry locates, not the meaning of localisation. Spreading firms of different products (A, D) is the opposite: dispersal. The trap: B describes a factor in industrial location, not localisation itself. Memory tip: Localisation = many firms of one industry in one Locality.",
 "|In developing countries, governments influence the location of industries in order to|spread development|redistribute wealth|encourage entrepreneurs|encourage industries to earn high profits": "Left to themselves, industries cluster in a few big cities (in Nigeria, Lagos, Kano, Port Harcourt), causing congestion in those places and neglect elsewhere. Governments in developing countries therefore influence location through tax holidays, industrial estates, infrastructure and siting public enterprises in backward areas. The aim is to spread development evenly, create jobs across the country, reduce rural–urban migration and promote balanced growth: A. Industries earning high profits (D) is the firms' own goal, not government's. Memory tip: government steers industry to Spread development.",
 "|A disadvantage of Nigeria's dependence on imported petroleum products is the|instability in the demand for the products|dominance of multinational firms|instability in the supply of the product|poor maintenance of the refineries": "Nigeria is a major exporter of crude oil but, because its refineries have long worked below capacity, it has relied heavily on imported petrol, diesel and kerosene. The main disadvantage is instability in supply: imports depend on ships, port clearing, foreign exchange and international prices, so delays cause fuel scarcity and long queues: C. Demand for fuel (A) is fairly stable. Poor maintenance of refineries (D) is a cause of the dependence, not a result of it. Today the large Dangote refinery and rehabilitated state refineries aim to reduce this dependence. Memory tip: import your fuel, import your queues.",
 "|The maximum number of shareholders for a limited liability company is|twenty|five|seven|infinite": "A limited liability company is owned by shareholders whose liability is limited to the amount of their shares. A private limited company may have at most 50 members (under Nigeria's Companies and Allied Matters Act). A public limited company (Plc) needs at least two members but has no maximum: its shares are sold to the public and traded on the stock exchange, so the number of shareholders can be unlimited: D. Twenty is the old maximum for an ordinary partnership, and seven was the old minimum for a public company. The trap: A (twenty) is the partnership limit. Memory tip: Plc = Public = no limit.",
 "|The primary motive for an individual engaging in production is to|make profit|satisfy basic human wants|redistribute wealth|put inputs into use": "Production is the creation of goods and services to satisfy human wants. For society as a whole, the purpose of production is to satisfy wants. But the question asks about an individual engaging in production. In a market economy an individual producer, the entrepreneur, takes the risk of producing mainly because he expects a reward, and the reward of the entrepreneur is profit: A. Satisfying human wants (B) is the purpose of production for society. Memory tip: society produces to satisfy wants; the entrepreneur produces for profit.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nYear ... Population (000) ... Working Population (000)\n2000 ... 25 ... 13\n2001 ... 30 ... 19\n2002 ... 40 ... 32\n2003 ... 60 ... 49\n\nWhat is the population growth rate in 2003?|33.3%|20.0%|11.0%|50%": "Population growth rate = (change in population ÷ previous year's population) × 100.\nStep 1: Population in 2002 = 40 (thousand); in 2003 = 60 (thousand).\nStep 2: Change = 60 − 40 = 20.\nStep 3: Growth rate = 20 ÷ 40 × 100 = 50%: D.\nThe trap: A (33.3%) comes from dividing the increase by the 2003 figure (20 ÷ 60) instead of the earlier year. Memory tip: percentage change always divides by the OLD figure.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nYear ... Population (000) ... Working Population (000)\n2000 ... 25 ... 13\n2001 ... 30 ... 19\n2002 ... 40 ... 32\n2003 ... 60 ... 49\n\nThe percentage of working population in 2002 is|50%|25%|8%|80%": "The percentage of the population that is working = (working population ÷ total population) × 100.\nStep 1: In 2002, working population = 32 (thousand); population = 40 (thousand).\nStep 2: Percentage = 32 ÷ 40 × 100 = 0.8 × 100 = 80%: D.\nCheck: the other 8 thousand (20%) are dependants such as children, the aged and the unemployed. The trap: C (8%) is the number of non-working people (40 − 32 = 8) misread as a percentage. Memory tip: part ÷ whole × 100.",
 "|One of the characteristics of free trade zone is|common tariff against non-member countries|different trade policies towards non-member countries|free factor mobility within the zone|harmonized trade among member countries": "Economic integration has stages. In a free trade area (zone), members remove tariffs and quotas on trade among themselves, so trade among members is free and harmonised (D), while each member keeps its own separate trade policy and tariffs towards non-members (B). Both D and B are accepted. A common external tariff against non-members (A) is the extra step that makes a customs union. Free movement of factors of production (C) is the further step that makes a common market. The trap: A describes a customs union, the next stage up from a free trade area. Memory tip: FTA = Free inside, each sets its own wall outside; customs union = one common wall.",
 "|If Nigeria imports vehicles from Japan the transaction will appear as a|debit on Japan's balance of payments|credit on Japan's balance of payments|credit on Nigeria's balance of trade|credit on Nigeria's balance of payment": "In the balance of payments, a transaction that brings foreign exchange into a country is a credit, and one that sends foreign exchange out is a debit. When Nigeria imports vehicles from Japan, Japan exports them and receives payment, so it is a credit on Japan's balance of payments: B. For Nigeria the same transaction is a debit (a visible import) in its balance of trade and balance of payments, so C and D are wrong. The trap: D says credit on Nigeria's balance of payments, mixing up the two countries. Memory tip: money in = credit; money out = debit; the exporter gets the credit.",
 "|One of the objectives of ADB is to|provide subsidies on imported goods to member countries|mobilize short-term loans for member countries|promote economic and social development of member countries|provide technical assistance to only poor member countries": "The African Development Bank (AfDB, often called ADB) was established in 1964 and began operations in 1966; its headquarters is in Abidjan, Côte d'Ivoire, and Nigeria is its largest shareholder. Its main objective is to promote the economic and social development of its regional member countries, by providing long-term loans and grants for projects in agriculture, infrastructure, education and health, and by mobilising development finance: C. Short-term balance of payments loans (B) are the work of the IMF. Technical assistance is given to all members, not only poor ones (D). Memory tip: ADB = African Development: long-term loans for development.",
 "|The choice of the method of production in an economy is determined by the|level of technical know-how|rate of population growth|availability of natural resources|level of income": "Every economy must decide how to produce: whether to use labour-intensive methods (many workers, simple tools) or capital-intensive methods (machines, automation). The choice depends mainly on the level of technology, the technical know-how available. A country with advanced know-how can use sophisticated machines; one with little technical knowledge relies on simple tools and more labour: A. Natural resources (C) and population (B) influence what is produced and factor prices, but the available technology sets which methods are possible. Memory tip: HOW to produce depends on know-HOW.",
 "|The amount of labour hired depends on the|number of skilled labour available|skill of labour|marginal productivity of labour|price of the inputs": "The marginal productivity theory of distribution says that a firm hires a factor up to the point where the value of its marginal product (or marginal revenue product) equals its price. For labour, the firm keeps employing extra workers as long as each adds at least as much to revenue as the wage paid. Since marginal product falls as more workers are hired, the number of workers employed depends on the marginal productivity of labour (together with the wage): C. The number of skilled workers available (A) and skills (B) affect productivity but are not the deciding rule. Memory tip: hire until the last worker earns just what he adds.",
 "|An effective way of controlling inflation in a mixed economy is to|increase productivity|reduce income tax|ration available output|increase imports": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level, often described as too much money chasing too few goods. One way to fight it is to reduce the money side (tight monetary or fiscal policy); the other is to increase the goods side. Raising productivity means more output from the same resources, so supply catches up with demand and prices ease, while incomes and jobs are protected: A. Cutting income tax (B) gives people more to spend and adds to inflation. Rationing (C) only hides the shortage and breeds black markets. More imports (D) can help briefly but drains foreign reserves and hurts local producers. Memory tip: inflation is a gap between money and goods, so produce more goods.",
 "|One of the limitations of the PPC assumption is that there is|no indication of technological development|no recognition of preferred goods for countries|technical inefficiency|abundant resources": "The production possibility curve (PPC) shows all the combinations of two goods an economy can produce when its resources are fully and efficiently used with given technology. It answers \"what CAN we produce?\" but not \"what do we WANT to produce?\". Every point on the curve is equally efficient, and the curve gives no guide to which combination the people or the government prefer: B. Technical inefficiency (C) is shown by a point inside the curve, and abundant resources (D) contradicts the idea of scarcity on which the PPC rests. Memory tip: the PPC is a menu of possibilities, not the order.",
 "|A major disadvantage of the arithmetic mean is that it is|not useful for large data|not suitable for further statistical analysis|cumbersome to determine the actual value|affected by extreme data": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of the values divided by their number. Because every value enters the sum, one extreme figure can distort it. Example: the monthly incomes ₦20,000, ₦25,000, ₦30,000 and ₦525,000 have a mean of ₦600,000 ÷ 4 = ₦150,000, which describes none of the four people. So the major disadvantage is that it is affected by extreme data: D. The mean is in fact easy to calculate, works for large data and is the basis of further statistics such as the variance and standard deviation, so A, B and C are its advantages turned upside down. Memory tip: the mean listens to everybody, including the loudest extreme.",
 "|Demand patterns are determined by the market on the basis of|scale of preference|consumer sovereignty|consumer rationality|price of the commodity": "Demand theory rests on the assumption of consumer rationality: each consumer spends a limited income so as to get the greatest possible satisfaction, buying more when the price falls and less when it rises. Add up these rational choices across all buyers and you get the market's pattern of demand: C. Consumer sovereignty (B) is a related idea but describes what follows from demand, namely that consumers' spending directs producers on what to produce. A scale of preference (A) belongs to one individual, and price (D) moves you along a demand curve rather than forming the pattern. Memory tip: rational buyers make demand; their demand makes them sovereign over producers.",
 "|A consumer surplus measures the|benefits derived from consuming a cheap commodity|excess of total expenditure over total utility|difference between marginal utility and marginal cost|excess of marginal utility over price": "Consumer surplus is the difference between what a consumer is willing to pay for a quantity of a good and what he actually pays. Willingness to pay reflects marginal utility: you would pay ₦500 for the first sachet of water on a hot day, less for the second, and so on, but the market price of every sachet is the same. On every unit before the last, the marginal utility (in money terms) exceeds the price, and that excess is the surplus: D. It is not about cheap goods only (A), and B reverses the idea, since expenditure is below the value enjoyed. Memory tip: surplus = what it is worth to you minus what you paid.",
 "|If the demand for one commodity excludes another, it is said to be|complementary demand|competitive demand|composite demand|derived demand": "When buying one good means not buying another, the two goods are substitutes and compete for the consumer's money: this is competitive demand, B. Examples are Peak and Three Crowns milk, or bus and motorcycle transport for the same trip. Complementary (joint) demand (A) is the opposite: goods wanted together, like a car and petrol. Composite demand (C) is one good wanted for several uses, like palm oil for cooking and for soap. Derived demand (D) is demand for a factor or input because of the final good it helps produce, like cement because of houses. Memory tip: Competitive = Choose one; Complementary = Combine them.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question:\nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (kg)\n1 ... 100\n2 ... 120\n3 ... 150\n4 ... 180\n5 ... 200\n\nThe graph of the schedule above will depict|a kinked demand curve|a normal demand curve|an abnormal demand curve|a diminishing marginal utility": "Read the schedule: as price rises from ₦1 to ₦2, ₦3, ₦4 and ₦5, the quantity rises from 100 kg to 120, 150, 180 and 200 kg. Price and quantity move in the same direction, so the curve slopes upwards from left to right. A normal demand curve slopes downwards (higher price, less bought), so this schedule depicts an abnormal or exceptional demand curve: C. Such curves occur with Giffen goods, goods of ostentation, and when buyers expect prices to rise further. The trap: B (a normal demand curve) catches students who see a demand table and stop without checking the direction of change. Memory tip: price and quantity rising together on a demand table means abnormal.",
 "|The median of an odd-numbered set of scores is the|middle value in the set|highest value in the set|arithmetic mean of the set|most frequently occurring score": "The median is the middle value of a set of scores arranged in order of size. With an odd number of scores there is exactly one middle value, at position (n + 1) ÷ 2. For 3, 7, 8, 12, 15, n = 5, so the median is the 3rd value, 8: A. With an even number of scores you take the average of the two middle values. The trap: C (the arithmetic mean) and D (the most frequent score, the mode) are the other two averages and are often mixed up with the median. Memory tip: Median sits in the Middle, like the median strip in the middle of the road.",
 "|If demand increases without a change in supply, equilibrium price and quantity will|remain unchanged|shift inward|fall|rise": "Equilibrium is where the demand and supply curves cross. If demand increases (for example because incomes rise) while supply is unchanged, the demand curve shifts to the right. At the old price there is now excess demand, so buyers bid the price up, and sellers respond by moving up the supply curve and offering more. The new equilibrium therefore has a higher price and a larger quantity: D. A fall in both (C) would follow a decrease in demand, and \"shift inward\" (B) describes curves, not price and quantity. Memory tip: demand up, price up and quantity up; demand down, both down.",
 "|In the process of production, total output is at maximum when|MP = 0|MP > 0|AP = 0|AP > 0": "Total product (TP) is the whole output, and marginal product (MP) is the extra output from one more unit of the variable factor. While MP is positive, each extra worker adds to TP, so TP is still rising. When MP falls to zero, the extra worker adds nothing and TP stops rising: it has reached its maximum. If more workers are added, MP turns negative and TP falls. So TP is at its maximum when MP = 0: A. Average product (AP) is TP divided by workers and is still positive at that point. The trap: B (MP > 0) describes the stage where total output is still increasing, not its peak. Memory tip: total is at the top when the margin adds zero.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question:\nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (bags)\n1 ... 60\n2 ... 120\n\nFrom the table above, the price elasticity of supply is|4|25|1|2": "Price elasticity of supply = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 120 − 60 = 60 bags; percentage change = 60 ÷ 60 × 100 = 100%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦2 − ₦1 = ₦1; percentage change = 1 ÷ 1 × 100 = 100%.\nStep 3: Elasticity = 100% ÷ 100% = 1: C.\nSupply is unitary elastic: quantity responds in exactly the same proportion as price. A straight-line supply curve through the origin always has elasticity 1, and these two points (₦1, 60) and (₦2, 120) lie on such a line. Memory tip: always divide percentage by percentage, using the original figures as the base.",
 "|When a consumer is at equilibrium, the MRSxy is equal to the|sum of the two prices|product of the two prices|ratio of the two prices|difference of the two prices": "A consumer buying two goods X and Y is in equilibrium where the budget line just touches the highest indifference curve he can reach. At that point the slope of the indifference curve, the marginal rate of substitution of X for Y (MRSxy), equals the slope of the budget line, which is the ratio of the prices, Px/Py: C. In words, the rate at which the consumer is willing to swap Y for X equals the rate at which the market lets him swap them. The same result appears in cardinal theory as MUx/Px = MUy/Py, which rearranges to MUx/MUy = Px/Py. Memory tip: at equilibrium, MRS = price ratio, just as MU ratio = price ratio.",
 "|Minimum price legislation by government will|reduce supply|increase supply|reduce demand and create surplus|increase demand and create scarcity": "A minimum price (price floor) is a legal price fixed ABOVE the equilibrium price, usually to protect producers or workers; the national minimum wage is an example. At the higher price, buyers take a smaller quantity and sellers offer a larger quantity, so more is offered than bought: a surplus, which the government may have to buy up and store. Option C (less demanded and a surplus) describes this result most fully: C. Option B (supply increases) is also true of the same change, since sellers offer more at the higher price, so both C and B are accepted. Strictly, both are movements along the curves, not shifts. Memory tip: a floor is set high and leaves goods piled on the floor.",
 "|Ranking is the method used in measuring|marginal utility|ordinal utility|cardinal utility|total utility": "There are two approaches to utility. The cardinal approach (Marshall) assumes satisfaction can be measured in units called utils, so we can speak of total and marginal utility in numbers. The ordinal approach (Hicks and Allen) says satisfaction cannot be measured, only ranked: a consumer can say he prefers bundle A to bundle B, or is indifferent between them. Ranking is therefore the method of ordinal utility, the basis of indifference curves: B. The trap: C (cardinal utility) sounds similar but is the measuring approach, not the ranking one. Memory tip: ORDinal = ORDer (first, second, third); CARDinal = counting.",
 "|If a firm is faced with an elastic supply curve, its revenue will|be supplied at a higher price|double at a higher price|increase by more than the percentage increase in price|equal percentage change in price": "Elasticity of supply compares the percentage change in quantity supplied with the percentage change in price. Supply is elastic when quantity changes by a larger percentage than price. Revenue = price × quantity, so if price rises by 10% and an elastic supply lets the firm sell, say, 25% more, revenue rises by about 1.10 × 1.25 = 1.375, a 37.5% increase, which is more than the 10% rise in price: C. Revenue is not \"supplied\" (A), it does not necessarily double (B), and it rises by more than, not the same as, the price change (D). Memory tip: elastic means the quantity stretches further than the price.",
 "|The optimal range of output for a perfectly competitive firm is where|AC is lowest|AVC is lowest|MC is rising|MC is falling": "A perfectly competitive firm is a price taker, so price = MR. It maximises profit where MC = MR, and that must be on the RISING part of the MC curve: if MC is still falling, producing more would lower cost per extra unit further and raise profit, so the firm would not stop there. The firm's relevant output range (and its short-run supply curve) is therefore the part of MC that is rising above AVC: C. The lowest point of AC (A) is a single point, the optimum size of firm reached in long-run equilibrium, and the lowest AVC (B) is the shutdown point. Memory tip: the competitive firm lives on the rising part of MC.",
 "|A firm will experience diseconomies of scale when|there are difficulties in coordinating production|there is shortage in labour supply|the size of market is small|there is an increase in the price of raw materials": "Economies of scale lower average cost as a firm grows; diseconomies of scale raise it when the firm grows too big. The chief cause is managerial: with many departments, branches and workers, information is slow, decisions are delayed and control weakens, so coordinating production becomes difficult: A. Labour shortage (B) and dearer raw materials (D) raise cost for any firm, large or small, and are not caused by the firm's size. A small market (C) limits the growth of a firm and so limits its economies of scale; it does not create diseconomies. Memory tip: diseconomies come from Difficulty in managing a giant.",
 "|The law of variable proportions is applicable only|in the long-run period|to large-scale enterprises|to small-scale enterprises|in the short-run period": "The law of variable proportions (law of diminishing returns) says that when more and more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, the marginal product eventually falls. It needs at least one factor to be fixed, such as land or the size of the factory, and factors are fixed only in the short run: D. In the long run every factor can be changed, and the relevant idea is returns to scale. The law applies to firms of every size, so B and C are wrong. The trap: A (the long run) catches students who confuse diminishing returns with decreasing returns to scale. Memory tip: one factor fixed = short run = variable proportions.",
 "|From the diagram above, the optimal point of production is|R|T|U|S": "The diagram shows three curves I, II and III, each further from the origin and so representing more output (or satisfaction), with a straight cost (budget) line from U to T. The best (optimal) point is where the line just touches the highest curve it can reach. Curve III and point R lie beyond the line, so R cannot be afforded. U and T are on the line but lie on the lowest curve I. The line is tangent to curve II at S, the highest attainable level: D. The trap: A (R) is on the highest curve and looks best, but it lies outside what the line allows. Memory tip: optimum = the Touching point of the line and the highest curve.",
 "|If real income increases while nominal income remains the same, it can be inferred that|unemployment rate has decreased|general prices have fallen|employment rate has risen|general prices have risen": "Nominal (money) income is the amount of naira received; real income is what that money can buy, i.e. nominal income divided by the price level. Real income = nominal income ÷ price index × 100. If nominal income stays at ₦100,000 but real income rises, the price index must have fallen; for example, if prices fall by 10%, the same ₦100,000 buys about 11% more. So general prices have fallen: B. Changes in employment (A, C) affect how many people earn, not the buying power of a fixed money income. The trap: D (prices have risen) is the reverse; rising prices reduce real income. Memory tip: real income = money ÷ prices, so prices down means real income up.",
 "|One of the characteristics of oligopoly is the availability of|few sellers|few buyers|many sellers|a single seller": "Oligopoly is a market structure with a few large sellers (firms) of similar or differentiated products, such as MTN, Airtel, Glo and 9mobile in telecoms, or the big cement makers. Because there are only a few, each firm watches its rivals' prices closely, which explains the kinked demand curve and the tendency to compete by advertising rather than price: A. Few buyers (B) is oligopsony, many sellers (C) is perfect or monopolistic competition, and a single seller (D) is monopoly. Memory tip: mono = one, duo = two, oligo = few, poly = many.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question.\nThe average total cost when 20 units are produced is|₦20.00|₦23.00|₦13.00|₦15.00": "Read the graph at 20 units. The dotted line from 20 on the quantity axis meets the ATC curve at its lowest point, and the dotted line across from that point meets the price axis at 15. So average total cost at 20 units is ₦15.00: D. The MC curve passes through this lowest point of ATC. This is always true, because when the marginal is below an average it pulls the average down, and when above it pulls it up. Memory tip: MC cuts AVC and ATC at their lowest points.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question.\nWhen 20 units of output is produced, TC will be|₦360|₦300|₦460|₦400": "Total cost = average total cost × quantity.\nStep 1: From the diagram, ATC at 20 units = ₦15 (the lowest point of ATC).\nStep 2: TC = ₦15 × 20 = ₦300: B.\nCheck: ₦300 ÷ 20 = ₦15 per unit.\nThe trap: A (₦360) comes from reading the higher ₦18 line instead of ₦15 (₦18 × 20 = ₦360). Memory tip: TC = ATC × Q, and AC = TC ÷ Q; read the value off the correct curve first.",
 "|The profit of a monopolist can be eliminated where price equals|AFC|MC|AC|AVC": "Profit per unit = price − average cost (AC). A monopolist sets a price above AC and earns supernormal profit, protected by barriers to entry. If the government fixes the price where it equals AC, every unit sells at exactly its average cost, total revenue equals total cost, and supernormal profit disappears; only normal profit, which is included in cost, remains: C. Price = MC (B) is the rule for allocative efficiency in perfect competition; price = AVC (D) is the shutdown point, and price = AFC (A) has no special meaning. The trap: B (MC) is the other famous pricing rule for regulating a monopoly, but it targets efficiency, not zero profit. Memory tip: P = AC means profit = zero (above normal).",
 "|Bank consolidation policy in Nigeria is a measure to increase|the capital base of banks|employment opportunities in banks|the number of shareholders|the number of branches": "In July 2004 the Central Bank of Nigeria, under Governor Charles Soludo, ordered every bank to raise its minimum shareholders' funds from ₦2 billion to ₦25 billion by 31 December 2005. Most banks could not do it alone, so they merged or were taken over, and the number of banks fell from 89 to 25. The aim was to build bigger, stronger banks able to finance large projects and withstand shocks, that is, to increase the capital base of banks: A. Branch numbers and jobs were by-products, and many jobs were in fact lost. Today, the CBN began a fresh recapitalisation in 2024, raising the minimum for international banks to ₦500 billion. Memory tip: consolidation = more capital, fewer, bigger banks.",
 "|An increase in the circulation of money without a corresponding increase in output will lead to|a rise in income levels|stagflation|inflation|deflation": "The quantity theory of money (Irving Fisher) is written MV = PT: money supply × velocity = price level × transactions (output). If velocity and output stay the same, an increase in M must raise P. So when more money circulates without more goods to buy, prices rise in general: inflation, C. Deflation (D) is the opposite, a general fall in prices. Stagflation (B) is inflation together with stagnant output and high unemployment, which needs more than a rise in money supply. Money incomes may rise (A), but real incomes do not. Memory tip: more naira, same goods, higher prices.",
 "|In national income accounts, an item counted as part of government spending is|salaries and wages|pension|scholarship|social welfare": "In the expenditure method, national income = C + I + G + (X − M), where G is government spending on goods and services. When the government pays teachers, doctors and civil servants, it receives their services in return, so salaries and wages are payment for current output and are counted in G: A. Pensions, scholarships and social welfare payments are transfer payments: money moved from taxpayers to others without any goods or services produced in return, so they are left out to avoid counting income that matches no output. The trap: B (pension) feels like government spending, but it rewards past work, not current production. Memory tip: G counts only spending that buys something produced now.",
 "|If aggregate demand is lower than total output in an economy, national income will|be constant|be at equilibrium|increase|fall": "In the circular flow, national income is in equilibrium when aggregate demand (total spending) equals total output. If aggregate demand is lower than output, firms cannot sell all they produce, so unsold stocks build up. Firms respond by cutting production, laying off workers and buying fewer inputs, which lowers incomes, which lowers spending further. National income therefore falls until a new, lower equilibrium is reached: D. It cannot be constant or at equilibrium (A, B) while spending and output differ, and it would increase (C) only if demand exceeded output. Memory tip: unsold goods mean falling income; empty shelves mean rising income.",
 "|During the era of barter, money was generally in the form of|notes|precious metals|coins|commodities": "Barter is the direct exchange of goods for goods, which suffers from the need for a double coincidence of wants. To overcome this, societies began to use certain widely accepted goods as a medium of exchange: commodity money, such as cowries, salt, cattle, iron bars and manillas in West Africa. So in that early era money was generally in the form of commodities: D. Later, metals were used because they are durable and divisible, then minted coins, and much later paper notes. The trap: B (precious metals) was an early form too, but it came after general commodity money, as a refinement of it. Memory tip: money evolved C-M-C-N: commodities, metals, coins, notes.",
 "|A country achieves economic development when there is|an increase in military expenditure|an increase in capacity utilization|a sustained increase in per capita income|an even distribution of goods and services": "Economic growth is an increase in a country's real output (GDP) over time. Economic development is wider: a sustained increase in income per head that raises the living standards of the people, with better health, education, jobs and structural change. The essential condition among the options is a sustained increase in per capita income: C. Military spending (A) does not raise living standards, a rise in capacity utilisation (B) can be a short-run recovery, and a perfectly even distribution of goods (D) is neither required nor realistic, although fairer distribution is part of development. Memory tip: development = sustained rise in income PER HEAD plus better lives.",
 "|An indicator of growth in an economy over a period of time is the|GDP gap|GDP at factor cost|GDP at market price|GDP deflator": "Economic growth is the increase in the total output of goods and services of a country over time, so it is shown by comparing gross domestic product from one year to the next. GDP at market prices is the main headline measure of output that is reported and compared year by year: C. GDP at factor cost (B) is a related measure that removes indirect taxes and adds subsidies. The GDP deflator (D) is a price index used to turn nominal GDP into real GDP; it measures prices, not growth. The GDP gap (A) measures how far actual output is below potential output. Memory tip: growth is about GDP itself; the deflator only cleans out price changes.",
 "|A country embarks on deficit financing in order to|increase revenue|reduce aggregate demand|curb inflation|stimulate investment": "Deficit financing (a deficit budget) is when the government deliberately plans to spend more than its revenue, and covers the gap by borrowing or by money creation. The extra spending raises aggregate demand, which is used during a recession or for development to stimulate investment, employment and growth: D. It does not increase revenue (A); it is a way of spending beyond revenue. Because it adds to demand, it does the opposite of reducing aggregate demand (B) or curbing inflation (C); those are aims of a surplus budget. Memory tip: deficit budget to boost the economy, surplus budget to cool it down.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question.\nThe deflationary gap is defined by|X|K|X + K|X − K": "In the diagram, AD is drawn as a horizontal line and AS as a line rising from the origin; they cross at E, the equilibrium, with income Y. To the right of E the AS line is above AD, so the output being produced is greater than what people want to spend: the gap K between them is a deflationary gap, which pushes income down: B. To the left of E, AD lies above AS, and that gap, X, is an inflationary gap. X + K or X − K (C, D) do not measure anything meaningful. The trap: A (X) is the gap on the other side of E, the inflationary one. Memory tip: Demand below supply = Deflationary.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question.\nWhat does X represent?|recessionary gap|GNP gap|inflationary gap|deflationary gap": "On the diagram, X lies to the left of the equilibrium point E, where the horizontal AD line is above the rising AS line. There, total spending is greater than the output produced, so excess demand pushes up prices: X is an inflationary gap, C. The gap K on the right of E, where output exceeds spending, is the deflationary gap. A recessionary gap (A) is another name for a deflationary gap, and the GNP gap (B) compares actual with potential output. The trap: D (deflationary gap) is the gap on the other side of E. Memory tip: demand above supply inflates prices; demand below supply deflates output.",
 "|The import-substitution strategy of industrialization is to encourage|domestic production|large-scale production|importation|exportation": "Import substitution industrialisation means setting up local factories to produce goods that a country used to import, such as textiles, beverages, cement and assembled vehicles. Governments protect these new industries with tariffs, quotas and import bans so that consumers buy the local product. The strategy therefore encourages domestic production: A. It aims to reduce, not encourage, importation (C), and its focus is the home market rather than exports (D), which is the export-promotion strategy. Nigeria followed it from the 1960s to the 1980s. Memory tip: import substitution = make it at home instead of buying it abroad.",
 "|Upstream oil activities involve the|management of pollution|marketing of refined products|exploration of crude oil|refining of crude oil": "The petroleum industry is divided into three stages. Upstream activities are about finding and producing crude oil: exploration (seismic surveys), drilling and production from oil wells: C. Midstream covers transporting and storing oil and gas through pipelines and tankers. Downstream activities turn crude into products and sell them: refining, distribution and marketing of petrol, kerosene and diesel. The trap: D (refining of crude oil) is the first downstream step and is often wrongly placed upstream; B (marketing) is also downstream. Memory tip: UP the stream is the source: get the crude out; DOWN the stream is the customer.",
 "|Agricultural production in Nigeria is constrained by|ineffective use of stabilization measures|inadequate demand|poor implementation of policies|balance of payments deficits": "Nigerian agriculture has had many policies and programmes: Operation Feed the Nation (1976), the Green Revolution (1980), River Basin Development Authorities, the Agricultural Transformation Agenda (2011) and the Anchor Borrowers' Programme. Yet output has stayed low because these were poorly or inconsistently implemented, with frequent changes of government, corruption, late release of funds and inputs that did not reach real farmers: C. Demand for food is high, not inadequate (B), and stabilisation measures and balance of payments deficits (A, D) are not the main constraint on farm production. Memory tip: good farm policies on paper, poor results in the field.",
 "|The main function of NNPC is to|oversee the development of the oil sector|develop the oil producing area|fix the price of products|ensure regular supply of products": "The Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation (NNPC) was created in 1977 by merging the Nigerian National Oil Corporation with the Ministry of Petroleum Resources. Its main job was to oversee and run the development of Nigeria's oil and gas sector on behalf of government: holding the state's interests in joint ventures with oil companies, exploration, refining, pipelines and marketing: A. Developing the oil-producing areas (B) was the job of OMPADEC and later the NDDC. Since the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 it is NNPC Limited, a commercial company, with regulation handled by NUPRC (upstream) and NMDPRA (midstream and downstream). Memory tip: NNPC = the national oil company; NDDC = the Niger Delta's development.",
 "|Investing heavily in the agricultural sector will lead to|sufficient food production|a vibrant commodity market|capital inflow|stable export prices": "Heavy investment in agriculture means more irrigation, machinery, fertiliser, improved seeds, storage, research and extension services. Its first and most direct result is higher farm output, so the country can produce enough food to feed its people (food security) and reduce food imports: A. A vibrant commodity market (B), capital inflow (C) and stable export prices (D) may follow in part, but export prices are set on world markets, and capital inflow depends on investors abroad. Memory tip: invest in the farm, fill the barn.",
 "|Industrial development in Nigeria can be encouraged through|signing WTO treaty|granting subsidies|the provision of efficient infrastructure|direct government participation": "Industries need reliable electricity, good roads and rail, water, ports and telecommunications. In Nigeria, firms spend heavily on their own generators and boreholes, which raises costs and makes local goods less competitive. Providing efficient infrastructure lowers the cost of production for every firm at once and attracts new investment, so it is the best way to encourage industrial development: C. Subsidies (B) help some firms for a time but burden the budget; direct government participation (D) has a poor record in Nigeria; and signing the WTO treaty (A) opens markets to competition rather than building industry, and Nigeria has been a member since 1995. Memory tip: power and roads first, then factories follow.",
 "|The record of a country's transactions in international trade is her|capital account|balance of payments|current account|balance of trade": "The balance of payments is a systematic record of ALL the economic transactions between the residents of a country and the rest of the world over a period, usually a year. It covers the current account (visible trade in goods, invisible trade in services, income and transfers), the capital and financial account, and official reserves: B. The current account (C) and capital account (A) are only parts of it. The trap: D (balance of trade) records only visible trade in goods (exports minus imports of goods), not all transactions. Memory tip: balance of TRADE = goods only; balance of PAYMENTS = everything.",
 "|The transfer of public shareholding corporations to private enterprises is|concession|incorporation|commercialization|privatization": "Privatisation is the transfer of ownership of public enterprises to private individuals or companies, usually by selling the government's shares: D. Nigeria privatised many enterprises through the Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE), including NITEL and the PHCN power generating and distribution companies in 2013. A concession (A) gives a private firm the right to run an asset for a period while government keeps ownership, and incorporation (B) is registering a company. The trap: C (commercialisation) also reforms public enterprises, but it keeps them in government hands and makes them run for profit without selling them. Memory tip: privatise = sell to private owners; commercialise = run for profit, still public.",
 "|A condition in which official and autonomous exchange rates coexist is|dual exchange rate system|managed floating rate system|market-determined exchange rate system|multiple exchange rate system": "A dual exchange rate system exists when two exchange rates operate side by side for the same currency: an official rate fixed or managed by the central bank for approved transactions, and an autonomous rate set by the market (such as the parallel or bureau de change market) for others: A. Nigeria operated such arrangements at various times, for example the official and second-tier markets under SAP in 1986; it unified its official windows in 2023. A managed float (B) and a market-determined system (C) have one rate. The trap: D (multiple exchange rate system) is similar but means several rates for different transactions, not just two. Memory tip: dual = two rates, multiple = many.",
 "|In modern economies, the Malthusian theory of population is ineffective because of|birth control measures|technical progress|government policies|natural disasters": "Thomas Malthus (1798) argued that population grows geometrically (2, 4, 8, 16) while food supply grows only arithmetically (1, 2, 3, 4), so population would outrun food, and famine, disease and war would check it. In modern economies this has not happened, mainly because of technical progress: improved seeds, fertilisers, irrigation, mechanisation and the Green Revolution have raised food output far faster than Malthus imagined: B. Birth control (A) has also slowed population growth in rich countries, but the central failure of his prediction concerns food production. Memory tip: Malthus forgot the tractor and the fertiliser.",
 "|Optimum population enables an economy to attain the highest level of|industrial development|income per head|revenue generation|economic development": "Optimum population is the size of population which, with the country's given natural resources, capital and technology, gives the maximum output per head, or the highest income per head: B. With fewer people (underpopulation), resources are not fully used and income per head could rise if population grew; with more people (overpopulation), there are too many workers for the resources and income per head falls. Industrial development, revenue or economic development (A, C, D) depend on many other things. Memory tip: optimum population = maximum income PER HEAD.",
 "|A change from one industry to another by a worker is an example of|geographical mobility|horizontal mobility|professional advancement|vertical mobility": "Mobility of labour is the ease with which workers move between places or jobs. Geographical mobility (A) is moving from one place to another. Occupational mobility is moving between jobs and has two forms: horizontal mobility, moving to a similar job at the same level in another firm or industry, and vertical mobility, moving up or down the ladder within the same occupation, such as a clerk being promoted to manager. A worker changing from one industry to another is horizontal mobility: B. The trap: D (vertical mobility) is the closely related form, but it means a change of grade or rank, not of industry. Memory tip: horizontal = across industries; vertical = up the ladder.",
 "|The money paid per hour for work done is|salary|wage rate|cost|bonus": "A wage is the payment for labour, usually calculated per hour, day or week, or per piece of work done. The amount paid per unit of time, such as ₦500 per hour, is the wage rate: B. A salary (A) is a fixed sum paid monthly or yearly regardless of the exact hours worked, usually to professional and administrative staff. A bonus (D) is an extra payment on top of the wage, for example for good performance. Cost (C) is too general. The trap: A (salary) is also payment for work, but it is fixed per month or year, not per hour. Memory tip: wage per hour or day; salary per month or year.",
 "|Trade takes place because of|marginal utility|inefficiency in production|opportunity cost|comparative advantage": "David Ricardo's principle of comparative advantage says that countries gain from trade by specialising in the goods they can produce at a lower opportunity cost than others, and importing the rest. Even a country that is better at producing everything (absolute advantage) gains by concentrating on what it is relatively best at. This difference in comparative costs is why trade takes place: D. Inefficiency (B) and marginal utility (A) are not the basis of trade. The trap: C (opportunity cost) is the tool used to measure comparative advantage, but trade arises from the difference in those costs, which is comparative advantage. Memory tip: trade where you are relatively best.",
 "|The World Trade Organization is responsible for|stabilizing and harmonization of oil prices|ensuring equal participation of countries in trade|modernising world economies|minimizing obstacles to international trade and investment": "The World Trade Organization (WTO) was set up on 1 January 1995 to replace the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT, 1947), and Nigeria is a founding member. Its main work is to reduce tariffs, quotas and other barriers to trade and investment through negotiated agreements, to enforce trade rules, and to settle trade disputes between members: D. Stabilising oil prices (A) is OPEC's aim. Ensuring \"equal participation\" (B) and modernising economies (C) are not its role, although it gives special treatment to developing countries. Memory tip: GATT became the WTO in 1995, and both work to cut barriers to trade.",
 "|A major disadvantage of a socialist economy is that|corruption is rampant|consumer's sovereignty is lost|income inequality is entrenched|there is high level of unemployment": "In a socialist (command) economy the state owns the main means of production and a central planning authority decides what to produce, how much and at what price. Consumers can only buy what the planners have chosen to make, so they lose the power to direct production by how they spend their money. That power, called consumer sovereignty, belongs to the capitalist system, where the goods people buy most are the goods firms make more of: B. Rampant corruption and unemployment can happen under any system, and entrenched income inequality (C) is a weakness usually charged against capitalism, not socialism. Memory tip: in the market the consumer is king; under central planning the planner is king.",
 "|The privatization of public enterprises will lead to efficient management of resources in the economy. This statement can best be described as|normative reasoning|inductive reasoning|deductive reasoning|positive reasoning": "Economic statements are of two kinds. Positive statements say what is, was or will be, and can in principle be tested against facts: \"privatization will lead to efficient management of resources\" is a prediction we can check by studying privatized firms. Normative statements say what ought to be and contain a value judgement, such as \"government should privatize all its enterprises\". Because the statement makes a testable claim and contains no \"should\" or \"ought\", it is positive: D. Inductive and deductive reasoning (B, C) are methods of building theories, not kinds of statements. The trap: A (normative) catches students who think any statement about government policy must be an opinion. Memory tip: Positive = Provable; Normative = \"it ought to be\".",
 "|The sign of the slope of a graph in economic analysis is important because it|shows whether a good is normal or inferior|shows the relationship between variables|reveals the magnitude of the change between variables|helps to determine the unit of measurement of variables": "The slope of a graph tells us how one variable changes when another changes. Its sign shows the direction of the relationship. A positive (upward) slope means a direct relationship: both variables rise together, as with price and quantity supplied. A negative (downward) slope means an inverse relationship: as one rises the other falls, as with price and quantity demanded. So the sign shows the relationship between the variables: B. How large the change is depends on the size, or steepness, of the slope, not on its plus or minus sign, so C does not describe the sign. Whether a good is normal or inferior is shown by income elasticity, not by the sign of any slope on a graph. Memory tip: the sign gives the direction; the size gives the magnitude.",
 "|Use the pie chart above to answer the question. \nThe percentage contribution of the agriculture sector is|67.5|40.0|32.4|25.0": "A pie chart shares a whole circle of 360° among the parts, so each sector's percentage is its angle ÷ 360° × 100.\nStep 1: The agriculture sector has an angle of 90°.\nStep 2: Percentage = 90/360 × 100 = 0.25 × 100 = 25%: D.\nCheck the whole chart: oil 160° ≈ 44.4%, agriculture 90° = 25%, mining 30° ≈ 8.3%, and manufacturing takes the remaining 80° ≈ 22.2%; together they make 100%. A quarter of a circle is a right angle, which is why agriculture's sector has a square corner at the centre. Memory tip: percentage = angle ÷ 3.6.",
 "|Use the pie chart above to answer the question. \nIf the GDP is ₦60 billion, what is the difference between the contributions of oil and manufacturing sectors?|₦11.7 billion|₦13.3 billion|₦21.7 billion|₦26.6 billion": "Each sector's value = its angle ÷ 360° × the total (GDP).\nStep 1: Find manufacturing's angle. The angles add to 360°: 360° − 160° (oil) − 90° (agriculture) − 30° (mining) = 80°.\nStep 2: Oil = 160/360 × ₦60 billion = ₦26.7 billion.\nStep 3: Manufacturing = 80/360 × ₦60 billion = ₦13.3 billion.\nStep 4: Difference = ₦26.7 billion − ₦13.3 billion = ₦13.3 billion: B.\nThe trap: D (₦26.6 billion) is roughly oil's own contribution, the figure you get if you forget to subtract manufacturing.\nMemory tip: subtract the angles first (160° − 80° = 80°), then convert once: 80/360 × total.",
 "|If the quantity of rice bought decreases from 250 tonnes to 200 tonnes owing to a 2% rise in price, it shows that there is a change in|consumers' income|demand|consumers' tastes|quantity demanded": "Economists separate a change in quantity demanded from a change in demand. A change in quantity demanded is caused only by a change in the good's own price: the buyer moves along the same demand curve. A change in demand is caused by other factors, such as income, tastes or the prices of other goods, and it shifts the whole curve. Here less rice is bought (250 to 200 tonnes) because the price of rice itself rose by 2%, so it is a movement along the curve, a change in quantity demanded: D. The trap: B (demand) is the closely related term that students often use loosely, but it means a shift of the whole curve. Memory tip: own price → Quantity demanded (move along); anything else → Demand (shift).",
 "|A rise in income will, ceteris paribus, bring about|a movement along the demand curve|a leftward shift of the demand curve|a rightward shift of the demand curve|no effect on the demand curve": "The demand curve is drawn holding income, tastes and the prices of other goods constant (ceteris paribus). If income rises, consumers can afford more of a normal good at every price, so the whole demand curve shifts to the right: C. A movement along the curve (A) happens only when the good's own price changes. A leftward shift (B) follows a fall in income for a normal good, or a rise in income for an inferior good such as gari for a household that moves on to rice. In JAMB questions, assume a normal good unless the question says otherwise. Memory tip: more money in the pocket pushes demand to the right.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (kg)\n50 ... 500\n70 ... 400\n\nFrom the above, calculate the price elasticity of demand.|5.0|2.0|1.0|0.5": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price, using the original figures as the base.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 500 − 400 = 100 kg; percentage change = 100/500 × 100 = 20%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦70 − ₦50 = ₦20; percentage change = 20/50 × 100 = 40%.\nStep 3: PED = 20% ÷ 40% = 0.5 (ignoring the minus sign): D.\nDemand is inelastic because 0.5 is less than 1. The trap: B (2.0) comes from turning the fraction upside down (40% ÷ 20%), a slip students really make. Memory tip: Quantity on top, Price below: Q over P.",
 "|If the price of an item increases by 8% while the quantity demanded falls from 1500 units to 1492 units, the demand is said to be|perfectly elastic|inelastic|elastic|perfectly inelastic": "Find the percentage change in quantity and compare it with the 8% change in price.\nStep 1: Fall in quantity = 1500 − 1492 = 8 units.\nStep 2: Percentage change in quantity = 8/1500 × 100 ≈ 0.53%.\nStep 3: PED = 0.53% ÷ 8% ≈ 0.07.\nThis is less than 1, so demand is inelastic: B. It is not perfectly inelastic, because quantity did change a little; perfect inelasticity means zero change in quantity. The trap: D (perfectly inelastic) tempts students because the change is tiny, but any change at all rules it out. Memory tip: PED below 1 = inelastic; exactly 0 = perfectly inelastic.",
 "|Comparison of interpersonal utility is impossible because|utility is measured in units|marginal utility is not observable|utility is subjectively determined|individual income differ": "Utility is the satisfaction a person gets from consuming a good. It is a feeling in the mind, so it is subjective: a plate of jollof rice may give one student great satisfaction and another very little. Because there is no common, objective unit for measuring satisfaction across people, we cannot say that one person's utility is greater than another's: interpersonal comparison of utility is impossible: C. Option A is false, because utility cannot be measured in real units (the \"utils\" of cardinal theory are imaginary). Different incomes (D) affect what people can buy, not whether their satisfaction can be compared. Memory tip: satisfaction lives in each person's head, so no one else can measure it.",
 "|When the slope of the total utility curve is declining, the marginal utility of a consumer will be increasing if he|stops consuming more of the commodity|reduces the quantity consumed|increases the quantity consumed|consumes more of another commodity": "Total utility (TU) is the whole satisfaction from all units consumed; marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from one more unit, which is the slope of the TU curve. When the slope of TU is declining, each extra unit adds less than the one before: this is the law of diminishing marginal utility. Running this backwards, if the consumer cuts the quantity he consumes, the last unit he now takes gives him more satisfaction than before, so MU rises: B. Increasing the quantity (C) makes MU fall further, and stopping (A) does not raise MU. Consuming another commodity (D) does not change MU along this curve. Memory tip: the fewer you have, the more the next one is worth.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nThe consumer is at equilibrium at point|N|P|M|K": "An indifference curve joins combinations of X and Y that give equal satisfaction, and higher curves give more satisfaction. The budget line shows what the consumer can afford. The consumer is in equilibrium on the highest indifference curve he can reach, which is where the budget line just touches (is tangent to) a curve: point K. There the slope of the indifference curve (MRS) equals the price ratio. N is affordable but lies on a lower curve where the budget line cuts it. P is inside the budget line, so some income is unspent. M lies beyond the budget line, so it cannot be afforded: D. Memory tip: equilibrium is where the budget line Kisses the highest curve.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nTo move from point M to K, the consumer has to increase the|consumption of X and reduce the consumption of Y|expenditure on Y|expenditure on X|consumption of Y and reduce the consumption of X": "Read the positions on the diagram. M lies far to the right and low down: it has a lot of X and little Y. K lies higher up and to the left: it has more Y and less X. So to move from M to K the consumer must increase his consumption of Y and reduce his consumption of X: D. Option A describes the opposite movement, from K to M. Options B and C talk of expenditure; the question is about the bundle of goods consumed, which is read off the two axes. Memory tip: up the diagram = more Y; left on the diagram = less X.",
 "|The supply of cocoa is influenced by|seasonal conditions|the efficacy of fertilizer used|the demand for beverages|the availability of close substitutes": "The supply of farm produce depends above all on natural conditions. Cocoa needs steady rainfall, warmth and humidity; a long dry season or a harsh harmattan cuts the crop, while good rains raise it. So seasonal conditions influence the supply of cocoa: A. The efficacy of the fertilizer used (B) also influences supply, because a better fertilizer raises the yield per hectare, so B is accepted too. The demand for beverages (C) and the availability of close substitutes (D) affect the demand for cocoa, not its supply. Memory tip: supply of crops = weather + inputs; demand = buyers' tastes and substitutes.",
 "|If quantity supplied is constant irrespective of price changes, the supply elasticity is|unitary|infinity|fairly elastic|perfectly inelastic": "Elasticity of supply measures how much quantity supplied responds to a change in price. If quantity supplied stays exactly the same however much price changes, the response is zero: elasticity of supply = 0, and supply is perfectly inelastic: D. The supply curve is then a vertical line. Examples are land in a fixed area, seats in a stadium on match day, or fish already landed on a given day. Infinity (B) is the opposite extreme, perfectly elastic supply, a horizontal curve. Unitary (A) means quantity changes in exactly the same proportion as price. Memory tip: vertical = no response = zero = perfectly inelastic.",
 "|The cost elasticity of supply is a useful instrument for measuring|profit|productivity|national income|price index": "Cost elasticity of supply compares the percentage change in total cost with the percentage change in output. If output can be raised by a larger proportion than costs, inputs are being used more productively, and the firm is enjoying economies of scale; if costs rise faster than output, productivity per unit of input is falling. So this measure is a useful instrument for judging productivity, how efficiently inputs are turned into output: B. It does not measure profit (A), which also needs revenue, nor national income (C) or a price index (D), which are economy-wide measures. Memory tip: cost against output tells you how productive you are.",
 "|The invisible hand promotes the interests of|consumers|society|government|producers": "Adam Smith, in \"The Wealth of Nations\" (1776), argued that in a free market each person seeks his own gain: the baker bakes to earn a living, not out of kindness. Yet through competition and the price system, this self-interest is guided, \"as if by an invisible hand\", to produce what people want at the lowest cost. The result is that the interest of society as a whole is promoted: B. Consumers (A) and producers (D) are part of society, but the point of the idea is that private self-interest benefits everyone together. The invisible hand works without government direction, so C is wrong. Memory tip: Smith's hand is invisible, but its gift is to Society.",
 "|Fixing price above equilibrium will cause|demand and supply to remain constant|an increase in quantity supplied|an increase in supply|a decrease in quantity supplied": "A price fixed above equilibrium is a minimum (floor) price. At this higher price, sellers move up along their supply curve and offer more for sale: an increase in quantity supplied: B. At the same time buyers move up their demand curve and buy less, so a surplus (excess supply) appears, as with a guaranteed minimum price for farmers. The trap: C (an increase in supply) means the whole supply curve shifts to the right, which a price change cannot cause; a price change only moves sellers along the curve. Memory tip: price above equilibrium → more Quantity supplied, less Quantity demanded → surplus.",
 "|An important function of the price system is to|ensure that producers' profits remain high|guarantee full employment of resources|allocate resources to most productive uses|protect the economic interests of government": "In a free market, prices act as signals. When consumers want more of a good, its price rises, profits in that line rise, and firms move labour, capital and land into producing it. When a good is less wanted, its price and profits fall and resources move out. In this way the price system allocates scarce resources to the uses that consumers value most and where they earn the highest return: C. The price system does not guarantee high profits (A) or full employment (B), as recessions show, and protecting government interests (D) is not its job. Memory tip: price is a traffic light: it tells resources where to go.",
 "|If all factors are variable in the long run, firms will experience|decreasing returns to scale|increasing returns to scale|diminishing returns|economies of scale": "In the short run at least one factor is fixed, so adding a variable factor eventually gives diminishing returns (C). In the long run all factors are variable, so the firm can change its whole scale of production, and the result is called returns to scale. As a firm first expands its scale, specialisation, better machines and bulk buying usually make output rise more than in proportion to inputs: increasing returns to scale: B. If it keeps growing, it may later meet constant and then decreasing returns (A) as management becomes harder. Economies of scale (D) describe falling cost per unit, the cost side of the same story. Memory tip: Short run = diminishing returns; Long run = returns to Scale.",
 "|The equilibrium point of a firm is attained at the point where the isoquant is|greater than the isocost|less than the isocost|tangent to the isocost|greater than the output": "An isoquant joins all combinations of two inputs, say labour and capital, that produce the same output. An isocost line joins all combinations of the inputs the firm can buy with a given outlay. The firm is in equilibrium (producing at least cost) at the point where the isocost line just touches the isoquant: they are tangent: C. At that point the slope of the isoquant (the marginal rate of technical substitution) equals the ratio of the input prices. This mirrors consumer equilibrium, where the budget line is tangent to an indifference curve. \"Greater than\" or \"less than\" (A, B) have no meaning for two curves. Memory tip: producer and consumer both find equilibrium where the lines just touch.",
 "|The long-run average cost curve touches the short-run average cost curves at the|minimum points of all short-run average cost curves|declining points of all short-run average cost curves|minimum point of only one of the short-run cost curves|rising points of all short-run average cost curves": "The long-run average cost (LRAC) curve is an \"envelope\" curve: for each output it shows the lowest cost from the best plant size, and it is tangent to each short-run average cost (SRAC) curve. Where the LRAC is falling (economies of scale), it touches each SRAC on that curve's falling side. Where the LRAC is rising, it touches each SRAC on its rising side. Only at the lowest point of the LRAC does it touch an SRAC at that curve's minimum, for one optimum plant: C. The simpler textbook picture in which the LRAC joins the minimum points of all SRACs (A) is often taught, but it is exact only when the LRAC is flat. Memory tip: the envelope touches each curve once, at the bottom only for the best plant.",
 "|If a firm doubles all inputs in the long run and the total output is less than doubled, this results in|diminishing returns|constant returns to scale|increasing returns to scale|decreasing returns to scale": "Returns to scale describe what happens to output when all inputs are changed in the same proportion in the long run. If inputs double and output more than doubles: increasing returns to scale. If output exactly doubles: constant returns to scale. If output rises by less than double: decreasing returns to scale: D. The trap: A (diminishing returns) sounds similar but belongs to the short run, when only one input is varied while others stay fixed. Decreasing returns to scale often come from the difficulty of managing and coordinating a very large firm. Memory tip: double the inputs and compare: more = increasing, same = constant, less = decreasing.",
 "|Patents and copyrights enable monopolists to|determine the quality of their products|determine the scale of their products|restrict information flow to new firms|restrict entry of new firms": "A monopoly survives only if new firms cannot enter the industry and compete away its profits. Patents (for inventions) and copyrights (for books, music and software) give the holder the sole legal right to make, use or sell the product for a period. Other firms are therefore legally barred from producing it, so patents and copyrights restrict the entry of new firms: D. Other barriers to entry include sole control of a raw material, huge start-up costs and government licences. They do not decide the quality (A) or the scale (B) of output, and they protect ideas rather than restrict general information (C). Memory tip: patent = a legal lock on the industry's door.",
 "|A discriminatory monopoly is characterized by|a common elasticity in different markets|different elasticities in different markets|a finite elasticity in all markets|zero elasticity in all markets": "A discriminating monopolist sells the same product at different prices in different markets, for reasons not based on cost differences. Two conditions make this profitable: the markets must be kept separate so goods cannot be resold from the cheap market to the dear one, and the elasticity of demand must differ between the markets. The monopolist then charges a higher price where demand is inelastic and a lower price where it is elastic: B. If elasticity were the same in every market (A), price discrimination would not raise profit. Examples are cheaper fares for students, or different charges for electricity to homes and to industries. Memory tip: different elasticities, different prices.",
 "|Net National Product is derived by deducting|net exports from GNP|subsidies from GDP|taxes from GDP|depreciation from GNP": "Gross National Product (GNP) is the total value of goods and services produced by a country's citizens and their resources in a year. During production, machines and buildings wear out. The value of this wear and tear is called depreciation, or capital consumption allowance. Net National Product = GNP − depreciation: D. NNP is the value of output available after replacing worn-out capital. Deducting indirect taxes and adding subsidies to NNP at market price gives NNP at factor cost, which is national income. Net exports (A) are added when moving from domestic to national figures, not deducted to get NNP. Memory tip: Net = Gross minus wear and tear.",
 "|The investment expenditure of an economy changes by ₦2 million and MPC is 0.75.\n\nWhat is the change in income?|₦0.5m|₦1.5m|₦2.6m|₦8.0m": "The investment multiplier shows how many times income rises for a change in investment, because each round of spending becomes someone else's income, which is partly spent again.\nStep 1: Multiplier (k) = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4.\nStep 2: Change in income = k × change in investment = 4 × ₦2 million = ₦8 million: D.\nThe trap: B (₦1.5m) comes from multiplying the investment by the MPC (0.75 × ₦2m), which gives only the first round of extra spending. Memory tip: multiplier = 1 ÷ MPS; then times the injection.",
 "|The investment expenditure of an economy changes by ₦2 million and MPC is 0.75.\n\nThe multiplier is|8|4|3|2": "The multiplier depends on how much of each extra naira of income people spend again.\nStep 1: MPC = 0.75, so the marginal propensity to save (MPS) = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25.\nStep 2: Multiplier = 1 ÷ MPS = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4: B.\nSo every ₦1 of new investment raises income by ₦4. The higher the MPC, the larger the multiplier: with MPC 0.8 it would be 5, with MPC 0.5 only 2. Memory tip: k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC).",
 "|The money that commands a higher market value than its face value is called|paper money|standard money|commodity money|fiat money": "Money can be compared in two ways: its face value (the value written on it) and its market or intrinsic value (what the material is worth as a commodity). Commodity money, such as gold and silver coins, cowries or salt in earlier times, is valuable as a commodity in its own right. When the metal in a coin becomes worth more than the figure stamped on it, its market value exceeds its face value, and people may melt or hoard such coins (Gresham's law): C. Paper and fiat money (A, D) are token money: the paper is worth far less than its face value. Standard money (B) has a face value equal to its intrinsic value. Memory tip: commodity money can be worth more than it says; token money is worth less.",
 "|If a basket of commodities cost ₦120 in the base year and ₦240 in the current year, calculate the price index.|100|200|240|300": "A simple price index compares the cost of a basket of goods in the current year with its cost in the base year, with the base year set at 100.\nStep 1: Price index = (current-year cost ÷ base-year cost) × 100.\nStep 2: = (₦240 ÷ ₦120) × 100 = 2 × 100 = 200: B.\nAn index of 200 means prices have doubled, a rise of 100% since the base year. The trap: C (240) is simply the current cost in naira, not an index. Memory tip: base year = 100; divide new by old, then multiply by 100.",
 "|The minimum amount which banks are required to deposit with the central bank is determined by the|liquidity ratio|cash reserve ratio|minimum lending rate|aggregate credit ceiling": "The Central Bank of Nigeria requires every commercial bank to keep a fixed percentage of its deposits as cash in an account with the CBN. This percentage is the cash reserve ratio (CRR): B. Raising the CRR leaves banks with less money to lend, which reduces the money supply and fights inflation; lowering it does the opposite. The trap: A (liquidity ratio) is closely related but different: it is the share of deposits a bank must hold in liquid assets such as cash and treasury bills, kept by the bank itself, not deposited with the central bank. Memory tip: Cash Reserve = Cash kept at the Central bank.",
 "|The major function of money market is to|provide funds for long-term financing|provide funds for short-term financing|stabilize the value of the local currency|stabilize domestic prices": "The financial market has two parts. The money market deals in short-term funds, usually for less than a year: call money, treasury bills, commercial papers, certificates of deposit and short loans between banks. Its major function is to provide funds for short-term financing of governments, banks and businesses: B. The capital market (the stock exchange, issuing houses and development banks) provides long-term funds (A) through shares, bonds and long-term loans. Stabilising the currency and domestic prices (C, D) are aims of the central bank's monetary policy, not the main function of the money market. Memory tip: Money market = Months; Capital market = years.",
 "|An ad valorem tax is imposed on|special commodities|exports|imports|the value of a commodity": "\"Ad valorem\" is Latin for \"according to value\". An ad valorem tax is charged as a percentage of the value (price) of a commodity, so the tax paid rises as the price rises: D. Value Added Tax (VAT), charged in Nigeria at 7.5% of the value of goods and services, is the best-known example. It differs from a specific tax, which is a fixed sum per unit (for example, ₦10 on each bottle) whatever the price. Ad valorem taxes may be placed on imports or exports (B, C), but what makes them ad valorem is that they are based on value. Memory tip: ad Valorem = by Value, a percentage.",
 "|A huge national debt is an indication that the gold reserves of a nation has|appreciated|decreased|depreciated|stagnated": "A country's reserves (gold and foreign exchange) are what it uses to pay for imports and service its foreign debts. When a nation's spending abroad persistently exceeds its earnings, it draws down its reserves and then has to borrow. A huge national debt therefore usually goes with reserves that have been run down, that is, decreased: B. \"Appreciated\" and \"depreciated\" (A, C) describe changes in the value of a currency, not the quantity of reserves held. Rising debt payments also drain reserves further, which is why heavily indebted countries often face foreign exchange shortages. Memory tip: debt up, reserves down.",
 "|Rapid economic development in Nigeria is realizable by|continuous dependence on oil|concentrating more on agriculture|developing the tourism industry|diversifying the economy": "Nigeria is a mono-product economy: crude oil provides most export earnings and a large part of government revenue. When world oil prices fall, as in 1986, 2015–2016 and 2020, government revenue and foreign exchange collapse and the whole economy suffers. Rapid and stable development therefore needs diversification: developing agriculture, manufacturing, solid minerals, tourism and services so the economy has many sources of income and jobs: D. Options B and C each name only one sector; concentrating on any single sector repeats the problem of over-dependence. Continuous dependence on oil (A) is the problem itself. Memory tip: do not put all your eggs in one basket.",
 "|The primary reason for desiring economic growth is to|control inflation|reduce poverty|redistribute income|raise standard of living": "Economic growth is a sustained rise in a country's real output (real GDP), usually measured per head. People desire it because more goods and services per person allow better food, housing, health and education, so the primary reason is to raise the standard of living: D. Reducing poverty (B) and redistributing income (C) are related aims, but growth by itself does not guarantee them; they are parts of what a higher standard of living means. Controlling inflation (A) is a separate goal of macroeconomic policy. The trap: B (reduce poverty) is very close, but it is one part of the wider aim of raising living standards. Memory tip: growth's goal = better living for all.",
 "|An emerging agricultural export crop in Nigeria is|cassava|cotton|cocoa|soya beans": "Nigeria's traditional export crops are cocoa, groundnuts, palm produce, cotton and rubber, which earned most foreign exchange before oil. Cassava was long grown only for food at home. Nigeria became the world's largest cassava producer, and from the early 2000s the Presidential Initiative on Cassava and later programmes promoted cassava chips, starch, flour and ethanol for export, making cassava an emerging export crop: A. Cotton and cocoa (B, C) are old, established export crops, not emerging ones. Soya beans (D) are grown mainly for local food and feed. Memory tip: cassava moved from the kitchen to the export market.",
 "|A strategy for improving agriculture in Nigeria will involve|controlling the prices of agricultural products|ensuring self-sufficiency in food production|reducing agricultural exports|establishing commodity boards": "Commodity boards (marketing boards) buy farm produce from farmers, grade and store it, and sell it at home or abroad. In theory they stabilise farmers' prices, provide storage, fund research and improve quality, so establishing commodity boards is a strategy for improving agriculture: D. Ensuring self-sufficiency in food (B) is a desirable goal, but it describes the result wanted rather than a strategy for getting there. Controlling prices of farm products (A) usually discourages farmers, and reducing exports (C) cuts their income. Nigeria's commodity boards were scrapped in 1986 under the Structural Adjustment Programme; today farm produce is marketed mainly by private buyers. Memory tip: a strategy is a means; self-sufficiency is an end.",
 "|A sugar industry is best located near the source of|labour|raw materials|power|capital": "Industries are located to keep costs low. Sugar cane is bulky and heavy, loses most of its weight when processed into sugar, and must be crushed soon after cutting before its sugar content falls. It is far cheaper to carry the finished sugar than the cane, so sugar factories are built near the source of raw materials, the cane plantations: B. This is why Nigeria's sugar companies, such as those at Bacita in Kwara State and Numan in Adamawa State, sit beside their estates. Industries whose products gain weight or are fragile, such as bottling, tend to locate near their markets instead. Memory tip: weight-losing raw material → locate at the source.",
 "|An important contribution of small-scale industries to the Nigerian economy is in the area of|technological development|foreign exchange earnings|raw materials processing|labour employment": "Small-scale industries include tailoring, welding, bakeries, furniture making, food processing and hairdressing. They use simple, labour-intensive methods, need little capital to start, and are spread across towns and villages. Their most important contribution to the Nigerian economy is therefore employment: they absorb a large share of the labour force, including school leavers and people with few skills: D. They also process some local raw materials (C) and train entrepreneurs, but their main role is job creation. They earn little foreign exchange (B) and contribute modestly to advanced technology (A). Memory tip: small firms, big employers.",
 "|A change in the pump price of petrol in Nigeria has a direct effect on the|prices of consumer goods|prices of essential goods|cost of raw materials|cost of transportation": "Petrol (premium motor spirit) is the main fuel for cars, buses, motorcycles and many trucks in Nigeria. When its pump price changes, transport fares and freight charges change at once, so the direct effect is on the cost of transportation: D. The prices of consumer goods, essential goods and raw materials (A, B, C) also change, but indirectly, because moving goods to market costs more or less. In 2023 the petrol subsidy was removed and pump prices rose sharply; transport fares rose first, then food and other prices. Memory tip: petrol hits transport first, then everything transport carries.",
 "|Long-term funds for investment projects are sourced from the|money market|commodity market|foreign exchange market|capital market": "Investment projects such as factories, roads and housing estates need funds that will not be repaid for many years. Such long-term funds come from the capital market: through the stock exchange, firms raise money by issuing shares and bonds, and development banks and insurance companies provide long-term loans: D. The money market (A) provides only short-term funds, usually under a year. The commodity market (B) trades goods such as cocoa or grains, and the foreign exchange market (C) trades currencies. The Nigerian Stock Exchange is now the Nigerian Exchange (NGX). Memory tip: Long-term funds come from the Capital market.",
 "|A policy aimed at enhancing globalization of the Nigerian economy is|indigenization|deregulation|commercialization|privatization": "Globalization is the growing integration of a country's economy with the world economy through free trade, capital flows and foreign investment. Deregulation means removing government controls over prices, interest rates, exchange rates, imports and entry into industries, allowing market forces to work. By opening sectors such as telecommunications, banking and petroleum to foreign and private firms, deregulation links the Nigerian economy with world markets: B. Indigenization (A) does the opposite: the 1972 and 1977 decrees reserved businesses for Nigerians and limited foreign ownership. Privatization and commercialization (C, D) change who owns or runs public enterprises. Memory tip: deregulation opens the doors; indigenization closes them.",
 "|The Malthusian theory was concerned about the relationship between|population growth rates of the rich and the poor nations|population density and national income|population growth rate and natural resources|age distribution of population": "Thomas Robert Malthus, an English clergyman and economist, published his \"Essay on the Principle of Population\" in 1798. He argued that population tends to grow geometrically (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), while food supply from a fixed area of land grows only arithmetically (1, 2, 3, 4, 5), because of diminishing returns. Population would therefore outrun the means of subsistence, and positive checks such as famine, disease and war would bring it back down, unless people practised preventive checks like late marriage. His theory was thus about population growth rate and natural resources (food): C. Memory tip: Malthus = mouths versus food.",
 "|In a village of 50 persons, 10 immigrated, 25 died and 5 emigrated in a year. Determine the total population.|10|20|30|50": "Population changes through four movements: births and immigration add people; deaths and emigration remove them.\nStep 1: Start with 50 persons.\nStep 2: Add immigrants: 50 + 10 = 60.\nStep 3: Subtract deaths: 60 − 25 = 35.\nStep 4: Subtract emigrants: 35 − 5 = 30: C.\nNo births are given, so none are added. The trap: A (10) comes from subtracting the 10 immigrants as if they had left (50 − 10 − 25 − 5 = 10); immigrants come in, emigrants go out. Memory tip: IMmigrants come IN; Emigrants Exit.",
 "|A deficit balance of payments is measured by subtracting the debits from the credits in the|current account|current and capital accounts|current and escrow accounts|capital and escrow accounts": "The balance of payments records all transactions between a country and the rest of the world. Credits are receipts (exports, money flowing in); debits are payments (imports, money flowing out). The current account covers visible and invisible trade and transfers; the capital account covers investment and loans. The overall balance is found by taking debits from credits across the current and capital accounts together: B. If debits exceed credits, there is a deficit, financed from reserves or borrowing. \"Escrow accounts\" (C, D) are not part of the balance of payments. Memory tip: overall BOP = current + capital.",
 "|One of the functions of ECOWAS is to|provide funds for infrastructural development|determine prices of exports|redistribute income among citizens|provide employment for citizens of member nations": "The Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) was formed by the Treaty of Lagos in 1975 to promote economic integration among West African countries. It set up the ECOWAS Fund for Cooperation, Compensation and Development, now part of the ECOWAS Bank for Investment and Development (EBID), to finance regional projects such as roads, telecommunications and power. So one of its functions is to provide funds for infrastructural development: A. ECOWAS does not fix export prices (B), redistribute income among citizens (C) or provide jobs for citizens (D); member governments do these things. Memory tip: ECOWAS builds bridges, both roads and links between nations.",
 "|The reward on machinery and equipment in the process of production is known as|profit|interest|rent|wages": "Each factor of production earns a reward. Land earns rent, labour earns wages, the entrepreneur earns profit, and capital earns interest. Machinery and equipment are capital goods, man-made aids to production, so the reward for their use is interest: B. The trap: C (rent) tempts students because machines can be hired out, but in the factor-reward list rent is the reward for land. Memory tip: Land–Rent, Labour–Wages, Capital–Interest, Entrepreneur–Profit: \"Real Workers Inspire Progress\".",
 "|From this diagram, the minimum wage OW will lead to|full employment of resources|unemployment|inflation|full employment of factors of production": "The diagram shows the labour market: DD is the demand for labour and SS is the supply of labour. They cross at the wage OW, the equilibrium wage. If the minimum wage is fixed at OW, the quantity of labour demanded equals the quantity supplied, so everyone willing to work at that wage finds a job: there is full employment: D. Labour is both a resource and a factor of production, so A says the same thing and is accepted too. Unemployment (B) would appear only if the minimum wage were fixed above OW, where supply exceeds demand. Memory tip: minimum wage at equilibrium clears the market; above it creates unemployment.",
 "|An economy in which both the public and private sectors contribute to economic growth is known as|feudal economy|capitalist economy|socialist economy|mixed economy": "Economies are grouped by who owns resources and who makes production decisions. In a capitalist economy private individuals and firms own most resources and the price mechanism guides production. In a socialist economy the state owns the main means of production and a planning authority decides. A mixed economy combines the two: the private sector (firms such as Dangote or the banks) and the public sector (government enterprises, roads, schools) both produce and both contribute to growth. Nigeria is a mixed economy: D. A feudal economy is the old system in which lords owned the land and serfs worked it. Memory tip: Mixed = Market + Ministry working side by side.",
 "|If the standard deviation of a given data is 53, what is the variance?|2082|2809|2808|2209": "Variance and standard deviation both measure how spread out data are. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, so the variance is the standard deviation squared.\nStep 1: Variance = (standard deviation)² = 53².\nStep 2: 53 × 53 = 50 × 53 + 3 × 53 = 2650 + 159 = 2809: B.\nThe trap: C (2808) is one less than the correct figure, set to catch a careless multiplication; D (2209) is 47², a slip in squaring. Memory tip: SD is the root; variance is the square.",
 "|Which of the following set of statistical tools is used for further economic analysis?|the median and standard deviation|the mean and mode|the mean and standard deviation|the mode and median": "For further statistical and economic analysis (comparing sets of data, testing theories, regression, index work) we need measures that use every value in the data and can be worked on with algebra. The arithmetic mean (Σx ÷ n) does this for the average, and the standard deviation does it for the spread: it is built from every deviation from the mean. The median and the mode only pick out a middle or most common value, so they cannot be combined algebraically in the same way. So the set used for further analysis is the mean and the standard deviation: C. Memory tip: mean and SD are the \"algebra-friendly\" pair.",
 "|An advantage of the range as a measure of dispersion is that it|can be used to calculate open-ended distribution|makes use of all values of observations in a distribution|takes all values into consideration|is useful for further statistical calculation": "The range is the simplest measure of dispersion: highest value − lowest value. Its real advantages are that it is quick to calculate and easy to understand. Its weaknesses are many: it uses only the two extreme values, not all the observations (so B and C are false); it cannot be found for an open-ended distribution such as \"60 and above\" (so A is false); and it cannot be used in further statistical calculations, where the standard deviation is used (so D is also not true). D is the answer intended, but none of the options is fully correct. Memory tip: the range is \"rough and ready\": simple, but it sees only the two ends.",
 "|Find the median of the following set of data 35, 10, 14, 38, 15, 18, 22, 30 and 28.|10|38|35|22": "The median is the middle value when the data are arranged in order of size.\nStep 1: Arrange the data: 10, 14, 15, 18, 22, 28, 30, 35, 38.\nStep 2: There are 9 values, so the median is the (9 + 1) ÷ 2 = 5th value.\nStep 3: The 5th value is 22: D.\nThe trap: C (35) is the 5th value in the list as printed, before it is put in order, a slip students really make. A (10) and B (38) are the lowest and highest values. Memory tip: Median = Middle, but only after you line them up.",
 "|An increase in demand without a corresponding change in supply will lead to|a decrease in equilibrium price and increase in equilibrium quantity|an increase in equilibrium price and quantity|a decrease in equilibrium price and quantity|an increase in equilibrium price and a decrease in equilibrium quantity": "Equilibrium is where the demand and supply curves cross. If demand increases (because of higher income, say) while supply stays the same, the demand curve shifts to the right. At the old price there is now excess demand, so buyers bid the price up, and sellers respond by moving up the supply curve and supplying more. At the new equilibrium both the price and the quantity are higher: B. A fall in both (C) follows a decrease in demand; a lower price with a higher quantity (A) follows an increase in supply. Memory tip: demand pulls price and quantity the same way; supply pushes them opposite ways.",
 "|An increase in the price of a commodity will result in|a decrease in the quantity demanded|an increase in demand|an increase in quantity demanded|a decrease in demand": "The law of demand says that, other things being equal, when the price of a commodity rises, the quantity demanded falls. This is a movement up along the same demand curve, called a decrease (contraction) in quantity demanded: A. A change in demand (B, D) means the whole curve shifts, and that is caused by something other than the good's own price, such as income or tastes. The trap: D (a decrease in demand) uses the closely related term students often use loosely, but \"demand\" refers to a shift of the whole curve. Memory tip: own price → Quantity demanded (move along); anything else → Demand (shift).",
 "|If the price of a bicycle changes from ₦120 to ₦80 and quantity bought changes from 300 to 500 units, the elasticity of demand for bicycle is|66.7|0.5|1.5|2.0": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price, using the original figures as the base.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 500 − 300 = 200 units; percentage change = 200/300 × 100 = 66.7%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦120 − ₦80 = ₦40; percentage change = 40/120 × 100 = 33.3%.\nStep 3: PED = 66.7% ÷ 33.3% = 2.0 (ignoring the minus sign): D.\nDemand is elastic because 2.0 is greater than 1. The trap: A (66.7) is only the percentage change in quantity, where students stop before dividing; B (0.5) comes from turning the fraction upside down. Memory tip: Quantity on top, Price below.",
 "|Use the graph above to answer the question. \nFrom the graph above, the price elasticity of demand is|unitarily elastic|perfectly elastic|perfectly inelastic|fairly inelastic": "The shape of a demand curve shows its elasticity. Here the demand curve D is a vertical line at quantity OQ: whatever the price, buyers take exactly the same quantity. A change in price causes no change in quantity demanded, so PED = 0% ÷ any % = 0. Demand is perfectly inelastic: C. Examples close to this are life-saving drugs such as insulin. A horizontal demand curve would be perfectly elastic (B), and a rectangular hyperbola would be unitary elastic (A). Memory tip: vertical = \"I\" for Inelastic; horizontal = flat-out elastic.",
 "|One of the assumptions of the cardinalist approach is|diminishing marginal rate of substitution|the consistency and transitivity of choice|that total utility depends on the quantity of the commodities consumed|unstable marginal utility of money": "There are two approaches to consumer behaviour. The cardinal (marginal utility) approach of Marshall assumes utility can be measured in units (\"utils\"), that total utility depends on the quantities of the commodities consumed, that marginal utility diminishes, and that the marginal utility of money is constant. So total utility depending on the quantities consumed is a cardinalist assumption: C. D is wrong because they assume the marginal utility of money is constant, not unstable. The ordinal (indifference curve) approach assumes consumers can only rank choices, with consistency and transitivity of choice (B) and a diminishing marginal rate of substitution (A). The trap: B sounds like a general assumption, but it belongs to the ordinal school. Memory tip: Cardinal = Counting utils; Ordinal = Ordering choices.",
 "|Utility is the satisfaction derived from the|distribution of goods and services|use of goods and services|demand of goods and services|production of goods and services": "Utility is the satisfaction or pleasure a consumer gets from using (consuming) a good or service. A glass of cold water gives utility when you drink it on a hot afternoon; it is the use of the good that brings the satisfaction: B. Production (D) and distribution (A) are what firms and traders do to make goods available, and demand (C) is the desire to buy backed by the ability to pay. Utility is personal: the same plate of food gives different satisfaction to different people. Memory tip: Utility comes from Use.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nFrom the indifference curve above, consumer will prefer combination|F|S|M|K": "An indifference curve joins combinations of two goods that give the consumer the same satisfaction, so all points on one curve are equally preferred. A curve further from the origin represents more of both goods, and so a higher level of satisfaction. In the diagram, K and F lie on I1, the lowest curve; M and N lie on I2; and S lies on I3, the highest curve. The consumer therefore prefers combination S to any of the others: B. Memory tip: the higher (further out) the curve, the happier the consumer.",
 "|One of the major factors that brings about changes in supply is|market discrimination|availability of storage facilities|the cost of storage|incentives granted to workers": "Supply is affected by things other than the good's own price: cost of production, technology, weather, government policy, the prices of other goods and, for farm produce and many goods, the availability of storage facilities. Where good storage (silos, warehouses, cold rooms) exists, producers can hold back stocks when prices are low and release them steadily, so the quantity supplied to the market at each price changes: B. Market discrimination (A) is a pricing practice, and incentives to workers (D) affect productivity only indirectly. The trap: C (the cost of storage) sounds very close, but it is only one item in the cost of production, while the availability of storage is the factor usually listed. Memory tip: no storage, no steady supply.",
 "|If P = 1/4(Qs + 10). What is the quantity supplied at ₦14?|14|60|46|32": "The supply equation links price (P) and quantity supplied (Qs).\nStep 1: Put P = ₦14 into P = ¼(Qs + 10): 14 = ¼(Qs + 10).\nStep 2: Multiply both sides by 4: 56 = Qs + 10.\nStep 3: Qs = 56 − 10 = 46: C.\nCheck: ¼ × (46 + 10) = ¼ × 56 = 14. ✓\nThe trap: A (14) simply repeats the price, and D (32) comes from subtracting 10 before multiplying by 4. Memory tip: undo the steps in reverse order: first × 4, then − 10.",
 "|If the supply of a product is elastic, a small reduction in price will|reduce the cost of production|reduce the quantity supplied|increase the quantity supplied|lead to no change in the quantity supplied": "The law of supply says price and quantity supplied move in the same direction: a fall in price reduces the quantity supplied. Elasticity tells us by how much. When supply is elastic (elasticity greater than 1), the percentage change in quantity supplied is larger than the percentage change in price. So a small reduction in price leads to a large reduction in the quantity supplied: B. A cut in price does not reduce the cost of production (A), and \"no change\" (D) would describe perfectly inelastic supply. The trap: C (increase the quantity supplied) mixes up supply with demand, where a fall in price raises the quantity bought. Memory tip: supply follows price; elasticity tells you how far.",
 "|The supply of beverages by firms in a monopolistic market is an example of|derived demand|competitive supply|composite supply|joint demand": "In a monopolistic (monopolistically competitive) market, many firms sell similar but differentiated products, such as the beverage brands Milo, Bournvita and Ovaltine. Each firm's brand is a close substitute for the others, so the firms compete for the same buyers, and the supply of beverages by these rival firms is competitive supply: B. Composite supply (C) describes a single want that is met from several different kinds of source, such as energy from electricity, gas and kerosene. Derived demand (A) and joint demand (D) are about demand, not supply. Memory tip: rival brands, rival suppliers: competitive supply.",
 "|If the price of a commodity is fixed below equilibrium, this will lead to|excess demand|a decrease in price|an increase in price|excess supply": "A price fixed by government below the equilibrium price is a maximum (ceiling) price. At this lower price consumers want to buy more, while producers are willing to supply less. Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, so there is excess demand, or a shortage: A. This is why price control often leads to queues, rationing and black markets, as happened with petrol in Nigeria. Excess supply (D) results from a minimum price set above equilibrium. The trap: D is the result of the opposite control, the minimum price above equilibrium. Memory tip: ceiling below equilibrium → shortage; floor above equilibrium → surplus.",
 "|One of the criticisms of the price mechanism is that|producers are sovereign|it provides low degree of freedom|it widens the inequitable gap|consumers are sovereign": "The price mechanism lets the forces of demand and supply decide what is produced and who gets it. Its main criticism is that it allocates goods to those who can pay. The rich, with more money \"votes\", get more of what they want, while the poor go without, and those who own property earn more and more. So the price mechanism widens the inequitable gap between rich and poor: C. Consumer sovereignty (D) and freedom of choice are advantages of the price mechanism, not criticisms. Memory tip: the market listens to money, not to need.",
 "|In Nigeria, government can reduce the cost of accommodation by fixing rent|at the prevailing rate|at the equilibrium price|above the equilibrium price|below the equilibrium price": "Rent control is a form of maximum price legislation. To make accommodation cheaper for tenants, government must fix the rent below the equilibrium (market) rent, the rent at which the demand for and supply of houses would be equal: D. Fixing rent at or above equilibrium (A, B, C) would not reduce the cost at all. The side effect is that at the lower rent more people want houses than landlords supply, so there is a shortage of accommodation, and landlords may charge illegal extra fees. Memory tip: to cut the cost, set the ceiling below the market.",
 "|If a refinery achieves a reduction in cost by purchasing and transporting crude oil in large quantities, it enjoys|economies of scale|specialization|division of labour|diseconomies of scale": "Economies of scale are the cost advantages a firm enjoys as it grows larger, so that its average cost per unit falls. Internal economies include technical, managerial, financial, marketing and purchasing economies. Buying crude oil in large quantities (bulk purchase) earns discounts, and transporting it in large quantities (big tankers and pipelines) lowers the cost per barrel. These are economies of scale: A. Division of labour (C) and specialisation (B) concern how work is organised among workers, and diseconomies of scale (D) mean rising average costs. Memory tip: buy big, pay less per unit.",
 "|An isoquant lying above to the right of another represents|a higher output level|constant returns to scale|over-capacity utilization|a lower output level": "An isoquant (equal-product curve) shows all the combinations of two factors, such as labour and capital, that produce the same level of output. A set of isoquants is an isoquant map. An isoquant lying above and to the right of another uses more of both factors, so it represents a higher level of output: A. One closer to the origin represents a lower output (D). Isoquants are to the producer what indifference curves are to the consumer. Memory tip: further from the origin, more output.",
 "|Use the graph above to answer the question. \nFrom the graph 1, R represents|marginal cost|total cost|variable cost|fixed cost": "In a total cost diagram there are three cost curves. Fixed cost (FC) does not change with output, so it is a horizontal line: T. Variable cost (VC) starts at zero output from the origin and rises as output rises: M. Total cost (TC) = FC + VC, so it starts at the level of fixed cost on the vertical axis and runs above the variable cost curve, keeping the same vertical gap (equal to fixed cost) all the way: R. So R represents total cost: B. Marginal cost (A) is a U-shaped per-unit curve, not drawn here. Memory tip: TC starts where FC is and runs parallel to VC.",
 "|Use the graph 2 above to answer the question. \nThe optimum level of output of the firm is|BC|OB|OS|BS": "The diagram shows a firm facing a downward-sloping demand curve D, with marginal revenue MR below it, and its cost curves. A firm maximises profit where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), at point C. Dropping a line from C to the output axis gives output OB, the firm's optimum (best, profit-maximising) output: B. Point S marks the output at the lowest point of the average cost curve, the technically most efficient size; the firm produces less than this, and the gap BS is its excess capacity. The trap: C (OS) is the lowest-cost output, which looks like an \"optimum\", but the firm chooses OB because that is where its profit is greatest. Memory tip: profit's best output is where MC meets MR.",
 "|Use the graph 2 above to answer the question. \nThe total revenue of the firm is represented by|GHEF|GHJC|EFJC|GHOB": "Total revenue = price × quantity. At the profit-maximising output OB (where MC = MR at C), the price is read from the demand curve directly above, at G, and on the price axis it is OH. So total revenue = OH × OB, the rectangle GHOB: D. GHEF (A), GHJC (B) and EFJC (C) are smaller areas lying between the price line and the cost or MR levels; they show profit or parts of cost, not the whole revenue. The trap: A (GHEF) is the area at the top of the diagram that shows the firm's supernormal profit, which students confuse with total revenue. Memory tip: revenue rectangle runs all the way down to the output axis; the profit rectangle stops at average cost.",
 "|A measure of national income used as comparison of standard of living among nations is|net national product|gross domestic product|gross national product|per capita income": "To compare living standards between countries we must allow for the size of each population. Per capita income = national income ÷ population; it shows the average income per person. A country with a large GDP but a huge population, such as Nigeria, may have a lower income per head than a smaller country. So per capita income is the measure used: D. GDP, GNP and NNP (A, B, C) are totals for the whole country and do not tell us how much each person has. Per capita income still hides inequality and non-income factors. Memory tip: per capita = per head.",
 "|The speculative demand for money is inversely related to the|interest rate|level of income|exchange rate|inflation rate": "Keynes gave three motives for holding money: transactions, precautionary and speculative. The speculative motive is holding idle money to take advantage of changes in bond prices. When interest rates are high, bond prices are low, so people use their money to buy bonds and hold little idle cash. When interest rates are low, bond prices are high and are expected to fall, so people hold more money. Speculative demand is therefore inversely related to the interest rate: A. Transactions and precautionary demand depend mainly on the level of income (B). Memory tip: Speculative demand Slides as interest rate rises.",
 "|If Mr. K obtains a ₦50,000 loan from a bank for the purpose of providing household needs, the demand for money is said to be|transactionary|speculative|precautionary and speculative|transactional and speculative": "The transactions motive is the demand for money to pay for day-to-day purchases: food, rent, school fees and other household needs. Mr. K borrows ₦50,000 to provide household needs, so he wants the money to spend on transactions: A. The precautionary motive is holding money for emergencies such as illness, and the speculative motive is holding money to profit from changes in bond prices or interest rates. Nothing in the question points to either of these. Memory tip: Transactions = Today's spending; Precaution = Problems later; Speculation = Shares and bonds.",
 "|Which of the following is used by the Central Bank of Nigeria to control inflation?|Tariff on imports|Tax rate|Exchange rate|Discount rate": "The Central Bank of Nigeria controls inflation through monetary policy, which works on the money supply and the cost of credit. Its tools include the discount rate (bank rate, now called the Monetary Policy Rate), open market operations, cash reserve ratio, liquidity ratio and moral suasion. Raising the discount rate makes borrowing dearer, reduces lending and money supply, and so dampens inflation: D. Tariffs and tax rates (A, B) are fiscal tools set by government, not the CBN. Memory tip: CBN works with money and rates; government works with taxes and spending.",
 "|If CBN reduces money supply, the interest rate will|fluctuate|rise|fall|remain unchanged": "The interest rate is the price of money. It is set by the demand for and the supply of money. If the Central Bank reduces the money supply (for example by selling government securities or raising the cash reserve ratio) while the demand for money stays the same, money becomes scarcer. Borrowers compete for the smaller supply of loanable funds, so the interest rate rises: B. A fall in the interest rate (C) would follow an increase in money supply. Memory tip: less money, dearer money.",
 "|An example of an expansionary fiscal policy action is|decrease in the corporate profit tax rates|decrease in welfare payments|purchase of government securities|decrease in the bank rate": "Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxation to influence the economy. An expansionary fiscal policy aims to raise aggregate demand, output and employment, through more government spending or lower taxes. A decrease in the corporate profit tax rate leaves firms with more after-tax profit to invest and pay out, so it expands demand: A. A decrease in welfare payments (B) would reduce demand, so it is contractionary. Purchasing government securities (C) and cutting the bank rate (D) are expansionary too, but they are monetary policy tools used by the Central Bank. Memory tip: Fiscal = Finance ministry: Taxes and spending.",
 "|A tax on land will ultimately fall|partly on agents and users|entirely on users|entirely on owners|partly on users and owners": "The incidence of a tax is where its burden finally rests. It depends on elasticity: the side of the market that is less elastic bears more of the tax. The supply of land is fixed by nature, so it is perfectly inelastic. A landowner cannot reduce the supply of land to push the price up, and users will not pay more just because the owner is taxed. So the whole burden of a tax on land falls on the owners: C. Memory tip: what cannot run away pays the tax; land cannot run away.",
 "|One of the goals of development plans in Nigeria is to|increase the profitability of multinational businesses|improve the country's GDP|achieve higher standard of living for the citizens|deregulate the economy": "Development plans set out government's goals and strategies for a period. Nigeria has had the First to Fourth National Development Plans (1962–85), rolling plans, Vision 20:2020 and later the Economic Recovery and Growth Plan. Their ultimate goal is to achieve a higher standard of living for the citizens: better incomes, health, education, housing and jobs: C. Raising GDP (B) is a means to that end, since growth without better living is not development. Deregulation (D) is a policy tool, and plans do not exist to raise the profits of multinationals (A). The trap: B (improve the country's GDP) is closely related, but it measures growth, not development. Memory tip: growth is the means; better living is the goal.",
 "|Life insurance companies contribute to economic development by holding a part of their assets in|long-term financial instruments|money market instruments|cash and near money|short-term financial instruments": "Life insurance companies collect premiums over many years and pay out only on death or at the end of a long policy. Because their liabilities are long-term and predictable, they can invest a large part of their funds in long-term financial instruments: government development stocks, bonds, mortgages and company shares. These provide long-term capital for building roads, factories and houses, which is how they contribute to development: A. Holding cash and near money (C) or short-term money market instruments (B, D) suits banks, whose deposits can be withdrawn at short notice. Memory tip: long life policy, long-term investment.",
 "|In order to add value to Nigeria agricultural produce, there is need to|cultivate high breed crops|process them into finished goods|adopt modern storage methods|advertise them in European markets": "Adding value means turning raw produce into goods worth more. Nigeria earns little when it exports raw cocoa, cassava or groundnuts. When the produce is processed into finished goods, such as cocoa into beverages, cassava into starch or ethanol, or groundnuts into oil, the products sell for more, create jobs in factories and earn more foreign exchange. So to add value to farm produce it must be processed into finished goods: B. Improved crops (A) raise output, storage (C) reduces waste, and advertising (D) raises sales, but none of these changes the produce itself. Memory tip: value is added in the factory.",
 "|The main reason for low agricultural produce in west Africa is due to|the presence of large-scale agro-allied industries|high dependency ratio|over dependence on agriculture for subsistence|the use of crude implements in farming process": "Agricultural output in West Africa is low mainly because most farmers are small-scale peasants using crude implements such as hoes and cutlasses. With these simple tools a farmer can cultivate only a small area and the work is slow and tiring, so yields per farmer are low. Other causes include poor storage, lack of credit, poor roads and land tenure problems. So the main reason given is the use of crude implements: D. Large-scale agro-allied industries (A) would raise output, not lower it. Memory tip: hoe and cutlass farming means small farms and small harvests.",
 "|The most important determinant for the location of a brick industry is the availability of|market|power supply|water|raw materials": "Industries are located to keep costs down. When the raw material is bulky, heavy and costly to transport, the industry is sited near the source of the raw material. Bricks are made mainly from clay, which is heavy and found in particular deposits, and a brick works uses large quantities of it. So the most important factor in locating a brick industry is the availability of raw materials: D. Water, power and markets matter too, but they are not the main factor. Cement factories near limestone (as at Ewekoro and Obajana) follow the same rule. Memory tip: heavy raw material, factory close by.",
 "|In Nigeria, efficiency in public corporations can be achieved through|public offer|indigenization|privatization|nationalization": "Nigerian public corporations (NEPA, NITEL, the refineries) were often inefficient because of political interference, overstaffing, lack of profit motive and corruption. Privatization sells all or part of the government's shares in such enterprises to private investors, whose aim of making profit forces them to cut waste and improve service: C. The 2005 sale of NITEL and the 2013 sale of the PHCN power companies (now DisCos and GenCos) were done for this reason. Nationalization (D) is the opposite, taking firms into government ownership. Memory tip: private owners watch costs because the money is theirs.",
 "|Government participation in the oil industry was necessitated by the|annual increase in production|formation of OPEC|high demand for crude oil|huge investment outlay": "The oil industry needs enormous sums of money: seismic surveys, drilling, offshore platforms, pipelines and refineries cost billions of dollars. Few Nigerian private investors could raise such capital, so government took part directly, setting up the Nigerian National Oil Corporation (1971), later the Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation (1977), and taking equity shares in joint ventures with foreign oil companies. So participation was necessitated by the huge investment outlay: D. Today the NNPC has become NNPC Limited, a commercial company, under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021. Memory tip: big oil needs big money.",
 "|The deregulation of the petroleum sector in Nigeria will bring about|efficiency in pricing and distribution of the products|an end to the importation of fuel|an end to foreign firms' dominance|fixing appropriate production quotas": "Deregulation removes government controls over prices, licensing and who may take part in a market. In Nigeria's petroleum sector it means allowing market forces to set pump prices and allowing private firms to import, refine and distribute products. Competition and market pricing encourage efficiency in the pricing and distribution of petroleum products, and reduce scarcity and queues: A. Deregulation does not by itself end fuel importation (B) or foreign dominance (C), and production quotas (D) are set by OPEC. Petrol subsidy was removed in 2023. Memory tip: free the market, and competition drives efficiency.",
 "|A distinguishing characteristic of consumer co-operative society is that the|the maximum number of shareholders is 20|members are the owners|members are the workers|the minimum number of shareholders is 5": "A co-operative society is a voluntary association of people who come together to serve their common interest. In a consumer co-operative, members contribute capital, buy goods in bulk and sell them to themselves at fair prices, sharing any surplus as a dividend in proportion to purchases. The members are therefore the owners of the society as well as its customers: B. Workers (C) are the members of a producer co-operative. A maximum of 20 members (A) is the old limit for a partnership. Memory tip: in a consumer co-op the customers own the shop.",
 "|A major disadvantage of partnership business is|difficulty in the transfer of shares|distrust among members|limited liability|large capital outlay": "A partnership is owned by 2 to 20 people who share capital, management and profits. Its disadvantages include unlimited liability, lack of continuity, difficulty in raising large capital, disagreement and distrust among partners, and difficulty in transferring a partner's interest. Distrust among members (B) is a major weakness, since one partner's action binds all the others. Difficulty in transferring shares (A) is also true, because a partner cannot sell his share without the consent of all the other partners. So both B and A are accepted. Limited liability (C) belongs to companies. Memory tip: partners sink or swim together.",
 "|The quality of labour force in Nigeria can be improved by|establishing more tertiary institutions|creating sufficient job opportunities|encouraging the study of science and technology|establishing more skills acquisition centres": "The quality of labour means its skill, efficiency and productivity, not its size. It is raised by education and training. Skills acquisition centres teach practical, job-ready skills such as welding, tailoring, ICT and catering, which directly improve the efficiency of the labour force: D. More tertiary institutions (A) and the study of science and technology (C) also help in the long run, but skills acquisition is the most direct way of raising the quality of workers. Creating jobs (B) increases employment, not quality. Memory tip: quality of labour comes from training.",
 "|The effect of emigration on a country's population is|decrease in the population|decrease in job opportunities|increase in population|increase in dependency ratio": "Emigration is the movement of people out of their own country to live in another country; immigration is moving into a country. When people emigrate, the population of the country they leave falls: A. If many young adults leave, it can also cause a \"brain drain\", as when Nigerian doctors and nurses move abroad (the \"japa\" wave). An increase in population (C) comes from immigration or natural increase. The trap: C describes immigration, the closely related term that students often mix up with emigration. Memory tip: Emigration = Exit; Immigration = In.",
 "|A measure for preventing the external value of the naira from falling is for the government to|increase its spending with foreign reserve|sell its own currency|reduce interest rate|buy its currency with foreign reserve": "The external value of the naira is its exchange rate against other currencies. It falls when the supply of naira in the foreign exchange market is greater than the demand for it. To stop it falling, the government (through the CBN) can buy naira with its foreign reserves, such as dollars. This raises the demand for the naira and supports its value: D. Selling naira (B) or reducing the interest rate (C) would push its value down further. Memory tip: to hold the naira up, buy the naira.",
 "|A fiscal policy instrument that can influence the demand pattern in an economy is|government spending|interest rate|income tax|tariff": "Fiscal policy uses the government budget: taxation and government spending. Income tax is a fiscal instrument that changes people's disposable income. Lower income tax leaves households more to spend, while higher income tax reduces what they can buy, so it influences the pattern and level of demand in the economy: C. The interest rate (B) is a monetary policy tool of the Central Bank. Progressive income tax also redistributes income, changing what different groups demand. Memory tip: fiscal tools come from the budget: tax and spending.",
 "|One of the main achievements of the Economic Commission for Africa is|eliminating trade restrictions among states|encouraging transport and communication development|guaranteeing a steady flow of foreign investment into Africa|providing the machinery for collaboration on monetary issues": "The Economic Commission for Africa (ECA) is a regional commission of the United Nations set up in 1958, with headquarters in Addis Ababa. It promotes the economic and social development of Africa. Among its achievements are the Trans-African Highway programme, the Pan-African Telecommunications Network (PANAFTEL) and the UN Transport and Communications Decade for Africa (1978–88), so encouraging transport and communication development is a real achievement: B. It also helped found the African Development Bank and supported regional groups such as ECOWAS, but trade restrictions among African states have not been eliminated, so A overstates its success. Memory tip: ECA built the roads and wires that link Africa.",
 "|The rate of interest charged on loans depends largely on|the prevailing exchange rate|marginal efficiency of capital|the risk associated with the loan|the prevailing tax rate": "The interest rate a lender charges on a particular loan is made up of a basic rate plus a premium for risk. The greater the risk that the borrower will default (fail to repay), the higher the rate charged. That is why an unsecured loan to a small business costs more than a loan to the federal government. So the rate of interest charged on loans depends largely on the risk associated with the loan: C. The marginal efficiency of capital (B) affects how much firms want to borrow, not what a bank charges on a given loan. Memory tip: more risk, more interest.",
 "|A valid explanation for real wage growth is|an increase in the rate of productivity|the rising cost of capital accumulation|a contraction of employment in service industries|an increase in the quantity of labour": "Real wages are money wages adjusted for changes in prices, so they measure what workers can actually buy. In the long run, real wages can rise only if workers produce more. When productivity (output per worker per hour) increases, through better skills, machines or technology, firms can afford to pay more without raising prices: A. An increase in the quantity of labour (D) would tend to lower wages, since supply rises. Rising capital costs (B) and fewer jobs (C) do not raise real wages. Memory tip: pay grows when productivity grows.",
 "|If Mr. X lost his clerical job at a store and searched for a similar job for ten months before finding one this implies that Mr. X was|structurally unemployed|frictionally unemployed|seasonally unemployed|cyclically unemployed": "Frictional unemployment is the temporary unemployment of people moving between jobs or searching for a suitable job. Mr. X lost a clerical job and searched until he found a similar clerical job, so his skills were still in demand; he was simply between jobs: B. Structural unemployment (A) happens when skills are no longer needed because the structure of the economy changes. Seasonal unemployment (C) affects workers in jobs tied to seasons, such as farming. Cyclical unemployment (D) comes from a general fall in demand during a recession. The trap: A (structural) tempts students because ten months is a long time, but his job type still exists. Memory tip: Frictional = between jobs; Structural = job type gone.",
 "|The choice of how to produce in a command economy is determined by|government|consumer|industrialists|labour unions": "Every economy must answer three questions: what to produce, how to produce and for whom to produce. In a command (centrally planned or socialist) economy the state owns the main means of production, and a central planning authority set up by the government decides all three, including the methods and techniques of production. Consumers, private industrialists and labour unions have little say; they follow the plan. So the choice of how to produce is made by the government: A. In a capitalist economy, by contrast, private firms choose the cheapest method of production in search of profit. Memory tip: Command = the government Commands; Capitalism = Consumers and firms Choose.",
 "|In capitalist economies, questions about what to produce are ultimately answered by|income level of households|available technical skills in the economy|output decisions of firms|spending decision of households": "In a capitalist (free market) economy the price mechanism answers the question \"what to produce\". Households cast \"money votes\" when they spend: goods that people buy in large quantities become profitable, so firms produce more of them, while goods nobody buys make losses and are dropped. Firms make the output decisions, but they only follow where household spending leads. This is called consumer sovereignty, and it means the final answer comes from the spending decisions of households: D. Household income (A) only limits how much can be spent, and technical skills (B) affect how goods are produced, not which ones. The trap: C (output decisions of firms) catches students because firms do the producing, but they decide only in response to what households buy. Memory tip: in the market, the consumer is king.",
 "|The best measure of dispersion to determine the tallest tree in a forest is|range|variance|standard deviation|mean deviation": "Measures of dispersion show how spread out a set of figures is. The range is the simplest: highest value minus lowest value. Variance, standard deviation and mean deviation measure how far, on average, all the values lie from the mean. To find anything about the tallest tree you must look at the extreme values, and only the range is built from them: range = height of the tallest tree − height of the shortest tree. So the range is the measure that uses the tallest tree: A. The question is oddly worded and B (variance) is the answer often given, but variance averages the spread of every tree and never picks out the tallest one, so A is the better answer. Memory tip: Range = the two Ends of the data.",
 "|Use the pie chart above to answer the question. \nWhat is the percentage contribution of services to the national income?|15%|10%|54%|24%": "A pie chart shares the whole circle, 360°, among the parts. Each part's percentage = its angle ÷ 360° × 100.\nStep 1: Add the angles shown: mining 126° + agric 72° + manufacturing 108° = 306°.\nStep 2: Services = 360° − 306° = 54°.\nStep 3: Percentage = 54/360 × 100 = 0.15 × 100 = 15%: A.\nThe trap: C (54%) catches students who write down the angle, 54°, as if it were the percentage.\nMemory tip: degrees are not percentages; divide by 360 first (every 3.6° is 1%).",
 "|Use the pie chart above to answer the question. \nIf the national income is ₦60m, the contribution of the manufacturing sector is|₦12m|₦9m|₦18m|₦17m": "Each sector's share of national income is its angle as a fraction of 360°, multiplied by the total.\nStep 1: Manufacturing has an angle of 108°.\nStep 2: Fraction = 108/360 = 0.3 (that is, 30%).\nStep 3: Contribution = 0.3 × ₦60m = ₦18m: C.\nCheck the others: mining = 126/360 × ₦60m = ₦21m, agric = 72/360 × ₦60m = ₦12m and services = 54/360 × ₦60m = ₦9m; ₦21m + ₦12m + ₦18m + ₦9m = ₦60m. The trap: A (₦12m) and B (₦9m) are the shares of agric and services, so they catch students who read the wrong sector.\nMemory tip: share = angle/360 × total.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nA combination of factors responsible for a shift from D₀D₀ to D₁D₁ are|consumers income and change in technology|price of the commodity and weather conditions|consumers income and population size|consumers taste and change in price": "The diagram shows the demand curve moving from D₀D₀ to D₁D₁, a shift to the right, which raises equilibrium price from P₀ to P₁ and quantity from Q₀ to Q₁. A whole demand curve shifts only when a condition of demand other than the good's own price changes: income, tastes, population size, prices of related goods, expectations. A rise in consumers' income and an increase in population size both raise demand at every price, so C is correct. Technology (A) and weather (B) are conditions of supply, and a change in the commodity's own price (B and D) causes a movement along the demand curve, not a shift. Memory tip: own price moves you ALONG the curve; anything else SHIFTS it.",
 "|Given that Qd = 40 − 2P and Qs = 6P + 24, calculate the equilibrium price.|₦4|₦2|₦36|₦16": "Equilibrium price is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nStep 1: Set Qd = Qs: 40 − 2P = 6P + 24.\nStep 2: Collect terms: 40 − 24 = 6P + 2P, so 16 = 8P.\nStep 3: P = 16 ÷ 8 = ₦2: B.\nCheck: Qd = 40 − 2(2) = 36 and Qs = 6(2) + 24 = 36, so both equal 36 units. The trap: C (₦36) is the equilibrium quantity, not the price, so it catches students who solve correctly but report the wrong figure.\nMemory tip: solve for P first, then substitute to get Q, and keep the two apart.",
 "|A change in demand for a normal good implies that there is a|change in the quantity demanded as price changes|shift in the demand curve|movement along a given demand curve|change in the price elasticity of demand": "Economists separate two kinds of change. A change in quantity demanded is caused only by a change in the good's own price, and it is shown as a movement along the same demand curve. A change in demand (an increase or decrease in demand) is caused by a change in any other condition of demand, such as income, tastes or the prices of other goods, and it is shown as a shift of the whole demand curve to the right or left. So a change in demand means a shift in the demand curve: B. Options A and C both describe a change in quantity demanded, and elasticity (D) is a different idea. The trap: C (movement along the curve) catches students who mix up \"demand\" with \"quantity demanded\". Memory tip: price → slide along; other factors → shift.",
 "|If a 10% rise in price causes a 5% decrease in the quantity demanded of a commodity, the elasticity of demand is|unitary elastic|zero elastic|elastic|inelastic": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price.\nStep 1: Percentage change in quantity demanded = 5%.\nStep 2: Percentage change in price = 10%.\nStep 3: PED = 5 ÷ 10 = 0.5.\nSince 0.5 is less than 1 (but more than 0), quantity responds less than proportionately to the price change, so demand is inelastic: D. Unitary elasticity would need exactly 1, elastic demand more than 1, and zero elasticity no change in quantity at all. Memory tip: below 1 = Inelastic (In for \"Insensitive\"); above 1 = elastic.",
 "|Use the graph above to answer the question. \nThe consumer will attain equilibrium at point|J|K|L|M": "A consumer's equilibrium on an indifference curve diagram is where the budget line is tangent to (just touches) the highest indifference curve the consumer can afford. In the diagram the budget line ending at X₂ touches indifference curve U₂ at point K. Any point on U₃, such as L, would give more satisfaction but lies beyond the budget line, so it cannot be bought. Points where the budget line cuts an indifference curve lie on lower curves, so the consumer could do better by moving towards K. Hence equilibrium is at K: B. Point J is the tangency on the inner budget line (ending at X₁), the position before the price of good X fell. Memory tip: equilibrium = the budget line just KISSES the highest curve.",
 "|A rightward shift of the budget line is caused by a|fall in consumer income|change in consumer taste|fall in the commodity relative price|rise in the commodity relative price": "The budget line shows all the combinations of two goods a consumer can buy with a given income at given prices. A change in income shifts it parallel (a rise to the right). When the relative price of a commodity falls, the consumer can buy more of it with the same income, so the line swings outward to the right along that good's axis. So a fall in the commodity's relative price moves the budget line rightward: C. A fall in income (A) and a rise in price (D) move it inward, and tastes (B) affect indifference curves, not the budget line. Memory tip: cheaper goods or more income = more you can reach.",
 "|Given the supply function P = ¼(Qs + 10), when P = ₦10, what is Qs?|20|15|50|30": "Substitute the price into the supply function and solve for Qs.\nStep 1: P = ¼(Qs + 10), and P = ₦10, so 10 = ¼(Qs + 10).\nStep 2: Multiply both sides by 4: 40 = Qs + 10.\nStep 3: Qs = 40 − 10 = 30: D.\nCheck: ¼(30 + 10) = ¼ × 40 = 10. The trap: C (50) comes from adding 10 instead of subtracting it at Step 3.\nMemory tip: undo the operations in reverse order: first remove the ¼ (multiply by 4), then remove the +10.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity supplied\n8 ... 20\n10 ... 24\n\nFrom the table above, calculate the price elasticity of supply.|0.50|0.80|2.00|1.25": "Price elasticity of supply (PES) = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price, using the original values as the base.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 24 − 20 = 4; percentage change = 4/20 × 100 = 20%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦10 − ₦8 = ₦2; percentage change = 2/8 × 100 = 25%.\nStep 3: PES = 20 ÷ 25 = 0.80: B.\nSupply is inelastic, as the figure is less than 1. The trap: D (1.25) is the answer upside down, price change ÷ quantity change, a slip students often make.\nMemory tip: elasticity always has the QUANTITY change on top.",
 "|When price is set below equilibrium, this will lead to|an increase in the quantity supplied|a new equilibrium|a decrease in the quantity supplied|a fall in price": "When the government fixes a price below the equilibrium (a maximum price or price control), two things happen at once. Consumers find the good cheaper, so quantity demanded rises; producers earn less per unit, so they cut back and quantity supplied falls. The result is a shortage (excess demand), often with queues, rationing and black markets. So the price set below equilibrium leads to a decrease in the quantity supplied: C. Quantity supplied increases (A) only when price rises, a new equilibrium (B) is not reached while the control holds, and the price itself is the one fixed, not one that falls freely (D). Memory tip: price ceiling → supply shrinks, shortage grows.",
 "|Price mechanism determines the prices of commodities through|auctioning|market forces|the sales of treasury bills|government legislation": "The price mechanism (price system) is the way a free market economy allocates resources: prices are set by the interaction of demand and supply, the market forces, without any central authority. When demand rises, the price goes up and producers supply more; when supply rises, the price falls. These price signals tell producers what to make and tell consumers what to buy. So prices are determined through market forces: B. Auctioning (A) is one particular way of selling, treasury bills (C) are a government borrowing tool, and government legislation (D) is the opposite of the price mechanism (price control). Memory tip: price mechanism = the \"invisible hand\" of demand and supply.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nUnit of labour ... Total product ... Average product ... Marginal product\n1 ... 8 ... 8 ... -\n2 ... 20 ... 10 ... 12\n3 ... E ... 9 ... 7\n4 ... 28 ... 7 ... F\n5 ... 40 ... 8 ... 12\n\nFrom the table above, find the values of E and F respectively.|27 and 1|68 and 12|28 and 5|12 and 19": "Use the links between total product (TP), average product (AP) and marginal product (MP).\nStep 1: AP = TP ÷ units of labour, so TP = AP × labour. For 3 units, E = 9 × 3 = 27.\nStep 2: Check with MP: MP of the 3rd unit = 27 − 20 = 7, which matches the table.\nStep 3: MP = change in TP. For the 4th unit, F = 28 − 27 = 1.\nSo E = 27 and F = 1: A. The trap: C (28 and 5) catches students who copy the next total product, 28, instead of calculating E.\nMemory tip: TP = AP × L; MP = TP now − TP before.",
 "|If the production of a large firm is higher than that of a small firm, it is experiencing|external economies of scale|external diseconomies of scale|internal economies of scale|internal diseconomies of scale": "Economies of scale are the cost advantages of producing on a large scale. Internal economies of scale arise inside one firm because of its own size: it can buy in bulk, use specialised machines and managers and borrow cheaply. These allow a large firm to produce far more, at a lower average cost, than a small firm. So a large firm producing more than a small firm is enjoying internal economies of scale: C. External economies (A) come from the growth of the whole industry and benefit all its firms, big and small. Diseconomies (B, D) raise costs. The trap: A (external economies) catches students because both are \"economies of scale\", but only internal ones come from the firm's own size. Memory tip: Internal = Inside the firm; External = Everywhere in the industry.",
 "|Division of labour requires that the tasks in a production line be performed|by specialists|in stages|by all workers|by unskilled labourers": "Division of labour means breaking the production of a commodity into separate stages or processes, with each worker or group of workers doing only one stage. In a shirt factory, for example, one group cuts the cloth, another sews, another fixes buttons and another irons and packs. Because each worker repeats one task, he becomes skilful and fast, and output rises. So division of labour requires that tasks be performed in stages: B. Specialisation by skill (A) is a result of division of labour, not what it requires, and the tasks are shared among workers rather than done by all of them (C). The trap: A (by specialists) is tempting because division of labour leads to specialisation, but the defining feature is splitting the work into stages. Memory tip: division of labour = DIVIDE the job into stages.",
 "|Given that FC = ₦500, VC = ₦1,500, and Q = 50 units. Find the average cost of the product.|₦30|₦40|₦10|₦20": "Average cost (average total cost) is total cost divided by output.\nStep 1: Total cost = fixed cost + variable cost = ₦500 + ₦1,500 = ₦2,000.\nStep 2: Average cost = ₦2,000 ÷ 50 units = ₦40: B.\nCheck by parts: average fixed cost = ₦500 ÷ 50 = ₦10 and average variable cost = ₦1,500 ÷ 50 = ₦30, and ₦10 + ₦30 = ₦40. The trap: A (₦30) is the average variable cost and C (₦10) is the average fixed cost, so each catches students who use only one of the costs.\nMemory tip: AC = AFC + AVC = TC ÷ Q.",
 "|Rent and administrative expenses are examples of|average fixed costs|average variable costs|fixed costs|variable costs": "In the short run a firm's costs are of two kinds. Fixed costs do not change with output and must be paid even when nothing is produced: rent on the factory, administrative expenses, insurance, interest on loans and the salaries of permanent staff. Variable costs change with output: raw materials, fuel and the wages of production workers. Rent and administrative expenses stay the same whether the firm makes 10 or 10,000 units, so they are fixed costs: C. Average fixed cost (A) is fixed cost divided by output, a figure calculated from them, not the expenses themselves. Memory tip: if you pay it even when the factory is idle, it is FIXED.",
 "|A perfect competitor will continue to expand output up to the point where|TC > TR|MR = AR|MC < MR|MC > MR": "Any firm maximises profit where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR). Below that output, each extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost (MC < MR), so profit rises if the firm produces more; the firm therefore keeps expanding while MC < MR. Beyond it, MC > MR and extra units reduce profit. For a perfect competitor, price is fixed by the market, so MR = AR = price at every level of output; MR = AR therefore cannot tell the firm where to stop. So the firm continues to expand output where MC < MR: C. B is the answer often given, but it is a feature of perfect competition, not the rule for output. Memory tip: keep going while MC < MR; stop when MC = MR.",
 "|One of the characteristics of a monopolist is that he can influence|quantity produced by other producers|prices charged by other producers|both price and quantity|price or quantity": "A monopolist is the sole seller of a product with no close substitutes, so he faces the whole downward-sloping market demand curve. He can choose the price, and then buyers decide how much to buy at that price; or he can choose the quantity to put on the market, and then buyers' demand decides the price. He cannot fix both, because he cannot force consumers to buy more than they want at his chosen price. So he can influence price or quantity: D. With no rivals in the market, he does not control other producers' output or prices (A, B). The trap: C (both price and quantity) catches students who think a monopolist controls everything. Memory tip: a monopolist chooses price OR quantity; demand decides the other.",
 "|A monopolist can boost up his revenue by|adjusting both price and output upward|reducing total output to match price|increasing price|reducing price": "A monopolist faces a downward-sloping demand curve, so price and quantity move in opposite directions: he can sell more only by lowering price, and he can raise price only by selling less. A monopolist therefore gains by restricting output: by reducing the total quantity he puts on the market, buyers bid the price up, and he moves to the point where MR = MC, earning abnormal profit. So he boosts his revenue (and profit) by reducing total output to match the higher price: B. He cannot raise both price and output (A) on a downward-sloping demand curve. Simply raising price (C) or cutting price (D) helps only for certain elasticities. Memory tip: monopoly means less output, higher price.",
 "|Which of the following can be used to measure the Gross National product in an open economy?|C+I+G+(X+M)|C+I+G+X|C+I+G|C+I+G+(X−M)": "By the expenditure method, total spending on goods made within the country is C + I + G + (X − M): consumption, investment, government spending and net exports (imports are produced abroad, so they are subtracted). This gives Gross Domestic Product (GDP). Gross National Product (GNP) adds net factor income from abroad: GNP = C + I + G + (X − M) + (income earned abroad by citizens − income paid to foreigners). No option includes that term, so none is fully correct for GNP, but D is the intended answer and the only correct form of the open-economy expenditure total: D. A and B treat imports wrongly and C leaves out foreign trade. Memory tip: GDP = C + I + G + (X − M); GNP = GDP + net income from abroad.",
 "|If MPC = ⅔ and investment is ₦100 million, the level of national income is|₦100 million|₦10 million|₦303 million|₦300 million": "The investment multiplier shows how much national income rises for each naira of investment.\nStep 1: Multiplier k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/(1 − ⅔) = 1/⅓ = 3.\nStep 2: National income = k × investment = 3 × ₦100 million = ₦300 million: D.\nEquivalently, MPS = ⅓, so k = 1/MPS = 3. The trap: C (₦303 million) is a near-identical figure that catches students who glance at the options, and A (₦100 million) forgets the multiplier altogether.\nMemory tip: k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/MPS.",
 "|The precautionary demand for money is determined by|the rate of interest|the level of savings|the level of income|general price level": "Keynes gave three motives for holding money (liquidity preference). The transactions motive is money held for day-to-day buying. The precautionary motive is money held for emergencies and unexpected expenses such as illness or accidents. The speculative motive is money held to take advantage of changes in bond prices and interest rates. The first two depend mainly on the level of income: the more a person earns, the more he can and does keep aside for emergencies. So the precautionary demand for money is determined by the level of income: C. The rate of interest (A) determines the speculative demand. The trap: A (rate of interest) catches students who mix up the precautionary and speculative motives. Memory tip: Transactions and Precautionary follow income; Speculative follows interest.",
 "|An inflation that co-exists with high rate of unemployment is|hyperinflation|stagflation|demand-pull inflation|cost-push inflation": "Normally inflation is linked with booming demand and low unemployment, while recession brings low inflation and high unemployment. Stagflation is the unusual case where both bad things happen together: prices keep rising while output stagnates and unemployment is high. It is often caused by supply shocks, such as a sharp rise in oil prices, which raise costs and cut output at the same time, as in many countries in the 1970s. So inflation with a high rate of unemployment is stagflation: B. Hyperinflation (A) is extremely rapid inflation, and demand-pull (C) and cost-push (D) describe causes of inflation. Memory tip: STAGnation + inFLATION = stagflation.",
 "|One of the challenges facing the banking industry in Nigeria is|ensuring technological security|providing employment|providing loans for investment|creating more money": "Nigerian banks now serve customers through ATMs, POS terminals, mobile apps and internet banking. This has made banking faster, but it has also exposed banks and their customers to card fraud, hacking, phishing and other cyber crimes. Ensuring the security of these technologies (protecting systems, data and customers' money) is therefore a major challenge for the banking industry: A. Providing employment (B) and giving loans for investment (C) are services banks perform, and creating money (D) through lending is a normal function of commercial banks, not a challenge. Today the CBN requires banks to follow cyber-security frameworks and uses BVN to fight fraud. Memory tip: a challenge is a problem banks must overcome, not a job they do.",
 "|Short-term loans for investment are usually obtained through the|stock market|development banks|money market|capital market": "Financial markets are divided by the length of the loans they deal in. The money market deals in short-term funds, usually for less than a year, through instruments such as treasury bills, call money, commercial papers and certificates of deposit; commercial banks, discount houses and the CBN are its main players. The capital market deals in long-term funds through shares and bonds; the stock market (stock exchange) is part of it, and development banks give long-term loans for projects. So short-term loans are obtained through the money market: C. The trap: D (capital market) catches students because both are financial markets, but the capital market is for long-term funds. Memory tip: Money market = short time; Capital market = long time.",
 "|Given a base year and the price index of 175% the following year, which of the following will arise?|The cost of living decreases of that year|The cost of living remains unchanged|The value of money rises by 75%|The value of money falls by 75%": "A price index compares prices with a base year, whose index is 100. An index of 175 means prices are 75% higher than in the base year, so the cost of living has risen and each naira buys less: the value of money has fallen. That rules out A, B and C. Strictly, the value of money moves inversely with prices: ₦1 now buys 100/175 ≈ 0.57 of what it bought, a fall of about 43%, not 75%. D is the intended answer and the only one with the right direction, but its figure is not exactly correct: D. Memory tip: value of money = 100 ÷ price index; prices up, value down.",
 "|Wage freeze is a policy measure aimed at|encouraging investors|curbing inflation|regulating standard of living|curbing deflation": "A wage freeze is an order by the government that wages and salaries must not be increased for a period. It is an anti-inflation measure, part of an incomes policy. Rising wages raise firms' costs, which they pass on as higher prices (cost-push inflation), and they also increase spending power (demand-pull inflation); freezing wages breaks this wage-price spiral. So a wage freeze is aimed at curbing inflation: B. During deflation (D) prices are already falling, so the problem is too little spending, and freezing wages would make it worse. The trap: D (curbing deflation) catches students who remember the word \"curbing\" but mix up inflation and deflation. Memory tip: freeze wages to cool prices.",
 "|A major obstacle to the development of Nigeria's economy is|low capital formation|rural-urban migration|over dependence on oil|poor developmental policies": "Nigeria has abundant natural and human resources, yet its development has been held back largely by poor developmental policies: plans that were badly designed, frequently changed with each government, poorly implemented and weakened by corruption. Poor policies also explain other problems in the options, such as the failure to diversify away from oil and the neglect of rural areas that drives rural-urban migration. So a major obstacle is poor developmental policies: D. Low capital formation (A) and over-dependence on oil (C) are real problems too, but they largely result from policy failures. The trap: C (over dependence on oil) is very tempting because it is the most talked-about weakness of the Nigerian economy. Memory tip: good resources need good policies to grow.",
 "|A major feature of an underdeveloped economy is|excess capacity utilization|low rate of population growth|low level of standard of living|low level of unemployment": "An underdeveloped (less developed) economy has several typical features: low income per head, low savings and capital formation, a high rate of population growth, heavy dependence on agriculture and primary products, high unemployment and underemployment, and under-use of industrial capacity. Low income means people cannot afford good food, housing, healthcare and education, so their standard of living is low: C. The other options describe the opposite of reality in such countries: capacity is under-used, not overused (A), population grows fast (B), and unemployment is high (D). Memory tip: underdeveloped = low income, low savings, low standard of living, but high population growth and unemployment.",
 "|An important role of agriculture in Nigeria's economic development is the|processing of raw materials for industries|regulation of price system|provision of infrastructure|provision of employment": "Agriculture plays many roles in Nigeria's development: it provides food for the population, raw materials for industries (cocoa, cotton, palm oil, rubber), foreign exchange from exports, a market for industrial goods, revenue for government and, above all, employment. It remains the largest single employer of labour in Nigeria, especially in rural areas. So an important role is the provision of employment: D. Agriculture supplies raw materials; processing them (A) is the work of industry. Regulating the price system (B) and providing infrastructure (C) are roles of government. The trap: A catches students because \"raw materials for industries\" is a well-known role of agriculture, but the option says processing, not supplying. Memory tip: farms grow and employ; factories process.",
 "|An advantage of large-scale farming over peasant farming is in the area of|providing research and massive employment of labour|redistributing national income to various regions of the country|encouraging the use of traditional implements|encouraging urban-rural migration": "Large-scale (commercial or mechanised) farming uses large areas of land, modern machines, improved seeds and fertilisers, and much capital. Because of its size and funds it can sponsor or use research into better crops and methods, and it employs large numbers of workers. Peasant farming, by contrast, is small, uses family labour and traditional tools such as hoes and cutlasses, and has no money for research. So the advantage of large-scale farming is in providing research and massive employment of labour: A. Encouraging traditional implements (C) is a feature of peasant farming, and redistributing income (B) or encouraging migration (D) is not its purpose. Memory tip: large scale = large capital, large research, large workforce.",
 "|A major disadvantage of localization of industry is|the risk of structural unemployment|over-utilization of installed industrial capacity|the risk of seasonal unemployment|under-utilization of installed industrial capacity": "Localisation of industry is the concentration of firms in one industry in a particular area, for example textile mills in Kaduna or the leather industry in Kano. It brings benefits such as skilled labour and ancillary firms, but a major danger is that the area depends heavily on that one industry. If demand for its product falls or technology changes, many firms close together and large numbers of workers whose skills suit only that industry lose their jobs. This is structural unemployment: A. Seasonal unemployment (C) is caused by the seasons, as in farming, not by localisation. The trap: C catches students because it is another type of unemployment, but it comes from the seasons, not the decline of an industry. Memory tip: all eggs in one basket → structural unemployment if the basket falls.",
 "|One major factor that determines the location of an industry is|tax exemption grant|its proximity to the market|the capital base|the social responsibility of the firm": "Factors that decide where an industry is located include nearness to raw materials, nearness to the market, availability of labour, power and water supply, transport facilities and government policy. Nearness to the market is especially important for industries whose products are bulky, fragile or perishable, such as bread, soft drinks and furniture, because it cuts transport costs and lets the firm serve customers quickly. So a major factor is its proximity to the market: B. Tax exemption (A) is a government incentive that may attract firms, but it is not a basic determinant of location; capital base (C) and social responsibility (D) do not decide where a factory stands. Memory tip: locate where costs are lowest, near raw materials or near the market.",
 "|The major contribution of OPEC to the Nigerian economy is the|provision of social infrastructures|granting of subsidies on petroleum products|stabilization of oil prices|building of refineries": "The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC), formed in 1960, coordinates the oil policies of its members. Nigeria joined in 1971. Its main tool is setting production quotas: when oil prices fall, members cut output; when prices are too high, they raise it. This helps keep world oil prices stable and so protects members' revenue. Because oil earns most of Nigeria's foreign exchange and government revenue, OPEC's major contribution to the Nigerian economy is stabilisation of oil prices: C. OPEC does not provide infrastructure (A), grant fuel subsidies (B) or build refineries (D) in member countries; these are national decisions. Memory tip: OPEC = quotas → stable prices.",
 "|The loading of crude oil at the terminal is an activity in the|downstream sector of the oil industry|upstream and downstream sector of the oil industry|upstream sector of the oil industry|midstream and upstream sectors of the oil industry": "The oil industry is divided into sectors. The upstream sector covers exploration, drilling and production of crude oil, including lifting (loading) it at export terminals for sale abroad. The downstream sector covers refining crude oil and distributing and marketing products such as petrol and kerosene. Pipelines and storage are sometimes called midstream. In Nigeria, the loading of crude oil at terminals for export is treated as part of the upstream sector: C. Under the Petroleum Industry Act (2021), the upstream sector is now regulated by the NUPRC, and the midstream and downstream sectors by the NMDPRA. Memory tip: UP = getting crude up and out; DOWN = refining and selling to consumers.",
 "|The short-run average variable cost of a firm will rise owing to|the expansion of factory space|the building of new warehouse|an increase in the cost of labour|an increase in the salaries of directors": "In the short run, fixed costs stay the same while variable costs change with output. Average variable cost (AVC) is total variable cost divided by output. The wages of production workers are a variable cost, because more workers are hired as output rises. So an increase in the cost of labour (higher wage rates) raises variable cost per unit, and AVC rises: C. Expanding factory space (A) and building a new warehouse (B) are changes in fixed capital, and directors' salaries (D) are fixed costs paid regardless of output; these raise fixed cost, not AVC. The trap: D (salaries of directors) catches students because salaries sound like labour costs, but directors are paid the same whatever the output. Memory tip: production wages are variable; management salaries are fixed.",
 "|Firms embark on vertical integration in order to|take over markets formerly controlled by other firms|prevent other firms from entering the market|reduce advertisement and management cost|enjoy economies of large-scale production": "Integration is the joining of firms. Horizontal integration joins firms at the same stage of production (two breweries). Vertical integration joins firms at different stages of the same production chain, backward towards raw materials or forward towards distribution, for example a textile firm buying a cotton farm and a chain of shops. This removes middlemen and repeated buying, selling and advertising between stages, and it lets one management run the whole chain. So firms embark on vertical integration to reduce advertisement and management cost: C. Enjoying economies of large-scale production (D) and taking over rivals' markets (A) are mainly reasons for horizontal integration. Memory tip: Vertical = up and down the chain; Horizontal = side by side with rivals.",
 "|The voting power in co-operative societies is vested in|management|members without loan|shareholders|members with the highest contribution": "A co-operative society is a business owned and controlled by its members, who join together to serve their common interest rather than to make maximum profit. Each member buys shares and so is a shareholder of the society. Control is democratic: voting power is vested in the shareholding members, and each member has one vote no matter how many shares he holds, unlike a joint-stock company where votes follow the number of shares. So voting power is vested in the shareholders: C. Management (A) carries out the members' decisions, and voting does not depend on whether a member has a loan (B) or has contributed the most (D). The trap: D catches students who apply the company rule (more shares, more votes) to co-operatives. Memory tip: co-operative = one member, one vote.",
 "|An accurate census figure is an important tool for planners in|providing employment|siting industries|providing social amenities|allocating resources": "A census is the official counting of all the people in a country at a given time, recording their age, sex, occupation and location. Planners use these figures to share the nation's resources among states, local governments and sectors: how much revenue each area should get, and how many schools, hospitals, roads and water projects are needed and where. So an accurate census is a tool for planners in allocating resources: D. Providing employment (A), siting industries (B) and providing social amenities (C) are particular uses that all come under the general task of allocating resources. Nigeria's last census was held in 2006. Memory tip: count the people, then share the resources.",
 "|An accurate population census is important in order to|produce more food for the growing population|provide employment opportunities for the people|estimate a country's per capita income|fast track industrial development": "Per capita income is the average income per person in a country: national income ÷ total population. If the census figure is too high, per capita income will be understated; if it is too low, it will be overstated. So an accurate census is needed to estimate a country's per capita income correctly: C. Per capita income is used to compare standards of living over time and between countries. Producing food (A), providing jobs (B) and speeding up industrialisation (D) are policy goals that census figures may help plan, but the census does not itself achieve them. Memory tip: per capita = per head, so you must count the heads.",
 "|A major determinant of floating exchange rate is|the highest denomination of the currency|an Act of the parliament|the system of government|the forces of demand and supply": "An exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. Under a fixed exchange rate system the government or central bank sets the rate. Under a floating (flexible) exchange rate system the rate is determined freely in the foreign exchange market by the demand for and supply of the currency. When demand for naira rises (for example, foreigners buy more Nigerian oil), the naira appreciates; when supply of naira rises (Nigerians buy more imports), it depreciates. So the major determinant is the forces of demand and supply: D. Since June 2023 the CBN has unified the official windows and moved towards a more market-determined naira rate. Memory tip: floating = free market; fixed = fixed by government.",
 "|If the importation of a commodity is limited to a definite quantity, the trade control measure imposed is|excise duties|import duties|quotas|tariff": "Governments use several tools to control imports. A quota is a limit on the physical quantity (or value) of a commodity that may be imported in a period, for example only 10,000 tonnes of rice a year. Tariffs or import duties (B, D) are taxes on imported goods, which raise their prices but do not fix the quantity. Excise duties (A) are taxes on goods produced and sold within the country. Since the measure limits imports to a definite quantity, it is a quota: C. Memory tip: Quota = Quantity; Tariff = Tax.",
 "|The main objective of WTO is to|ensure adequate protection of infant industries|provide a mechanism for tariff reduction|assist countries with chronic balance of payments problem|assist developing countries to execute developmental projects": "The World Trade Organization (WTO) was set up on 1 January 1995 to replace the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT). Its main objective is to promote free and fair world trade by providing a forum and mechanism for negotiating reductions in tariffs and other trade barriers, enforcing trade rules and settling trade disputes between member countries. So its main objective is to provide a mechanism for tariff reduction: B. Assisting countries with chronic balance of payments problems (C) is the work of the IMF, and financing development projects (D) is the work of the World Bank. Protecting infant industries (A) is a reason for trade barriers, which the WTO seeks to reduce. Memory tip: WTO = trade; IMF = balance of payments; World Bank = development projects.",
 "|The major determinant of the total volume of output in an economy is the|level of total expenditure|composition of consumer spending|number of farmers|size of the labour force": "According to Keynes, in the short run the total output (national income) and employment of an economy are determined by aggregate demand, the level of total expenditure: C + I + G + (X − M). Firms produce only what they expect to sell; if total spending falls, unsold goods pile up and firms cut output and lay off workers, and if spending rises, output rises. So the major determinant of total output is the level of total expenditure: A. The composition of consumer spending (B) affects which goods are produced, not how much in total. The size of the labour force (D) limits how much can be produced at full employment, but output is below that limit when spending is low. Memory tip: spending creates output, which creates income.",
 "|The labour force of a country is determined by the|age structure of the population|geographical distribution of the population|sex distribution of the population|number of people available for work": "The labour force (working population) is the part of a population that is of working age and is able and willing to work, whether employed or looking for work. Its size is determined mainly by the age structure of the population: a country with many people aged about 15–64 has a large labour force, while one with many children or old people has a smaller one. So the labour force is determined by the age structure of the population: A. Geographical distribution (B) affects where workers are, not how many there are. The trap: D (number of people available for work) is close because it describes what the labour force is, rather than what determines its size. Memory tip: working age decides the working force.",
 "|The supply of colanuts is influenced by|rate of interest on agricultural loans|weather conditions|population size|taste of the consumers": "Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to offer at each price. For farm produce such as colanuts, the most important non-price factor is the weather. Colanut trees need good rainfall at the right time; drought, too much rain or harmattan winds can sharply reduce the harvest, so supply rises and falls with weather conditions: B. Population size and consumers' tastes (C, D) are determinants of demand, not supply. The interest rate on loans can affect farmers' costs over time, but for a tree crop whose yearly output comes from trees already planted, the weather is the factor that directly decides how much reaches the market. Memory tip: for crops, supply follows the sky.",
 "|Calculate the mean of 9, 7, 6, 9, 10, 12, 14, 15 and 17.|10|11|12|9": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of the values divided by the number of values.\nStep 1: Add them: 9 + 7 + 6 + 9 + 10 + 12 + 14 + 15 + 17 = 99.\nStep 2: Count them: there are 9 numbers.\nStep 3: Mean = 99 ÷ 9 = 11: B.\nNotice that 9 appears twice; both must be counted, or the sum and the count go wrong. The trap: A (10) is the median (the middle value of 6, 7, 9, 9, 10, 12, 14, 15, 17), which students give when they mix up the two averages. Memory tip: Mean = add them all and share equally; Median = the middle one in order.",
 "|The equilibrium output of an oligopolist is where|AR = MC = AC|MR = MC = AC|MR = MC|MR = AC": "Every profit-maximising firm, whatever the market structure, is in equilibrium where marginal revenue (MR) equals marginal cost (MC). Producing one more unit adds MR to revenue and MC to cost; as long as MR is bigger, output should rise, and once MC is bigger it should fall. Profit is greatest where they are equal. An oligopolist, one of a few large firms such as the big telecom operators, follows the same rule: C. The extra condition \"= AC\" (A, B) describes a perfectly competitive firm in long-run equilibrium earning only normal profit; oligopolists, protected by barriers to entry, usually earn supernormal profit, so AC is not generally equal to MR or MC. Memory tip: MR = MC is the golden rule for every firm.",
 "|The graph above illustrates|a change in supply|a change in demand|a decrease in the quantity supplied|an increase in the quantity supplied": "The graph shows three parallel supply curves, S, S₁ and S₂, so the whole supply curve has moved to a new position. A shift of the curve is called a change in supply: A. It is caused by factors other than the good's own price, such as changes in the cost of inputs, technology, weather, taxes or subsidies. A change in quantity supplied (C, D) is different: it is a movement along one supply curve caused only by a change in the good's own price. A change in demand (B) concerns demand curves, which slope downwards; these curves slope upwards, so they are supply curves. The trap: D (an increase in the quantity supplied) uses the related phrase students mix up with a change in supply. Memory tip: own price → move along; anything else → shift.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nYear 1990 (₦) ... Year 1991 (₦)\n5 ... 10\n\nFrom the table above, determine the price index taking 1991 as the base year.|150|200|50|66.66": "A price index compares the price in any year with the price in the base year, which is given the value 100.\nStep 1: The base year is 1991, so its price (₦10) = 100.\nStep 2: Index for 1990 = (price in 1990 ÷ price in base year) × 100 = 5/10 × 100 = 50: C.\nAn index of 50 means prices in 1990 were half their 1991 level. The trap: B (200) is what you get if you use 1990 as the base (10/5 × 100), the slip of putting the wrong year at the bottom of the fraction. Memory tip: the base year always goes at the bottom.",
 "|Unemployment caused by changes in the demand pattern is|structural|seasonal|frictional|cyclical": "Structural unemployment occurs when there is a lasting change in the structure of the economy, such as a change in the pattern of demand or in technology. When consumers turn away from a product, the industry producing it shrinks and its workers lose their jobs; their skills may not suit the industries that are growing. So unemployment caused by changes in the demand pattern is structural: A. Seasonal unemployment follows the seasons (e.g. farm work), frictional unemployment occurs while people move between jobs, and cyclical unemployment comes from a general fall in demand during a recession. The trap: D (cyclical) also involves demand, but a fall in total demand, not a change in its pattern. Memory tip: a change in what people buy changes the structure of jobs.",
 "|In Nigeria, PAYE is a typical example of|a regressive tax|an indirect tax|a progressive tax|value added tax": "PAYE (Pay-As-You-Earn) is the system by which personal income tax is deducted from workers' salaries by their employers and paid to the state government. In Nigeria the Personal Income Tax Act applies graduated rates: the more a person earns, the higher the rate on the extra income, so higher earners pay a larger proportion of their income. A tax whose rate rises as income rises is progressive: C. PAYE is a direct tax, paid by the person who bears it, so it is not indirect (B). Value Added Tax (D) is an indirect tax on goods and services, and a regressive tax (A) takes a larger share from the poor. Memory tip: progressive tax progresses (rises) as income rises.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nUnit of labour ... Total product ... Marginal product\n0 ... 0 ... -\n1 ... 5 ... 5\n2 ... 9 ... 4\n3 ... X ... 3\n4 ... 14 ... 2\n5 ... 15 ... 1\n\nFrom the table above, find the value of X.|12|15|9|10": "Marginal product (MP) is the extra output added by one more worker, so total product (TP) grows by each worker's MP.\nStep 1: TP with 2 workers = 9.\nStep 2: The 3rd worker adds MP = 3, so X = 9 + 3 = 12: A.\nStep 3: Check: the 4th worker adds 2, giving 12 + 2 = 14, which matches the table.\nThe falling MP (5, 4, 3, 2, 1) shows the law of diminishing returns. The trap: C (9) is the TP of 2 workers, chosen by students who read off the row above instead of adding the MP. Memory tip: TP now = TP before + MP now.",
 "|The problem of food shortages in Nigeria can be curbed by|practising subsistence farming|practising mechanized farming|increasing extension services|re-establishing marketing boards": "Food shortages arise mainly because most Nigerian farmers work small plots with hoes and cutlasses, so output is low. Mechanized farming uses tractors, harvesters and other machines, allowing large areas to be cultivated quickly with less labour, and so raises food output on a large scale: B. Subsistence farming (A) produces only enough for the farmer's family and is a cause of the problem, not a cure. Extension services (C) help, but they spread knowledge rather than directly expanding production. Marketing boards (D) handled the buying and export of cash crops; they were scrapped in 1986 under the Structural Adjustment Programme and did little for food crops. Memory tip: machines multiply the farmer's hands.",
 "|The major factor that influenced the siting of petrochemical industries in Rivers State is|nearness to market|the availability of skilled manpower|the availability of power supply|the availability of raw materials": "Industries are usually located where their main costs are lowest. A petrochemical plant uses crude oil products and natural gas as its raw materials, which are bulky and costly to move. Rivers State is at the heart of the Niger Delta oil and gas region, with the Port Harcourt refineries close by, so the Eleme Petrochemicals plant (commissioned in 1995) was sited near its raw materials: D. Nearness to market, skilled labour and power supply matter for many industries, but they are not the main reason for this location. Eleme Petrochemicals was privatised in 2006 and is now run by Indorama. The trap: A (nearness to market) is another classic location factor and Port Harcourt is a big city, but the plant sells across Nigeria and abroad. Memory tip: heavy, bulky raw materials pull industry towards them.",
 "|A country is said to attain optimum population when|resources are adequate to cater for the growing population|output per head is at its highest point|total population increases with a given volume of resources|national resources increase as population increases": "Optimum population is the size of population which, working with the country's existing resources and technology, produces the highest output (income) per head: B. If the population is smaller than this, there are too few people to use the resources fully (underpopulation); if it is larger, each extra person adds less than the average and output per head falls (overpopulation). Option A is vague and describes a hope rather than the definition, and option C describes the very situation that leads to overpopulation. Option D describes resources growing, not the ideal size of population. Memory tip: optimum means the most output for each person.",
 "|One of the factors responsible for the downward sloping of the demand curve from the left to the right is|increasing marginal utility|the price of other commodities|marginal rate of substitution|diminishing marginal utility": "The demand curve slopes downward from left to right because consumers buy more of a good only when its price falls. The reason is the law of diminishing marginal utility: each extra unit of a good gives less additional satisfaction than the one before, so a consumer is willing to pay less for each extra unit. To persuade him to buy more, the price must fall: D. Increasing marginal utility (A) is the opposite of what happens. The prices of other commodities (B) shift the demand curve rather than give it its slope, and the marginal rate of substitution (C) belongs to indifference-curve analysis. Memory tip: less satisfaction from each extra unit means a lower price to buy it.",
 "|The shutdown point for a firm in a competitive market is|MR = MC|AR = MC|P = AC|P = AVC": "In the short run a firm must pay its fixed costs (rent, loan repayments) whether it produces or not. It is therefore worth producing as long as the price covers average variable cost (AVC), because any revenue above AVC helps to pay part of the fixed costs. If the price falls below AVC, the firm loses more by producing than by closing, so it shuts down. The shutdown point is where price equals minimum AVC: D. P = AC (C) is the break-even point, where the firm makes only normal profit; MR = MC (A) is the condition for profit maximisation. The trap: C (P = AC) is the closely related break-even point, which students often confuse with shutdown. Memory tip: break even at AC; shut down at AVC.",
 "|The physical factor affecting the geographical distribution of a population includes|relief|industrial activities|birth rate|death rate": "The geographical distribution of population is how people are spread over a country. Physical (natural) factors include relief, climate, soil fertility, water supply and vegetation. Relief means the shape and height of the land: plains, valleys and gentle slopes attract settlement and farming, while steep mountains, rocky highlands and swamps have few people. So relief is a physical factor: A. Industrial activities (B) are an economic or human factor, while birth and death rates (C, D) determine the size and growth of population, not where people live. Memory tip: physical factors are the ones nature made: land, weather, water, soil.",
 "|Developing countries can improve their balance of trade by|producing quality goods for exports|importing standard goods from developed nations|fully deregulating their economies|exporting large quantities of raw materials": "The balance of trade is the difference between the value of a country's visible exports and its visible imports. To improve it, a country must earn more from exports or spend less on imports. Producing quality goods for export makes them competitive on world markets, so they sell more and at better prices, raising export earnings: A. Importing more goods (B) worsens the balance, and full deregulation (C) can increase imports. Exporting large quantities of raw materials (D) is what many developing countries already do, but raw materials earn low and unstable prices. Memory tip: to fix the trade balance, sell more and better abroad.",
 "|If the price of commodity T changes, this will result in|a movement along the same demand curve|a new equilibrium quantity|an inward shift of the demand curve|an outward shift of the demand curve": "A demand curve shows how much of a good people will buy at each price, holding other things constant. When the good's own price changes, buyers simply move to a different point on the same demand curve: a higher price means a smaller quantity demanded and a lower price a larger one: A. The curve shifts inward or outward (C, D) only when something other than the good's own price changes, such as income, tastes or the price of a related good. A new equilibrium quantity (B) needs a change in demand or supply conditions, not just a statement that price changed. Memory tip: own price → slide along the curve.",
 "|If Mr. T changes the monthly quantity of garri he usually consumes from 5kg to 8kg as a result of increase in income, this is an example of|an increase in demand|an increase in the quantity demanded|an increase in per capita income|a decrease in demand": "A change in demand happens when a factor other than the good's own price changes. Here Mr. T buys more garri (from 5kg to 8kg a month) because his income rose, not because garri became cheaper. At every price he now buys more, so his demand curve shifts to the right: this is an increase in demand: A. An increase in quantity demanded (B) would be caused by a fall in the price of garri. A rise in his own income is not the same as a rise in per capita income (C), which is the national average. A decrease in demand (D) is the opposite direction. The trap: B is the closely related phrase that catches students who do not check what caused the change. Memory tip: income changes demand; price changes quantity demanded.",
 "|The major reason why government influences the location of industries is to|enhance the performance of these industries|encourage cooperation among the firms|reduce rural-urban migration|increase the profit earnings of the industrialists": "Left to themselves, firms tend to crowd into a few big cities such as Lagos, where markets and services are good. This draws young people from villages into overcrowded towns. Government therefore influences the location of industries, through incentives, industrial estates and restrictions, to spread them to rural and less developed areas. Jobs created there keep people at home and so reduce rural-urban migration: C. Government's aim is balanced development and fair distribution of employment, not simply to raise the profits of industrialists (D) or the performance of particular firms (A). Memory tip: take jobs to the people and the people stay home.",
 "|The GDP of a country is calculated as|gross national product less depreciation|gross national product plus net income from abroad|net domestic product less depreciation|net domestic product plus depreciation": "The national income measures are linked by two adjustments: depreciation (capital consumption) and net income from abroad.\nGDP − depreciation = NDP (net domestic product).\nSo GDP = NDP + depreciation: D.\nAlso, GNP = GDP + net income from abroad, and NNP = GNP − depreciation. Option A (GNP less depreciation) gives NNP, and option B (GNP plus net income from abroad) does not give GDP: to get GDP from GNP you subtract net income from abroad. NDP less depreciation (C) would subtract depreciation twice. Memory tip: Gross = Net + depreciation; National = Domestic + net income from abroad.",
 "|One of the problems faced by OPEC as a cartel is|language barrier|difference in currency|fluctuation in world oil prices|over-reliance on crude oil": "OPEC (Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries, founded in 1960; Nigeria joined in 1971) acts as a cartel: members agree on production quotas to influence the world price of oil. Its main problem is that the world price still fluctuates widely. Demand changes with the state of the world economy, non-OPEC producers expand output, and some members produce above their quotas to earn more, so prices rise and fall sharply, as in 1986, 2008, 2014 and 2020: C. Language and currency differences (A, B) are not serious problems for the cartel. Over-reliance on crude oil (D) is a problem of member economies such as Nigeria, not of OPEC as a cartel. Memory tip: a cartel's constant headache is keeping prices steady.",
 "|The indigenization policy in Nigeria was aimed at|eliminating foreign participation in the economy|providing enabling environment for the expatriates|enhancing foreign monopoly of the economy|eliminating Nigerians participation in the economy": "The Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decrees of 1972 and 1977, known as the indigenization decrees, reserved certain businesses wholly for Nigerians and required foreign firms in others to sell 40% or 60% of their shares to Nigerians. The aim was to transfer ownership and control of the economy from foreigners to Nigerians, reducing or eliminating foreign participation in the economy: A. Options B, C and D state the opposite of the policy. The decrees were relaxed in 1989 and replaced in 1995 by the Nigerian Investment Promotion Commission Act, which now welcomes foreign investment in most sectors. Memory tip: indigenous means native, so indigenization puts the economy in Nigerian hands.",
 "|The reward that accrues to a proprietor of a business is|wages|profit|rent|interest": "Each factor of production earns its own reward: land earns rent, labour earns wages, capital earns interest and the entrepreneur earns profit. The proprietor of a business is its owner, the entrepreneur who organises the other factors and bears the risks of the business. His reward is whatever is left after paying for the other factors, which is profit: B. If he employs workers, they earn wages (A); if he rents a shop, the landlord earns rent (C); if he borrows, the lender earns interest (D). Memory tip: Land-Rent, Labour-Wages, Capital-Interest, Entrepreneur-Profit.",
 "|A perfect competitor is at equilibrium when the marginal cost is equal to|total revenue|average cost|average revenue|marginal revenue": "Every profit-maximising firm is in equilibrium where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR): D. A perfect competitor is a price taker: it can sell any quantity at the ruling market price, so every unit sold adds exactly the price to revenue. Therefore price = average revenue = marginal revenue (P = AR = MR). It follows that at equilibrium MC also equals AR (and price), so C is equally correct and both are accepted. MC is not compared with total revenue (A), and MC = AC (B) holds only in long-run equilibrium. Memory tip: in perfect competition, P = AR = MR = MC at equilibrium.",
 "|Given that Qd = 34 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 5P, find the equilibrium price.|₦7|₦2|₦32|₦14": "At equilibrium, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nStep 1: Set Qd = Qs: 34 − 2P = 20 + 5P.\nStep 2: Collect terms: 34 − 20 = 5P + 2P, so 14 = 7P.\nStep 3: P = 14 ÷ 7 = ₦2: B.\nCheck: Qd = 34 − 4 = 30 and Qs = 20 + 10 = 30, so the market clears at 30 units. The trap: D (₦14) is the figure on the left of 14 = 7P, given by students who stop before dividing. Memory tip: equate, collect, divide.",
 "|Deflation will occur in an economy when there is a|decrease in production cost|rise in nominal interest rate|fall in the general price level|rise in the general price level": "Deflation is a persistent fall in the general price level, so the value of money rises: C. It is the opposite of inflation, which is a persistent rise in the general price level (D). Deflation is often linked with falling demand, rising unemployment and recession, because firms cut prices when they cannot sell their goods. A decrease in production costs (A) may lower some prices but is a possible cause, not the meaning. A rise in interest rates (B) is a policy used to fight inflation. Memory tip: inflation inflates prices; deflation deflates them.",
 "|Which of the following commodities has a negative substitution effect?|Honey|Medicated soap|Maggi|Rice": "When the price of a good changes, its effect on quantity demanded has two parts. The substitution effect is the change caused by the good becoming dearer or cheaper relative to other goods; it is always negative, because buyers switch towards a good that has become relatively cheaper and away from one that has become dearer. The effect is largest for goods with many close substitutes. Maggi seasoning cubes compete with many similar brands of seasoning, so if Maggi's price rises, buyers quickly switch to other cubes and buy much less Maggi: C. Honey, medicated soap and rice have fewer ready brand substitutes in the shopper's mind. Memory tip: many close substitutes, big (negative) substitution effect.",
 "|In a monopolistic competition, if the demand for goods is inelastic, the monopolist will|shut down production|charge higher prices|give discount to buyers|charge lower prices": "Price elasticity of demand decides how total revenue changes when price changes. If demand is inelastic (less than 1), a rise in price reduces the quantity sold by a smaller percentage than the price rises, so total revenue increases. A firm in monopolistic competition, which has some control over the price of its branded product, will therefore raise its price: B. Lowering prices or giving discounts (C, D) would reduce revenue when demand is inelastic; these make sense only when demand is elastic. Shutting down (A) is not related to elasticity. Memory tip: inelastic demand: raise the price, raise the revenue.",
 "|A major contribution of oil to the Nigerian economy is in the area of|manpower development|technology advancement|revenue generation|employment generation": "Since the 1970s oil has been Nigeria's main source of foreign exchange and government revenue. It has earned about 90% of export earnings and, for many years, more than half of government revenue through petroleum profits tax, royalties and the sale of crude oil, shared among the three tiers of government: C. The oil industry is capital-intensive and employs relatively few Nigerians, so its contribution to employment (D) is small, and most of its technology is imported. Since the 2014 oil-price fall, Nigeria has tried harder to diversify its revenue, but oil remains its key earner. Memory tip: oil fills the treasury, not the job market.",
 "|An economy in which government makes all major production and distribution decisions is a|market economy|subsistence economy|feudal economy|planned economy": "In a planned economy (also called a command, centrally planned or socialist economy), the government owns the main means of production and a central planning authority decides what to produce, how to produce it and how to distribute it: D. Prices and output are set by the planners, not by demand and supply. In a market economy (A) these decisions are made by private individuals through the price mechanism. A subsistence economy (B) produces mainly for the producers' own use, and a feudal economy (C) was based on lords owning land worked by serfs. Examples of planned economies are the former Soviet Union and North Korea. Memory tip: planned = the government plans everything.",
 "|The graph above illustrates|an inelastic supply curve|a unitary elastic curve|a perfectly inelastic supply curve|an elastic supply curve": "Elasticity of supply measures how much quantity supplied responds to a change in price. In the graph the supply curve SS is very flat and starts from the price axis. A small rise in price, from P₁ to P₂, causes a large rise in quantity supplied, from q₂ to q₁. Because the percentage change in quantity is greater than the percentage change in price, supply is elastic: D. An inelastic supply curve (A) would be steep, a perfectly inelastic one (C) vertical, and a straight supply curve through the origin has unitary elasticity (B). Any straight-line supply curve that cuts the price axis is elastic. Memory tip: flat curve, flexible supply; steep curve, stubborn supply.",
 "|The achievement of efficient fuel distribution in Nigeria is mainly hampered by|the activities of middlemen|low level of technical know-how|poor road network|inadequate skilled manpower": "Petroleum products in Nigeria pass from depots through many middlemen (marketers, tanker owners and filling-station operators) before reaching the consumer. Some of them hoard fuel to sell later at higher prices, divert products meant for one area to another, or smuggle them to neighbouring countries, creating artificial scarcity and long queues: A. Poor roads and pipeline vandalism also slow distribution, but the activities of middlemen are the main reason given for inefficient fuel distribution. In 2023 the petrol subsidy was removed and the downstream sector was deregulated, which has reduced hoarding for price gain. The trap: C (poor road network) is also a real problem, which makes it tempting. Memory tip: scarcity at the pump often starts with hoarding in the middle.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nMonthly wages (₦1000) ... No. of employees\n5 ... 1\n8 ... 4\n10 ... 1\n11 ... 2\n20 ... 2\n\nFrom the table above, find the modal wage.|₦10000|₦8000|₦5000|₦1500": "The mode is the value that occurs most often; in a frequency table it is the value with the highest frequency.\nStep 1: Find the highest number of employees: 4.\nStep 2: Read across: 4 employees earn 8 (in thousands of naira).\nStep 3: Modal wage = 8 × ₦1000 = ₦8000: B.\nRemember the wages are given in ₦1000s, so 8 means ₦8000. The other options are wages earned by fewer employees, or not in the table at all. Memory tip: the mode is the most popular value.",
 "|A country can correct balance of payments deficit by|increasing production of quality goods for exports|revaluing her currency|increasing inputs|banning the importation of harmful goods": "A balance of payments deficit means a country pays out more foreign exchange than it earns. It can be corrected by raising export earnings or cutting imports. Increasing the production of quality goods for export makes the country's goods competitive abroad, so more foreign exchange flows in: A. Revaluing the currency (B) makes exports dearer for foreigners and imports cheaper at home, which worsens a deficit; devaluation is the measure usually suggested. Increasing inputs (C) is vague, and banning harmful goods (D) is a health measure that saves little foreign exchange. Memory tip: earn more abroad than you spend abroad.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nEmployed ... Unemployed\n230,000 ... 20,000\n\nFrom the table above, the unemployment rate is|8.7%|8.0%|11.5%|9.2%": "The unemployment rate is the number of unemployed as a percentage of the labour force (employed + unemployed).\nStep 1: Labour force = 230,000 + 20,000 = 250,000.\nStep 2: Unemployment rate = 20,000 ÷ 250,000 × 100 = 8.0%: B.\nThe trap: A (8.7%) comes from dividing the unemployed by the employed only (20,000 ÷ 230,000 × 100 ≈ 8.7%), a slip students really make. Memory tip: unemployed over everyone who wants work.",
 "|Increase in per capita GNP of a country is an indicator of|exchange rate stabilization|economic growth|high cost of living|economic development": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real output of goods and services, usually measured by GDP or GNP and by GNP per head (per capita GNP). So a rise in per capita GNP is an indicator of economic growth: B. Economic development (D) is wider: it means growth together with improvements in people's welfare, such as better health, education, housing, less poverty and fairer income distribution. A country can grow without developing if the extra income goes to a few people. The trap: D (economic development) is the closely related term that students often treat as the same as growth. Memory tip: growth is more output; development is a better life.",
 "|A market structure in which there are many sellers of differentiated products is|perfect competition|monopolistic competition|monopoly|duopoly": "Monopolistic competition is a market structure with many sellers, each selling a product that is slightly different from the others through branding, packaging, quality or location: B. Examples are hairdressing salons, restaurants and brands of soap or toothpaste. Each firm has a little control over its price, but entry is free, so in the long run profits are normal. Perfect competition (A) also has many sellers, but their products are identical (homogeneous). A monopoly (C) has a single seller, and a duopoly (D) has two. The trap: A (perfect competition) also has many sellers, so the word \"differentiated\" is what decides it. Memory tip: many sellers + different brands = monopolistic competition.",
 "|One of the features of an underdeveloped economy is|absence of administrative bottlenecks|high level of unemployment|high level of literacy|even distribution of income": "Underdeveloped (less developed) economies share common features: low income per head, dependence on agriculture and primary exports, rapid population growth, low literacy, poor infrastructure, unequal income distribution, and high levels of unemployment and underemployment: B. The other options describe the opposite: administrative bottlenecks (red tape, corruption) are common in these economies, literacy is low, and income is unevenly distributed. Memory tip: an underdeveloped economy has too few jobs and too much red tape.",
 "|The graph above illustrates|isoquant|isorevenue map|isocost|indifference map": "An indifference curve joins all combinations of two goods that give a consumer the same level of satisfaction, so he is \"indifferent\" between them. It slopes downward and is convex to the origin because of the diminishing marginal rate of substitution. A set of such curves (I₁, I₂, I₃), each further from the origin showing higher satisfaction, is an indifference map: D. The axes in the graph are labelled \"Commodity X\" and \"Commodity Y\", which shows a consumer choosing between two goods. An isoquant (A) has the same shape but shows combinations of factors of production (labour and capital) giving the same output, and an isocost line (C) is straight. The trap: A (isoquant) looks identical, but its axes would be factors, not commodities. Memory tip: indifference = goods and satisfaction; isoquant = inputs and output.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput ... F.C (₦) ... V.C (₦) ... T.C (₦)\n0 ... 100 ... - ... 100\n1 ... 100 ... 100 ... 200\n2 ... 100 ... 150 ... 250\n3 ... 100 ... X ... 300\n4 ... 100 ... 250 ... 350\n\nFrom the table above, find the value of X.|₦300|₦400|₦100|₦200": "Total cost (TC) = fixed cost (FC) + variable cost (VC), so VC = TC − FC.\nStep 1: At output 3, TC = ₦300 and FC = ₦100.\nStep 2: X = VC = ₦300 − ₦100 = ₦200: D.\nCheck the pattern: VC rises 100, 150, 200, 250 while TC rises 200, 250, 300, 350, always ₦100 (the fixed cost) apart. The trap: A (₦300) is the total cost, chosen by students who read across the row instead of subtracting. Memory tip: TC = FC + VC, so VC = TC − FC.",
 "|A joint stock company is an example of a|limited liability company|partnership|sole-proprietorship|cooperative society": "A joint stock company is a business owned by many shareholders who contribute capital by buying shares. It is a separate legal entity from its owners, and each shareholder's liability for the company's debts is limited to the amount he paid or agreed to pay for his shares. That is why such companies are called limited liability companies, either private (Ltd) or public (Plc): A. In a partnership and a sole proprietorship (B, C) the owners usually have unlimited liability. A cooperative society (D) is owned by its members for their mutual benefit, with one member one vote. Memory tip: shares in a joint stock company limit the shareholder's risk.",
 "|Government deficit financing in an economy will lead to|a reduction in the levels of employment|a contraction in income|a decrease in output|an increase in the economic output": "Deficit financing means a government deliberately spends more than its revenue and covers the gap by borrowing (from the public, banks or the Central Bank). The extra spending on salaries, roads and other projects raises aggregate demand. If the economy has unused resources, firms respond by producing more and employing more workers, so output, income and employment rise: D. A reduction in employment, a contraction in income and a decrease in output (A, B, C) are the opposite effects. If the economy is already at full employment, deficit financing mainly causes inflation rather than more output. Memory tip: more government spending, more demand, more output.",
 "|In a free enterprise economy, economic problems are solved by|a planning committee|government agencies|both the public and the private sectors|the price mechanism": "A free enterprise (capitalist or market) economy leaves the basic economic problems of what, how and for whom to produce to private individuals and firms. Their decisions are guided by the price mechanism: rising prices signal that consumers want more, so producers supply more, and falling prices signal the opposite. Prices also ration goods among buyers: D. A planning committee or government agencies (A, B) decide in a planned economy, and a mix of the public and private sectors (C) describes a mixed economy such as Nigeria. Adam Smith called the price mechanism the \"invisible hand\". Memory tip: in a free market, prices do the planning.",
 "|Which of the following is a normative reasoning?|The higher the price the lower the quantity demanded|A rise in the interest rate will lead to a fall in the borrowing rate|The higher the price the higher the quantity supplied|firms should not maximize profits in the sale of their products": "Positive statements describe what is, was or will be and can be tested against facts; normative statements express a value judgement about what ought to be and usually contain words such as \"should\" or \"ought\". \"Firms should not maximize profits in the sale of their products\" is an opinion about how firms ought to behave, so it is normative: D. \"The higher the price the lower the quantity demanded\" (A, the law of demand) and \"the higher the price the higher the quantity supplied\" (C, the law of supply) are positive, testable statements, and so is option B, a prediction about interest rates and borrowing. Memory tip: look for \"should\": it signals a normative statement.",
 "|The graphs above illustrate|derived demand|complementary demand|composite demand|competitive demand": "The two graphs show the demand curves for commodity I and commodity II. At the same price P, the demand curve for each commodity shifts to the right at the same time, so more of both is bought together. Goods whose demand rises and falls together because they are used together, such as cars and petrol or bread and butter, are in joint or complementary demand: B. Competitive demand (D) would show the demand for one good rising while the demand for its substitute falls. Derived demand (A) is demand for a factor because of demand for what it helps to produce, and composite demand (C) is demand for one good with several uses. Memory tip: complements move together; competitors move apart.",
 "|A distinguishing characteristic of a perfect market is that there is|the absence of free entry and exit|preferential treatment|presence of heterogeneous goods|the absence of transportation cost": "A perfect market is one where buyers and sellers have full knowledge of prices and one price rules for the same good everywhere. Its assumptions include many buyers and sellers, homogeneous (identical) goods, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge, no preferential treatment, perfect mobility of factors and the absence of transport costs. Without transport costs, a good costs the same wherever it is bought, which is why a single price can rule: D. Options A, B and C are the opposite of perfect market conditions: they describe barriers to entry, favouritism and differentiated goods. Memory tip: in a perfect market nothing gets in the way, not even transport costs.",
 "|One of the instruments used by the Central Bank to control money supply is|development bonds|the reserve requirements|tax rate|the exchange rate": "The Central Bank of Nigeria controls the money supply through monetary policy instruments: open market operations (buying and selling government securities), the monetary policy rate (bank rate), reserve requirements (the cash reserve ratio and liquidity ratio), special deposits, selective credit control and moral suasion. Raising the reserve requirement forces commercial banks to keep more of their deposits idle, so they lend less and money supply falls; lowering it does the opposite: B. The tax rate (C) is a fiscal policy tool of government, not of the Central Bank. Development bonds (A) are a way for government to borrow, and the exchange rate (D) is a price the Central Bank manages, not a tool for controlling money supply. Memory tip: hold more in reserve, lend less money.",
 "|The economic problem of how to produce is answered by the|number of people in a country|available resources in a country|system of economy practised|available technical know-how": "\"How to produce\" is the basic economic problem of choosing the method of production: whether to use more labour (labour-intensive) or more machines (capital-intensive), and which techniques to apply. This choice depends mainly on the technical know-how (technology) available in the country. A country with advanced technology can use automated factories; one with simple technology relies on manual methods: D. The number of people (A) and available resources (B) affect what and how much can be produced, while the economic system (C) decides who makes the decision. Memory tip: what to produce depends on wants; how to produce depends on know-how.",
 "|A monopolist attains the optimum level of output when|MR = MC|MR < MC|MR = AC|MR > MC": "A monopolist, like any profit-maximising firm, produces the output at which marginal revenue (MR) equals marginal cost (MC): A. If MR is greater than MC (D), producing more would add to profit, so output should rise. If MR is less than MC (B), the last unit costs more than it brings in, so output should fall. Only when MR = MC is profit at its maximum. The monopolist then charges the highest price consumers will pay for that output, read off the demand (AR) curve. MR = AC (C) has no special meaning. Memory tip: MR = MC for every firm.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNo. of Workers ... Total Product ... Marginal Product\n1 ... 5 ... -\n2 ... 15 ... 10\n3 ... V ... 15\n4 ... 46 ... W\n5 ... 52 ... 6\n\nDetermine the value of W.|16|17|10|15": "Marginal product (MP) is the change in total product (TP) when one more worker is added.\nStep 1: Find V first: V = TP of 2 workers + MP of the 3rd worker = 15 + 15 = 30.\nStep 2: W = TP of 4 workers − TP of 3 workers = 46 − 30 = 16: A.\nStep 3: Check: TP of 5 workers = 46 + 6 = 52, which matches the table.\nThe trap: D (15) repeats the MP of the 3rd worker, given by students who do not work out V first. Memory tip: MP = this row's TP − the previous row's TP.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNo. of Workers ... Total Product ... Marginal Product\n1 ... 5 ... -\n2 ... 15 ... 10\n3 ... V ... 15\n4 ... 46 ... W\n5 ... 52 ... 6\n\nCalculate the value of V.|30|35|20|25": "Total product (TP) grows by the marginal product (MP) of each new worker, so TP of 3 workers = TP of 2 workers + MP of the 3rd worker.\nStep 1: TP of 2 workers = 15.\nStep 2: MP of the 3rd worker = 15.\nStep 3: V = 15 + 15 = 30: A.\nCheck: with V = 30, the 4th worker's MP = 46 − 30 = 16, and the 5th worker's MP = 52 − 46 = 6, which matches the table. Memory tip: TP now = TP before + MP now.",
 "|Development plans fail mainly because of|corruption and political instability|over-dependence on foreign aid|high cost of plan implementation|shortage of personnel": "A development plan sets out what government intends to achieve over a period, such as Nigeria's First National Development Plan (1962–68) and the later rolling plans. Many of these plans failed, and the main reasons were corruption and political instability. Corruption diverts the money meant for planned projects into private pockets, so roads, factories and schools are left half-built. Political instability, such as the coups and frequent changes of government in Nigeria's history, means each new regime abandons its predecessor's plan. These two problems explain plan failure more than foreign aid, cost or staffing: A. Over-dependence on foreign aid and shortage of skilled personnel are real but smaller problems. Memory tip: plans fail when money is stolen and governments keep changing.",
 "|One of the major uses of national income statistics in Nigeria is to|equally distribute national income among citizens|determine the total population|compare population growth among nations|estimate per capita income": "National income is the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. Its statistics have many uses: measuring growth, comparing living standards, planning and policy-making. One important use is estimating per capita income: Per capita income = national income ÷ population. This tells us the average income per person, a rough guide to the standard of living, and it is used to compare Nigeria with other countries: D. National income figures cannot themselves distribute income equally (A), and the population is found by a census, not from national income data (B, C). Memory tip: per capita means \"per head\": divide the national cake by the number of people.",
 "|The major characteristic of an underdeveloped nation is|over-dependence on foreign products|dominance of the industrial sector|high per capita income|efficient and sensitive financial sector": "An underdeveloped (developing) country has low per capita income, a large agricultural sector, a weak industrial base, high population growth and a poor financial system. Because it manufactures little, it depends heavily on imports of finished goods (cars, machines, drugs, even food) from developed countries: A. Options B, C and D (a dominant industrial sector, high per capita income and an efficient, sensitive financial sector) are all signs of a developed economy, the opposite of what is asked. Memory tip: underdeveloped countries export raw materials and import finished goods.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT among the financial institutions in Nigeria?|Federal Mortgage Bank|Insurance companies|Microfinance companies|Federal Ministry of Finance": "Financial institutions are organisations that deal in money and credit: they accept deposits, lend, insure or channel savings into investment. In Nigeria they include the Central Bank, commercial and merchant banks, development banks such as the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (which finances housing), insurance companies, microfinance banks, the stock exchange and pension fund administrators. The Federal Ministry of Finance is a government ministry: it prepares the budget, manages government revenue and runs fiscal policy, but it does not take deposits or lend to the public, so it is not a financial institution: D. Memory tip: a ministry makes policy; a financial institution handles people's money.",
 "|Given 0.25, 1.25, 1.80, 1.10, 10.20, 10.15 and 1.55, determine the range.|0.25|10.00|1.55|9.95": "The range is the simplest measure of dispersion: the highest value minus the lowest value.\nStep 1: Find the highest value: 10.20 (it is larger than 10.15).\nStep 2: Find the lowest value: 0.25.\nStep 3: Range = 10.20 − 0.25 = 9.95: D.\nThe figures are not in order, so scan them all (or arrange them: 0.25, 1.10, 1.25, 1.55, 1.80, 10.15, 10.20) before picking the extremes. Memory tip: Range = Top − Bottom.",
 "|One of the problems facing the Nigerian oil and gas industry is|over-capacity utilization|the discovery of alternative oil sources|shortage of indigenous manpower|the excess supply of petroleum products": "The Nigerian oil and gas industry faces many problems: refineries working far below capacity (under-capacity utilization), pipeline vandalism and oil theft, gas flaring and pollution in the Niger Delta, frequent shortages of petrol, and heavy dependence on foreign oil companies and expatriate staff because there are too few skilled indigenous engineers and technicians: C. Options A and D state the opposite of the truth: capacity is under-used, not over-used, and petroleum products have often been scarce, not in excess supply. The Nigerian Content Act of 2010 was passed to build up local manpower and firms. Memory tip: Nigeria has the oil but has long lacked enough of its own people with the skills to run the industry.",
 "|The demand curve faced by a monopolist is|positive sloped|vertical|horizontal|negatively sloped": "A monopolist is the only seller of a good with no close substitutes, so its demand curve is the whole market demand curve. Market demand obeys the law of demand: more is bought at lower prices. So the monopolist faces a downward-sloping (negatively sloped) demand curve: if it wants to sell more, it must lower its price: D. A horizontal demand curve (C) is faced by a firm in perfect competition, which can sell any amount at the ruling market price. The trap: C (horizontal) catches students who mix up the monopolist with the perfectly competitive firm. Memory tip: the monopolist sets the price OR the quantity, never both, because its demand curve slopes down.",
 "|The rate of interest charged on loans is determined by the|exchange rate|fiscal policy|risk associated with the loan|rate of production in the country": "Interest is the price paid for borrowing money. The rate a lender charges on a loan depends on several things: the central bank's policy rate, the length of the loan, the amount, the security (collateral) offered and, above all, the risk that the borrower will not repay. A risky borrower, such as a new business with no collateral, is charged a higher rate to compensate the lender for the chance of loss: C. The exchange rate, fiscal policy and the rate of production do not directly fix the rate on a particular loan. Memory tip: higher risk, higher interest.",
 "|A country that is over-populated will face the problem of|low birth rate|low per capita income|high wage rate|full employment": "A country is over-populated when its population is greater than its resources and capital can support at the best standard of living (the optimum population). With too many people sharing a limited national income, the income per head is low: per capita income = national income ÷ population, and a large population makes the result small: B. Over-population also brings unemployment, low wages, pressure on schools and hospitals, and congestion. A low birth rate, high wages and full employment (A, C, D) are the opposite of what over-population causes. Memory tip: share the same cake among more people and each slice is smaller.",
 "|If an additional unit of a variable factor input causes a fall in the marginal product, this implies that|total product starts declining|average product has become zero|diminishing returns has set in|increasing returns is operating": "Marginal product (MP) is the extra output from one more unit of a variable factor, such as one more worker on a fixed piece of land. The law of diminishing returns says that as more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, MP eventually falls. So once an extra unit causes a fall in MP, diminishing returns has set in: C. Total product does not start declining yet (A); it falls only when MP becomes negative. Average product is still positive (B). The trap: A tempts students who confuse a fall in marginal product with a fall in total product; total output still rises, only more slowly. Memory tip: MP falls first, TP falls only when MP goes below zero.",
 "|From the graph above, P₂ in price control situation is referred to as|minimum price|shut-down price|maximum price|mark-up price": "Government sometimes fixes prices by law. A minimum price (price floor) is set ABOVE the equilibrium price to protect producers, for example farmers' incomes or workers' wages (a minimum wage). A maximum price (price ceiling) is set BELOW equilibrium to protect consumers. In the graph, the demand and supply curves cross at the equilibrium price, and P₂ is drawn above that point, so P₂ is a minimum price: A. At P₂ quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded, creating a surplus, which is the usual result of a minimum price. The trap: C (maximum price) catches students who think \"highest line on the graph\" means maximum price; a maximum price lies below equilibrium. Memory tip: a floor is above the ground (equilibrium); a ceiling is held down below it.",
 "|The additional cost incurred by producing an additional unit of output is known as|fixed cost|total cost|average cost|marginal cost": "Marginal cost (MC) is the addition to total cost when one more unit of output is produced: MC = change in total cost ÷ change in output. For example, if 10 bags of feed cost ₦50,000 and 11 bags cost ₦54,000, the marginal cost of the 11th bag is ₦4,000: D. Fixed cost (A) does not change with output, total cost (B) is the whole cost of all units, and average cost (C) is total cost divided by output. The trap: C (average cost) is the closely related concept students often confuse with marginal cost; average is cost per unit of all output, marginal is the cost of the extra unit. Memory tip: Marginal = More: the cost of one more.",
 "|One of the factors that determine the supply of agricultural produce in Nigeria is|size of the population|technological development|consumer's choice|consumers' income": "The supply of farm produce depends on the price of the produce, the cost of inputs, the weather and season, government policy (taxes and subsidies), the number of farmers, and the state of technology. Technological development, such as improved seed varieties, fertilizers, irrigation and tractors, raises output per hectare and shifts the supply curve to the right: B. Consumers' choice (tastes), consumers' income and the size of the population are determinants of demand, not supply, so they do not decide how much farmers produce for sale. Memory tip: technology works on the farm (supply); income and tastes work in the market (demand).",
 "|From the graph above, fixing maximum price of garri below equilibrium price at P₁ will|create an excess supply of garri|encourage the production of garri|increase the consumption of garri|decrease the consumption of garri": "A maximum price (price ceiling) fixed below equilibrium is meant to make a necessity like garri cheaper. In the graph, equilibrium is at P₂ with quantity Q₂. At the lower price P₁:\nStep 1: Buyers want Q₃ (read where P₁ meets the demand curve).\nStep 2: Farmers supply only Q₁ (read where P₁ meets the supply curve).\nStep 3: Only what is supplied can be bought and eaten, so consumption falls from Q₂ to Q₁: D.\nThe gap Q₃ − Q₁ is a shortage (excess demand), not excess supply (A), and the low price discourages production (B). The trap: C (increase consumption) catches students who confuse the larger quantity people want at the low price with what they can actually get. Memory tip: you cannot eat garri that was never produced.",
 "|In the theory of consumer behaviour, a consumer is said to maximize utility when|marginal utility of a commodity is equal to the price paid for it|marginal utility of a commodity X is equal to the price of the commodity Y|average utility of a commodity is equal to the price paid for it|total utility of a commodity is equal to the price paid for it": "Under cardinal utility theory, a consumer buying one good maximizes satisfaction when the marginal utility of the last unit bought equals the price paid for it: MU = P. If MU is greater than the price, buying one more unit adds more satisfaction than it costs, so he buys more; as he buys more, MU falls until it equals the price. If MU is less than the price, he buys less: A. With several goods, the rule becomes MUx/Px = MUy/Py. Comparing MU of X with the price of Y (B) makes no sense, and average or total utility (C, D) is not the deciding margin. Memory tip: keep buying until the last unit is worth just what you pay for it.",
 "|If Mr. Wale took a loan for the purpose of investment, the demand for money is|precautionary motive|transactionary and precautionary motives|speculative motive|speculative and transactionary motives": "Keynes gave three motives for holding (demanding) money. The transactions motive is for day-to-day spending; the precautionary motive is for emergencies; the speculative motive is for taking advantage of investment opportunities, such as buying bonds or shares when their prices are expected to rise or interest rates to change. Mr. Wale borrowed money for investment, to make a gain from buying assets, so his demand for money is the speculative motive: C. The trap: D (speculative and transactionary) tempts students who think any business use counts as transactions, but the purpose here is investment, not paying for daily purchases. Memory tip: Transactions = Today's spending; Precautionary = Problems; Speculative = Stocks and shares.",
 "|The overall demand for crude oil for various uses is an example of|derived demand|competitive demand|composite demand|complementary demand": "Composite demand is the total demand for a good that has several different uses. Crude oil is refined into petrol, diesel, kerosene, aviation fuel and bitumen, and its by-products go into plastics and chemicals, so the overall demand for it is the sum of the demands for all these uses: C. Derived demand (A) is demand for a thing because of what it helps produce, like the demand for labour. Competitive demand (B) is for substitutes such as Coke and Pepsi, and complementary demand (D) is for goods used together such as cars and petrol. The trap: A (derived demand) is closely related, since oil is wanted to make other products, but \"overall demand for various uses\" is the definition of composite demand. Memory tip: composite = one good, many uses, added up.",
 "|One of the factors limiting the effectiveness of division of labour in the production process is the|prevailing wage rate|level of technology|unemployment|population size": "Division of labour (specialisation) means breaking production into small tasks, each done by a different worker. Its effectiveness is limited by the size of the market, the nature of the product and the level of technology. Breaking work into many specialised stages usually needs machines, tools and organised methods of production; where the level of technology is low, as in much traditional craft work, a product cannot be split into many mechanised steps, so division of labour can go only a little way: B. The prevailing wage rate and unemployment do not decide how far work can be divided. Population size matters only through the size of the market. Memory tip: the further the technology, the finer the division of labour.",
 "|One of the important features of perfect competition is|adequate knowledge of existing prices|the influence on prices by individual economic units|the restriction in terms of movement of goods and services|the administration of a centralized price system": "Perfect competition has these features: many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous (identical) product, free entry and exit, free mobility of factors and goods, and perfect knowledge of market prices. Because every buyer and seller knows the ruling price, no seller can charge more, and every firm is a price taker: A. Option B (individual units influencing price) describes monopoly or oligopoly. Option C (restriction on movement) is the opposite of free mobility. Option D (centralized pricing) is a feature of a command economy. Memory tip: in perfect competition everybody knows the price and nobody can change it.",
 "|What effect would a change in price of a commodity have on its supply?|An increase in supply|No change in supply|A decrease in supply|A change in the quantity supplied": "Economists separate supply from quantity supplied. A change in the commodity's own price causes a movement along the same supply curve: a change in quantity supplied: D. A change in supply, an increase (A) or a decrease (C), means the whole supply curve shifts, and is caused by other factors such as cost of production, technology, weather or taxes. The trap: A (an increase in supply) catches students who use \"supply\" loosely; a rise in price increases the quantity supplied, not supply. Memory tip: own price → move along (quantity supplied); anything else → shift (supply).",
 "|All pairs of quantities from which a consumer derives equal satisfaction can be plotted on|an indifference curve|an exceptional demand curve|a budget line|an isoquant map": "An indifference curve shows all the combinations of two goods, say rice and beans, that give a consumer the same level of satisfaction, so he is indifferent between them. It slopes downward and is convex to the origin, because to keep satisfaction constant he must give up some of one good to get more of the other: A. A budget line (C) shows the combinations he can afford with his income, not those giving equal satisfaction. An isoquant (D) is the producer's version: equal output from combinations of two inputs. The trap: D (isoquant map) is the closely related producer concept; \"equal satisfaction\" means consumer, so indifference curve. Memory tip: Indifference = equal satisfaction; Isoquant = equal quantity of output.",
 "|Which of the following is a major determinant of price elasticity of demand?|The price of the commodity|Availability of factors of production|The prices of factors of production|Income of the consumers": "Price elasticity of demand measures how strongly quantity demanded reacts to a change in price. Its determinants include the availability of substitutes, the nature of the good (necessity or luxury), the number of uses, time, and the price of the commodity in relation to income. When a commodity is expensive and takes a large share of income, such as a car or a television, a price change affects the buyer greatly, so demand is elastic. When it is cheap, such as salt or matches, a price change hardly matters, so demand is inelastic: A. Factors of production (B, C) affect supply, not demand. Memory tip: the dearer the good, the more a price change hurts, the more elastic the demand.",
 "|If Nigeria has comparative advantage over Ghana in producing cocoa, this means|Nigeria produces cocoa more cheaply than Ghana|Nigeria and Ghana produce at the same level|Ghana produces cocoa more than Nigeria|Nigeria produces more cocoa than Ghana": "Comparative advantage, from David Ricardo, means a country can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country, that is, more cheaply in terms of the other goods it gives up. If Nigeria has comparative advantage in cocoa, Nigeria produces cocoa more cheaply (relatively) than Ghana, and both gain if Nigeria specialises in cocoa and trades: A. It does not mean producing a larger quantity (D) or the same level (B). The trap: D (Nigeria produces more cocoa) catches students who confuse a larger output with lower cost; advantage is about cost, not quantity. Memory tip: comparative advantage = cheaper in sacrifice, not bigger in size.",
 "|One of the ways of improving the marketing of agricultural produce is by|reviewing the land tenure system|ensuring even distribution of farm inputs|embarking on agricultural mechanisation|establishing agricultural marketing boards": "Marketing of agricultural produce covers buying from farmers, grading, storing, transporting and selling. Marketing boards were set up to do exactly this: they buy produce at guaranteed prices, grade and store it, and sell it at home and abroad, protecting farmers from middlemen and price swings: D. Reviewing the land tenure system, distributing farm inputs evenly and mechanising farming all raise production, not marketing. Nigeria's commodity boards were scrapped in 1986 under the Structural Adjustment Programme, and today produce is sold through private buyers, commodity exchanges and cooperatives, but marketing boards remain the textbook answer. Memory tip: production = land, inputs, machines; marketing = boards, storage, transport.",
 "|The study of economics is necessary mainly because of|unemployment|unlimited resources|scarcity of resources|overpopulation": "Economics exists because of scarcity. Human wants are unlimited, but the resources for satisfying them (land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship) are limited. So individuals, firms and governments must choose which wants to satisfy and give up others: this choice creates opportunity cost. The study of economics is therefore necessary mainly because of the scarcity of resources: C. Unemployment and overpopulation (A, D) are particular problems studied in economics, not the reason it exists, and resources are not unlimited (B). Memory tip: no scarcity, no choice; no choice, no economics.",
 "|If price of yams decreases from ₦15.00 per tuber to ₦13.50 and the quantity supplied decreases by 20%, what is the elasticity of supply?|2.00|0.50|1.50|1.00": "Elasticity of supply = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price.\nStep 1: Change in price = ₦15.00 − ₦13.50 = ₦1.50.\nStep 2: % change in price = 1.50/15.00 × 100 = 10%.\nStep 3: % change in quantity supplied = 20% (given).\nStep 4: Elasticity of supply = 20% ÷ 10% = 2.00: A.\nSupply is elastic because the answer is greater than 1. The trap: B (0.50) comes from dividing the wrong way (10 ÷ 20), a slip students really make. Memory tip: Quantity on top, Price below.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a source of government revenue?|Taxes, fees, licenses and fines|Personal income, disposable income and transfer earnings|Interest, dividends, profits and earnings|Grants, aids and borrowing": "Government revenue comes from taxes (income tax, company tax, VAT, customs and excise duties), fees, licences and fines; from interest, dividends and profits earned on government investments and enterprises; from grants and aid; and from borrowing at home and abroad. Personal income and disposable income belong to households, and transfer earnings are what a factor of production earns in its next best use, so none of these is a source of government revenue: B. Memory tip: government earns by taxing, charging, investing, receiving and borrowing, not from people's own pay packets directly.",
 "|Public limited liability companies are owned by|the federal government|private and individual organisations|the state governments|the federal and state governments": "A public limited liability company (plc), such as Dangote Cement Plc or MTN Nigeria, is a joint-stock company whose shares are sold to the general public on the stock exchange. Its owners are the shareholders: private individuals and organisations such as pension funds and other companies: B. \"Public\" here means its shares are open to the public, not that government owns it. Companies owned by federal or state governments (A, C, D) are public enterprises or public corporations, such as the NNPC. The trap: D (the federal and state governments) catches students who read \"public\" as \"government\". Memory tip: a public company is owned by the public who buy its shares; a public corporation is owned by government.",
 "|The type of inflation that emanates from excess demand over supply is|galloping inflation|imported inflation|demand-pull inflation|cost-push inflation": "Demand-pull inflation arises when aggregate demand exceeds the supply of goods and services, often described as \"too much money chasing too few goods\". Buyers bid up prices, so the excess demand pulls the general price level up: C. Cost-push inflation (D) comes from rising costs of production, such as higher wages or fuel prices. Imported inflation (B) comes from dearer imports, and galloping inflation (A) describes how fast prices rise, not the cause. The trap: D (cost-push) is the closely related term; ask whether the push comes from costs (supply side) or the pull from spending (demand side). Memory tip: demand Pulls, costs Push.",
 "|The price elasticity of supply of perishable goods is|elastic|unitary|inelastic|zero": "Elasticity of supply measures how far quantity supplied responds to price changes. Perishable goods such as tomatoes, fresh fish, milk and vegetables cannot be stored for long, so when price falls the seller cannot hold the goods back and must sell them anyway; when price rises he cannot quickly bring more to market, because new crops take time to grow. Supply therefore responds little to price: it is inelastic: C. Durable goods that can be stored, such as grains in silos, have more elastic supply. Zero (D) would mean no response at all, which is too extreme. Memory tip: if it rots, you must sell it, so supply cannot adjust.",
 "|In Nigeria, most agricultural policies are aimed at|ensuring equitable distribution of income|liberalizing all sectors of the nation's economy|encouraging the development of manufacturing sector|achieving self-sufficiency in food production": "Most of Nigeria's agricultural policies and programmes, including Operation Feed the Nation (1976), the Green Revolution (1980), the River Basin Development Authorities, the National Accelerated Food Production Programme and, more recently, the Anchor Borrowers Programme (2015), aim mainly at producing enough food for the growing population and reducing food imports: achieving self-sufficiency in food production: D. Equitable income distribution (A), liberalising all sectors (B) and developing manufacturing (C) are goals of other policies. Memory tip: feed the nation first.",
 "|One major problem encountered by ECOWAS is|conflict of interest among members|increasing population|weakness in the trade relationships|language barrier": "ECOWAS (Economic Community of West African States) was formed in 1975 by the Treaty of Lagos to promote trade and integration in West Africa. It faces several problems. Members often put national interests first, and some belong to rival groups such as the French-speaking UEMOA, so there is a conflict of interest (A). Trade between members is weak because they produce similar primary goods and trade more with their former colonial powers (C). Members also speak English, French or Portuguese, a language barrier to trade and cooperation (D). The marked answer is conflict of interest among members: A. C and D are accepted too; only increasing population (B) is not a problem of the organisation. Memory tip: ECOWAS problems: interests clash, trade is thin, languages differ.",
 "|One of the characteristics of private enterprises is|provision of welfare to the citizens|that tax payers bear all the risks|public ownership of shares|individual funding of business activities": "A private enterprise is a business owned and controlled by private individuals: sole proprietorships, partnerships and private or public limited companies. The owners provide the capital themselves (individual funding), run the business to make profit, and bear the risk of loss: D. Provision of welfare to citizens (A), taxpayers bearing the risks (B) and public ownership (C) are features of public enterprises owned by government. Memory tip: private enterprise: private money, private risk, private profit.",
 "|The concept of marginal utility indicates the relationship between utility and|price|satisfaction|cost|revenue": "Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit of a good. The marginal utility theory of value says it is MU, not total utility, that determines the price a consumer is willing to pay. A consumer pays for a unit of a good no more than the satisfaction that unit gives him, so in equilibrium MU = price. Because MU falls as more is consumed, he will buy more only at a lower price, which is why the demand curve slopes downward. So marginal utility links utility and price: A. Satisfaction (B) is simply another word for utility, and cost and revenue (C, D) belong to the theory of the firm. Memory tip: what you will pay depends on the satisfaction from the last unit.",
 "|In Nigeria, unemployment can be minimized through|diversification of the economy|strict immigration law|paying unemployment benefits|restrictive fiscal policy": "Nigeria's economy depends heavily on crude oil, which earns most of its foreign exchange but employs very few people because it is capital-intensive. Diversification means developing other sectors (agriculture, manufacturing, solid minerals, tourism, ICT and services), which are labour-intensive and can absorb millions of job seekers: A. Strict immigration laws (B) protect a few jobs at best; unemployment benefits (C) support the unemployed but do not create work; restrictive fiscal policy (D) cuts spending and demand, which raises unemployment. Memory tip: put the eggs in many baskets and many more hands get work.",
 "|The demand for two or more commodities to satisfy a particular want is|derived|composite|competitive|joint": "Joint (complementary) demand is demand for two or more goods that are used together to satisfy one want: a car and petrol, a phone and a SIM card, bread and butter. A rise in the price of one reduces demand for the other: D. Derived demand (A) is demand for something because of what it helps produce, such as labour. Composite demand (B) is total demand for one good with several uses, and competitive demand (C) is for substitutes that satisfy the same want in place of each other. The trap: C (competitive) catches students because both involve two goods and one want, but competing goods replace each other while joint goods go together. Memory tip: Joint = Join together.",
 "|The supply curve above is|fairly inelastic|perfectly inelastic|unitary elastic|perfectly elastic": "Elasticity of supply is measured from the supply curve. A straight-line supply curve that passes through the origin has elasticity of exactly 1 at every point, because the percentage change in quantity always equals the percentage change in price. The supply curve in the diagram is a straight line running towards the origin, so supply is unitary elastic: C. A vertical supply curve would be perfectly inelastic (B), and a horizontal one perfectly elastic (D). A straight supply line that meets the quantity axis would be inelastic, and one that meets the price axis would be elastic. The trap: A (fairly inelastic) tempts students who judge elasticity by how steep the line looks rather than where it starts. Memory tip: from the origin = one.",
 "|One of the factors affecting change in demand for a commodity is the|supply of the commodity|availability of substitutes|price of the commodity|technical know-how": "A change in demand means the whole demand curve shifts. It is caused by factors other than the good's own price: income, tastes, population, and the prices and availability of substitutes and complements. If more substitutes become available, buyers switch to them and demand for the commodity falls (shifts left): B. A change in the commodity's own price (C) only moves buyers along the same curve: that is a change in quantity demanded. Supply (A) and technical know-how (D) affect supply, not demand. The trap: C (price of the commodity) catches students who forget that own price causes a change in quantity demanded, not a change in demand. Memory tip: own price → move along; other factors → shift.",
 "|Public ownership of productive factors is a feature of|socialism|capitalism|feudalism|mixed economy": "In a socialist (command) economy the state owns and controls the main means of production (land, factories, mines and banks) on behalf of the people, and a central planning authority decides what to produce: A. Under capitalism (B) these factors are privately owned, and a mixed economy (D) combines private and public ownership. Feudalism (C) was a medieval system in which lords owned the land and peasants worked it. The trap: D (mixed economy) has some public ownership, but public ownership of productive factors as the defining feature is socialism. Memory tip: Socialism = State owns.",
 "|If the marginal propensity to save is 0.8, calculate the multiplier.|1.25|5.00|1.30|2.25": "The multiplier shows how many times national income rises when investment rises by ₦1. It is the reciprocal of the marginal propensity to save.\nStep 1: Multiplier (k) = 1 ÷ MPS.\nStep 2: k = 1 ÷ 0.8 = 1.25: A.\nA check: MPC = 1 − MPS = 0.2, and k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.8 = 1.25, the same. The trap: B (5.00) is what you get by treating 0.8 as the MPC (1 ÷ 0.2 = 5), a slip students really make. Memory tip: the more people save, the smaller the multiplier.",
 "|Which of the following is a set of measures of dispersion?|Mean and median|Mean and standard deviation|Range and variance|Mode and mean": "Statistics describes data in two main ways. Measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode) give a typical or average value. Measures of dispersion show how spread out the values are: range (highest − lowest), mean deviation, variance and standard deviation. Only option C contains two measures of dispersion, range and variance: C. Options A and D contain only averages, and option B mixes an average (the mean) with a dispersion measure (standard deviation). The trap: B catches students who spot \"standard deviation\" and stop reading; both items must be measures of dispersion. Memory tip: dispersion = spread: range, variance, standard deviation.",
 "|The slow pace of industrial growth in Nigeria can be attributed to|lack of raw materials|lack of market for produce|inadequate skilled labour|overdependence on the oil sector": "Nigeria's industrial growth has been slow for many reasons: poor infrastructure (especially power), lack of capital, shortage of skilled labour, policy inconsistency and, importantly, overdependence on the oil sector. Since the oil boom of the 1970s, oil has provided most of government revenue and export earnings, so other sectors were neglected and a strong naira made imported goods cheaper than local manufactures. This \"Dutch disease\" left manufacturing weak: D. Nigeria has plenty of raw materials and a large market of over 200 million people, so A and B do not apply. Inadequate skilled labour (C) is a real problem but is less basic than the neglect caused by oil dependence. Memory tip: easy oil money crowded out the factories.",
 "|Consumers allocate their resources based on the|law of diminishing marginal utility|value in use and value in exchange principle|law of returns to scale|diamond and water paradox": "Because of the law of diminishing marginal utility, each extra unit of a good gives less satisfaction than the one before. A rational consumer with limited income therefore does not spend it all on one good; he spreads it over different goods so that the last naira spent on each gives the same marginal utility (the equi-marginal principle, MUx/Px = MUy/Py). So consumers allocate their resources based on the law of diminishing marginal utility: A. The value paradox (B, D) explains why water is cheap and diamonds dear, and the law of returns to scale (C) is about production. Memory tip: the last naira should buy equal satisfaction everywhere.",
 "|A country's terms of trade can be improved by|revaluation of currency|collective bargaining|reducing demand for imported goods|imposing lower export duties": "Terms of trade (TOT) = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100. They improve when export prices rise or import prices fall. Reducing demand for imported goods lowers the prices paid for imports, so the TOT rise: C. Revaluation of the currency (A) also improves the TOT: Nigeria's exports cost more in foreign currency and its imports cost less in naira, so export prices rise relative to import prices. Devaluation does the opposite and worsens them. Both A and C are therefore accepted. Collective bargaining (B) is about wages, and lower export duties (D) make exports cheaper, which does not raise their prices. Memory tip: TOT improve when you sell dear and buy cheap.",
 "|A government budget proposing to spend more than its expected revenue in a year will create|opportunity for combating inflation|depression in the economy|shortage in money supply|opportunity for full employment": "A budget in which government plans to spend more than its expected revenue is a deficit budget. It is an expansionary fiscal policy: the extra government spending raises aggregate demand, which encourages firms to produce more and hire more workers. It is used in a depression or when unemployment is high, to move the economy towards full employment: D. It does not combat inflation (A); a surplus budget does that. It fights depression rather than causing it (B), and deficit financing usually increases the money supply rather than causing a shortage (C). Memory tip: deficit budget = more spending = more jobs.",
 "|Population growth is determined by|dependency ratio|labour force|per capita income|net migration": "The size of a population changes through births, deaths and migration. Population growth = (births − deaths) + net migration, where net migration = immigrants − emigrants. So net migration directly determines population growth: D. The dependency ratio (A) and the labour force (B) describe the structure of a population, not what changes its size, and per capita income (C) is income per head. Memory tip: people arrive by birth or by bus; they leave by death or by departure.",
 "|Which of the following can cause oil glut in the international market?|Excess demand over supply|Under-capacity utilization|Excess supply over demand|Economic boom": "An oil glut is a situation in which the world supply of crude oil is greater than demand, so there is a surplus and prices fall sharply, as in the 1980s and again in 2014–2016, when the US shale boom raised supply. So excess supply over demand causes a glut: C. Excess demand (A) and an economic boom (D) do the opposite: they push oil prices up. Under-capacity utilization (B) means producing less, which reduces supply. Memory tip: glut = too much oil, too few buyers.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity supplied\n20 ... 100\n16 ... 58\n\nFrom the table above, the elasticity of supply is|inelastic supply|perfectly elastic|elastic supply|unitary elastic": "Elasticity of supply = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price, using the first figures as the base.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 100 − 58 = 42; % change = 42/100 × 100 = 42%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦20 − ₦16 = ₦4; % change = 4/20 × 100 = 20%.\nStep 3: Es = 42% ÷ 20% = 2.1.\nSince 2.1 is greater than 1, supply is elastic: C. Using the lower figures as base (42/58 ÷ 4/16 ≈ 2.9) also gives a value greater than 1, so the answer is the same. Memory tip: Es above 1 = elastic; below 1 = inelastic; exactly 1 = unitary.",
 "|Localization of industry is encouraged by|low interest charge on loans|high standard of living|low foreign exchange rate|improvements in infrastructure": "Localization of industry is the concentration of many firms of the same industry in one area, such as the textile mills that grew up in Kaduna or the industrial estates at Ikeja. Firms cluster where they find good infrastructure (reliable power, roads, rail, ports and water), raw materials, a market, skilled labour and supporting services. Improvements in infrastructure therefore encourage localization: D. Low interest charges and the exchange rate (A, C) affect firms everywhere in the country, not one particular area, and a high standard of living (B) does not by itself draw an industry into one place. Memory tip: build the roads, power and water, and the factories will come.",
 "|A company's expenditure on raw materials is termed as|prime cost|explicit cost|average cost|implicit cost": "Costs can be classified in several ways. Explicit costs are actual money payments to outsiders for inputs: wages, rent, and payment for raw materials. Implicit costs are the opportunity costs of the owner's own resources, which involve no cash payment, such as the salary an owner forgoes. A company's spending on raw materials is a cash payment, so it is an explicit cost: B. It is also a prime cost (A): prime cost is the direct (variable) cost of production, made up mainly of raw materials and direct labour, so both answers are accepted. Average cost (C) is total cost per unit. Memory tip: Explicit = Exchanged cash; Implicit = Imagined (opportunity) cost.",
 "|Goods sold in perfectly competitive markets are generally|homogenous|intermediate and final|durable and non-durable|heterogenous": "Perfect competition is a market structure with very many buyers and sellers, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge and, most important here, a homogeneous product. Every firm sells exactly the same good, so a buyer has no reason to prefer one seller's maize or cement to another's, and no firm can charge more than the market price. That is why each firm is a price taker facing a horizontal demand curve: A. Heterogeneous (differentiated) products, D, belong to monopolistic competition, where brands and packaging make goods slightly different. The trap: D is the exact opposite word, and students who read quickly pick it. Memory tip: perfect competition = identical products; monopolistic competition = differentiated products.",
 "|One of the reasons why Nigeria is a mono-economy is because of her|huge volume of petroleum activities in the country|diversified system of economic activities|high demand for foreign commodities|high rate of unemployment": "A mono-economy (mono-product or single-product economy) depends on one main commodity for most of its export earnings and government revenue. Since the oil boom of the 1970s, petroleum has provided about 90% of Nigeria's export earnings and most federal revenue, while agriculture, once the backbone of the economy, was neglected. The huge volume of petroleum activity is therefore the reason Nigeria is called a mono-economy: A. A diversified system of economic activities (B) is the cure for a mono-economy, not its cause. High demand for foreign goods and unemployment are effects or separate problems. Memory tip: \"mono\" means one; Nigeria's one is oil.",
 "|The bowed shape of the Production Possibilities Curve illustrates|that production is inefficient|that demand is relatively inelastic|that production is unattainable|the law of increasing marginal cost": "The production possibilities curve (PPC) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with all its resources fully employed. It is usually bowed outward (concave to the origin) because resources are not equally good at producing both goods. As the economy moves more resources into producing, say, rice instead of cement, it must use resources less and less suited to rice, so each extra bag of rice costs more and more cement. This rising opportunity cost is the law of increasing marginal (opportunity) cost: D. Inefficient production is shown by a point inside the curve (A), and unattainable output by a point outside it (C). Memory tip: bowed out = increasing cost; a straight-line PPC = constant cost.",
 "|A firm owned and managed by a family is an example of|joint stock exchange|sole proprietorship|cooperative society|partnership": "Business organisations are classified by who owns and controls them. A sole proprietorship (one-man business) is owned and controlled by one person who takes all the profit and bears unlimited liability. Most family businesses in Nigeria, such as a family shop, farm or bakery, are owned and run by the head of the family, with spouse and children helping out; they are therefore treated as sole proprietorships: B. A partnership (D) needs a formal agreement among 2 to 20 partners who share profit, and a cooperative is formed by many members for their common welfare. \"Joint stock exchange\" (A) is not a business form at all. Memory tip: one owner, helped by family = sole proprietorship.",
 "|Determine the level of a nation's national income if government expenditure is ₦15, investment is ₦20, consumption is ₦25, export is ₦10, import 8 and MPC = 0.82.|₦284|₦62|₦120|₦344": "National income by the expenditure approach adds up all spending on final goods and services: Y = C + I + G + (X − M).\nStep 1: Net exports = X − M = ₦10 − ₦8 = ₦2.\nStep 2: Y = 25 + 20 + 15 + 2 = ₦62: B.\nThe MPC (0.82) is extra information. Because total consumption (₦25) is already given, there is no need to use the multiplier; dividing ₦62 by (1 − 0.82) to get about ₦344 (D) would count consumption twice. The trap: D catches students who feel they must use every figure given. Memory tip: when C itself is given, just add C + I + G + X − M; use the multiplier only for changes in spending or when C is given as a function.",
 "|One of the major causes of farmer's unstable income in Nigeria is|the effect of diseases and pests|industrial pollution|mining and extraction activities|population growth": "Farm incomes in Nigeria are unstable, rising and falling from year to year. The main reasons are natural: most farming is rain-fed, and crops and animals are attacked by diseases and pests such as locusts, armyworms, quelea birds, cassava mosaic disease and bird flu. An outbreak can destroy a whole season's output, and with it the farmer's income. Price swings caused by changes in supply add to the instability. So the effect of diseases and pests is a major cause: A. Industrial pollution and mining affect some local areas only, and population growth actually raises the demand for food. Memory tip: farm income depends on harvest, and pests and weather decide the harvest.",
 "|Given that x̄ = 7 and ΣFX = 56, calculate the value of N.|9|7|8|6": "For grouped or frequency data, the mean is x̄ = ΣFX ÷ N, where N = ΣF is the total number of items.\nStep 1: Rearrange: N = ΣFX ÷ x̄.\nStep 2: N = 56 ÷ 7 = 8: C.\nCheck: 56 ÷ 8 = 7, which is the mean given. Memory tip: mean = total ÷ number, so number = total ÷ mean.",
 "|Given that Qd = 10 − 2P while Qs = 5 + 3P, if P = ₦1 determine the equilibrium quantity.|7|6|5|8": "Equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nStep 1: Check the price given: at P = ₦1, Qd = 10 − 2(1) = 8.\nStep 2: Qs = 5 + 3(1) = 8.\nStep 3: Qd = Qs = 8, so ₦1 is indeed the equilibrium price and the equilibrium quantity is 8 units: D.\nYou could also solve 10 − 2P = 5 + 3P, giving 5 = 5P and P = ₦1, then substitute. Memory tip: set Qd = Qs to find P, then put P back into either equation to find Q.",
 "|The main determinant of a change in the quantity of a commodity demanded is the|price of the commodity|elasticity of the commodity|consumer's rationality|government policy": "Economists separate a change in quantity demanded from a change in demand. A change in quantity demanded is a movement along the same demand curve, and it is caused only by a change in the price of the commodity itself: a fall in price raises the quantity demanded, a rise lowers it. A change in demand (a shift of the whole curve) is caused by other factors such as income, tastes, prices of related goods, population and government policy (D). So the main determinant is the price of the commodity: A. Elasticity measures how strongly quantity responds to price; it does not cause the change. Memory tip: own price moves you ALONG the curve; anything else SHIFTS it.",
 "|Which of the following budgets will increase government expenditure?|Zero base budget|Surplus budget|Deficit budget|Balanced budget": "A budget compares planned government revenue with planned expenditure. In a balanced budget, expenditure equals revenue; in a surplus budget, revenue exceeds expenditure, so spending is held below revenue to fight inflation. In a deficit budget, planned expenditure exceeds revenue, and the gap is financed by borrowing or by printing money. It is used to expand the economy in a recession, so it increases government spending: C. A zero-base budget is a method of preparing a budget, justifying every item from zero each year; it does not by itself raise spending. Memory tip: deficit = spend more than you earn; surplus = earn more than you spend.",
 "|Inflationary trends in an economy will negatively affect, majorly|debtors|creditors|the business class|flexible income earners": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level, which reduces the purchasing power of money. People who lent money (creditors) suffer most: when the loan is repaid, the same number of naira buys fewer goods than when it was lent, unless the interest rate is higher than the inflation rate. Debtors (A) gain, because they repay with \"cheaper\" money. Businessmen often gain too, because prices rise faster than some costs, and people whose incomes rise with prices (flexible income earners) are protected. The losers are creditors and fixed income earners such as pensioners: B. The trap: A, debtors, is the reverse: inflation helps borrowers. Memory tip: inflation robs lenders and rewards borrowers.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a source of government revenue?|Grants, aids and borrowing|Taxes, fees, licences and fines|Interest, dividends, profit and earnings|Personal income, disposable income and transfer earnings": "Government revenue comes from taxes (income tax, company tax, customs and excise duties, VAT), fees, licences and fines, rents and royalties (such as oil royalties), interest, dividends and profits of public enterprises, and grants, aids and borrowing. Personal income and disposable income are incomes received by households; disposable income is what is left after paying tax. Transfer earnings are the minimum a factor must earn to stay in its present use, a term from the theory of rent. None of these is collected by government: D. Memory tip: government revenue comes from Taxes, Fees, Fines, Profits and Borrowing.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nQuantity consumed ... Total utility ... Marginal utility\n0 ... 0 ... -\n1 ... 10 ... 10\n2 ... 15 ... 5\n3 ... 17 ... 2\n4 ... 18 ... 1\n5 ... 18 ... 0\n\nFrom the table above, it can be inferred that there is|diminishing return to scale|excess of demand|diminishing marginal productivity|diminishing marginal utility": "Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit: MU = change in total utility.\nFrom the table: the 1st unit adds 10, the 2nd 15 − 10 = 5, the 3rd 17 − 15 = 2, the 4th 18 − 17 = 1, the 5th 18 − 18 = 0.\nTotal utility keeps rising but by smaller and smaller amounts, and it stops rising when MU reaches zero. This is the law of diminishing marginal utility: D. Diminishing returns and diminishing marginal productivity (A, C) are laws of production, about output, not satisfaction. The trap: C sounds almost the same but refers to production, not consumption. Memory tip: the first plate of jollof is the sweetest.",
 "|The graph above represents|a decrease in quantity supplied|an increase in supply|a decrease in supply|an increase in quantity supplied": "The diagram shows ONE supply curve, S. When the price rises from P₁ to P₂, the producer moves up along that same curve from point (Q₁, P₁) to point (Q₂, P₂), and supplies more. A movement along the supply curve caused by a change in the good's own price is called a change in quantity supplied; here quantity rises, so it is an increase in quantity supplied: D. An increase in supply (B) would mean the whole curve shifting to the right, caused by factors such as better technology or lower costs; no second curve is drawn here. The trap: B sounds similar, but \"supply\" means the whole curve. Memory tip: one curve and a change of price = quantity supplied; a new curve = supply.",
 "|The appropriate formula for the computation of standard deviation of a good data is|S = √(Σx − x̄) ÷ n|S = √(x − x̄) ÷ n|S = √[Σ(fx − x̄) ÷ n]|S = √[Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n]": "Standard deviation measures how widely data are spread around the mean.\nStep 1: Find the mean, x̄.\nStep 2: Find each deviation, x − x̄.\nStep 3: Square each deviation, so negatives do not cancel positives: (x − x̄)².\nStep 4: Add them and divide by n: this is the variance, Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n.\nStep 5: Take the square root: S = √[Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n]: D.\nWithout squaring (A, B), the deviations add up to zero. For frequency data use S = √[Σf(x − x̄)² ÷ Σf]. Memory tip: standard deviation = square root of the average squared deviation.",
 "|A partnership sourcing for funds to expand its business would approach|a central bank|a money market|an insurance company|a stock exchange": "A partnership is owned by 2 to 20 partners. It is not a public company, so it cannot sell shares to the public on the stock exchange (D), which deals in the shares and bonds of public companies and government. To raise extra funds, partners can add their own capital, admit new partners, or borrow. Borrowing is done through the money market, chiefly from commercial (deposit money) banks, which give loans and overdrafts: B. The Central Bank (A) is the bankers' bank and lends only to banks and government, and insurance companies (C) mainly offer cover against risks. The trap: D tempts students who link \"funds to expand\" with the stock exchange. Memory tip: only public companies go to the stock exchange.",
 "|Excess supply over demand will place a price advantage on|government|foreign investor|suppliers|consumers": "When supply exceeds demand at the current price, there is a surplus or glut. Sellers left with unsold goods compete for the few buyers, so they cut prices; this happens with tomatoes or oranges at harvest time. The falling price benefits buyers, who get more for their money and can bargain hard. So excess supply gives a price advantage to consumers: D. Excess demand would favour suppliers (C), because buyers would compete and push the price up. The trap: C is the reverse case, excess demand. Memory tip: a glut is good for buyers; a shortage is good for sellers.",
 "|Which of the following institutions operates in the money market?|Central Bank|Mortgage banks|Money deposit banks|Insurance companies": "The money market deals in short-term funds (loans of up to about one year), traded through instruments such as treasury bills, call money and commercial papers. Its institutions include deposit money (commercial) banks, merchant banks, discount houses and the Central Bank. Deposit money banks are the main dealers, since they accept deposits and give short-term loans and overdrafts: C. The Central Bank (A) is also part of the money market: it issues treasury bills and is lender of last resort, so it is called the apex of the money market. Both A and C are accepted. Mortgage banks and insurance companies provide long-term funds in the capital market. Memory tip: money market = short-term; capital market = long-term.",
 "|The national income of a country can be measured by using|output method|economic solution|working population|level of industrialisation": "National income is the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. It can be measured by three methods that should give the same total. The output (product or value-added) method adds the value added by every sector (agriculture, mining, manufacturing, services). The income method adds all factor incomes: wages, rent, interest and profit. The expenditure method adds C + I + G + (X − M). So the output method is correct: A. \"Economic solution\", \"working population\" and \"level of industrialisation\" are not methods of measuring national income. Memory tip: national income has three doors in: Output, Income and Expenditure (O-I-E).",
 "|Given a consumption function C = 10 + 0.6Y, determine the value of C if Y is 20.|36.6|22.00|30.60|26.00": "A consumption function shows how consumption depends on income: C = a + bY, where a is autonomous consumption (spending even at zero income) and b is the MPC.\nStep 1: C = 10 + 0.6Y, with Y = 20.\nStep 2: 0.6 × 20 = 12.\nStep 3: C = 10 + 12 = 22: B.\nHere autonomous consumption is 10 and the MPC is 0.6, so 60 kobo of every extra naira of income is spent. Memory tip: C = a + bY: first multiply, then add.",
 "|The population theory that is concerned with the cases of over population and shortage of food production is|population density|Malthusian population theory|national population theory|demographic transition theory": "Thomas Robert Malthus, an English economist, published his Essay on the Principle of Population in 1798. He argued that population grows in geometric progression (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) while food production grows only in arithmetic progression (1, 2, 3, 4, 5) because of diminishing returns on land. Population would therefore outrun food supply, causing over-population and shortage of food, until checked by famine, disease and war (positive checks) or by late marriage and moral restraint (preventive checks): B. The demographic transition theory (D) explains how birth and death rates change as a country develops. Memory tip: Malthus = mouths outrun food.",
 "|The modal value of 3, 8, 4, 6, 3, 5, 2, 7, 3 and 5 is|6|3|4|5": "The mode is the value that occurs most often in a set of data.\nStep 1: Count each value in 3, 8, 4, 6, 3, 5, 2, 7, 3, 5.\nStep 2: 3 appears three times; 5 appears twice; 2, 4, 6, 7 and 8 appear once each.\nStep 3: The mode is 3: B.\nThe trap: D, 5, is the value that appears twice, and it is also close to the mean (46 ÷ 10 = 4.6). Memory tip: Mode = Most often.",
 "|Which of the factors of production is inelastic?|land and labour|capital|labour|land": "The supply of a factor is inelastic when it cannot be increased much even if its reward rises. Land, meaning all natural resources, is a free gift of nature; its total quantity is fixed and cannot be increased by man, however high rent becomes. So the supply of land is perfectly inelastic: D. Labour can increase over time through population growth and training, and capital can be built up through saving and investment, so their supply responds to reward. Option A wrongly includes labour. Memory tip: God made the land, and He is not making any more of it.",
 "|Cost-push inflation occurs when|production cost is high|government embarks on deficit financing|factor prices decrease|there is too much money in circulation": "Cost-push inflation occurs when the costs of production rise and firms pass them on to consumers as higher prices. Causes include wage increases won by trade unions, higher prices of raw materials and imported inputs (often after the naira falls), higher fuel and electricity costs, and higher taxes on production. So cost-push inflation occurs when production cost is high: A. Deficit financing and too much money in circulation (B, D) cause demand-pull inflation, where too much money chases too few goods. Falling factor prices (C) would lower costs, not raise prices. The trap: B and D are real causes of inflation, but of demand-pull, not cost-push. Memory tip: cost-push = costs push prices up; demand-pull = spending pulls prices up.",
 "|Government proposed projects for 2013 fiscal year: \nI. Dam – completed\nII. Road – uncompleted\nIII. Airport – completed\nIV. Hospital – uncompleted\n\nFrom the information provided above, government opportunity cost would be|II only|I and II|I only|II and IV": "Opportunity cost is the alternative forgone when a choice is made. Government had four proposed projects in 2013 but limited funds, so it had to choose. It completed I (the dam) and III (the airport). The projects it gave up, the road (II) and the hospital (IV), were left uncompleted; these are what society sacrificed to have the dam and airport. So government's opportunity cost is II and IV: D. Options C and B include the dam, which was actually obtained, not forgone. Memory tip: opportunity cost = what you did NOT get.",
 "|One of the unique features that distinguishes perfect and imperfect markets is|profit motive principles|absence of price control board|price determines production and distribution|number of sellers and buyers": "Markets are classified by how competitive they are, and the main dividing line is the number of buyers and sellers. Perfect competition has very many small buyers and sellers, none of whom can affect the price. Imperfect markets have one seller (monopoly), a few sellers (oligopoly), two sellers (duopoly) or many sellers of differentiated products (monopolistic competition), so firms have some control over price: D. The profit motive (A) and the use of price to guide production (C) are found in both kinds of market. Memory tip: count the sellers: very many = perfect; one or few = imperfect.",
 "|The most effective measure to reduce unemployment is to|use population control measure|restructure the educational curriculum|diversify the economy|remove administrative constraints": "Unemployment means people willing and able to work cannot find jobs. Nigeria's economy depends heavily on oil, which earns much revenue but employs few people. Diversifying the economy into agriculture, agro-processing, manufacturing, solid minerals, tourism and ICT directly creates large numbers of jobs for those already looking for work, so it is the most effective measure: C. Population control (A) is sometimes suggested, but it only slows the growth of the labour force about 15 to 20 years later and creates no jobs for today's unemployed, so C is the better answer. Curriculum reform helps reduce structural unemployment, but only slowly. Memory tip: jobs come from many sectors, so spread the economy wide.",
 "|Which of the following is an indirect tax?|Poll tax|Personal income tax|Company tax|Excise duties": "A direct tax is paid by the person or firm on whom it is levied: personal income tax, company income tax, poll tax and capital gains tax. An indirect tax is levied on goods and services, and its burden can be shifted to the consumer through higher prices. Excise duties are indirect taxes on goods produced within the country, such as beer, cigarettes and soft drinks; others are import and export duties, VAT and sales tax: D. Poll tax (A) is a fixed tax on every adult, paid directly. Memory tip: if you pay it when you buy something, it is indirect.",
 "|Localization of industry is encouraged because of|low interest charges on loans|improvements in infrastructure|low foreign exchange rate|high standard of living": "Localization of industry is the concentration of firms in one industry in a particular area, such as textile firms in Kano and Kaduna or the motor spare parts industry at Nnewi. Firms are drawn to an area by good infrastructure (power, water, roads, railways, ports and communications), nearness to raw materials and markets, available labour and government policy. Improvements in infrastructure cut costs and attract firms, so they encourage localization: B. A high standard of living (D) is a result of industrial growth, not a reason firms of one industry settle in one place. Memory tip: industries go where the roads, power and raw materials are.",
 "|A country's term of trade can be improved by|collective bargaining|imposing lower export duties|revaluation of currency|reducing demand for imported goods": "Terms of trade = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100. They improve when export prices rise relative to import prices, so each unit of exports buys more imports. Revaluation of the currency raises the foreign-currency price of a country's exports and lowers the naira price of imports, so the ratio rises: C. Reducing demand for imported goods (D) lowers the prices paid for imports and also raises the ratio, so D is accepted too. Imposing lower export duties (B) makes exports cheaper abroad, lowering export prices and worsening the terms of trade. The trap: B sounds like it helps exports, but cheaper exports mean worse terms of trade. Memory tip: terms of trade are about prices, not volumes.",
 "|One of the functions of the price system is that it|encourages high volume of production|dictates what the citizens will consume|discourages the consumption of harmful goods|regulates production and consumption": "The price system (price mechanism) is the free interaction of demand and supply in markets. Prices act as signals: a rising price shows that consumers want more of a good and that it is profitable to produce, so producers move resources into it; a falling price does the opposite. At the same time, price rations goods among consumers, since only those willing and able to pay buy them. In this way the price system regulates both production and consumption: D. It does not dictate consumption (B): consumers are free to choose. Nor does it discourage harmful goods (C); that needs taxes or laws. Memory tip: price is the traffic light of the market economy.",
 "|Petro-chemical industries are located in the Rivers State of Nigeria because of|favourable soil|coal deposit|crude oil deposit|favourable climate": "Industries are often located near their main raw materials, especially when the raw materials are bulky or are supplied by pipeline. Petrochemical industries turn crude oil and natural gas into products such as plastics, fertilizers, polypropylene and carbon black. Rivers State is in the oil-rich Niger Delta, has the Port Harcourt refineries and the Eleme Petrochemicals plant, and is close to the oil and gas fields, so petrochemical industries are sited there because of the crude oil deposit: C. Coal (B) is found mainly in Enugu, not Rivers State. Memory tip: petro = petroleum, so look for the oil fields.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nUnit of labour ... Total output (kg)\n1 ... 18\n2 ... 70\n3 ... 150\n4 ... 283\n5 ... 350\n\nFrom the table above, the average product of the 3rd unit of labour is|50|70|350|150": "Average product (AP) is total output divided by the number of units of the variable factor: AP = TP ÷ L.\nStep 1: With 3 units of labour, total output = 150 kg.\nStep 2: AP = 150 ÷ 3 = 50 kg: A.\nThe trap: D, 150, is the total product of the three workers, not the output per worker. (The marginal product of the 3rd unit would be 150 − 70 = 80 kg.) Memory tip: Average = Total ÷ number; Marginal = change in Total.",
 "|Which of the following is an indicator of economic growth?|High tax rate|Technology development|High gross domestic product|High interest rate": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real output of goods and services over time. It is measured by the rise in real gross domestic product (GDP) or real GDP per head. So a high gross domestic product is the indicator: C. Technology development (B) is a cause or source of growth, not the measure of it, while high tax and interest rates (A, D) can slow growth. Economic development is broader: it also covers better health, education, income distribution and quality of life. Memory tip: growth = more output (GDP); development = better lives.",
 "|The storage of goods in order to derive more satisfaction in future is|marginal utility|place utility|form utility|time utility": "Utility is the satisfaction derived from a good. Production creates several kinds of utility. Form utility comes from changing the shape of a good (turning cassava into garri). Place utility comes from moving goods to where they are needed (transporting yams from Benue to Lagos). Time utility comes from storing goods until they are needed, as when a wholesaler stores grain harvested in November and sells it in the lean months. Storing goods to give more satisfaction in future therefore creates time utility: D. Marginal utility (A) is the extra satisfaction from one more unit consumed. The trap: B, place utility, is created by transport, the twin of storage. Memory tip: transport = place utility; warehousing = time utility.",
 "|The production within domestic territory of a country is called the|net domestic product|gross domestic product|net income|disposable income": "Gross domestic product (GDP) is the total money value of all final goods and services produced within the geographical boundaries (domestic territory) of a country in a year, whether by citizens or foreigners. Gross national product (GNP) adds net income from abroad, so it measures what the citizens produce wherever they are. So production within the domestic territory is GDP: B. Net domestic product (A) is GDP minus depreciation, so it is not simply all production. Nigeria rebased its GDP in 2014, making it Africa's largest economy at the time. The trap: A also says \"domestic\", but net means after depreciation. Memory tip: Domestic = inside the borders; National = by the nationals.",
 "|A well conducted census is important for|distribution of educational materials in the cities|revenue generation|economic planning|providing economic opportunities in the rural areas": "A census is an official count of the population, giving its size, age and sex structure, occupation and geographical distribution. Nigeria's last census was in 2006. Accurate census figures are needed for economic planning: government uses them to plan for schools, hospitals, housing, roads, water and jobs, to share revenue among states and local governments, and to calculate per capita income: C. Options A and D are only small parts of planning, and a census is not itself a means of revenue generation (B). Memory tip: you cannot plan for people you have not counted.",
 "|The economic questions about what and how to produce in an economy is solely answered by the|government|labour union|private and public enterprise|system of economy practised": "Every economy faces the basic questions: what to produce, how to produce, for whom to produce, and how efficiently. Who answers them depends on the economic system practised. In a capitalist economy, the price mechanism and private firms decide. In a socialist (command) economy, a central planning authority of government decides. In a mixed economy such as Nigeria's, both the market and government decide. So the questions are answered by the system of economy practised: D. Government (A) answers them only in a socialist system. Memory tip: the system decides who decides.",
 "|The basic principle of cooperative societies is to|maintain the integrity of their members|protect the interests and pursue the welfare of members|ensure better working conditions for members|provide voluntary services to the members": "A cooperative society is a voluntary association of people with a common economic need who pool resources to help themselves. Examples are consumers', producers' (such as farmers'), and thrift and credit cooperatives. Its basic principle is to protect the interests and promote the welfare of its members, rather than to make profit; surpluses are shared as dividends according to patronage, and each member has one vote whatever his or her capital: B. Better working conditions (C) is the aim of trade unions, and voluntary services (D) describe charities. Memory tip: cooperative = \"each for all\": members helping members.",
 "|The main aim of Economic Community of West African States is the|discouragement of trade among member states|encouragement of revolts against former colonial masters|establishment of its headquarters in each member state|liberalisation of trade among member states": "The Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) was set up by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975, with headquarters in Abuja (it has only one headquarters, not one in each member state). Its main aim is economic integration of West Africa: removing tariffs and other barriers to trade, allowing free movement of people, goods and capital, and eventually a common market and monetary union. So its main aim is the liberalisation of trade among member states: D. Options A and B are the opposite of its purpose. Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger announced their withdrawal in 2024, leaving twelve members. Memory tip: ECOWAS = free trade and free movement in West Africa.",
 "|The most unique quality of money is its|durability|measure of value|standard of deferred payment|recognition": "Money has qualities that make it work well: general acceptability (recognition), durability, portability, divisibility, homogeneity, scarcity and stability of value. Of these, the one that sets money apart from every other asset is that people everywhere recognise and accept it in settling debts. A thing that is not generally accepted cannot serve as money, however durable it is: D. Durability (A) matters, but many durable things are not money. Measure of value and standard of deferred payment (B, C) are functions of money, what money does, not qualities it must have. Memory tip: money is money only because everybody agrees to accept it.",
 "|The U-shape of the long-run average cost curve is explained by|the law of variable proportions|the law of diminishing returns|marginal utility|returns to scale": "The long-run average cost (LRAC) curve is U-shaped. In the long run every factor can be changed, so the firm changes its scale of production. At first, larger scale brings increasing returns to scale (economies of scale: bulk buying, specialisation, cheaper finance), so LRAC falls. Beyond the optimum size, decreasing returns to scale (diseconomies: poor coordination, red tape) push LRAC up. So returns to scale explain the U-shape: D. The law of variable proportions (diminishing returns), A and B, explains the U-shape of the SHORT-run cost curves, where at least one factor is fixed. The trap: B (law of diminishing returns) catches students who forget that it applies only when a factor is fixed. Memory tip: short run, diminishing returns; long run, returns to scale.",
 "|In Nigeria, import substitution industrialization strategy is faced with the problem of|funding|preference for foreign goods|politicization|scarcity of local goods": "Import substitution industrialization (ISI) means producing at home goods that used to be imported, such as textiles, beverages and assembled vehicles, usually behind tariff walls. In Nigeria the strategy met several problems: dependence on imported raw materials and machines, poor power supply, and a strong preference among consumers for foreign goods, which they see as better quality and more prestigious. When buyers still choose imported products, local substitutes do not sell and imports are not reduced: B. Funding and politicization (A, C) are general problems, not the special weakness of ISI, and local goods are not scarce (D) because of the strategy. Memory tip: \"made in Nigeria\" fails if Nigerians still prefer \"imported\".",
 "|A cosmetic dealer sourcing for capital to expand his business will approach|commercial banks|Central Bank|mortgage banks|development banks": "Businesses raise capital from personal savings, partners, shares and loans. A trader such as a cosmetic dealer who needs money to expand would go to a commercial bank, which accepts deposits and gives loans and overdrafts to individuals and businesses: A. The Central Bank (B) is the bankers' bank and government's bank; it does not lend to traders. Mortgage banks (C) lend for building and buying houses. Development banks (D), such as the Bank of Industry, give long-term finance to large industrial and agricultural projects. Today a small trader might also use a microfinance bank. Memory tip: commercial banks are the traders' banks.",
 "|One of the advantages of using labour-intensive strategy of production is that|labour is expensive|capital is cheap|labour is cheap|capital is surplus": "Production can be labour-intensive (much labour, little capital, e.g. hand-weaving) or capital-intensive (much machinery, little labour, e.g. a bottling plant). A firm chooses the cheaper mix of factors. In a country like Nigeria, where labour is plentiful and wages are low while machines are costly and often imported, a labour-intensive method keeps costs down and also creates many jobs: C. Expensive labour (A), cheap capital (B) and surplus capital (D) are conditions that favour capital-intensive production instead. Memory tip: use more of whatever factor is cheap and plentiful.",
 "|A business organisation in which there is limitation by law to the amount of losses a shareholder can sustain in the event of business failure is|limited liability company|sole proprietorship|public enterprise|all of the above": "Liability means responsibility for a firm's debts. In a limited liability company the law limits each shareholder's loss to the amount he paid, or agreed to pay, for his shares; his personal property cannot be taken to pay the company's debts, because the company is a separate legal person: A. A sole proprietor (B) has unlimited liability: if the business fails, his house and car can be sold to pay its creditors. A public enterprise (C) is owned by government, not shareholders. So \"all of the above\" (D) is wrong. Memory tip: \"Ltd\" and \"Plc\" after a name warn creditors that liability is limited.",
 "|Government capacity to raise tax revenue in Nigeria is being hampered by|trade union activities|political instability|foreign influences|none of the above": "Government raises revenue through taxes such as personal income tax, company tax, VAT and customs duties. Its ability to raise more is limited by tax evasion and avoidance, a large informal sector, poor tax administration and resistance from taxpayers. In Nigeria, organised labour (the Nigeria Labour Congress and Trade Union Congress) has repeatedly resisted new or higher taxes and charges on workers and consumers, such as VAT increases, through protests and strikes, forcing government to drop or reduce them: A. Political instability (B) and foreign influences (C) are not the usual reasons given for weak tax collection. Memory tip: every new tax must get past the unions.",
 "|The effect of an expansionary monetary policy above full employment level is|deflationary|inflationary|balanced|deficit budget": "Expansionary monetary policy increases the money supply, for example by cutting interest rates or the cash reserve ratio, to raise spending. Below full employment the extra spending raises output and jobs. But once the economy is at full employment, all resources are already in use and output cannot rise further, so the extra demand only pulls up prices: demand-pull inflation: B. Deflationary (A) is the opposite effect. Balanced and deficit budget (C, D) describe fiscal policy, not the effect of monetary policy. Memory tip: more money chasing the same goods means higher prices.",
 "|The maximum amount of money a company is allowed to raise by issuing shares to the public is|paid-up capital|issued capital|authorized capital|capital premium": "A company's share capital has several meanings. Authorized (nominal or registered) capital is the maximum amount the company may raise by issuing shares, stated in its memorandum of association: C. Issued capital (B) is the part of authorized capital actually offered to and taken up by shareholders. Paid-up capital (A) is the part of issued capital that shareholders have actually paid for. Capital (share) premium (D) is the amount received above the nominal value of shares. The trap: B (issued capital) catches students who mix up what has been issued with the most that may be issued. Memory tip: Authorized ≥ Issued ≥ Paid-up.",
 "|The value of money will appreciate if|government executes projects|deficit budget is employed|balance of payments is favourable|surplus budget is employed": "The value of money is its purchasing power, and it moves opposite to the general price level. Money appreciates (gains value) when prices fall, which happens when there is less money in circulation. A surplus budget means government collects more in taxes than it spends, so it withdraws money from the economy, demand falls and prices fall: D. A deficit budget (B) and spending on government projects (A) pump money into the economy and push prices up, reducing the value of money. Memory tip: surplus budget squeezes money out and makes the naira buy more.",
 "|In Nigeria, government can ensure continuous supply of agricultural produce in all the seasons by|providing storage facilities|promoting marketing of agricultural produce|providing fertilizers|going into farming": "Most crops are harvested in one season, so produce is plentiful and cheap at harvest but scarce and dear later in the year; much of it also rots for lack of storage. If government provides storage facilities, such as silos, warehouses and cold rooms, harvests can be kept and released steadily, ensuring supply in all seasons: A. Promoting marketing (B) and providing fertilizers (C) help farmers but do not carry produce from one season to the next. Government going into farming (D) does not remove the seasonal problem. Memory tip: storage turns a seasonal crop into an all-year supply.",
 "|Rural-urban migration is influenced by|availability of social infrastructure in the urban area|availability of social amenities in the rural area|plenty of agricultural work in the urban area|none of the above": "Rural-urban migration is the movement of people from villages to towns. It is driven by push factors in rural areas (poverty, few jobs, poor amenities) and pull factors in towns: job opportunities and social infrastructure such as electricity, pipe-borne water, good roads, hospitals, schools and entertainment: A. Amenities in rural areas (B) would keep people in the village, and towns do not offer plenty of agricultural work (C). Memory tip: the bright lights of the city pull youths out of the village.",
 "|The demand curve for a perfectly competitive firm is|vertical|horizontal|downward sloping|upward sloping": "Under perfect competition there are very many sellers of an identical product, so no single firm can change the market price. The firm is a price-taker: it can sell as much as it wants at the ruling price, but would sell nothing if it charged more. Its demand curve is therefore a horizontal line at the market price, and this line is also its average revenue and marginal revenue curve: B. The INDUSTRY's demand curve slopes downward (C), and so does a monopolist's. The trap: C (downward sloping) catches students who confuse the firm's demand curve with the market demand curve. Memory tip: perfect competition firm, perfectly flat demand.",
 "|The most important determinant in the supply of agricultural products is|favourable weather condition|large number of youths|government funding|mixed number of youths and old people": "The supply of farm produce depends on price, input costs, technology, government policy and, above all, the weather. Most Nigerian farming is rain-fed, so good rainfall and sunshine give a bumper harvest, while drought, floods or pests can wipe out supply. Because nature controls so much of farm output, weather is the most important determinant of agricultural supply: A. A large number of youths or a mixture of young and old people (B, D) does not directly decide output, and government funding (C) helps but cannot replace good weather. Memory tip: in farming, the weather is the boss.",
 "|The marginal propensity to consume measures the relationship between|consumption and income|consumption|change in consumption and income|change in consumption and respective change in income": "The marginal propensity to consume (MPC) is the fraction of an extra unit of income that is spent on consumption: MPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY. For example, if income rises by ₦10,000 and consumption rises by ₦8,000, MPC = 8,000 ÷ 10,000 = 0.8. So it measures the relationship between a change in consumption and the respective change in income: D. Consumption and income (A) gives the average propensity to consume, APC = C ÷ Y. The trap: A catches students who confuse the marginal propensity (changes) with the average propensity (totals). Memory tip: Marginal means change: MPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY, and MPC + MPS = 1.",
 "|The major problem confronting agriculture in Nigeria includes|rural-urban migration|over-funding of the sector|under-funding of the sector|over-dependence on imported goods": "Nigerian agriculture faces many problems: rural-urban migration, use of crude tools, poor storage and transport, low prices, inadequate credit, land tenure problems and pests. A major one is rural-urban migration: young, able-bodied people leave the villages for the cities in search of white-collar jobs, leaving farming to old people with crude implements, so output and food supply fall: A. Agriculture has never been over-funded (B), and over-dependence on imported goods (D) is a problem of the whole economy rather than of agriculture itself. Memory tip: when the youths leave the farm, the farm grows old.",
 "|In Nigeria, export promotion strategy is aimed at|promoting foreign firms|improving government tax revenue|encouraging local firms|discouraging foreign investors": "Export promotion is an industrialization strategy that encourages local producers to make goods for foreign markets, so as to earn foreign exchange and reduce dependence on oil. Nigeria has used the Nigerian Export Promotion Council (1976), export incentives, export processing zones and the Export Expansion Grant to help local firms compete abroad: C. It is not meant to promote foreign firms (A) or mainly to raise tax revenue (B), and it does not discourage foreign investors (D). Memory tip: export promotion helps Nigerian firms sell outside Nigeria.",
 "|A distinguishing feature of free enterprise economy is that|profit maximization|state-ownership of resources|private ownership of resources|social welfare maximization": "A free enterprise (capitalist or market) economy has these features: private ownership of resources, the profit motive, the price mechanism, consumer sovereignty, competition and little government control. Producers own their factories and land and use them to make as much profit as possible. Both profit maximization (A) and private ownership of resources (C) therefore mark out a free enterprise economy, and both are accepted. State ownership of resources (B) and social welfare maximization (D) are features of a socialist (command) economy, where government owns resources and aims at the welfare of all. Memory tip: capitalism = private owners chasing profit.",
 "|The growth of private enterprise is constrained by|government|owners|market size|competition": "Private enterprises can grow only if they can sell more. The size of the market, which depends on population, people's incomes, transport links and access to foreign markets, sets the limit on how much a firm can sell and so how large it can grow: C. Adam Smith noted that \"the division of labour is limited by the extent of the market\". In a country where incomes are low, the market is small and firms stay small. Government (A), the owners (B) and competition (D) affect firms, but the market size is the basic limit. Memory tip: a firm can only be as big as its market.",
 "|The initial deposit of a deposit bank is 500,000. Calculate the total amount of credit the bank creates with a cash ratio of 20%.|100,000|600,000|400,000|2,500,000": "Commercial banks create credit because they need keep only a fraction of deposits as cash and can lend out the rest, which returns to banks as new deposits. The total credit (deposits) created = initial deposit ÷ cash ratio.\nStep 1: Cash ratio = 20% = 0.2.\nStep 2: Total credit = 500,000 ÷ 0.2 = 500,000 × 5 = 2,500,000: D.\nThe bank multiplier is 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5. The trap: C (400,000) is only the first loan the bank can make (500,000 − 20% of 500,000), not the total credit created through the whole banking system. Memory tip: credit created = deposit ÷ cash ratio.",
 "|Which of the following represents the features of a less developed country?|Low per capita income|High per capita income|High literacy|High birth rate": "A less developed country (LDC) has low per capita income, high birth and population growth rates, low literacy, a large agricultural sector, poor infrastructure and dependence on primary exports. Both low per capita income (A) and a high birth rate (D) are standard features, so both are accepted. High per capita income (B) and high literacy (C) describe developed countries such as the UK and Japan; in many LDCs a large share of adults cannot read or write. Memory tip: LDCs are low in income and literacy, but high in birth rate.",
 "|The major factor to be considered in the location of timber and plywood industries is|availability of timber and plywood trees|availability of labour|proximity of market|none of the above": "Industries are located by considering raw materials, market, labour, power, transport and government policy. Where the raw material is bulky and loses weight during processing, the industry is sited near its source to save transport cost. Timber and plywood mills use heavy logs, so they are located near the forests that supply the trees, e.g. the African Timber and Plywood company at Sapele: A. Labour (B) and market (C) matter less for such a weight-losing industry. Memory tip: heavy, weight-losing raw materials pull industry to their source.",
 "|One of the consequences of overpopulation is|high level of literacy|low mortality rate|high level of social vices|urban-rural migration": "Overpopulation exists when a country's population is too large for its resources and capital. The results include unemployment, low per capita income, pressure on schools, hospitals and housing, congestion, and a rise in social vices such as armed robbery, prostitution, drug abuse and cybercrime, as idle and poor people seek ways to survive: C. A high level of literacy and low mortality rate (A, B) are signs of development, not consequences of overpopulation. Overpopulation drives people from rural to urban areas, not urban-rural migration (D). Memory tip: too many people, too few jobs, more crime.",
 "|The price of a commodity increases from 2.00 to 7.00, while the quantity of its complement decreases from 75 to 50. Calculate the cross elasticity of demand.|15/2|2/15|1/15|7/10": "Cross elasticity of demand measures how the quantity demanded of one good responds to a change in the price of another good.\nStep 1: % change in price = (7 − 2) ÷ 2 = 5/2.\nStep 2: % change in quantity of the complement = (50 − 75) ÷ 75 = −25/75 = −1/3.\nStep 3: Cross elasticity = (−1/3) ÷ (5/2) = −1/3 × 2/5 = −2/15: B.\nThe sign is negative because the goods are complements (when one becomes dearer, less of both is bought). The trap: A (15/2) is the answer students get when they turn the fraction upside down, dividing the price change by the quantity change. Memory tip: elasticity = % change in QUANTITY ÷ % change in PRICE.",
 "|A principal factor that determines the value of money is the|price|face value|purchasing power|all of the above": "The value of money is its purchasing power: the amount of goods and services a unit of money can buy. It is determined mainly by the general price level, and the two move in opposite directions: Value of money = 1 ÷ price level. When prices rise (inflation), each naira buys less, so money loses value; when prices fall, money gains value: A. Purchasing power (C) is what the value of money IS, not the factor that determines it, and the face value (B) printed on a note does not change with what it can buy. Memory tip: prices up, value of money down.",
 "|The reduction of high fertility rate is a measure of population control designed|to increase total population|to reduce total population|to raise the birth rate|to increase the death rate": "Fertility rate is the average number of children born per woman. Countries with rapid population growth, like Nigeria, try to cut high fertility through family planning, education of girls and women, and campaigns for smaller families. The aim is to slow population growth and so reduce the total population, easing pressure on food, schools, jobs and health care: B. Increasing the population (A) is the opposite aim. Reducing fertility lowers, not raises, the birth rate (C), and no population policy aims to raise the death rate (D). Memory tip: fewer births per woman means a smaller population.",
 "|Capital accumulation for low income earners is better done through|savings|investment|funds|loans": "Capital accumulation means building up a stock of capital (tools, equipment, money for business) over time. For low-income earners the best way is saving: putting aside part of income regularly, however small, through thrift and credit societies, esusu or ajo contributions, cooperatives and savings accounts: A. Saving creates the funds that can later be invested. Investment (B) needs funds that have first been saved, and loans (D) must be repaid with interest, which is a heavy burden on a low income. Memory tip: you must save before you can invest.",
 "|The government of Nigeria can conserve foreign exchange by|buying foreign currency|improving international reserve|employing currency exchange|buying local currency": "Foreign exchange is needed to pay for imports and foreign debt. Nigeria earns it mainly from oil exports and keeps it as external (international) reserves at the Central Bank. Government conserves foreign exchange by building up and improving these reserves: increasing export earnings, cutting unnecessary imports and limiting the use of foreign currency for non-essential purposes: B. Buying foreign currency (A) uses up naira and does not save foreign exchange, and buying local currency (D) spends foreign exchange. Memory tip: conserve foreign exchange = earn more, spend less, keep the reserves.",
 "|The minimum number of persons who are legally allowed to operate a partnership business is between|two and five persons|seven and fifty persons|two and fifty persons|two and above": "A partnership is a business owned by two or more persons who share capital, management, profits and losses under an agreement (deed of partnership). The law requires at least two partners, and it sets an upper limit, generally 20 partners for an ordinary trading partnership; professional firms such as lawyers and accountants may have more. The minimum is therefore two persons, and a partnership is formed by two and above: D. The ranges in A, B and C are not the legal rule: the minimum is two, and seven is not required. Memory tip: one person is a sole trader; two or more make a partnership.",
 "|GNP is not a good measure of social welfare because there are unrecorded|social services|economic services|accounting services|engineering services": "GNP is the total value of goods and services produced by a country's citizens in a year. It is a poor measure of social welfare because many economic activities are never recorded: subsistence farming, housewives' services, do-it-yourself work, the large informal sector and illegal trade. These unrecorded economic services make GNP understate real output and welfare: B. GNP also ignores income distribution, leisure, pollution and the kind of goods produced. Accounting and engineering services (C, D) are paid for and recorded. Memory tip: if no one records it, GNP cannot count it.",
 "|One of the major goals of OPEC is to|set the international price of crude oil|protect member countries|protect crude oil production|control crude oil supply of member countries": "The Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was founded in Baghdad in 1960 and has its headquarters in Vienna; Nigeria joined in 1971. Its goals are to coordinate members' petroleum policies, stabilise oil prices and secure a steady income for members. Its main tool is the production quota: it controls how much crude oil each member supplies, raising or cutting output to influence prices: D. OPEC does not directly set the world price (A), since prices are set in the market. The trap: A tempts students because OPEC aims at stable prices, but it works by controlling supply. Memory tip: OPEC turns the tap; the market sets the price.",
 "|One of the factors considered in the determination of wages is|value of output|value of labour|all of the above|none of the above": "Wages are the price of labour, determined by the demand for and supply of labour, trade union bargaining and government minimum wage laws. On the demand side, the marginal productivity theory says an employer will pay a worker up to the value of the output he produces (marginal revenue product = marginal product × price). The more valuable a worker's output, the higher the wage the employer can pay: A. This is why skilled and productive workers earn more. Memory tip: a worker is paid according to the value of what he produces.",
 "|If government expenditure exceeds its revenue, this will give rise to a|surplus balance of payments|deficit balance of payments|surplus budget|deficit budget": "A budget is government's estimate of revenue and expenditure for a year. A balanced budget has revenue equal to expenditure. A surplus budget has revenue greater than expenditure. A deficit budget has expenditure greater than revenue, so government must borrow or draw on savings to cover the gap: D. Options A and B concern the balance of payments, which records a country's transactions with the rest of the world, not government's own spending and revenue. The trap: B (deficit balance of payments) catches students who see \"deficit\" and forget it is about trade, not the budget. Memory tip: spend more than you collect = deficit budget.",
 "|The demand curve for a firm operating under perfect competition is|downward sloping|vertical|horizontal|upward sloping": "A firm under perfect competition is one of very many sellers of an identical product, so it accepts the market price as given. It can sell any quantity at that price, so its demand curve is a horizontal straight line, perfectly elastic, and equal to its average revenue and marginal revenue: C. A downward-sloping demand curve (A) faces the whole industry, a monopolist or a monopolistic competitor. Memory tip: the price-taker's demand curve is flat.",
 "|One of the factors responsible for an outward shift in the PPC of a society is the|improvement in technology|reduction in production of good X for good Y|reduction in production of good Y for X|improvement in quantity of labour": "The production possibility curve (PPC) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources and technology. It shifts OUTWARD when the economy can produce more of both goods: through improved technology (A) or an increase in resources, such as a larger or better labour force (D). Both are accepted. Producing less of X to get more of Y (B, C) is a movement along the same curve: the opportunity cost of one good in terms of the other. Memory tip: more resources or better technology push the PPC out; choices move you along it.",
 "|The typical feature of a market economy is that|private ownership of means of production|public ownership of means of production|welfare maximization|profit maximization": "A market (capitalist) economy is one where prices determined by demand and supply decide what, how and for whom to produce. Its typical features are private ownership of the means of production, the profit motive (profit maximization), consumer sovereignty and competition. Profit maximization (D) and private ownership (A) are both typical features, so both are accepted. Public ownership of the means of production (B) and welfare maximization (C) belong to a socialist or command economy. Memory tip: market economy = private owners, profit motive.",
 "|Given that Qd = 40 − 4p and Qs = 12p − 24, determine the equilibrium price.|2.25|1|2|4": "Equilibrium price is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nStep 1: Set Qd = Qs: 40 − 4p = 12p − 24.\nStep 2: Collect terms: 40 + 24 = 12p + 4p, so 64 = 16p.\nStep 3: p = 64 ÷ 16 = 4: D.\nCheck: Qd = 40 − 16 = 24 and Qs = 48 − 24 = 24, so both equal 24 units. Memory tip: equilibrium = put demand equal to supply and solve.",
 "|Petrochemical industries are located in Rivers State of Nigeria because of|Rivers State is in Nigeria|Rivers State is in the Niger Delta|availability of rivers and seas|proximity to raw materials": "Industries tend to locate near their raw materials when those materials are bulky or costly to move. Petrochemical industries use crude oil and natural gas as raw materials to make plastics, fertilizers, carbon black and other chemicals. Rivers State is a major oil and gas producing area, so the Eleme petrochemical plant near Port Harcourt was sited close to its raw materials: D. Being in Nigeria or in the Niger Delta (A, B) does not by itself explain the location; it is the oil and gas that matter. Memory tip: oil-based industries sit near the oil.",
 "|In an imperfect market the monopolist can influence|price and quality|price only|quantity only|B and C": "A monopolist is the sole seller, so unlike a perfectly competitive firm it can influence the market. But it faces a downward-sloping demand curve, so it cannot fix both price and quantity at once. It can fix the price and let consumers decide how much to buy, or fix the quantity and let demand decide the price. So it can influence price only or quantity only, but not both at the same time: D. Memory tip: the monopolist chooses the price OR the quantity; demand decides the other.",
 "|In the production process, the law of diminishing returns begins to set in when|all inputs are variable|the last variable input results in a decrease in total product|the last variable input adds less in relation to the previous variable input|all inputs are fixed": "The law of diminishing returns states that as more units of a variable factor (e.g. labour) are added to a fixed factor (e.g. land), the extra output from each additional unit (marginal product) will eventually fall. It begins to set in at the point where the last variable input adds less to total product than the one before it: C. Total product falling (B) happens much later, when marginal product becomes negative. The law applies only when some inputs are fixed and some variable, not when all are variable or all are fixed (A, D). The trap: B catches students who confuse a fall in marginal product with a fall in total product. Memory tip: diminishing returns start when the next worker adds less than the last."
};
